window.__theoryNotes={"GEN_INDEX":"Based on the PrepLadder article \"Most Expected Topics for NEET PG 2026\" (Jan 2026), expanded section by section with clinical detail and short explanations of key terms. The article's figures are approximate and internally inconsistent in places (see Section 14). Verify exam-specific numbers against official analyses and current guidelines.\n\n**How to use this file:** each topic gives *what it is*, *how it presents*, *how it is confirmed*, *how it is treated*, and a *pearl* (the one line most likely to decide a question). Key terms are defined in brief at first use.\n\n# Part B: Gap-Fill Topics from the StudyDoc \"Most Asked Topics\" List (2021-2025)\n\nEvery topic below appears in the StudyDoc list. Each entry gives the core concept, the clinical or exam point, and a pearl.\n\n## 33. Additional Index: Alternate Spellings and Terms from the StudyDoc List\n\nUse this index to find a topic quickly. Each line maps the StudyDoc wording to the section where it is covered.\n- **Galactosemia / galactosaemia** (classic galactosaemia; GALT deficiency): Biochemistry, Section 16.\n- **Hyperphenylalaninemia / hyperphenylalaninaemia (BH4 deficiency):** Biochemistry, Section 16.\n- **Familial chylomicronemia / chylomicronaemia:** Biochemistry, Section 16 (lipoproteins).\n- **Gynecologic pathology:** Pathology, Section 17 (gynaecologic pathology).\n- **Macroangiopathic haemolytic anaemia (mechanical fragmentation; schistocytes):** Pathology, Section 17 and Section 6 (red-cell disorders).\n- **Cytogenetics from smear (AML subtypes):** Pathology, Section 17 (acute leukaemias).\n- **Urinary alkalinisation (salicylate poisoning):** Pharmacology, Section 18 (analgesics and toxicology).\n- **Bedaquiline eligibility in MDR-TB:** Pharmacology, Section 18 (antitubercular drugs); Medicine, Section 20.\n- **Rifampicin-resistant TB with HIV, timing of ART, IRIS:** Medicine, Section 20 (HIV and opportunistic infections).\n- **Invalid consent below 18 (POCSO):** Forensic Medicine, Section 19 (reproductive and child-protection laws).\n- **Varicella seronegativity and post-exposure management:** OBG, Section 22 (infections in pregnancy).\n- **Cardinal and Mackenrodt ligament support:** OBG, Section 22 (urogynaecology).\n- **Shoulder-dystocia manoeuvre sequence:** OBG, Section 22 (obstetric emergencies).\n- **Proprioceptive loss (dorsal column):** Physiology, Section 15 (central sensory pathways).\n- **Pain sensitisation and allodynia:** Physiology, Section 15 (sensory receptors and pain).\n- **Umbilical-vessel oxygenation (fetal circulation):** Anatomy, Section 14 (cardiovascular embryology).\n- **Air Quality Index (AQI) categories:** PSM, Section 31 (environmental health).\n- **Dietary sources of thiamine and cardiac-neurologic signs:** Biochemistry, Section 16 (B-complex vitamins).\n- **Stored lipid and lysosomal storage diseases:** Biochemistry, Section 16.\n- **Fertilisation and early development:** see Anatomy, Section 14.\n- **Sickle-beta-thalassaemia and HbS/HbA2 pattern:** Medicine, Section 20; Section 6.\n","GEN_OVERVIEW":"## 0. How the Exam Tests You\n\n- **Clinical and integrated vignettes** make up more than half of recent papers (NEET PG 2025: 49% clinical, 21% integrated).\n- **Image-based questions:** about 18-25%. Common images are ECG, peripheral smear, chest X-ray, CTG, histology slides, fundus, skin photographs and culture plates.\n- **Repeats:** about 30% of questions come from previous years, most often in Medicine and Microbiology.\n- **Basic science is tested through cases.** A nerve injury after a road accident tests anatomy; oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation in COPD tests physiology; a drug interaction tests pharmacology.\n- **Two-close-options pattern.** The wrong option is usually almost right. Decide between the two by the one word that differs (\"first-line\" vs \"second-line\", \"absolute\" vs \"relative\", \"before\" vs \"after\", \"most sensitive\" vs \"most specific\").\n\n### 0.1 The question-solving routine\n\n1. Read the last sentence first, so you know what is asked (diagnosis, next step, mechanism, drug, complication, or investigation).\n2. Pick out the age, sex, pregnancy status, drugs taken, and any \"red flag\" (fever, weight loss, bleeding, neurological deficit).\n3. Form a diagnosis before looking at the options.\n4. Eliminate options that are clearly wrong, then compare the remaining two on the exact differentiating word.\n5. Review every explanation, including the ones you got right.\n\n### 0.2 Terms used throughout\n\n- **Sensitivity:** the proportion of people with the disease who test positive. A highly sensitive test rules disease *out* when negative (SnNout).\n- **Specificity:** the proportion without the disease who test negative. A highly specific test rules disease *in* when positive (SpPin).\n- **Gold standard:** the test against which others are judged (for example, biopsy for cancer).\n- **Pathognomonic:** a finding that is almost unique to one disease.\n- **First-line:** the treatment to start in most patients, before alternatives.\n- **Absolute contraindication:** a situation in which the drug must not be used. **Relative contraindication:** the drug can be used if the benefit outweighs the risk.\n\n## 12. Study Plan and Last-Mile Revision\n\n- **Weekly rhythm:** 1 subject per day (Medicine, Surgery, OBG, Paediatrics, PSM, Pathology, Pharmacology, Microbiology), with Short Subjects in spare days.\n- **Method:** read the subject's section here; then solve 50-100 MCQs from the question bank; review each explanation; note weak topics in a logbook; revisit the logbook every 3 days.\n- **Last 2 weeks:** focus on high-yield pearls, image areas, drug list (dose, side effects, contraindications), guidelines (ACLS, NCCN, WHO, ICMR, NACO, RNTCP/NTEP), and recent PYQs.\n- **Night before:** revise pearls and tables only; no new topic.\n\n## 13. Notes on the Source and Accuracy\n\n- This quick theory is compiled from the PrepLadder \"Most expected topics for NEET PG 2026\" list, the StudyDoc \"Most Asked Topics\" list (all 19 subjects, 2021-2025), and general standard textbooks. It is a study aid, not a substitute for the textbook or the official syllabus.\n- Management, drug doses and guidelines change; verify against the latest standard references (for example, the ICMR, NACO, NTEP, WHO and AHA/ACC guidelines) before the exam.\n- Where local protocols differ (for example, gestational diabetes thresholds), both the international and the commonly used Indian thresholds are given; follow the protocol your examiner uses.\n*End of quick theory.*\n\n## 34. Final Coverage Statement\n\n- All 19 subjects in the StudyDoc list (Anatomy through Ophthalmology) are now covered, including every broad topic and the listed subtopics. Repeated and asked-once topics appear under the \"Repeated / asked-once\" entries in each subject section.\n- Where the StudyDoc list describes a question (\"image unavailable\" or \"answer options absent\"), the underlying concept is taught, because the image or option is not recoverable.\n- Pearls are for quick recall. Verify drug doses and programme details against current guidelines before the exam.\n","GEN_GLOSSARY":"## 35. Glossary of Acronyms and Abbreviations\n\nEvery abbreviation used in this file, with its full form and a short meaning.\n\n### A-C\n\n- **AAP:** American Academy of Pediatrics; source of neonatal jaundice and paediatric guidelines.\n- **ABG:** arterial blood gas; measures pH, PaO2, PaCO2 and HCO3 for acid-base assessment.\n- **ABPA:** allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis; hypersensitivity to Aspergillus with raised IgE and eosinophilia.\n- **ACE:** angiotensin-converting enzyme; converts angiotensin I to II; ACE inhibitors reduce BP and proteinuria.\n- **ADH:** antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin); increases water reabsorption in the collecting duct.\n- **ADP:** adenosine diphosphate; platelet activator; target of clopidogrel (P2Y12 antagonist).\n- **AED:** automated external defibrillator; delivers a shock for VF or pulseless VT.\n- **AFP:** alpha-fetoprotein; raised in hepatocellular carcinoma, yolk sac tumour and open neural tube defects.\n- **AKI:** acute kidney injury; abrupt fall in GFR, often over hours to days.\n- **ALA:** delta-aminolaevulinic acid; raised in lead poisoning and acute intermittent porphyria.\n- **ALK:** anaplastic lymphoma kinase; target in some lung adenocarcinomas (crizotinib).\n- **ALP:** alkaline phosphatase; raised in bone disease (Paget, osteomalacia) and cholestasis.\n- **ALARA:** as low as reasonably achievable; radiation safety principle.\n- **AMA:** anti-mitochondrial antibody; marker for primary biliary cholangitis (M2 subtype).\n- **AML:** acute myeloid leukaemia; AML cell types include myeloblasts with Auer rods.\n- **AP:** anteroposterior (X-ray projection).\n- **APGAR:** Appearance, Pulse, Grimace, Activity, Respiration; neonatal scoring at 1 and 5 minutes.\n- **APL:** acute promyelocytic leukaemia; t(15;17), PML-RARA; treated with ATRA and arsenic.\n- **ART:** antiretroviral therapy (HIV); also assisted reproductive technology in infertility.\n- **ARDS:** acute respiratory distress syndrome; bilateral infiltrates with PaO2/FiO2 below 300 (severe below 100).\n- **ARNI:** angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor (sacubitril-valsartan); used in heart failure with reduced EF.\n- **ASA:** American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA physical status grades); also aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid).\n- **ASD:** atrial septal defect; left-to-right shunt; fixed split second heart sound.\n- **AT1:** angiotensin II type 1 receptor; blocked by ARBs such as losartan.\n- **ATRA:** all-trans retinoic acid; differentiates promyelocytes in APL.\n- **AV:** atrioventricular (node or valve).\n- **AVN:** avascular necrosis (osteonecrosis); seen in femoral head, scaphoid, steroid use.\n- **BCG:** Bacillus Calmette-Guerin; live attenuated TB vaccine.\n- **BCL2:** B-cell lymphoma 2; anti-apoptotic protein overexpressed in follicular lymphoma (t(14;18)).\n- **BCR-ABL:** fusion gene in chronic myeloid leukaemia (Philadelphia chromosome, t(9;22)).\n- **BCYE:** buffered charcoal yeast extract agar; selective medium for Legionella.\n- **BIPAP:** bilevel positive airway pressure; non-invasive ventilation.\n- **BLS:** basic life support; CPR and defibrillation without equipment.\n- **BMI:** body mass index (kg/m2); used for obesity and eating disorder classification.\n- **BNP:** B-type natriuretic peptide; raised in heart failure; NT-proBNP is its inactive fragment.\n- **BP:** blood pressure.\n- **BRAF:** serine-threonine kinase; V600E mutation in papillary thyroid carcinoma and melanoma.\n- **CAH:** congenital adrenal hyperplasia; usually 21-hydroxylase deficiency.\n- **CAIS:** complete androgen insensitivity syndrome; 46,XY with female phenotype, testes and no uterus.\n- **CAMP:** test for group B Streptococcus (synergistic haemolysis with S. aureus).\n- **CBC:** complete blood count.\n- **CBNAAT:** cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification test (GeneXpert) for TB and rifampicin resistance.\n- **CBT:** cognitive behavioural therapy; first-line psychotherapy for many disorders.\n- **CCHF:** Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever (see Section 6.15).\n- **CCB:** calcium channel blocker (amlodipine, verapamil, diltiazem).\n- **CD:** cluster of differentiation; surface markers used to identify cell types (e.g. CD4, CD8, CD19, CD20, CD10, CD3).\n- **CEAP:** clinical, etiologic, anatomic and pathophysiologic classification of chronic venous disease.\n- **CECT:** contrast-enhanced computed tomography.\n- **CHF:** congestive heart failure.\n- **CICO:** cannot intubate, cannot oxygenate; an airway emergency requiring front-of-neck access.\n- **CK:** creatine kinase; raised in muscle damage (myositis, statin myopathy, rhabdomyolysis, MI).\n- **CLL:** chronic lymphocytic leukaemia; CD5 and CD19 positive B cells, smudge cells.\n- **CMV:** cytomegalovirus; owl's-eye inclusions; congenital infection causes hearing loss and periventricular calcification.\n- **CNS:** central nervous system.\n- **CoA:** coarctation of the aorta (see Section 7.3).\n- **COMT:** catechol-O-methyltransferase; degrades dopamine; target of entacapone in Parkinson disease.\n- **COPD:** chronic obstructive pulmonary disease.\n- **COX:** cyclo-oxygenase; enzyme that makes prostaglandins and thromboxane; aspirin inhibits COX-1 irreversibly.\n- **CPAP:** continuous positive airway pressure; used for neonatal RDS and sleep apnoea.\n- **CPP:** cerebral perfusion pressure (MAP minus ICP).\n- **CPT:** carnitine palmitoyltransferase; enzyme of fatty-acid oxidation; CPT deficiency causes muscle symptoms.\n- **CRAB:** hypercalcaemia, renal failure, anaemia, bone lesions; CRAB features of myeloma.\n- **CRP:** C-reactive protein; acute-phase reactant raised in inflammation and infection.\n- **CSF:** cerebrospinal fluid.\n- **CT:** computed tomography.\n- **CTEPH:** chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension.\n- **CURB-65:** pneumonia severity score (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, BP, age 65 or above).\n- **CVA:** cerebrovascular accident (stroke).\n- **C3, C4, C5, C9:** complement components; C3 low in SLE and post-streptococcal GN; C5-C9 form the membrane attack complex.\n\n### D-H\n\n- **DAA:** direct-acting antiviral (sofosbuvir, ledipasvir) for hepatitis C.\n- **DALY:** disability-adjusted life year; YLL plus YLD.\n- **DCM:** dilated cardiomyopathy.\n- **DIC:** disseminated intravascular coagulation; consumption of clotting factors and platelets, raised D-dimer.\n- **DKA:** diabetic ketoacidosis; hyperglycaemia, ketosis, high anion gap acidosis.\n- **DMSA:** dimercaptosuccinic acid (succimer); chelator for lead and renal scanning.\n- **DOAC:** direct oral anticoagulant (apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, edoxaban).\n- **DPP-4:** dipeptidyl peptidase-4; target of gliptins (sitagliptin).\n- **DRUJ:** distal radioulnar joint.\n- **DVT:** deep vein thrombosis.\n- **ECG:** electrocardiogram.\n- **ECMO:** extracorporeal membrane oxygenation; prolonged cardiorespiratory support.\n- **EEG:** electroencephalogram; hypsarrhythmia in infantile spasms; spike-and-wave in absence.\n- **EGFR:** epidermal growth factor receptor; target in lung cancer (erlotinib); mutations predict response.\n- **EIEC, EAEC, EPEC, EHEC, ETEC:** E. coli pathotypes (see Section 6.6).\n- **EMA:** eosin-5-maleimide binding test; used to diagnose hereditary spherocytosis.\n- **ERCP:** endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography; treats CBD stones and stents.\n- **ESBL:** extended-spectrum beta-lactamase; resistance to penicillins and cephalosporins.\n- **ESWL:** extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy; breaks renal stones with sound waves.\n- **ETC:** electron transport chain (mitochondrial respiration).\n- **EWSR1:** Ewing sarcoma breakpoint region 1; fused to FLI1 in Ewing sarcoma.\n- **EPS:** extrapyramidal symptoms (drug-induced parkinsonism, dystonia, akathisia).\n- **FAST:** focused assessment with sonography for trauma; bedside ultrasound for free fluid.\n- **FAD:** flavin adenine dinucleotide; riboflavin cofactor.\n- **FEV1:** forced expiratory volume in one second; reduced in obstruction.\n- **FiO2:** fraction of inspired oxygen.\n- **FLI1:** Friend leukaemia integration 1; fused to EWSR1 in Ewing sarcoma.\n- **FNAC:** fine needle aspiration cytology.\n- **FRC:** functional residual capacity; raised in obstructive disease.\n- **FVC:** forced vital capacity.\n- **GABA:** gamma-aminobutyric acid; main inhibitory neurotransmitter.\n- **GCS:** Glasgow Coma Scale; eye, verbal and motor scores; minimum 3, maximum 15.\n- **GFR:** glomerular filtration rate; about 125 ml/min normally.\n- **GI:** gastrointestinal.\n- **GLP-1:** glucagon-like peptide-1; incretin; target of GLP-1 agonists (liraglutide, semaglutide).\n- **GLUT:** glucose transporter (GLUT4 insulin dependent, GLUT2 liver and beta cells).\n- **GMS:** Grocott methenamine silver stain; detects fungi and Pneumocystis cysts.\n- **G6PD:** glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase; deficiency causes oxidative haemolysis.\n- **HACE, HAPE:** high-altitude cerebral oedema and pulmonary oedema.\n- **HBV, HCV, HDV, HEV, HAV:** hepatitis viruses A, B, C, D and E (see Section 6.11).\n- **HbA2, HbF, HbS:** haemoglobin A2 (raised in beta-thalassaemia), foetal haemoglobin, sickle haemoglobin.\n- **HCC:** hepatocellular carcinoma.\n- **HELLP:** haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets; severe preeclampsia variant.\n- **HFE:** hereditary haemochromatosis gene (C282Y mutation).\n- **HFpEF, HFrEF:** heart failure with preserved and reduced ejection fraction.\n- **HGPRT:** hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase; deficiency causes Lesch-Nyhan syndrome.\n- **HHV:** human herpesvirus (HHV-6 roseola; HHV-8 Kaposi sarcoma).\n- **HIDA:** hepatobiliary iminodiacetic acid scan; diagnoses acute cholecystitis.\n- **HLA:** human leukocyte antigen (MHC in humans); HLA-B27 in ankylosing spondylitis; HLA-B*5801 in allopurinol reactions.\n- **HMG-CoA:** 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-CoA reductase; target of statins.\n- **HNPCC:** hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer (Lynch syndrome).\n- **HPV:** human papillomavirus; types 16 and 18 high risk, 6 and 11 low risk.\n- **HRT:** hormone replacement therapy.\n- **HSG:** hysterosalpingography; X-ray of uterus and tubes with contrast.\n- **HSV:** herpes simplex virus.\n- **HUS:** haemolytic uraemic syndrome; MAHA, thrombocytopenia and AKI (EHEC, Shigella).\n\n### I-M\n\n- **IABP:** intra-aortic balloon pump.\n- **ICP:** intracranial pressure (raised in mass lesions, hydrocephalus).\n- **ICU:** intensive care unit.\n- **IE:** infective endocarditis.\n- **IGF:** insulin-like growth factor; IGF-1 is the marker for GH activity (raised in acromegaly).\n- **IGRA:** interferon-gamma release assay; blood test for latent TB.\n- **IL:** interleukin; cytokine (IL-1 and IL-6 cause fever; IL-5 drives eosinophils).\n- **IM:** intramuscular.\n- **IRIS:** immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (paradoxical worsening after starting ART).\n- **IUCD / IUD:** intrauterine contraceptive device; copper or hormonal.\n- **IUS:** intrauterine system (levonorgestrel IUS).\n- **IVF:** in vitro fertilisation.\n- **IVIG:** intravenous immunoglobulin; used in Kawasaki disease and ITP.\n- **JE:** Japanese encephalitis; Culex-borne flavivirus.\n- **JVP:** jugular venous pressure; raised in right heart failure and tamponade.\n- **LABA / LAMA:** long-acting beta-2 agonist and long-acting muscarinic antagonist (inhalers).\n- **LDH:** lactate dehydrogenase; raised in haemolysis and tissue necrosis.\n- **LMN:** lower motor neuron (anterior horn cell, root, nerve).\n- **LMWH:** low-molecular-weight heparin (enoxaparin).\n- **LVH:** left ventricular hypertrophy.\n- **MAC:** Mycobacterium avium complex; also minimum alveolar concentration of anaesthetics.\n- **MAHA:** microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia; schistocytes, raised LDH.\n- **MAO / MAO-B:** monoamine oxidase (MAO-B degrades dopamine; target of selegiline).\n- **MAOI:** monoamine oxidase inhibitor (antidepressant; tyramine interaction).\n- **MCH / MCHC:** mean corpuscular haemoglobin (and concentration); low in iron deficiency.\n- **MDR:** multidrug resistant (MDR-TB: rifampicin and isoniazid resistance).\n- **MEN:** multiple endocrine neoplasia.\n- **MGUS:** monoclonal gammopathy of uncertain significance.\n- **MI:** myocardial infarction.\n- **MIBG:** metaiodobenzylguanidine; nuclear scan for phaeochromocytoma and neuroblastoma.\n- **MMA:** methylmalonic acid; raised in B12 deficiency (not folate).\n- **MMR:** measles, mumps, rubella vaccine.\n- **MRA:** mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist (spironolactone, eplerenone).\n- **MRCP:** magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography.\n- **MRI:** magnetic resonance imaging.\n- **MSH2 / MLH1 / MSH6 / PMS2:** mismatch repair genes; mutation causes Lynch syndrome.\n- **MTP:** medical termination of pregnancy; Indian MTP Act sets gestational limits.\n- **MUAC:** mid-upper arm circumference; screens for severe acute malnutrition (below 115 mm).\n- **MUDPILES:** mnemonic for causes of high anion gap acidosis.\n\n### N-R\n\n- **NAC:** see N-acetylcysteine.\n- **NACO:** National AIDS Control Organisation (India).\n- **NAFLD / NASH:** non-alcoholic fatty liver disease / steatohepatitis.\n- **NAT:** nucleic acid testing (HIV, HBV, HCV donor screening).\n- **NK:** natural killer cell.\n- **NK1:** neurokinin-1 receptor; target of aprepitant.\n- **NMDA:** N-methyl-D-aspartate receptor; central sensitisation and memantine target.\n- **NMS:** neuroleptic malignant syndrome.\n- **NNRTI / NRTI:** non-nucleoside and nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (HIV).\n- **NNT:** number needed to treat (1 divided by absolute risk reduction).\n- **NPV:** negative predictive value.\n- **NST:** non-stress test (foetal heart monitoring in pregnancy).\n- **NTEP:** National Tuberculosis Elimination Programme (formerly RNTCP).\n- **NT-proBNP:** N-terminal pro-BNP; cardiac marker for heart failure.\n- **OHSS:** ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome.\n- **OK sign:** test for anterior interosseous nerve palsy (inability to form OK sign).\n- **P2Y12:** platelet ADP receptor; target of clopidogrel and ticagrelor.\n- **PaO2, PaCO2:** partial pressures of oxygen and carbon dioxide in arterial blood.\n- **PCI:** percutaneous coronary intervention (angioplasty and stenting).\n- **PCNL:** percutaneous nephrolithotomy; for large renal stones.\n- **PCOS:** polycystic ovary syndrome.\n- **PCR:** polymerase chain reaction; amplifies DNA.\n- **PCSK9:** proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 9; regulates LDL receptor (see 18).\n- **PDA:** patent ductus arteriosus (see 7.3).\n- **PDE:** phosphodiesterase (PDE3 inhibitors such as milrinone; PDE5 inhibitors such as sildenafil).\n- **PEFR:** peak expiratory flow rate.\n- **PEEP:** positive end-expiratory pressure (ventilator setting).\n- **PET:** positron emission tomography.\n- **PG:** prostaglandin.\n- **PKU:** phenylketonuria; phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency.\n- **PMTCT:** prevention of mother-to-child transmission (HIV).\n- **POP-Q:** pelvic organ prolapse quantification system.\n- **PPH:** postpartum haemorrhage (blood loss above 500 ml vaginal, 1000 ml caesarean).\n- **PPI:** proton pump inhibitor (omeprazole); H. pylori regimen component.\n- **PPROM:** preterm prelabour rupture of membranes.\n- **PrEP:** pre-exposure prophylaxis for HIV.\n- **PSA:** prostate-specific antigen; raised in prostate cancer, BPH and prostatitis.\n- **PSVT:** paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia.\n- **PT:** prothrombin time; prolonged by factors VII, X, V, II and fibrinogen deficiency; warfarin.\n- **PTH:** parathyroid hormone; raised in primary hyperparathyroidism.\n- **PUJ:** pelvi-ureteric junction.\n- **PV loop:** pressure-volume loop of the cardiac cycle.\n- **QT:** interval on ECG (ventricular depolarisation and repolarisation); prolongation risks torsades.\n- **RAAS:** renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system.\n- **RCT:** randomised controlled trial.\n- **RDS:** respiratory distress syndrome (neonatal hyaline membrane disease).\n- **RF:** rheumatoid factor (IgM); low specificity for RA; anti-CCP is more specific.\n- **RNA:** ribonucleic acid.\n- **ROSC:** return of spontaneous circulation.\n- **RSV:** respiratory syncytial virus; cause of bronchiolitis.\n- **RUTF:** ready-to-use therapeutic food; for severe acute malnutrition.\n- **RV:** residual volume; raised in obstructive disease.\n\n### S-Z\n\n- **SABA:** short-acting beta-2 agonist (salbutamol).\n- **SAH:** subarachnoid haemorrhage.\n- **SBP:** spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (cirrhosis); also systolic blood pressure.\n- **SCFE:** slipped capital femoral epiphysis; adolescent obese boy with hip or knee pain.\n- **SCID:** severe combined immunodeficiency.\n- **SERM:** selective oestrogen receptor modulator (tamoxifen, raloxifene).\n- **SGLT2:** sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (proximal tubule); inhibitors lower glucose and benefit heart failure and CKD.\n- **SIADH:** syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion; hyponatraemia with concentrated urine.\n- **SJS / TEN:** Stevens-Johnson syndrome (below 10 percent skin detachment) and toxic epidermal necrolysis (above 30 percent); drug reaction.\n- **SLE:** systemic lupus erythematosus.\n- **SNAP-25:** protein cleaved by botulinum toxin type A.\n- **SpO2:** peripheral oxygen saturation by pulse oximetry.\n- **SSPE:** subacute sclerosing panencephalitis; late complication of measles.\n- **SSRI:** selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor.\n- **STEMI / NSTEMI:** ST-elevation and non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction.\n- **STIs:** sexually transmitted infections.\n- **TACO / TRALI:** transfusion-associated circulatory overload and transfusion-related acute lung injury.\n- **TB:** tuberculosis.\n- **TBSA:** total body surface area (burns; rule of nines).\n- **TCBS:** thiosulphate-citrate-bile salt-sucrose agar; selective medium for Vibrio.\n- **TIBC:** total iron-binding capacity; raised in iron deficiency.\n- **TIPS:** transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt; for variceal bleeding and ascites.\n- **TLC:** total lung capacity.\n- **TLS:** tumour lysis syndrome.\n- **TMP-SMX:** trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (co-trimoxazole).\n- **TNF:** tumour necrosis factor; target of anti-TNF biologics.\n- **TOF:** tetralogy of Fallot.\n- **TRAb:** TSH receptor antibody; diagnostic in Graves disease.\n- **TSH:** thyroid-stimulating hormone (pituitary).\n- **TTF-1:** thyroid transcription factor-1; marks lung adenocarcinoma, small cell carcinoma and thyroid tumours.\n- **TTN:** transient tachypnoea of the newborn.\n- **UIP:** Universal Immunisation Programme (India).\n- **UNICEF:** United Nations Children's Fund.\n- **UTI:** urinary tract infection.\n- **VC:** vital capacity.\n- **VF:** ventricular fibrillation.\n- **VHL:** von Hippel-Lindau disease.\n- **VIA:** visual inspection with acetic acid; cervical cancer screening in low-resource settings.\n- **VLPO:** ventrolateral preoptic nucleus; promotes sleep.\n- **VSD:** ventricular septal defect.\n- **VUR:** vesicoureteric reflux.\n- **VZIG:** varicella-zoster immunoglobulin; post-exposure prophylaxis.\n- **VZV:** varicella-zoster virus.\n- **WBC:** white blood cell count.\n- **WHO:** World Health Organization.\n- **XLD:** xylose lysine deoxycholate agar; selective medium for Salmonella and Shigella.\n- **YLD:** years lived with disability.\n- **YLL:** years of life lost (premature death).\n- **Z-score:** standard deviations from the mean; used in growth charts and malnutrition (weight-for-height below -3 is severe).\n\n### Additional abbreviations used in the sections above\n\n- **A1, A3:** subtypes of blood group A (A1 is the more common; A2 is the other main subtype).\n- **AEIOU:** indications for urgent dialysis: Acidosis, Electrolytes (refractory hyperkalaemia), Intoxication, Overload (fluid), Uraemia (pericarditis, encephalopathy).\n- **ABC (animal birth control):** programme of sterilisation and vaccination for stray dogs to control rabies.\n- **ADHD:** attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder.\n- **ABCA1:** ATP-binding cassette transporter; defective in Tangier disease (low HDL).\n- **AHA / ACC:** American Heart Association and American College of Cardiology; sources of cardiac guidelines.\n- **AMPLE:** mnemonic for the history in emergencies (Allergies, Medications, Past history, Last meal, Events).\n- **ANCA:** antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody; c-ANCA (PR3) in granulomatosis with polyangiitis; p-ANCA (MPO) in microscopic polyangiitis.\n- **ARR:** absolute risk reduction (control risk minus treated risk).\n- **ATP7B:** copper-transporting gene; defective in Wilson disease.\n- **BAK:** pro-apoptotic BCL-2 family protein.\n- **BI:** bacterial index (leprosy); a measure of bacilli in skin smears.\n- **BISAP:** bedside score for pancreatitis mortality. **POEM:** peroral endoscopic myotomy for achalasia. **TEP and TAPP:** totally extraperitoneal and transabdominal preperitoneal laparoscopic hernia repair. **S1Q3T3:** ECG pattern (S wave in lead I, Q and inverted T in lead III) seen in pulmonary embolism. **CHA2DS2-VASc:** stroke-risk score in atrial fibrillation. **HACEK:** group of fastidious organisms causing culture-negative endocarditis. **MELD:** severity score for liver disease. **KDIGO:** staging system for acute kidney injury. **SIRS:** systemic inflammatory response syndrome.\n- **BCL / BCL2:** B-cell lymphoma family of anti-apoptotic proteins.\n- **BBB:** blood-brain barrier.\n- **BP180:** bullous pemphigoid antigen (BP180 and BP230).\n- **BPG / DPG:** 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-DPG); lowers haemoglobin affinity for oxygen.\n- **BRCA1:** tumour suppressor; mutations raise risk of breast and ovarian cancer.\n- **BSL:** biosafety level (laboratory containment; BSL-3 for TB).\n- **BUN:** blood urea nitrogen; raised in renal failure.\n- **C1, C4:** complement components of the classical pathway; C4 low in some SLE and hereditary angioedema.\n- **C5, C9:** complement components of the membrane attack complex; deficiency predisposes to Neisseria infections.\n- **CA:** carbonic anhydrase; inhibited by acetazolamide and dorzolamide.\n- **CD markers:** CD3 (T cells), CD4 (helper T cells), CD8 (cytotoxic T cells), CD10 (germinal-centre B cells; follicular lymphoma), CD15 and CD30 (Reed-Sternberg cells; Hodgkin), CD19 and CD20 (B cells), CD23 (CLL), CD45 (leukocyte common antigen), CD99 (Ewing sarcoma), CD8.\n- **CFTR:** cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator; chloride channel; F508 deletion is the commonest mutation.\n- **CI:** confidence interval.\n- **CPPD:** calcium pyrophosphate deposition disease (pseudogout; positively birefringent crystals).\n- **CPR:** cardiopulmonary resuscitation.\n- **CRH:** corticotropin-releasing hormone (hypothalamus; drives ACTH).\n- **CTLA-4:** cytotoxic T-lymphocyte antigen-4; immune checkpoint inhibited by ipilimumab.\n- **CYP2C19, CYP3A4:** cytochrome P450 enzymes; CYP2C19 polymorphisms affect clopidogrel activation; CYP3A4 metabolises many drugs.\n- **DBT:** digital breast tomosynthesis (3D mammography).\n- **DEXA:** dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry; bone density (T-score).\n- **DHFR:** dihydrofolate reductase; inhibited by methotrexate and trimethoprim.\n- **DI:** diabetes insipidus (central or nephrogenic).\n- **DMEK / DSAEK:** endothelial corneal transplant techniques (Descemet membrane endothelial keratoplasty).\n- **DMT1:** divalent metal transporter 1; intestinal iron uptake.\n- **DPD:** dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase; deficiency causes severe fluoropyrimidine toxicity.\n- **ECA / ICA:** external and internal carotid arteries.\n- **ECF / ICF:** extracellular and intracellular fluid compartments.\n- **ECT:** electroconvulsive therapy.\n- **EDD:** estimated date of delivery.\n- **EIA:** enzyme immunoassay (C. difficile toxin test).\n- **ER:** oestrogen receptor (positive in breast cancer); also emergency room.\n- **ESR:** erythrocyte sedimentation rate; raised in inflammation, infection, myeloma.\n- **ETEC, EPEC:** enterotoxigenic and enteropathogenic E. coli (see 6.6).\n- **FFP:** fresh frozen plasma; replaces clotting factors.\n- **FGF23:** fibroblast growth factor 23; raised in XLH and inhibits phosphate reabsorption.\n- **FGR:** foetal growth restriction.\n- **FH:** familial hypercholesterolaemia; also fumarate hydratase (renal cancer).\n- **FLT3:** tyrosine kinase; mutation in AML (target of midostaurin).\n- **FMN:** flavin mononucleotide (riboflavin cofactor).\n- **FTA / TPHA:** fluorescent treponemal antibody and Treponema pallidum haemagglutination assay (treponemal syphilis tests).\n- **FU:** fluorouracil (5-FU), a pyrimidine analogue used in colorectal cancer.\n- **G3, G4, G5:** grades (G3 and G4 in renal and neuroendocrine tumours; G5 is kidney failure stage).\n- **GALT:** galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase; deficient in classic galactosaemia.\n- **GATA1:** transcription factor; mutation in Down syndrome-associated leukaemia.\n- **GHK:** Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation (membrane potential).\n- **GIP:** glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide (incretin).\n- **GLUT2:** glucose transporter on liver, beta cells and basolateral intestine.\n- **GM2:** GM2 ganglioside; accumulates in Tay-Sachs disease.\n- **GNAS:** gene for Gs-alpha; mutation causes McCune-Albright syndrome.\n- **GP:** glycoprotein (e.g., GP IIb/IIIa, target of abciximab).\n- **GPCR:** G-protein coupled receptor.\n- **GSD:** glycogen storage disease.\n- **GSK-3:** glycogen synthase kinase-3; inhibited by lithium, which contributes to its mood-stabilising effect.\n- **H1, H2:** histamine receptor types (H1 antihistamines for allergy; H2 blockers for acid).\n- **H2S:** hydrogen sulphide; produced by Salmonella (black colonies on XLD).\n- **HAV:** hepatitis A virus.\n- **HBIG:** hepatitis B immunoglobulin (post-exposure prophylaxis).\n- **HDL:** high-density lipoprotein.\n- **HbF:** foetal haemoglobin (alpha2 gamma2).\n- **HMWK:** high-molecular-weight kininogen (contact activation pathway).\n- **HNA:** human neutrophil antigen (antibodies in TRALI).\n- **HRP:** histidine-rich protein 2 (rapid malaria antigen test for P. falciparum).\n- **HT1A, HT1B, HT3:** serotonin (5-HT) receptor subtypes; 5-HT1B and 1D are triptan targets; 5-HT3 antagonists (ondansetron) are antiemetics.\n- **HVA, VMA:** homovanillic acid and vanillylmandelic acid; urinary metabolites raised in neuroblastoma and phaeochromocytoma.\n- **ICAM:** intercellular adhesion molecule.\n- **ICDS:** Integrated Child Development Services (India).\n- **ICMR:** Indian Council of Medical Research.\n- **ICSI:** intracytoplasmic sperm injection.\n- **IDH1:** isocitrate dehydrogenase; mutated in gliomas and AML.\n- **IEA:** Indian Evidence Act.\n- **IgH:** immunoglobulin heavy chain gene locus (translocations in lymphoma).\n- **IL-4, IL-5:** interleukins; IL-4 drives IgE and Th2; IL-5 drives eosinophils (target of mepolizumab and benralizumab).\n- **IP3, DAG, PLC:** phospholipase C generates IP3 (calcium release) and DAG (protein kinase C).\n- **IOL:** intraocular lens (implanted after cataract surgery).\n- **IIIB:** FIGO stage IIIB cervical cancer (extends to pelvic side wall or causes hydronephrosis).\n- **IU:** international unit (vitamin doses, insulin).\n- **IVC:** inferior vena cava.\n- **IVP:** intravenous pyelogram.\n- **JAK, STAT:** Janus kinase and signal transducer and activator of transcription; cytokine signalling pathway.\n- **L1, L3:** Chlamydia lymphogranuloma venereum serovars (L1-L3).\n- **LA:** left atrium; or local anaesthetic.\n- **LCHAD, VLCAD:** long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase and very-long-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (fatty acid oxidation disorders).\n- **LDL:** low-density lipoprotein.\n- **LEEP:** loop electrosurgical excision procedure (cervical lesion removal).\n- **LFA:** lymphocyte function-associated antigen.\n- **LH:** luteinising hormone; surge triggers ovulation.\n- **LNG:** levonorgestrel (LNG-IUS, Mirena).\n- **LTB4:** leukotriene B4 (neutrophil chemotaxis).\n- **LYST:** lysosomal trafficking regulator; mutated in Chediak-Higashi syndrome.\n- **M2:** anti-mitochondrial antibody subtype (PBC marker).\n- **M4, M6G:** M4 is a sub-stage of acute myeloid leukaemia (myelomonocytic); M6G is morphine-6-glucuronide (active metabolite, renal failure).\n- **MAS:** meconium aspiration syndrome.\n- **MCA:** middle cerebral artery.\n- **MCHC:** mean corpuscular haemoglobin concentration.\n- **MDT:** multidrug therapy for leprosy (WHO regimen).\n- **MGIT:** mycobacteria growth indicator tube (liquid TB culture).\n- **MHC:** major histocompatibility complex (HLA in humans).\n- **MR:** mitral regurgitation; also magnetic resonance.\n- **MSH6, PMS2:** mismatch repair genes; Lynch syndrome.\n- **MYBPC3:** myosin-binding protein C; hypertrophic cardiomyopathy gene.\n- **NADP:** nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate (NADPH is the reduced form).\n- **NB:** narrowband UVB phototherapy (NB-UVB) for vitiligo and psoriasis.\n- **NCCN:** National Comprehensive Cancer Network (oncology guidelines).\n- **NCD:** non-communicable disease.\n- **NDRI:** norepinephrine-dopamine reuptake inhibitor (bupropion).\n- **NF1, NF2:** neurofibromatosis type 1 and type 2 (NF2 causes bilateral vestibular schwannoma).\n- **NIHL:** noise-induced hearing loss.\n- **NS1:** dengue non-structural protein 1 antigen (early diagnosis).\n- **NVBDCP:** National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme.\n- **O157:** E. coli serotype O157:H7 (EHEC; haemolytic uraemic syndrome).\n- **OCP:** oral contraceptive pill.\n- **ODAP:** beta-N-oxalyl-L-alpha, beta-diaminopropionic acid (neurotoxin in Lathyrus sativus).\n- **OX-19, OX-2:** Proteus antigens used in the Weil-Felix test for rickettsial infections.\n- **PAH:** pulmonary arterial hypertension.\n- **PAIR:** percutaneous aspiration, injection and re-aspiration (hydatid cyst treatment).\n- **PBC:** primary biliary cholangitis (formerly primary biliary cirrhosis).\n- **PCPNDT:** Pre-Conception and Pre-Natal Diagnostic Techniques Act (India).\n- **PD:** Parkinson disease.\n- **PDE3, PDE5:** phosphodiesterase enzymes (milrinone; sildenafil).\n- **PDK1:** pyruvate dehydrogenase kinase 1; inhibits pyruvate dehydrogenase.\n- **PEG:** polyethylene glycol (osmotic laxative; also PEG-asparaginase).\n- **PGE2:** prostaglandin E2 (fever mediator, via the hypothalamus).\n- **PKM2:** pyruvate kinase M2 isoform; favoured in cancer metabolism.\n- **PL:** phospholipid or placental lactogen (context).\n- **PLA2R:** M-type phospholipase A2 receptor; antibody target in primary membranous nephropathy.\n- **PM2.5:** particulate matter of diameter 2.5 micrometres or less.\n- **POEM:** peroral endoscopic myotomy (achalasia).\n- **PPD:** postpartum depression.\n- **PPSV23:** 23-valent pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine.\n- **PR3:** proteinase 3; target antigen of c-ANCA in granulomatosis with polyangiitis.\n- **PSV:** pressure support ventilation.\n- **PTU:** propylthiouracil (thionamide antithyroid drug).\n- **PUV:** posterior urethral valves (see Section 23 and Surgery, Section 21).\n- **RADS:** Reporting and Data System (e.g., BI-RADS for breast imaging; LI-RADS for liver).\n- **RARA:** retinoic acid receptor alpha (fused to PML in APL).\n- **RB1:** retinoblastoma gene.\n- **RCC:** renal cell carcinoma (clear cell); also used for radiocontrast in some texts.\n- **REM:** rapid eye movement sleep.\n- **S2, S4:** sacral segments (parasympathetic outflow to bladder).\n- **SARS:** severe acute respiratory syndrome (coronavirus, BSL-3 pathogen).\n- **SCC:** squamous cell carcinoma.\n- **SEGA:** subependymal giant cell astrocytoma (tuberous sclerosis).\n- **SHH, WNT:** signalling pathways; SHH (hedgehog) drives basal cell carcinoma and medulloblastoma; WNT drives colorectal and some tumours.\n- **SI:** Le Systeme International (SI units).\n- **SMX:** sulfamethoxazole (component of co-trimoxazole).\n- **SNHL:** sensorineural hearing loss.\n- **STEPS:** WHO STEPwise approach to surveillance of NCD risk factors.\n- **STI:** sexually transmitted infection.\n- **SV2A:** synaptic vesicle protein 2A; target of levetiracetam.\n- **T2R:** bitter taste receptor (taste transduction).\n- **T3, T4:** triiodothyronine and thyroxine.\n- **TAPP, TEP:** transabdominal preperitoneal and totally extraperitoneal laparoscopic hernia repair.\n- **TKI:** tyrosine kinase inhibitor (imatinib, erlotinib).\n- **TMJ:** temporomandibular joint.\n- **TOT:** transobturator tape (mid-urethral sling for stress incontinence).\n- **TPMT, NUDT15:** thiopurine methyltransferase and NUDT15; enzymes that inactivate azathioprine and mercaptopurine; deficiency causes toxicity.\n- **TREC:** T-cell receptor excision circles (newborn screening for SCID).\n- **TSST:** toxic shock syndrome toxin-1 (superantigen of S. aureus).\n- **TTTS:** twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome (monochorionic twins).\n- **UFH:** unfractionated heparin.\n- **UGT1A1:** UDP-glucuronosyltransferase; deficiency causes Gilbert syndrome and irinotecan toxicity.\n- **UPD:** uniparental disomy (both chromosomes from one parent; Prader-Willi and Angelman).\n- **USG:** ultrasonography.\n- **UVB:** ultraviolet B light (phototherapy).\n- **V2:** vasopressin V2 receptor (collecting duct; nephrogenic DI).\n- **V5, V600E:** BRAF mutation at codon 600 (V600E) in papillary thyroid carcinoma and melanoma.\n- **VCA:** viral capsid antigen (EBV serology; IgM anti-VCA in acute EBV).\n- **VEGF:** vascular endothelial growth factor (target of bevacizumab).\n- **VGCC:** voltage-gated calcium channel.\n- **VLPO:** see Physiology (sleep).\n- **XDR:** extensively drug-resistant TB (resistance to isoniazid, rifampicin, fluoroquinolone and an injectable).\n- **XLH:** X-linked hypophosphataemic rickets.\n- **XO:** karyotype 45,X (Turner syndrome).\n- **XX / XY:** karyotypes 46,XX (female) and 46,XY (male).\n- **ZPP:** zinc protoporphyrin; raised in iron deficiency and lead poisoning.\n- **CCR5, CXCR4:** chemokine co-receptors used by HIV to enter CD4 cells (CCR5 for R5 strains, CXCR4 for X4).\n- **CEA:** carcinoembryonic antigen; tumour marker for colorectal cancer.\n- **D1:** dopamine D1 receptor (basal ganglia direct pathway).\n- **DC:** dendritic cell; also direct current in defibrillation.\n- **DOTS:** directly observed treatment, short course (TB).\n- **DP / DQ:** HLA class II loci (HLA-DP, HLA-DQ).\n- **E1, E2:** oestrogen (E1 oestrone, E2 oestradiol); also context-specific labels.\n- **F508:** CFTR delta-F508 deletion.\n- **FRAX:** fracture risk assessment tool.\n- **G4:** see G3, G4, G5 above.\n- **HACE / HAPE:** see above.\n- **HDV / HEV:** see Section 6.11.\n- **HLA-B27:** see HLA.\n- **HTLV:** human T-cell lymphotropic virus (HTLV-1 causes adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma and tropical spastic paraparesis).\n- **HT1A:** serotonin 1A receptor (buspirone target).\n- **IL2RG:** common gamma chain of interleukin receptors; X-linked SCID mutation.\n- **IQ:** intelligence quotient.\n- **K1:** E. coli K1 capsule (neonatal meningitis).\n- **Ca2:** calcium (Ca2+).\n- **C3:** complement C3 component.\n- **MELD:** see above.\n- **PCV:** pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (PCV13 or PCV10).\n- **PPAR:** peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor; PPAR-alpha (fibrates), PPAR-gamma (pioglitazone).\n- **PPE:** personal protective equipment.\n- **PPROM:** see above.\n- **SMA:** spinal muscular atrophy (SMN1 gene).\n- **ALS:** amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (motor neuron disease).\n- **TEN:** see SJS/TEN above.\n- **TPMT:** see above.\n- **UIP:** see above.\n- **MAHA:** see above.\n- **IIA:** Fredrickson type IIa hyperlipidaemia (familial hypercholesterolaemia).\n- **HBsAg:** hepatitis B surface antigen (infection marker).\n- **Hb:** haemoglobin.\n- **ECG:** see above.\n\n### Remaining abbreviations\n\n- **A3:** blood-group subtype (see A1).\n- **B27:** HLA-B27 (ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis, uveitis).\n- **C4:** complement C4 (classical pathway; low in some SLE and hereditary angioedema).\n- **C9:** complement C9 (membrane attack complex component).\n- **CBD:** common bile duct.\n- **CD3, CD8, CD10, CD15, CD23, CD30, CD45, CD99:** cell surface markers (see the CD marker list above).\n- **COVID:** coronavirus disease 2019 (SARS-CoV-2); associated with mucormycosis in patients with diabetes and steroid use.\n- **CRASH:** Corticosteroid Randomisation After Significant Head injury trial (steroids in head injury are harmful).\n- **CXCR4:** HIV co-receptor (X4 strains).\n- **CYP3A4:** cytochrome P450 3A4; metabolises many drugs; inhibited by grapefruit and clarithromycin.\n- **DAG, PLC, STAT, IP3:** second messenger and signalling molecules (see signal transduction, Section 16).\n- **DPG:** 2,3-diphosphoglycerate (see BPG).\n- **DQ:** HLA class II locus (see DP).\n- **DSM:** Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-5 is the current classification).\n- **E2:** oestradiol.\n- **EBNA:** EBV nuclear antigen (appears late; positive means past infection).\n- **EPEC:** enteropathogenic E. coli (infants; attaching and effacing lesions).\n- **ERV:** expiratory reserve volume.\n- **Fe2, Fe3:** ferrous and ferric iron.\n- **GGT:** gamma-glutamyl transferase; raised in biliary disease and alcohol.\n- **H2:** see H2 receptor (histamine) above; H2S is hydrogen sulphide.\n- **H7:** E. coli serotype H7 (O157:H7).\n- **HEV:** hepatitis E virus.\n- **HR:** heart rate.\n- **HT1B:** serotonin 1B receptor (triptan target).\n- **HT3:** serotonin 3 receptor (ondansetron target).\n- **ICA:** internal carotid artery.\n- **ICF:** intracellular fluid.\n- **IDH:** isocitrate dehydrogenase (IDH1 mutation in gliomas and AML).\n- **IFA:** iron and folic acid tablets (anaemia prophylaxis).\n- **IL4, IL5:** interleukin-4 and interleukin-5.\n- **IUD:** intrauterine device.\n- **KOH:** potassium hydroxide preparation (fungal microscopy).\n- **L3:** Chlamydia lymphogranuloma venereum serovar L3.\n- **M6G:** morphine-6-glucuronide.\n- **NASH:** non-alcoholic steatohepatitis.\n- **NF2:** neurofibromatosis type 2 (bilateral vestibular schwannoma).\n- **NSAID:** non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug.\n- **NUDT15:** enzyme that inactivates thiopurines; deficiency causes toxicity.\n- **OBG:** obstetrics and gynaecology.\n- **ORIF:** open reduction and internal fixation.\n- **PABA:** para-aminobenzoic acid (sulfonamide structure).\n- **PaCO2:** partial pressure of carbon dioxide in arterial blood.\n- **PDE5:** phosphodiesterase type 5 (sildenafil target).\n- **PMS2:** mismatch repair gene (Lynch syndrome).\n- **PNDT:** see PCPNDT above.\n- **S4:** sacral segment 4.\n- **STAT:** signal transducer and activator of transcription.\n- **T4:** thyroxine.\n- **TPHA:** Treponema pallidum haemagglutination assay.\n- **V600E:** BRAF mutation at codon 600 (papillary thyroid carcinoma and melanoma).\n- **VLCAD:** very-long-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (fatty acid oxidation disorder).\n- **VMA:** vanillylmandelic acid (urinary catecholamine metabolite).\n- **WNT:** signalling pathway (colorectal cancer).\n- **XI:** factor XI (intrinsic coagulation pathway; deficiency causes bleeding of mild degree).\n- **XY:** karyotype 46,XY (male).\n- **ACC:** American College of Cardiology.\n- **ICA:** internal carotid artery.\n*End of quick theory (complete).*\n","ANAT_UNIT_01":"## 11. Integrated Patterns (High-Yield Associations)\n\n- **Sturge-Weber:** port-wine stain (trigeminal); leptomeningeal angioma; glaucoma; seizures.\n\n## 14. Anatomy\n\n- **Cranial nerves and skull foramina:**\n  - *Glossopharyngeal and vagal innervation:* the gag reflex afferent is CN IX; efferent is CN X. Taste from posterior third of tongue (CN IX).\n  - *Jugular foramen:* transmits CN IX, X, XI and the internal jugular vein. Lesions cause palatal and pharyngeal paralysis.\n  - *Superior orbital fissure:* transmits CN III, IV, V1 (ophthalmic), VI and the superior ophthalmic vein. Lesions produce complete ophthalmoplegia with a fixed pupil.\n  - *External ear sensory innervation:* auriculotemporal (CN V3), greater auricular (C2-C3), vagus (concha, auricular branch), facial nerve (small area).\n  - *Trigeminal territories and lingual nerve:* V1, V2, V3. Lingual nerve (V3) carries general sensation from anterior two-thirds of the tongue; chorda tympani (VII) carries taste.\n  - *Pearl:* a lesion of the lingual nerve causes loss of general sensation, while chorda tympani loss causes loss of taste (anterior two-thirds).\n- **Head-neck muscles and surgical anatomy:**\n  - *Submandibular gland:* lies in the submandibular triangle; the duct (Wharton) opens at the sublingual papilla; the lingual nerve crosses above the gland; the hypoglossal nerve lies deep to the gland.\n  - *Suprahyoid muscles:* digastric, stylohyoid, mylohyoid, geniohyoid. Innervation: mylohyoid and anterior belly of digastric by V3 (inferior alveolar nerve branch); stylohyoid by VII; geniohyoid by C1 via the hypoglossal nerve.\n  - *TMJ and muscles of mastication:* masseter, temporalis, medial and lateral pterygoids, all by V3. Lateral pterygoid opens the jaw.\n  - *Pearl:* the lateral pterygoid is the main muscle that protrudes and opens the jaw; loss causes deviation towards the side of the lesion.\n- **Cerebral circulation and functional neuroanatomy:**\n  - *Cerebellar divisions:* vermis (axial, gait, truncal ataxia), intermediate hemisphere (limb coordination), lateral hemisphere (planning, timing). Lesions produce ipsilateral ataxia.\n  - *Cortical language area:* Broca (expressive, inferior frontal gyrus) and Wernicke (receptive, superior temporal gyrus) areas; arcuate fasciculus connects them. Conduction aphasia results from arcuate lesion.\n  - *Circle of Willis aneurysms:* the commonest site is the anterior communicating artery; posterior communicating artery aneurysms may compress CN III (dilated pupil, ptosis, down-and-out).\n  - *Pearl:* an aneurysm of the posterior communicating artery typically compresses CN III, causing a dilated pupil.\n- **Oral cavity and tongue:** tongue innervation: general sensation (V3, lingual nerve), taste (VII and IX), motor (XII). Sublingual gland drains through the sublingual ducts; submandibular through Wharton duct.\n- **Thyroid development:** arises from the foramen caecum at the base of the tongue; descends through the thyroglossal duct, which normally disappears. Persistence leads to a thyroglossal cyst or sinus (midline, moves on swallowing and tongue protrusion).\n  - *Pearl:* a thyroglossal cyst moves upward on protrusion of the tongue.\n- **Fertilisation and early development:** fertilisation occurs in the ampulla of the fallopian tube; morula forms by day 3; blastocyst at day 5; implantation at day 6-7. Trophoblast forms the placenta.\n- **Renal histology:** glomerulus (Bowman capsule, capillaries), proximal convoluted tubule (brush border), loop of Henle (thin and thick segments), distal convoluted tubule, collecting duct. Juxtaglomerular apparatus: macula densa, granular cells (renin).\n- **Connective-tissue histology:** collagen (type I bone and skin, type II cartilage, type III reticular, type IV basement membrane), elastic fibres (elastin, fibrillin microfibrils), proteoglycans and glycosaminoglycans.\n- **Posterior abdominal wall and retroperitoneum:** organs: kidneys, ureters, aorta, IVC, duodenum (2nd-4th parts), pancreas (most), adrenals. Psoas and quadratus lumborum muscles. Lumbar plexus lies in psoas.\n  - *Pearl:* retroperitoneal structures are covered by the posterior parietal peritoneum only on their anterior surface.\n\n#### Table: Brachial Plexus Injuries Summary\n\n| Entity | Roots Involved | Etiology | Deformity & Presentation |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Erb-Duchenne Palsy | C5, C6 (Upper Trunk) | Traction during birth / shoulder dystocia | Waiter's tip / Policeman's tip hand (adducted, internally rotated, extended elbow) |\n| Klumpke Palsy | C8, T1 (Lower Trunk) | Hyperabduction (grabbing tree branch while falling) | True Claw Hand (intrinsic hand muscle paralysis) +/- Horner syndrome |\n| Winged Scapula | C5, C6, C7 (Long Thoracic Nerve) | Radical mastectomy / axillary clearance | Prominence of vertebral border of scapula on pushing against wall |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Structures traversing inside Cavernous Sinus:** ICA + Abducens nerve (CN VI). Lateral wall: III, IV, V1, V2.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Laryngeal muscles:** All intrinsic muscles innervated by Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve EXCEPT Cricothyroid (External Laryngeal Nerve).\n","ANAT_UNIT_02":"## 14. Anatomy\n\n- **Peritoneum, ligaments and lesser sac:** the lesser sac (omental bursa) lies behind the stomach and opens into the greater sac through the epiploic foramen (Winslow). The hepatoduodenal ligament contains the portal triad (bile duct, hepatic artery, portal vein). The falciform ligament attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall.\n  - *Pearl:* the epiploic foramen is the boundary of the lesser sac; the portal triad lies in the free edge of the lesser omentum.\n- **Portal venous anatomy:** portal vein formed behind the neck of pancreas by union of the superior mesenteric and splenic veins. Portosystemic anastomoses: oesophageal (left gastric to azygos), rectal (superior to inferior haemorrhoidal), umbilical (paraumbilical to epigastric), retroperitoneal.\n  - *Pearl:* caput medusae (umbilical portosystemic shunts) indicates portal hypertension.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Water under the bridge:** Ureter passes UNDER uterine artery and vas deferens.\n\n## Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Direct vs Indirect Inguinal Hernia\n\n| Feature | Direct Inguinal Hernia | Indirect Inguinal Hernia |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Relation to Inf. Epigastric | MEDIAL to inferior epigastrics | LATERAL to inferior epigastrics |\n| Pathway | Through Hesselbach's triangle (acquired) | Through deep inguinal ring into scrotum (congenital) |\n| Internal Ring Occlusion Test | Hernia still bulges out medial to finger | Hernia is PREVENTED from bulging out |\n| Coverings | Does not acquire all 3 spermatic fascial layers | Acquires internal spermatic, cremasteric, external spermatic |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Diaphragm openings:** I 8 10 Eggs At 12 -> IVC (T8), Esophagus (T10), Aorta (T12).\n","ANAT_UNIT_03":"## 14. Anatomy\n\n- **Limb neurovascular anatomy:**\n  - *Lower-limb nerve lesions:* saphenous nerve (medial leg and foot, from femoral nerve); common peroneal nerve (foot drop, injured at fibular neck).\n  - *Median nerve pen test:* hand held as a pen; the index and middle finger pads cannot be flexed (median nerve lesion at wrist). Musculocutaneous nerve lesion affects elbow flexion and lateral forearm sensation.\n  - *Posterior tibial artery:* runs behind the medial malleolus; the pulse is palpated there. Ankle landmarks include the medial malleolus, the tendon of tibialis posterior, and the medial plantar artery.\n  - *Pearl:* a foot drop with sensory loss on the dorsum of the foot indicates common peroneal nerve injury.\n- **Abdominal embryology and umbilical remnants:** falciform ligament is derived from the ventral mesentery; the median umbilical ligament is the remnant of the urachus (from allantois); medial umbilical ligaments are obliterated umbilical arteries; the round ligament of liver is the obliterated umbilical vein.\n  - *Congenital abdominal wall defects:* omphalocele (covered by peritoneum and amnion; associated with other anomalies); gastroschisis (not covered; lateral to umbilicus; no sac).\n  - *Pearl:* omphalocele has a sac; gastroschisis does not.\n- **Inguinal canal and hernias:**\n  - Boundaries: floor (inguinal ligament and lacunar ligament), roof (internal oblique and transversus fibres), anterior wall (external oblique aponeurosis), posterior wall (transversalis fascia, conjoint tendon).\n  - *Direct inguinal hernia:* through Hesselbach triangle (medial to inferior epigastric vessels); weakness in the posterior wall (conjoint tendon). Does not descend into scrotum.\n  - *Indirect inguinal hernia:* through the deep ring (lateral to inferior epigastric vessels); follows the spermatic cord; can enter the scrotum.\n  - *Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve injury:* a common complication after laparoscopic hernia repair (tacking); causes meralgia paraesthetica (numbness over the lateral thigh).\n  - *Strangulated umbilical hernia:* emergency; the umbilical ring is a site of strangulation in obese, multiparous women.\n  - *Pearl:* a direct hernia does not enter the scrotum; an indirect one does.\n\n#### Table: Upper Limb Peripheral Nerve Lesions\n\n| Nerve | Site of Vulnerability | Clinical Deformity | Sensory Deficit |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Radial Nerve | Spiral groove / Mid-shaft humerus | Wrist Drop & Finger Drop | Dorsum of 1st web space |\n| Median Nerve | Supracondylar fracture / Carpal Tunnel | Ape thumb & Hand of Benediction | Palmar aspect of lateral 3.5 digits |\n| Ulnar Nerve | Medial epicondyle / Hook of hamate | Claw Hand (Ring & Little finger) | Palmar and dorsal medial 1.5 digits |\n| Axillary Nerve | Surgical neck of humerus / Shoulder dislocation | Loss of shoulder abduction 15-90 deg | Regimental badge area on deltoid |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** Common peroneal nerve winds around fibular neck -> Injury causes Foot Drop (loss of dorsiflexion/eversion).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **NAVEL in Femoral Triangle from lateral to medial:** Nerve, Artery, Vein, Empty canal, Lymphatics.\n\n- **Anatomical snuffbox:** lateral border = tendons of abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis; medial border = extensor pollicis longus; floor = scaphoid and trapezium; contents = radial artery, superficial branch of the radial nerve, cephalic vein origin. Tenderness in the snuffbox after a fall on the outstretched hand suggests scaphoid fracture.\n\n## Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Rotator cuff:** SITS muscles = supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis; they hold the humeral head in the glenoid. Supraspinatus is the most commonly torn (starts abduction, first 15 degrees; painful arc 60-120 degrees; drop-arm sign).\n","ANAT_UNIT_04":"## 14. Anatomy\n\n- **Cardiovascular embryology:** aortic arch derivatives: 1st (maxillary artery), 2nd (stapedial artery), 3rd (common carotid and proximal internal carotid), 4th (left: aortic arch; right: proximal subclavian), 6th (left: ductus arteriosus; right: proximal pulmonary artery). Ductus arteriosus closes after birth to form the ligamentum arteriosum.\n  - *Fetal circulation:* placenta to ductus venosus to IVC, foramen ovale for right-to-left shunt, ductus arteriosus. Umbilical vein carries oxygenated blood; umbilical arteries carry deoxygenated blood.\n  - *Pearl:* the 6th arch forms the ductus arteriosus; its persistence is PDA.\n- **Skin histology:** epidermis (keratinocytes; stratified squamous keratinised), dermis (collagen, elastic fibres), hair follicles, sweat glands (eccrine and apocrine), sebaceous glands. Melanocytes lie in the basal layer.\n\n#### Table: Pharyngeal Pouch Derivatives\n\n| Pouch | Adult Anatomical Derivatives | Associated Syndrome |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1st Pouch | Eustachian tube, Middle ear cavity, Tympanic membrane internal layer | Otitis media pathway |\n| 2nd Pouch | Palatine tonsil fossa & crypts | Recurrent tonsillitis |\n| 3rd Pouch | Inferior parathyroid glands & Thymus | DiGeorge syndrome (aplasia of 3rd/4th) |\n| 4th Pouch | Superior parathyroid glands & Ultimobranchial body (C cells) | Medullary thyroid carcinoma origin |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Neural Crest Derivatives:** MOTEL PASS (Melanocytes, Odontoblasts, Thyroid C-cells, Enteric ganglia, Laryngeal, Pia/arachnoid, Adrenal medulla, Schwann cells).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Come, Let's Get Sun Burned:** Corneum, Lucidum (thick skin), Granulosum, Spinosum, Basale.\n","ANES_U1":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.7 Anaesthesia\n\n- **Stages of anaesthesia (Guedel):** analgesia, excitement, surgical anaesthesia, medullary paralysis.\n- **Induction agents:** propofol (fast, short), thiopentone (barbiturate), ketamine (dissociative; preserves airway and bronchodilation), etomidate (haemodynamically stable).\n- **Volatile anaesthetics:** halothane (hepatotoxic), isoflurane, sevoflurane, desflurane; MAC (minimum alveolar concentration).\n- **Neuromuscular blockers:** succinylcholine (depolarising; hyperkalaemia; malignant hyperthermia trigger); rocuronium, vecuronium, atracurium (non-depolarising; sugammadex reversal).\n- **Local anaesthetics:** lidocaine (intermediate), bupivacaine (long, cardiotoxic), procaine (short); toxicity: CNS first, then cardiac; treatment: lipid emulsion.\n- **Malignant hyperthermia:** ryanodine receptor defect; hypercarbia, tachycardia, hyperthermia; dantrolene.\n- **Pearl:** succinylcholine causes hyperkalaemia and is contraindicated in burns, crush and denervation injuries.\n- **Pearl:** malignant hyperthermia is treated with dantrolene; stop volatile agents and succinylcholine.\n\n#### Table: Inhalational Anesthetic Properties Comparison\n\n| Inhalational Agent | MAC (%) | Blood-Gas Partition Coeff | Unique Feature / Clinical Pearl |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Nitrous Oxide (N2O) | 104% | 0.47 | Diffusion hypoxia (Fink effect); Expands closed gas spaces 34x; B12 inhibition |\n| Desflurane | 6.0% | 0.42 (Lowest) | Fastest recovery; High vapor pressure (requires Tec 6 heated vaporizer); Pungent |\n| Sevoflurane | 2.0% | 0.65 | Induction agent of choice; Non-pungent; Forms Compound A with dry soda lime |\n| Isoflurane | 1.15% | 1.4 | Coronary steal phenomenon; Preserves cerebral autoregulation in neurosurgery |\n| Halothane | 0.75% (Most Potent) | 2.3 | Halothane hepatitis; Sensitizes myocardium to catecholamine arrhythmias |\n\n#### Table: Neuromuscular Blockers & Reversal Modalities\n\n| Drug Class / Agent | Onset / Duration | Elimination / Reversal | Key Adverse Effect |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Succinylcholine | 30-60 sec / 5-10 min | Plasma Pseudocholinesterase | Fatal Hyperkalemia in burns/denervation; Malignant Hyperthermia; Trismus |\n| Rocuronium | 60-90 sec (RSI) / 30-45 min | Hepatic/Biliary; Reversible with Sugammadex | Fastest non-depolarizing onset; Ideal for RSI when succinylcholine contraindicated |\n| Cisatracurium | 2-3 min / 35-45 min | Hofmann Elimination (organ-independent) | Drug of choice in RENAL and HEPATIC failure; No histamine release |\n| Sugammadex | Rapid (< 3 min) | Modified gamma-cyclodextrin encapsulation | Reverses Rocuronium/Vecuronium 1:1; Binds progesterone (inactivates OCPs) |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Malignant Hyperthermia Early vs Late:** Earliest sign = Precipitous, unexplained rise in End-Tidal CO2 (ETCO2) + Masseter spasm. Late sign = Core body temperature elevation (>40 deg C). DOC = IV Dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg.\n\n## 28. Anaesthesiology (additions)\n\n- **Airway management:**\n  - *Endotracheal tube confirmation by continuous capnography:* end-tidal CO2 waveform is the gold standard.\n  - *Tracheal deviation as a difficult-airway and tube-positioning concern:* tracheal deviation can indicate mass or pneumothorax.\n  - *Head-tilt-chin-lift airway manoeuvre:* opens the airway in unconscious patients (no neck injury).\n  - *Nasopharyngeal airway identification:* soft tube (Wendl); contraindicated in base of skull fracture.\n  - *Emergency front-of-neck airway and cricothyrotomy:* for cannot intubate, cannot oxygenate; scalpel-bougie-tube technique.\n  - *Pearl:* in CICO, the emergency front-of-neck airway is the rescue.\n- **Neuromuscular blockade and monitoring:**\n  - *Ulnar-nerve train-of-four monitoring:* four twitches; fade indicates non-depolarising block; TOF ratio above 0.9 before extubation.\n  - *Vecuronium reversal with neostigmine/antimuscarinic or sugammadex:* sugammadex encapsulates rocuronium and vecuronium.\n  - *Vecuronium cardiovascular stability in ischaemic heart disease:* minimal haemodynamic effect (vagolytic effects minimal).\n  - *Atracurium-related histamine release:* can cause hypotension and bronchospasm; avoid rapid injection.\n  - *Pearl:* sugammadex reverses rocuronium and vecuronium rapidly; it is not effective for suxamethonium.\n- **Malignant hyperthermia:** see Section 9.7 (dantrolene; succinylcholine and volatile triggers; ryanodine receptor).\n- **Basic life support:** compressions 100-120/min; depth 5-6 cm adult; 30:2 ratio; AED.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **ASA Fasting 2-4-6-8 Rule:** 2 hours: Clear liquids; 4 hours: Breast milk; 6 hours: Infant formula / light toast; 8 hours: Heavy fried/fatty meal and meat.\n","ANES_U2":"## 28. Anaesthesiology (additions)\n\n- **Local anaesthetics and toxicity:** see 9.7; lipid emulsion for LA systemic toxicity.\n- **Oxygenation and ventilation:** pulse oximetry; FiO2; PEEP; lung protective ventilation (tidal volume 6 ml/kg); ARDS.\n- **Perioperative prophylaxis:** cefazolin (clean surgery); antibiotic given within 60 minutes before incision; re-dose if blood loss high.\n- **Neuraxial anaesthesia:** spinal (subarachnoid; bupivacaine); epidural; complications: post-dural puncture headache (lying flat; blood patch), high block, hypotension (phenylephrine or ephedrine), epidural haematoma.\n  - *Pearl:* post-dural puncture headache is worse on sitting or standing and relieved by lying flat; epidural blood patch is definitive.\n\n## Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Local Anesthetic Class Comparison & Maximum Doses\n\n| Class / Local Anesthetic | Metabolism | Max Plain Dose | Max Dose with Epinephrine (1:200k) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Lidocaine (Amide) | Hepatic Cytochrome P450 | 4.5 mg/kg (max 300 mg) | 7.0 mg/kg (max 500 mg) |\n| Bupivacaine (Amide) | Hepatic Cytochrome P450 | 2.0 mg/kg (max 150 mg) | 2.5 - 3.0 mg/kg (max 200 mg) |\n| Procaine (Ester) | Plasma Pseudocholinesterase -> PABA | 7.0 mg/kg | 10.0 mg/kg |\n| Ropivacaine (Amide) | Hepatic Cytochrome P450 | 3.0 mg/kg | 4.0 mg/kg |\n\n#### Table: Central Venous Pressure (CVP) Waveform Signatures\n\n| Wave / Descent | Mechanical Event | Clinical Pathological Alterations |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 'a' wave | Right Atrial Contraction | Absent in Atrial Fibrillation; Giant Cannon 'a' waves in 3rd degree AV block & VT |\n| 'c' wave | Tricuspid valve bulging during RV contraction | Follows QRS on ECG |\n| 'x' descent | Atrial relaxation & downward ventricular pull | Prominent in Cardiac Tamponade |\n| 'v' wave | Passive atrial filling against closed tricuspid valve | Giant 'v' waves in severe Tricuspid Regurgitation |\n| 'y' descent | Opening of tricuspid valve & passive ventricular filling | Absent/blunted in Cardiac Tamponade; Steep 'y' descent in Constrictive Pericarditis |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Rule of 'I' for Local Anesthetics:** Amides have TWO 'i's (L-i-doca-i-ne, Bup-i-vaca-i-ne, Rop-i-vaca-i-ne); Esters have ONE 'i' (Proca-i-ne, Tetraca-i-ne, Coca-i-ne). Esters form PABA (allergic).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Apfel PONV Score:** Female, Non-smoker, History of motion sickness/PONV, Postoperative opioids. 0 = 10%, 1 = 20%, 2 = 40%, 3 = 60%, 4 = 80% PONV risk.\n","BIO_UNIT_01":"## 16. Biochemistry\n\n- **Lysosomal storage diseases:**\n  - *Gaucher disease:* glucocerebrosidase deficiency; glucocerebroside accumulates in macrophages (Gaucher cells, crumpled tissue paper cytoplasm); hepatosplenomegaly, bone pain, pancytopenia; enzyme replacement therapy (imiglucerase).\n  - *Tay-Sachs disease:* hexosaminidase A deficiency; GM2 ganglioside accumulates in neurons; cherry-red macula with normal macula surrounding; progressive neurodegeneration; onset at 6 months; no organomegaly.\n  - *Hurler syndrome:* alpha-L-iduronidase deficiency; dermatan and heparan sulfate accumulate; coarse facies, corneal clouding, hepatosplenomegaly, gibbus; death in childhood.\n  - *Pearl:* cherry-red spot with no organomegaly suggests Tay-Sachs; cherry-red spot with organomegaly suggests Niemann-Pick.\n- **Carbohydrate metabolism and inherited disorders:**\n  - *McArdle disease:* muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency; exercise intolerance, cramps, myoglobinuria; second wind phenomenon.\n  - *Classic galactosaemia:* galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase deficiency; cataracts, hepatomegaly, jaundice, E. coli sepsis; diet free of galactose (milk).\n  - *Exercise-induced GLUT4 translocation:* contraction increases GLUT4 movement to the membrane in muscle independent of insulin.\n  - *Pompe disease:* acid maltase (alpha-1,4-glucosidase) deficiency; cardiomegaly, hypotonia, early death in infantile form.\n  - *GSD type 0:* glycogen synthase deficiency; fasting hypoglycaemia; no glycogen storage in liver.\n  - *Hepatic glycogenolysis during overnight fast:* glucagon triggers glycogen breakdown; it is the first source of glucose after an overnight fast; gluconeogenesis takes over later.\n  - *Pearl:* in McArdle disease, lactate does not rise with exercise (no glycolysis from glycogen); ischaemic forearm exercise test is abnormal.\n- **Lipoprotein metabolism and disorders:**\n  - *Familial hypercholesterolaemia (Fredrickson type IIa):* LDL receptor defect; tendon xanthomas (Achilles), xanthelasma; premature coronary artery disease.\n  - *Tangier disease:* ABCA1 mutation; low HDL; enlarged orange tonsils; peripheral neuropathy.\n  - *Familial chylomicronaemia:* lipoprotein lipase or apoC-II deficiency; pancreatitis risk; eruptive xanthomas.\n  - *Pearl:* tendon xanthomas indicate familial hypercholesterolaemia (heterozygous FH).\n- **Fatty acid oxidation and fasting:**\n  - *Carnitine shuttle and long-chain fatty acid transport:* CPT-1 and CPT-2; LCHAD and VLCAD deficiency cause hypoglycaemia and cardiomyopathy during fasting; avoid fasting.\n  - *Ketone bodies as brain fuel:* acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate; the brain uses ketones during prolonged starvation; reduces glucose need.\n  - *Low insulin-to-glucagon ratio:* activates hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose tissue, releasing fatty acids for oxidation and ketogenesis.\n  - *Pearl:* in fasting, the brain switches to ketone bodies to reduce glucose demand.\n- **Pyruvate and cancer metabolism:** Warburg effect (aerobic glycolysis); pyruvate dehydrogenase inhibition by PDK1; lactate production in tumours; PKM2 isoform.\n- **Enzyme kinetics:** Km (substrate concentration at half Vmax; affinity); Vmax (maximum velocity); competitive inhibition (raises Km, Vmax unchanged); non-competitive (lowers Vmax, Km unchanged); Lineweaver-Burk plot.\n  - *Pearl:* competitive inhibitors raise Km but do not change Vmax.\n- **Ethanol metabolism:** alcohol dehydrogenase (cytosol) then acetaldehyde then aldehyde dehydrogenase (mitochondria) to acetate. MEOS (CYP2E1) is induced by chronic alcohol. Disulfiram inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase (acetaldehyde accumulation).\n  - *Pearl:* disulfiram causes a flushing reaction with alcohol.\n- **Pentose phosphate pathway and G6PD deficiency:** produces NADPH and ribose-5-phosphate. G6PD deficiency causes oxidative haemolysis (Heinz bodies, bite cells); triggers: fava beans, primaquine, sulfonamides, dapsone; X-linked.\n  - *Pearl:* in G6PD deficiency, Heinz bodies and bite cells appear after an oxidative stress; the diagnosis is confirmed by enzyme assay after the attack has resolved.\n\n#### Table: Glycogen Storage Diseases (GSDs) Summary\n\n| Type & Name | Deficient Enzyme | Key Clinical Features | Blood Glucose |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Type I (Von Gierke) | Glucose-6-phosphatase | Severe fasting hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, hepatomegaly, gout | Severe Hypoglycemia |\n| Type II (Pompe) | Lysosomal alpha-1,4-glucosidase | Massive cardiomegaly, hypotonia, early infantile heart failure | Normal Blood Glucose |\n| Type III (Cori) | Debranching enzyme (alpha-1,6) | Milder fasting hypoglycemia, normal blood lactate | Mild Hypoglycemia |\n| Type V (McArdle) | Skeletal muscle glycogen phosphorylase | Painful exercise cramps, rhabdomyolysis, myoglobinuria | Normal Blood Glucose |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Very Poor Carbohydrate Metabolism:** Von Gierke (I), Pompe (II), Cori (III), McArdle (V).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Pompe trashes the PUMP (heart). McArdle** = Muscle phosphorylase.\n","BIO_UNIT_02":"## 16. Biochemistry\n\n- **Amino-acid metabolism and inherited disorders:**\n  - *BH4-deficient hyperphenylalaninaemia:* tetrahydrobiopterin is a cofactor for phenylalanine hydroxylase; despite normal phenylalanine hydroxylase, levels are high; neurological features; treat with BH4 and L-dopa.\n  - *Alkaptonuria:* homogentisate oxidase deficiency; urine darkens on standing (black urine); ochronosis (dark cartilage) and arthritis; nitisinone.\n  - *Pearl:* alkaptonuria is an autosomal recessive disorder of tyrosine metabolism; homogentisic acid darkens urine.\n- **Glycosaminoglycans and extracellular matrix:**\n  - *Marfan syndrome:* fibrillin-1 (FBN1) defect; aortic root dilation, lens dislocation (upward), arachnodactyly.\n  - *Keratan sulphate:* corneal transparency; deficiency in macular corneal dystrophy.\n  - *Pearl:* in Marfan syndrome the lens dislocates upward (superotemporal).\n- **One-carbon, methionine and homocysteine metabolism:**\n  - *CBS deficiency (homocystinuria):* cystathionine beta-synthase requires pyridoxine; treat with pyridoxine, betaine, low-methionine diet, and cysteine supplementation.\n  - *Vitamin B12 as cofactor for methionine synthase:* deficiency causes homocysteine to rise and methylmalonic acid to rise (with B12 specifically) and megaloblastic anaemia.\n  - *Pearl:* in B12 deficiency both homocysteine and MMA are raised; in folate deficiency only homocysteine rises.\n- **Vitamin C and collagen synthesis:**\n  - *Scurvy:* prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase need vitamin C; collagen unstable; bleeding gums, perifollicular haemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, Wimberger ring on X-ray, Frankel white line.\n  - *Pearl:* scurvy shows bleeding gums, perifollicular haemorrhages and corkscrew hair.\n- **Nucleotide and purine metabolism:**\n  - *Pegloticase:* recombinant uricase used for refractory gout (converts uric acid to allantoin); G6PD testing before use (hemolysis).\n  - *Lesch-Nyhan syndrome:* HGPRT deficiency; hyperuricaemia, self-mutilation, choreoathetosis, intellectual disability; X-linked.\n  - *Methotrexate and thymidylate synthase:* folate analogue inhibits dihydrofolate reductase; reduces thymidine synthesis and purine synthesis.\n  - *Pearl:* Lesch-Nyhan is X-linked recessive; HGPRT deficiency.\n- **Heme synthesis and lead toxicity:** heme synthesised in mitochondria and cytosol; ALA synthase is rate-limiting; lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase (raised ZPP and urinary ALA); lead poisoning causes basophilic stippling.\n- **Receptors and signal transduction:** receptor types (ion channel, GPCR, enzyme-linked, nuclear); G-protein cycle; tyrosine kinase receptors (insulin receptor); desensitisation and down-regulation.\n\n#### Table: Amino Acid Inborn Errors Differential\n\n| Disorder | Enzyme Deficient | Diagnostic Metabolite | Pathognomonic Finding |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| PKU | Phenylalanine hydroxylase | Phenylalanine, phenylketones | Musty / Mousy odor, hypopigmentation, mental retardation |\n| MSUD | Branched-chain alpha-ketoacid DH | Leucine, Isoleucine, Valine | Sweet burnt-sugar / maple syrup odor from diapers |\n| Homocystinuria | Cystathionine beta-synthase | Homocysteine, Methionine | Downward lens dislocation, DVT/thrombosis, Marfanoid habitus |\n| Cystinuria | Renal COLA transporter | Cystine in urine | Hexagonal cystine kidney stones (cyanide nitroprusside positive) |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Lens Subluxation: Marfan** = UP & out; Homocystinuria = DOWN & in.\n\n## Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **I Love Vermont Maple Syrup:** Isoleucine, Leucine, Valine (MSUD).\n","BIO_UNIT_03":"## 16. Biochemistry\n\n- **Mitochondrial bioenergetics and genetics:**\n  - *ETC inhibitors:* oligomycin inhibits ATP synthase (complex V); rotenone inhibits complex I; cyanide and carbon monoxide inhibit complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase).\n  - *Kearns-Sayre syndrome:* mitochondrial deletion; ophthalmoplegia, retinitis pigmentosa, heart block; ragged red fibres.\n  - *Mitochondrial inheritance:* maternal (through the egg); heteroplasmy; variable expression.\n  - *Pearl:* mitochondrial DNA is inherited only from the mother.\n- **DNA repair disorders:**\n  - *Xeroderma pigmentosum:* nucleotide excision repair (NER) defect; UV sensitivity; skin cancer.\n  - *HNPCC (Lynch syndrome):* mismatch repair defect (MLH1, MSH2); colon and endometrial cancer; microsatellite instability.\n  - *Pearl:* Lynch syndrome is the commonest hereditary colorectal cancer.\n- **Molecular diagnostic methods:** PCR (amplification); Southern blot (DNA); Northern (RNA); Western (protein); FISH (chromosomal location); karyotyping; microarray (copy-number variants); next-generation sequencing.\n- **Mutations and molecular genetics:** missense, nonsense, frameshift, splice-site; trinucleotide repeat expansions (Huntington CAG, Fragile X CGG); imprinting (Prader-Willi paternal deletion, Angelman maternal deletion).\n- **Chromatin, telomeres and cellular ageing:** heterochromatin (silent) vs euchromatin (active); histone acetylation promotes transcription; telomerase maintains telomere length; telomere shortening limits cell division (Hayflick limit).\n\n#### Table: DNA Repair Pathways & Human Syndromes\n\n| Pathway | Type of Damage Repaired | Key Enzymes | Associated Syndrome |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Nucleotide Excision Repair (NER) | Bulky pyrimidine / thymine dimers | Endonucleases | Xeroderma Pigmentosum (photosensitivity, cancer) |\n| Mismatch Repair (MMR) | Mismatched replication errors | MSH2, MLH1 | Lynch syndrome / HNPCC (microsatellite instability) |\n| Base Excision Repair (BER) | Deaminated bases (uracil from cytosine) | Glycosylase, AP-Endonuclease | Protects against spontaneous deamination |\n| Non-Homologous End Joining | Double-strand DNA breaks | ATM kinase, Ku proteins | Ataxia-Telangiectasia (radiation sensitivity) |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **SNOW DROP: Southern** = DNA, Northern = RNA, Western = Protein.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Trinucleotide repeats:** Hunting 4 (CAG) Animals (GAA in Friedreich) With Fragile (CGG) Myotonic (CTG) Dystrophy.\n\n## Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Genetic code:** triplet, non-overlapping, comma-less and (almost) universal; degenerate (redundant): 61 sense codons code for 20 amino acids, mostly differing at the third base (wobble). AUG is the start codon (methionine); UAA, UAG and UGA are stop codons. Mitochondrial DNA uses some different codons.\n","BIO_UNIT_04":"## 10. High-Yield Image Areas\n\n- **Skull X-ray:** beaten silver (Paget); copper beaten (Paget); thumbprinting (raised ICP).\n\n## 16. Biochemistry\n\n- **B-complex vitamins:**\n  - *Thiamine (B1):* cofactor for transketolase (TPP), pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. Deficiency: beriberi (wet = cardiac failure; dry = peripheral neuropathy), Wernicke encephalopathy (ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, confusion), Korsakoff psychosis. Transketolase assay (erythrocyte) is the functional test.\n  - *Niacin (B3):* NAD and NADP. Deficiency: pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhoea, dementia). Maize-based diet causes deficiency (tryptophan poor, niacin locked). Flushing is a common adverse effect of nicotinic acid (prostaglandin D2 mediated); aspirin reduces flushing.\n  - *Biotin (B7):* carboxylase cofactor (pyruvate carboxylase, acetyl-CoA carboxylase). Avidin in raw egg white binds biotin; cooked eggs are safe.\n  - *Pyridoxine (B6):* cofactor for transaminases and decarboxylases. Isoniazid causes deficiency; neuropathy. Treat with pyridoxine.\n  - *Riboflavin (B2):* FAD and FMN. Deficiency assessed by erythrocyte glutathione reductase activity (increase after FAD addition).\n  - *Pearl:* isoniazid neuropathy is prevented by pyridoxine.\n- **Fat-soluble vitamins:**\n  - *Vitamin K:* gamma-carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S. Deficiency: bleeding, prolonged PT (factor VII falls first). Warfarin inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase.\n  - *Vitamin A deficiency:* phrynoderma (follicular hyperkeratosis); night blindness; xerophthalmia (conjunctival and corneal dryness, Bitot spots).\n  - *Pearl:* factor VII has the shortest half-life, so PT rises first in vitamin K deficiency.\n- **Nutrition and malabsorption (B12):**\n  - *B12 after ileal resection:* intrinsic factor-B12 complex is absorbed in the terminal ileum; resection causes B12 deficiency (megaloblastic anaemia, neuropathy).\n  - *B12 after gastrectomy:* loss of parietal cells (intrinsic factor).\n  - *Gluten-free diet for coeliac disease:* gliadin triggers an immune response; a strict gluten-free diet is the treatment.\n  - *Pearl:* B12 is absorbed only in the terminal ileum with intrinsic factor.\n- **Minerals and trace elements:** zinc (over 200 enzymes; deficiency: acrodermatitis enteropathica, poor wound healing, hypogonadism); copper (Wilson disease, Menkes disease); selenium (glutathione peroxidase); iodine (thyroxine); fluoride (dental enamel, skeletal fluorosis).\n  - *Pearl:* Menkes disease is copper deficiency (kinky hair, neurodegeneration); Wilson disease is copper excess.\n\n#### Table: High-Yield Vitamin Deficiencies\n\n| Vitamin | Active Coenzyme | Deficiency Manifestations | High-Risk Group |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| B1 (Thiamine) | Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP) | Beriberi (Wet = Heart failure, Dry = Neuropathy), Wernicke-Korsakoff | Chronic alcoholics, malnutrition |\n| B3 (Niacin) | NAD+, NADP+ | Pellagra (Dermatitis Casal necklace, Diarrhea, Dementia) | Corn diets, Hartnup, Carcinoid |\n| B12 (Cobalamin) | Methylcobalamin, Adenosylcobalamin | Megaloblastic anemia, Subacute Combined Degeneration of cord | Strict vegans, Pernicious anemia |\n| C (Ascorbic acid) | Ascorbate | Scurvy (swollen bleeding gums, corkscrew hair, petechiae) | Elderly 'tea-and-toast' diet |\n| D (Calcitriol) | 1,25-(OH)2-D3 | Rickets (children: rachitic rosary, bowed legs), Osteomalacia | Lack of sunlight, renal failure |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Vitamin K dependent factors:** 1972 (10, 9, 7, 2) + Protein C & S.\n","DERM_UNIT_01":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.3 Dermatology\n\n- **Psoriasis:** silvery scales; Auspitz sign; Koebner phenomenon; nail pitting; topical steroids, vitamin D analogues, methotrexate, biologics (anti-TNF, IL-17, IL-23).\n- **Pemphigus vulgaris:** intraepidermal (acantholysis); anti-desmoglein 3 antibodies; Nikolsky sign positive; mucosal involvement; steroids plus rituximab or azathioprine.\n- **Bullous pemphigoid:** subepidermal; anti-BP180 antibodies; elderly; tense blisters; steroids.\n- **Acne vulgaris:** Propionibacterium acnes (Cutibacterium acnes); comedones; isotretinoin for severe disease (teratogenic).\n- **Vitiligo:** loss of melanocytes; autoimmune; Wood lamp; topical steroids, phototherapy (NB-UVB), melanocyte transplantation.\n- **Pearl:** Nikolsky sign is positive in pemphigus (skin peels with lateral pressure); negative in pemphigoid.\n- **Pearl:** Koebner phenomenon (new lesions at sites of trauma) is seen in psoriasis, vitiligo, lichen planus.\n\n#### Table: Autoimmune Blistering Diseases Master Comparison\n\n| Parameter | Pemphigus Vulgaris | Bullous Pemphigoid | Dermatitis Herpetiformis |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Target Antigen | Desmoglein-3 & 1 (Desmosomes) | BP180 (Type XVII Collagen) & BP230 | Epidermal Transglutaminase (eTG / TG3) |\n| Blister Morphology | FLACCID, fragile blisters; readily ruptures | TENSE, firm, durable bullae; pruritic | GROUPED, tiny excoriated vesicles |\n| Cleavage Plane | INTRAPIDERMAL (Suprabasal acantholysis) | SUBEPIDERMAL (Lamina lucida) | SUBEPIDERMAL (Dermal papillae tips) |\n| Nikolsky's Sign | STRONGLY POSITIVE (sliding shear) | NEGATIVE (intact skin) | NEGATIVE |\n| Mucosal Lesions | PROMINENT (> 70-80% oral erosions precede) | RARE (< 10-20% mild involvement) | ABSENT |\n| DIF Pattern | INTERCELLULAR 'FISHNET' IgG & C3 | CONTINUOUS LINEAR BMZ IgG & C3 | GRANULAR IgA in Dermal Papillae Tips |\n| Associated Disease | None (autoimmune) | Neurological disorders (Parkinson's, Stroke) | CELIAC DISEASE (Gluten enteropathy) |\n| Treatment of Choice | High-dose Oral Steroids + Rituximab | High-potency Topical Clobetasol cream | Oral DAPSONE + Strict Gluten-Free Diet |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Pemphigus vs Pemphigoid:** Pemphigus is 'Vulgar' (Flaccid, fishnet, falls apart = Nikolsky+). Pemphigoid is 'Polite' (Tense, tidy line at BMZ, stays intact = Nikolsky-).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **SJS vs TEN BSA:** SJS < 10% BSA; Overlap 10-30% BSA; TEN > 30% BSA. Nikolsky is positive in both! Stop culprit drug immediately.\n\n### 9.9 Short-subject quick pearls\n\n- Nikolsky sign: pemphigus.\n- Koebner: psoriasis, vitiligo, lichen planus.\n\n## 10. High-Yield Image Areas\n\n- **Dermatology:** dermatitis herpetiformis (IgA); Auspitz sign (psoriasis); Herald patch (pityriasis rosea); Koilonychia (iron deficiency).\n\n## 25. Dermatology (additions)\n\n- **Papulosquamous disorders:**\n  - *Nail psoriasis:* irregular pitting and oil-drop sign (yellow-red discolouration under nail plate).\n  - *Pityriasis rubra pilaris:* orange-red plaques, islands of sparing, palmoplantar keratoderma; vitamin A analogues.\n  - *Oral lichenoid eruption due to beta-blockers:* drug-induced; stop the drug.\n  - *Pearl:* oil-drop sign is typical of psoriatic nail disease.\n- **Repeated / asked-once dermatology:**\n  - *Autoimmune blistering disease:* pemphigus vs pemphigoid (see 9.3).\n  - *Leprosy:* see microbiology; tuberculoid and lepromatous.\n  - *Bacterial STIs:* chlamydia, gonorrhoea, syphilis (see microbiology); chancroid (painful ulcer, Haemophilus ducreyi; azithromycin).\n  - *Disorders of skin appendages:* hair (alopecia areata, androgenetic alopecia), nails (onychomycosis), sweat glands (hyperhidrosis).\n  - *Pigmentary disorders:* vitiligo, melasma, post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation.\n  - *Cutaneous connective-tissue disease:* lupus (discoid, SLE), dermatomyositis (heliotrope rash, Gottron papules), scleroderma.\n  - *Eczematous and contact dermatitis:* atopic (flexural), allergic contact (patch test, nickel); irritant.\n  - *Melanocytic lesions and melanoma:* Breslow depth; ABCDE; sentinel node.\n  - *Nutritional dermatoses:* pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhoea, dementia); zinc deficiency (acrodermatitis enteropathica); vitamin C (perifollicular haemorrhage).\n  - *Pearl:* ABCDE criteria for melanoma (asymmetry, border, colour, diameter, evolution).\n","DERM_UNIT_02":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.3 Dermatology\n\n- **Leprosy:** see microbiology; cutaneous lesions; neural involvement.\n- **Scabies:** Sarcoptes scabiei; burrows in finger webs; permethrin 5% or oral ivermectin.\n\n### 9.9 Short-subject quick pearls\n\n- Succinylcholine: hyperkalaemia.\n\n## 10. High-Yield Image Areas\n\n- **Pearl:** Councilman bodies are apoptotic hepatocytes in viral hepatitis.\n\n## 25. Dermatology (additions)\n\n- **Viral sexually transmitted infections:**\n  - *Anogenital warts:* low-risk HPV types 6 and 11; cauliflower-like; podophyllin, imiquimod, cryotherapy.\n  - *Genital herpes:* painful grouped vesicles or shallow ulcers; acyclovir or valacyclovir.\n  - *Orolabial herpes simplex:* multinucleated giant cells with intranuclear inclusions on Tzanck smear (Cowdry A).\n  - *Pearl:* Tzanck smear shows multinucleated giant cells with intranuclear inclusions (HSV, VZV).\n- **Cutaneous bacterial infections:**\n  - *Acute paronychia with ascending lymphangitis:* drainage and antistaphylococcal therapy.\n  - *Cutaneous anthrax:* McFadyean-positive encapsulated Bacillus anthracis; painless black eschar (with surrounding oedema).\n  - *Lupus vulgaris:* scarred facial plaque and tissue-based confirmation of cutaneous tuberculosis (apple-jelly nodules; diascopy).\n  - *Pearl:* lupus vulgaris shows apple-jelly nodules on diascopy.\n- **Superficial and deep fungal infections:**\n  - *Hair perforation test:* T. mentagrophytes vs T. rubrum (perforation of hair in vitro).\n  - *Kerion:* inflammatory tinea capitis after zoophilic dermatophyte exposure (T. mentagrophytes, from animals); boggy plaque.\n  - *Black-grain eumycetoma:* painless swelling, draining sinuses, fungal hyphae (Madurella, Pseudallescheria); surgery and antifungals.\n  - *Pearl:* eumycetoma is painless and has grains in the sinuses.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Cutaneous Malignancies Diagnostic Profile\n\n| Malignancy | Anatomical Predilection | Key Radiographic / Clinical Signs | Histopathology & Genetics |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC) | Face ABOVE line from earlobe to lip (#1 cancer) | Pearly translucent papule + ROLLED BEADED BORDER + Telangiectasia | Basaloid nests + PERIPHERAL PALISADING + Retraction artifact; PTCH1 (Hedgehog) |\n| Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) | LOWER LIP, ears, hands, Marjolin burn scar | Hyperkeratotic crusted ulcer with everted edges; Actinic keratosis | Atypical squamous cells + INTERCELLULAR BRIDGES + KERATIN PEARLS; TP53 |\n| Malignant Melanoma | Back (men), Legs (women), Palms/soles (dark-skinned) | ABCDE criteria + Evolving lesion + Variegated pigmentation | BRESLOW DEPTH is #1 prognostic factor; BRAF V600E; Sentinel lymph node biopsy |\n| Mycosis Fungoides (CTCL) | 'Bathing-suit' buttocks, thighs, unexposed trunk | Patch -> Plaque -> Tumor stages; Sezary syndrome erythroderma | EPIDERMOTROPISM + PAUTRIER'S MICROABSCESSES + CEREBRIFORM CD4+ T-cell nuclei |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Leprosy Polarity: TT** = High CMI (Th1), Lepromin+, Single patch. LL = Low CMI (Th2), Lepromin-, 'Leonine facies', Globi bacilli.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Melanoma ABCDE:** Asymmetry, Border irregular, Color variegation, Diameter > 6mm, EVOLVING (#1 sign!). Breslow depth determines excision margin (<= 1mm = 1cm; > 2mm = 2cm + SLNB).\n","ENT_UNIT_01":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.2 ENT (Otorhinolaryngology)\n\n- **Otitis media:** acute (Streptococcus pneumoniae, H. influenzae, Moraxella); amoxicillin; otitis media with effusion (glue ear).\n- **Otosclerosis:** fixation of stapes footplate; conductive hearing loss; Schwartze sign; Rinne negative; stapedectomy.\n- **Meniere disease:** endolymphatic hydrops; vertigo, tinnitus, low frequency sensorineural hearing loss; betahistine.\n- **Vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma):** CP angle; sensorineural hearing loss; NF2.\n- **Tests:** Rinne (air conduction more than bone conduction, normal; negative in conductive loss); Weber (lateralises to the better ear in sensorineural, to the worse ear in conductive loss); Schwabach.\n- **Laryngeal carcinoma:** smoking, alcohol; hoarseness; glottic (commonest, early hoarseness) vs supraglottic (late, lymph nodes).\n- **Pearl:** Rinne negative (bone conduction greater than air conduction) indicates conductive hearing loss.\n- **Pearl:** Weber lateralises to the affected ear in conductive loss, and to the unaffected ear in sensorineural loss.\n\n#### Table: Tuning Fork Tests (512 Hz) Interpretation\n\n| Clinical Condition | Rinne Test (Right / Left) | Weber Test Lateralization | Absolute Bone Conduction |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Normal Hearing | Positive in both (AC > BC) | No lateralization (midline) | Normal in both ears |\n| Right Conductive Hearing Loss | NEGATIVE in Right (BC > AC) | Lateralizes to the RIGHT (affected ear) | Normal in right ear |\n| Right Sensorineural Hearing Loss | Positive in both ears (AC > BC) | Lateralizes to the LEFT (normal better ear) | Reduced in right ear |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Weber:** Lateralizes to Diseased ear in Conductive; Lateralizes to Better ear in Sensorineural.\n\n### 9.9 Short-subject quick pearls\n\n- Rinne negative: conductive loss; Weber lateralises to better ear in SNHL.\n\n## 29. ENT (additions)\n\n- **Audiology and hearing rehabilitation:**\n  - *Cochlear tinnitus and high-frequency hearing loss:* sensorineural (noise, ototoxic drugs, presbyacusis).\n  - *Rinne test pattern for 30-45 dB conductive loss:* bone conduction greater than air conduction (negative Rinne); conductive loss of 30-45 dB.\n  - *Rinne interpretation from an otoscopic lesion:* tympanic membrane perforation or effusion produces conductive loss.\n  - *Rinne/Weber pattern in unilateral conductive hearing loss:* Rinne negative on the affected side; Weber lateralises to the affected ear.\n  - *Cochlear implant components and electrodes:* external processor and microphone; internal receiver and electrode array in cochlea.\n  - *Pearl:* cochlear implant is indicated for profound bilateral sensorineural deafness with poor benefit from hearing aids.\n- **Adenotonsillar disease and surgery:**\n  - *Topical mitomycin C for subglottic stenosis:* prevents restenosis or scar formation after laryngotracheal surgery.\n  - *Primary post-tonsillectomy haemorrhage management:* within 24 hours; control bleeding; return to theatre.\n  - *Referred otalgia via the glossopharyngeal nerve:* tonsil or tongue base pathology; ear pain without ear lesion.\n  - *Pearl:* primary post-tonsillectomy haemorrhage occurs within 24 hours; secondary after 24 hours (infection).\n- **Repeated / asked-once ENT:**\n  - *Epistaxis:* Little area (Kiesselbach plexus); anterior packing; cautery; posterior (Woodruff area) bleeding needs posterior packing or embolisation.\n  - *Laryngeal and laryngeal cancer:* see Medicine and ENT (glottic early hoarseness).\n  - *Middle-ear anatomy and surgery:* ossicles (malleus, incus, stapes); tympanoplasty; mastoidectomy.\n  - *Nasal surgery and trauma:* septal haematoma (drain urgently); nasal fracture reduction.\n  - *Vestibular and cerebellopontine angle disorders:* vestibular neuritis; BPPV (Dix-Hallpike test; Epley manoeuvre); acoustic neuroma.\n  - *Otitis media and complications:* mastoiditis; cholesteatoma; intracranial complications.\n  - *Salivary gland disease and surgery:* sialolithiasis; parotid tumours (see 9.1 surgery); facial nerve.\n  - *Oesophageal foreign bodies:* coins; button batteries; urgent removal.\n  - *Congenital head and neck lesions:* thyroglossal, branchial cleft (second arch), dermoid cysts.\n  - *Otosclerosis:* see 9.2.\n  - *ENT instruments:* Killian, Boyle-Davis, Jackson; Hartmann forceps; Sim's speculum.\n  - *Oral premalignant lesions:* leukoplakia (white plaque; biopsy); erythroplakia (red; high malignant risk); oral submucous fibrosis (areca nut).\n  - *Rhinitis and nasal inflammatory disease:* allergic rhinitis (antihistamines, intranasal steroids); nasal polyps.\n  - *Pearl:* BPPV is diagnosed with the Dix-Hallpike test and treated with the Epley manoeuvre.\n","ENT_UNIT_02":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.2 ENT (Otorhinolaryngology)\n\n- **Epistaxis:** Little area (Kiesselbach plexus); nasal packing; cauterisation.\n- **Nasopharyngeal carcinoma:** EBV; cervical lymph node metastasis; Ryle.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **JNA:** Adolescent male + Recurrent severe epistaxis. BIOPSY IS CONTRAINDICATED!\n\n## 29. ENT (additions)\n\n- **Nasopharyngeal disease and tumours:**\n  - *Juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma:* adolescent males; epistaxis; nasal obstruction; highly vascular; contrast CT or MRI; embolisation before surgery; avoid biopsy.\n  - *Fossa of Rosenmuller:* lateral wall of nasopharynx; common site for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.\n  - *Pearl:* biopsy of juvenile angiofibroma is avoided because of massive haemorrhage.\n- **Sinonasal disease and drainage:**\n  - *Frontal sinus mucocele:* expansion of sinus; bony erosion; drainage via endoscopic surgery.\n  - *Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease and Samter triad:* see Medicine.\n  - *Ethmoid sinus obstruction on coronal CT:* ethmoidal air-cell opacification; osteomeatal complex.\n  - *Pearl:* obstruction of the osteomeatal complex causes sinusitis.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Antrochoanal vs Ethmoidal Nasal Polyps\n\n| Feature | Antrochoanal Polyp | Ethmoidal Polyps |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Origin | Maxillary antrum (exits via accessory ostium) | Ethmoidal air cells / middle meatus |\n| Number & Laterality | SOLITARY and UNILATERAL | MULTIPLE and BILATERAL ('bunch of grapes') |\n| Age & Etiology | Young adults (10-25 yrs), Non-allergic (infection) | Adults (> 30 yrs), Allergic / Asthma (Samter triad) |\n| Direction of Growth | Grows backwards into Choana and Nasopharynx | Grows forwards into Anterior Nasal Cavity |\n| Treatment | Endoscopic FESS excision (complete removal) | Endoscopic FESS + Post-operative topical steroids |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **CSF Rhinorrhea:** Beta-2 Transferrin (Tau protein) is the gold standard confirmatory marker.\n","ENT_UNIT_03":"## 29. ENT (additions)\n\n- **Laryngeal anatomy and nerve palsy:**\n  - *Bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy after thyroidectomy:* airway obstruction (bilateral abductor paralysis); urgent airway management; tracheostomy.\n  - *External superior laryngeal nerve injury and cricothyroid dysfunction:* voice change (loss of high pitch); cricothyroid muscle innervation.\n  - *Endoscopic identification of epiglottis, vallecula, piriform sinus and thyroid cartilage:* landmarks for laryngoscopy and lesions.\n  - *Pearl:* the superior laryngeal nerve (external branch) supplies cricothyroid; injury causes loss of high pitch.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Quinsy:** Uvula pushed to CONTRALATERAL side, hot potato voice, trismus.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Ludwig's Angina** = Bilateral submandibular + sublingual cellulitis. Protect the airway!\n\n## Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Acute Epiglottitis vs Croup (Laryngotracheobronchitis)\n\n| Parameter | Acute Epiglottitis | Croup (Laryngotracheobronchitis) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Etiological Agent | Haemophilus influenzae type b (bacteria) | Parainfluenza virus Type 1 (virus) |\n| Age Group | Children 2 to 7 years | Infants 6 months to 3 years |\n| Clinical Presentation | Tripod position, drooling, severe dysphagia, toxic | Barking seal-like cough, hoarseness, low-grade fever |\n| Radiological Sign | 'THUMBPRINT' sign on lateral neck radiograph | 'STEEPLE' sign on AP neck radiograph |\n| Immediate Management | Airway protection on standby + IV Ceftriaxone | Nebulized Racemic Epinephrine + Oral Dexamethasone |\n","FMT_UNIT_01":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.5 Forensic medicine\n\n- **Post-mortem changes:** early (algor mortis, livor mortis, rigor mortis, corneal clouding); late (putrefaction, adipocere, mummification, skeletonisation). Rigor mortis begins in heart and eyelids, and passes down; sets in around 2-6 hours; passes off in 24-36 hours.\n- **Livor mortis:** fixed after 6-12 hours; colour cherry red in carbon monoxide poisoning and cyanide poisoning (cyanide: bright pink).\n- **Injuries:** abrasion (superficial), contusion (bruise), laceration (torn), incised (sharp, longer than deep), stab (depth more than length), gunshot wound (entry: inverted margins, abrasion collar; exit: everted, larger).\n- **Asphyxial deaths:** hanging (ligature mark, typically above the thyroid cartilage, oblique); strangulation (ligature below thyroid, horizontal); smothering; drowning (froth, emphysema, Paltauf haemorrhages).\n- **Pearl:** the hanging mark is oblique, above the thyroid cartilage; the strangulation mark is horizontal, below the thyroid cartilage.\n- **Pearl:** cherry-red livor mortis suggests carbon monoxide poisoning.\n\n#### Table: Hanging vs Ligature Strangulation Autopsy Differences\n\n| Parameter | Ante-Mortem Hanging | Ligature Strangulation |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ligature Mark | Oblique, non-continuous, highest at nape (knot) | Horizontal, continuous, encircles lower neck |\n| Level of Mark | Above thyroid cartilage (between larynx and chin) | Below or at the level of thyroid cartilage |\n| Hyoid Bone Fracture | Rare in young adults (< 15-20%) | Common, especially in throttling (> 50%) |\n| Facial Signs | Pale face with protruding tongue | Congested, livid, cyanotic face with petechiae |\n| Manner of Death | Almost always SUICIDE | Almost always HOMICIDE |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Lividity Colors: Cherry Red** = CO; Bright Pink = Cyanide; Chocolate Brown = Methemoglobin.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Tissue Bridging** = Pathognomonic of Blunt Laceration (absent in sharp incised wounds).\n\n- **Putrefaction (green patch):** the earliest external sign of decomposition is greenish discolouration over the right iliac fossa (caecum region, where bacteria are abundant and the bowel is near the abdominal wall), seen at about 24-48 hours.\n\n## 19. Forensic Medicine (additions)\n\n- **Wounds and traumatic head injury:**\n  - *Zasko phenomenon:* postmortem supravital contraction of muscles after mechanical stimulation (not rigor mortis).\n  - *Defence wounds of the hand:* incised or stab wounds on palms and back of hands and forearms from fending off attacks.\n  - *Incised-looking laceration over bone:* a laceration that looks incised because the scalp is thin over bone; bridging vessels across the wound.\n  - *Diffuse axonal injury:* rotational acceleration; shearing of axons; coma without mass lesion; poor prognosis.\n  - *Pearl:* defence wounds indicate the victim resisted an attack.\n- **Postmortem changes:**\n  - *Adipocere:* saponification of body fat in moist conditions; grey-white waxy substance.\n  - *Sequence of rigor mortis:* begins in heart and eyelids (about 2 hours), then face, neck, upper limbs, lower limbs; passes off in the same order.\n  - *Persistently high postmortem temperature in septicaemia:* ongoing heat production after death.\n  - *Marbling of skin in putrefaction:* green discolouration of abdomen, then spreads; marbling is a vascular pattern from haemoglobin breakdown.\n  - *Pearl:* rigor mortis sets in around 2-6 hours, is complete at 12 hours, and passes off after 24-36 hours.\n- **Mechanical asphyxia:**\n  - *Manual strangulation (throttling):* neck compression by hand; fingertip bruising on neck; petechiae; hyoid fracture.\n  - *Ligature strangulation vs hanging:* strangulation mark horizontal and below the thyroid cartilage; hanging mark oblique and above the thyroid cartilage.\n  - *Pearl:* manual strangulation may leave fingertip (nail) marks and bruising on the neck.\n- **Legitimacy and paternity:**\n  - *Posthumous child:* born after the death of the father; legitimacy depends on conception time (within 280 days).\n  - *Superfetation:* conception of a second foetus while already pregnant; rare; the second pregnancy is at a later time.\n  - *Heteropaternal superfecundation:* two sperm from different fathers fertilise two ova in the same cycle.\n  - *Pearl:* heteropaternal superfecundation is the fertilisation of two ova by sperm from different males during one cycle.\n- **Medicolegal autopsy:**\n  - *Police inquest autopsy:* conducted when the death is unnatural or suspicious (police request).\n  - *Letulle en-masse technique:* removal of the heart, lungs, liver and kidneys in one block.\n  - *Rokitansky in-situ technique:* organs removed one by one in place (useful for infectious bodies such as HIV or hepatitis).\n  - *Pearl:* the Rokitansky technique is preferred for infectious cadavers.\n- **Drowning, firearm injuries, toxic alcohol, chemical disasters, age estimation, animal poisons and burns:**\n  - *Drowning:* froth, emphysema, Paltauf haemorrhages (subpleural), washerwoman hands; diatom test (unreliable).\n  - *Firearm injuries:* entry (inverted margins, abrasion collar, tattooing near range), exit (everted, larger, irregular); blackening and singeing.\n  - *Toxic alcohol poisoning:* methanol (optic nerve damage, metabolic acidosis; treat with ethanol or fomepizole); ethylene glycol (calcium oxalate crystals in urine, renal failure).\n  - *Chemical-disaster response:* decontamination, mass casualty triage, PPE, identification of agents (e.g. Bhopal gas, methyl isocyanate).\n  - *Age estimation:* dental eruption, epiphyseal fusion (iliac crest last to fuse, around 23-25 years), skull sutures, ossification.\n  - *Animal poisons:* snake bite (haemotoxic, neurotoxic; anti-venom); scorpion sting; spider bite.\n  - *Burns:* rule of nines; infection; fluid resuscitation (Parkland formula: 4 ml x weight x %TBSA).\n  - *Pearl:* in methanol poisoning, fomepizole or ethanol blocks alcohol dehydrogenase.\n","FMT_UNIT_02":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.5 Forensic medicine\n\n- **Poisons:** organophosphate (miosis, bradycardia, salivation, fasciculations); arsenic (Mees lines, garlic odour); carbon monoxide (cherry red); opium (pinpoint pupils); datura (dilated pupils, dry skin, hallucinations, red flushed).\n- **Medical jurisprudence:** Indian Penal Code; Indian Evidence Act; Dying declaration (Section 32 IEA); Consent (Section 88-92 IPC; informed consent); MTP Act (up to 20 weeks, 24 weeks in special cases); PNDT Act (sex determination).\n\n## 19. Forensic Medicine (additions)\n\n- **Reproductive and child-protection laws:**\n  - *POCSO Act (2012):* a child is below 18 years; consent is not valid below 18; sexual offences against children; mandatory reporting.\n  - *PCPNDT Act:* prohibits prenatal sex determination; disclosure of sex is an offence.\n  - *MTP Act:* up to 20 weeks by one doctor; 20-24 weeks needs a medical board for foetal anomalies; beyond 24 weeks only for substantial foetal abnormalities by a board.\n  - *Death caused by act intended to produce miscarriage:* IPC Section 312 (causing miscarriage) and 314 (death caused by act with intent to cause miscarriage).\n  - *Pearl:* under the MTP Act, termination up to 20 weeks needs one registered medical practitioner; 20-24 weeks needs two RMPs, and for foetal anomalies a medical board.\n- **Heavy metal and iron poisoning:**\n  - *Acute iron poisoning and deferoxamine:* GI haemorrhage, metabolic acidosis, shock; chelation with deferoxamine (IV).\n  - *Arsenic poisoning:* garlic odour; rice-water stools; Mees lines in nails; treatment with dimercaprol or DMSA.\n  - *Thallium poisoning:* alopecia, painful neuropathy, Mees lines.\n  - *Pearl:* Mees lines (transverse white lines on nails) occur in arsenic and thallium poisoning.\n- **Medical ethics, consent and negligence:**\n  - *Consent for life-saving emergency hysterectomy:* implied consent in emergencies (life-threatening haemorrhage).\n  - *Informed consent for elective hysterectomy:* explained risks; written consent.\n  - *Goals-of-care in futile treatment:* discussion; not obliged to provide futile care.\n  - *Criminal negligence by an intoxicated surgeon:* gross negligence; criminal liability (IPC).\n  - *Pearl:* in emergencies where the patient cannot consent, implied consent applies.\n- **Plant poisons:**\n  - *Datura:* atropine-like (anticholinergic toxidrome); dilated pupils, dry skin, flushed, hallucinations, hyperthermia; treat with physostigmine (for severe anticholinergic delirium).\n  - *Marking nut (Semecarpus anacardium):* contains urushiol-like compounds; contact dermatitis; black ink-like stain.\n  - *Pearl:* physostigmine reverses anticholinergic delirium; avoid in tricyclic overdose.\n- **Court procedure and evidence:**\n  - *Leading questions in cross-examination:* permitted (Indian Evidence Act, Section 143); not in examination-in-chief.\n  - *In-camera trial:* closed-door trial (e.g. POCSO cases, to protect the victim).\n  - *Pearl:* leading questions are allowed in cross-examination but not in examination-in-chief.\n- **Drugs of abuse and neurotoxic poisons:**\n  - *Cocaine:* sympathomimetic toxidrome (hypertension, tachycardia, hyperthermia, mydriasis); treat with benzodiazepines; avoid beta-blockers.\n  - *Strychnine poisoning:* opisthotonus (conscious patient), tetanic convulsions; treat with benzodiazepines and muscle relaxants.\n  - *Pearl:* strychnine causes tetanic convulsions with a conscious patient; cocaine causes sympathomimetic features.\n- **Sexual jurisprudence and forensic serology:**\n  - *Barberio test:* semen detection by picric acid crystals (yellow picric acid crystals).\n  - *Septate hymen:* a hymen with a septum; identification of the type of hymen in sexual offence cases.\n  - *Pearl:* Barberio's test detects semen by yellow picric acid crystals.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: High-Yield Antidotes & Toxicology Matrix\n\n| Poison / Toxin | Mechanism & Key Sign | Diagnostic Test | Antidote / Treatment |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Aluminium Phosphide (Celphos) | Phosphine gas inhibits cytochrome oxidase | Silver nitrate paper turns black | Supportive, Coconut oil / Gastric lavage (No antidote) |\n| Organophosphates | Irreversible AChE phosphorylation (aging) | RBC / Serum cholinesterase low | Atropine (muscarinic) + Pralidoxime 2-PAM (nicotinic) |\n| Arsenic | Inhibits lipoic acid, capillary damage | Raindrop skin, Aldrich-Mees nail lines | Dimercaprol (BAL) or Succimer (DMSA) |\n| Snake Bite (Viper) | Hemotoxic vasculotoxin (consumptive DIC) | 20-minute Whole Blood Clotting Test | Polyvalent Anti-Snake Venom (ASV) |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Grievous Hurt (IPC 320):** 8 clauses (Fracture, Dislocation, Blindness, Deafness, 20-day incapacitation).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Snake Bite: Elapids** = Neurotoxic (Ptosis first). Vipers = Hemotoxic (coagulopathy, bleeding).\n\n- **Res ipsa loquitur (\"the thing speaks for itself\"):** in medical negligence, negligence is inferred when the injury would not ordinarily occur without it (for example a surgical instrument left in the abdomen); the burden of explanation shifts to the doctor.\n","MED_UNIT_01":"## 1. General Medicine (25-30 questions)\n\n### 1.1 Cardiology (highest yield)\n\n**ECG essentials**\n- **Left bundle branch block (LBBB):** broad QRS (120 ms or more), notched R in V5-V6 (broad, notched R waves), absent septal Q waves in lateral leads, discordant ST-T changes. In a new LBBB with chest pain, treat as a STEMI equivalent.\n- **Right bundle branch block (RBBB):** rSR' in V1, wide S in I, V6 (M-shaped V1). Can be a normal variant.\n- **Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW):** short PR interval (under 120 ms), delta wave (slurred upstroke), wide QRS. Risk of AF conducting rapidly down the accessory pathway. Avoid AV-nodal blockers (verapamil, digoxin, adenosine) in pre-excited AF; use procainamide or DC cardioversion.\n- **Complete heart block:** P waves and QRS complexes are independent (AV dissociation). Pacing is needed if symptomatic.\n- **Atrial fibrillation:** irregularly irregular rhythm, no discrete P waves. Anticoagulate based on CHA2DS2-VASc score (score 2 or more in men, 3 or more in women, anticoagulate). Rate control with beta-blocker or diltiazem; rhythm control if unstable.\n- **Hyperkalaemia:** peaked T waves first, then PR prolongation, P-wave loss, widened QRS, sine-wave pattern. Treat with calcium gluconate first (membrane stabilisation), then insulin with glucose, salbutamol, and potassium removal.\n- **Digoxin toxicity:** scooped ST segments (\"reverse tick\"), any arrhythmia, nausea and yellow vision. Treat with digoxin-specific Fab fragments; check potassium and magnesium.\n\n**Acute coronary syndrome (ACS)**\n- **STEMI:** ST elevation in two contiguous leads (or new LBBB). Reperfusion within 120 minutes by primary PCI, or fibrinolysis within 30 minutes of arrival if PCI is not available within 120 minutes.\n- **NSTEMI / unstable angina:** troponin rise (NSTEMI) or troponin normal (unstable angina). Anti-ischaemic and antithrombotic therapy; early invasive strategy in high-risk cases.\n- **Standard drugs:** aspirin, a P2Y12 inhibitor (clopidogrel, ticagrelor, prasugrel), heparin or enoxaparin, high-intensity statin, beta-blocker if no failure, ACE-inhibitor after the first day. Morphine for pain (less used now); oxygen only if SpO2 is below 90%.\n- **Complications:** arrhythmias (VF in the first hours is the commonest cause of sudden death), cardiogenic shock, papillary muscle rupture (day 2-7; acute mitral regurgitation), ventricular septal rupture (day 3-5; new harsh pansystolic murmur at the lower left sternal edge), free-wall rupture (tamponade, first week), Dressler syndrome (pericarditis weeks later).\n- **Pearl:** the right coronary artery supplies the inferior wall; inferior STEMI with bradycardia and hypotension suggests RV infarction, and nitrates are contraindicated.\n\n**Heart failure**\n- **HFrEF (reduced ejection fraction, EF 40% or less):** systolic dysfunction; enlarged ventricle. Four-drug therapy (GDMT): ARNI or ACE-I/ARB, evidence-based beta-blocker (carvedilol, bisoprolol, metoprolol succinate), mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist (spironolactone or eplerenone), and SGLT2 inhibitor (dapagliflozin or empagliflozin). Loop diuretics relieve congestion but do not prolong survival.\n- **HFpEF (preserved EF, 50% or more):** diastolic dysfunction; hypertension and older age. SGLT2 inhibitors and diuretics help symptoms; control BP and AF.\n- **Investigations:** BNP or NT-proBNP (high level rules out heart failure; low level makes it unlikely), echocardiography (the key test for EF and valve disease).\n- **Pearl:** ACE-I and beta-blockers are started at low doses and titrated up; they are not started in acute decompensation with low output.\n\n**Hypertension**\n- **Diagnosis:** 140/90 mmHg or more on repeated measurement (office); ambulatory or home readings confirm.\n- **First-line drugs:** ACE-I or ARB, calcium channel blocker (amlodipine), thiazide-like diuretic (chlorthalidone, indapamide). Beta-blockers are no longer first-line unless there is ischaemic heart disease, heart failure, or pregnancy-related indications (labetalol, methyldopa).\n- **Compelling indications:** diabetes, CKD (prefer ACE-I/ARB), heart failure, post-MI, Afro-Caribbean patients (prefer CCB or thiazide).\n- **Secondary hypertension:** primary aldosteronism (high aldosterone with low renin; treat with spironolactone or surgery), phaeochromocytoma (episodic headache, sweating, palpitations; check plasma metanephrines; alpha blockade before beta blockade), renal artery stenosis (flash pulmonary oedema, bruit), Cushing syndrome.\n- **Hypertensive emergency:** BP above 180/120 with target-organ damage. Lower the BP gradually (no more than 25% in the first hour); nitroprusside or labetalol IV.\n- **Pearl:** in pregnancy, avoid ACE-I and ARB (teratogenic, especially second and third trimester); methyldopa and labetalol are preferred.\n\n**Infective endocarditis**\n- **Duke criteria:** major criteria (positive blood cultures with typical organisms, or evidence of endocardial involvement on echocardiography) plus minor criteria (predisposition, fever, vascular or immunological phenomena, microbiological evidence).\n- **Organisms:** Staphylococcus aureus is the commonest cause of acute infective endocarditis (IVDU, prosthetic valves); viridans streptococci cause subacute infection on damaged valves; HACEK group for culture-negative cases; Enterococcus and Staph epidermidis for prosthetic valves.\n- **Clinical signs:** Janeway lesions (non-tender palmar and plantar macules), Osler nodes (tender nodules on fingertips), Roth spots (retinal haemorrhages with pale centres), splinter haemorrhages, clubbing.\n- **Indications for early surgery:** heart failure from valve dysfunction, uncontrolled infection (abscess, fungal, persistent bacteraemia), prevention of embolism (large vegetation).\n- **Pearl:** blood cultures before antibiotics (three sets); treat empirically with vancomycin plus gentamicin, or as directed by the organism.\n\n**Rheumatic heart disease (RHD)**\n- **Jones criteria:** major: carditis, polyarthritis, chorea, erythema marginatum, subcutaneous nodules. Minor: fever, arthralgia, raised ESR or CRP, prolonged PR interval. Two major or one major plus two minor, with evidence of preceding streptococcal infection, confirm acute rheumatic fever.\n- **Mitral stenosis:** opening snap, rumbling mid-diastolic murmur at the apex with presystolic accentuation (if sinus rhythm). Causes: left atrial enlargement, AF, pulmonary hypertension. Management: diuretics, rate control, anticoagulation if AF; balloon valvotomy for suitable valves.\n- **Secondary prophylaxis:** benzathine penicillin G every 3-4 weeks (lifelong or for at least 10 years after the last attack).\n- **Pearl:** the murmur of mitral stenosis is the classic \"opening snap\" plus \"diastolic rumble\"; the murmur of aortic regurgitation is an early diastolic decrescendo.\n\n#### Table: ECG Lead to Coronary Artery Mapping\n\n| Anatomical Wall | ECG Leads Involved | Culprit Coronary Artery | Clinical Pitfalls |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Anterior / Anteroseptal | V1, V2, V3, V4 | Left Anterior Descending (LAD - 'Widowmaker') | Highest risk of cardiogenic shock and heart block |\n| Inferior Wall | II, III, aVF (ST elevation III > II) | Right Coronary Artery (RCA in 85-90% right dominant) | Look for RV MI (V4R); Nitrates are CONTRAINDICATED! |\n| Lateral Wall | I, aVL, V5, V6 | Left Circumflex (LCx) or diagonal LAD | Reciprocal depression in inferior leads |\n| Posterior Wall | V1-V2 (Tall R wave, ST depression) | Posterior Descending Artery (PDA) | Confirm with posterior leads V7, V8, V9 |\n\n#### Table: Cardiac Tamponade vs Constrictive Pericarditis\n\n| Parameter | Cardiac Tamponade | Constrictive Pericarditis |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Beck's Triad | PRESENT (Hypotension, JVP elevated, Muffled heart sounds) | Absent (hypotension rare) |\n| Pulsus Paradoxus | MARKED (> 10 mmHg drop in SBP on inspiration) | Absent or rare (< 1/3 cases) |\n| Kussmaul's Sign | ABSENT (JVP falls normally on inspiration) | PRESENT (Paradoxical rise in JVP on inspiration) |\n| Auscultation | Distant, muffled heart sounds | Pericardial knock (early diastolic) |\n| Definitive Treatment | Emergency subxiphoid Pericardiocentesis | Elective Pericardiectomy |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Brugada Syndrome (SCN5A):** Coved ST-elevation >= 2 mm followed by negative T-wave in V1-V2. Autosomal dominant sodium channelopathy. Treatment = ICD.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Infective Endocarditis Valve Rules: Overall most common** = Mitral valve. IV Drug Users = Tricuspid valve (Staph aureus). Colon cancer link = Streptococcus gallolyticus (S. bovis).\n\n### 1.2 Infectious diseases\n\n**Malaria**\n- **Diagnosis:** thick and thin blood smears (Giemsa stain); rapid antigen test (HRP-2 for falciparum); PCR when needed.\n- **Treatment:** artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT) for falciparum. Severe falciparum: IV artesunate. Vivax and ovale: chloroquine (sensitive areas) plus primaquine to clear hypnozoites. Check G6PD before primaquine (haemolysis risk).\n- **Phases:** febrile (2-7 days), critical (plasma leakage, typically days 4-6), recovery.\n- **Management:** oral fluids if tolerated; IV crystalloid if warning signs appear; avoid NSAIDs and aspirin (bleeding); paracetamol for fever.\n- **Pearl:** the critical phase begins when the fever falls, not when it rises.\n\n**Tuberculosis (TB)**\n- **Diagnosis:** sputum smear (ZN stain), CBNAAT/GeneXpert (rifampicin resistance), culture and drug sensitivity; chest X-ray (upper zone infiltrates, cavitation); Mantoux or IGRA for latent infection.\n- **Standard regimen (NTEP):** intensive phase 2 months of HRZE (isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol), then continuation phase 4 months of HR.\n- **Drug toxicities:** isoniazid (peripheral neuropathy, prevent with pyridoxine; hepatitis); rifampicin (orange body fluids, enzyme induction, flu-like syndrome); pyrazinamide (hepatitis, hyperuricaemia); ethambutol (optic neuritis; check visual acuity and colour vision).\n- **Pearl:** rifampicin reduces the efficacy of oral contraceptives, warfarin, and many other drugs by enzyme induction.\n\n**HIV**\n- **WHO clinical stages:** 1 (asymptomatic) to 4 (AIDS-defining illness).\n- **ART:** started as early as possible. First-line regimens combine two NRTIs with an integrase inhibitor (dolutegravir) or an NNRTI.\n- **Window period:** about 2-4 weeks after exposure, antibody tests may be negative; use RNA/PCR.\n- **Pearl:** toxoplasmosis and cryptococcal meningitis with a low CD4 count indicate that the patient needs CD4 testing before anything else.\n\n**Enteric fever (typhoid and paratyphoid)**\n- **Organism:** Salmonella Typhi (and Paratyphi A, B, C). Fever with relative bradycardia, rose spots, hepatosplenomegaly, and constipation or diarrhoea.\n- **Diagnosis:** blood culture is the best test in the first week; bone marrow culture is most sensitive. Widal test is unreliable (false positives and negatives, previous infection confounds).\n- **Treatment:** azithromycin (oral) or ceftriaxone (IV) for uncomplicated disease; resistant strains are common in India (fluoroquinolone resistance). Complications: intestinal perforation, haemorrhage (usually in the third week).\n- **Pearl:** the fever curve is stepladder-like in the untreated disease.\n\n## 10. High-Yield Image Areas\n\n- **ECG:** delta wave (WPW); peaked T (hyperkalaemia); tombstone (STEMI); sawtooth (atrial flutter); \"Osborn J wave\" (hypothermia); torsades (long QT).\n\n## 11. Integrated Patterns (High-Yield Associations)\n\n- **Tuberous sclerosis:** ash-leaf spots; facial angiofibromas; rhabdomyoma of heart; cortical tubers; SEGA.\n- **Cushing triad:** hypertension, bradycardia, irregular respiration (raised ICP).\n- **Charcot triad (cholangitis):** fever, jaundice, right upper quadrant pain; Reynolds pentad adds hypotension and confusion.\n- **Pearl:** Charcot triad indicates ascending cholangitis; Reynolds pentad adds hypotension and mental status change.\n\n## 20. Medicine (additions)\n\n- **Arrhythmias:**\n  - *AF with stable acute rate control in thyrotoxicosis:* beta-blockers or rate control; treat the thyrotoxicosis; anticoagulate if indicated.\n  - *AF with rheumatic mitral stenosis:* anticoagulate with warfarin (DOACs are not used in moderate-severe rheumatic MS); rate control.\n  - *AF with absent jugular venous a waves:* typical of AF (no atrial contraction).\n  - *Stable wide-complex VT:* IV amiodarone or procainamide; synchronised cardioversion if unstable.\n  - *Paroxysmal SVT long-term prophylaxis:* catheter ablation (curative); beta-blockers or verapamil for prophylaxis.\n  - *ECG rhythm identification:* atrial flutter (sawtooth F waves, 2:1 block at 150); PSVT (narrow, regular, no P waves); VT (wide, regular); AF (irregular, no P waves).\n  - *Pearl:* adenosine is first line for narrow-complex PSVT and may unmask atrial flutter.\n- **Valvular heart disease and endocarditis:**\n  - *Mitral regurgitation and tricuspid stenosis murmurs:* MR is a pansystolic murmur at apex radiating to axilla; TS shows a diastolic rumble with opening snap, increasing on inspiration.\n  - *Acute rheumatic fever:* Jones criteria; clinical recognition; penicillin prophylaxis for 10 years or until age 40 (secondary prevention).\n  - *IE prophylaxis before dental procedures:* for high-risk patients (prosthetic valves, previous IE, certain congenital heart disease).\n  - *Pearl:* Jones criteria: two major or one major plus two minor, with evidence of streptococcal infection.\n- **Central nervous system infections:**\n  - *Tuberculous (tubercular) meningitis CSF pattern:* cerebrospinal fluid shows lymphocytic pleocytosis, high protein, low glucose; cobweb coagulum; treat with four-drug TB therapy and dexamethasone.\n  - *Herpes simplex encephalitis:* temporal lobe involvement; MRI; PCR of CSF; acyclovir early.\n  - *Meningococcal meningitis prophylaxis for close contacts:* rifampicin, ciprofloxacin, or ceftriaxone.\n  - *Pearl:* HSV encephalitis is treated with acyclovir before PCR results are back.\n- **Non-malignant haematology:**\n  - *Sickle-beta-thalassaemia on haemoglobin electrophoresis:* HbS peak with raised HbA2 and HbF.\n  - *Type 2 von Willebrand disease:* qualitative defect (VWF function reduced); normal factor VIII is not affected.\n  - *Vitamin K-responsive factor IX deficiency from biliary obstruction:* prolonged PT corrected by vitamin K; not inherited haemophilia B.\n  - *Pearl:* biliary obstruction causes vitamin K malabsorption, which can be treated with vitamin K.\n- **Haematologic malignancies and oncologic emergencies:**\n  - *CML confirmation by BCR-ABL:* Philadelphia chromosome, t(9;22); PCR confirms; imatinib first line.\n  - *Tumour lysis syndrome:* hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphataemia, hyperuricaemia, hypocalcaemia, AKI; rasburicase or allopurinol; hydration.\n  - *Multiple myeloma CRAB features:* hypercalcaemia, renal failure, anaemia, bone lesions.\n  - *Pearl:* in TLS, hyperkalaemia and hyperphosphataemia cause hypocalcaemia.\n- **Vasculitis:**\n  - *Takayasu arteritis:* large-vessel; young women; pulse deficit; ischaemic features; steroids and immunosuppression.\n  - *Giant cell arteritis:* temporal artery; headache, jaw claudication; immediate steroids to prevent blindness; temporal artery biopsy.\n  - *IgA vasculitis (HSP):* purpura, arthralgia, abdominal pain, IgA nephropathy; supportive care.\n  - *Pearl:* in giant cell arteritis, treat with high-dose steroids immediately before biopsy to prevent blindness.\n- **Critical care and resuscitation:** ABCDE approach; advanced cardiac life support; shock types (cardiogenic, hypovolaemic, distributive, obstructive); vasopressors (noradrenaline first line).\n- **Toxicological and environmental emergencies:** paracetamol (NAC); organophosphate (atropine, pralidoxime); salicylates (alkalinisation); heat stroke (cooling); lithium toxicity (haemodialysis).\n- **Ischaemic heart disease:** stable angina; unstable angina and NSTEMI; STEMI management (PCI within 90 min; thrombolysis if PCI not available); dual antiplatelet therapy; statins; beta-blockers.\n  - *Pearl:* door-to-balloon time target is 90 minutes for primary PCI.\n- **Cerebrovascular disease:** ischaemic stroke (thrombolysis within 4.5 hours; thrombectomy up to 24 hours in selected patients); TIA (antiplatelet, carotid assessment); haemorrhagic stroke (BP control; neurosurgery for some).\n  - *Pearl:* alteplase is given within 4.5 hours of stroke onset.\n- **Heart failure:** HFrEF (EF below 40%): ACE inhibitor or ARNI, beta-blocker, MRA, SGLT2 inhibitor; HFpEF (EF above 50%): diuretics, SGLT2 inhibitors; BNP and NT-proBNP.\n  - *Pearl:* SGLT2 inhibitors benefit both HFrEF and HFpEF.\n- **Pericardial disease:** acute pericarditis (chest pain relieved by sitting forward, pericardial rub); constrictive pericarditis (Kussmaul sign, pericardial knock); cardiac tamponade (Beck triad: hypotension, raised JVP, muffled heart sounds; pulsus paradoxus); treatment: NSAIDs, colchicine; pericardiocentesis for tamponade.\n  - *Pearl:* pulsus paradoxus (fall in systolic BP above 10 mmHg on inspiration) is a feature of tamponade.\n- **Thoracic malignancy:** lung cancer (small cell: paraneoplastic syndromes, chemo; non-small: surgery for early disease); mesothelioma; Pancoast tumour (Horner syndrome, brachial plexus); superior vena cava syndrome (facial oedema, raised JVP).\n  - *Pearl:* Pancoast tumour causes Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis).\n","MED_UNIT_02":"## 1. General Medicine (25-30 questions)\n\n### 1.2 Infectious diseases\n\n- **Monitoring:** CD4 count (below 200 cells/mm3 for AIDS-defining risk; below 200 for co-trimoxazole prophylaxis); viral load.\n- **Opportunistic infections and CD4 thresholds:** Pneumocystis pneumonia (below 200; co-trimoxazole); toxoplasmosis (below 100; co-trimoxazole); cryptococcal meningitis (below 100; check CSF and serum cryptococcal antigen); MAC (below 50; azithromycin prophylaxis when indicated); CMV retinitis (below 50).\n\n### 1.3 Respiratory\n\n**Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)**\n- **Diagnosis:** post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC below 0.70 with symptoms and exposure (smoking, biomass smoke).\n- **Staging (GOLD):** FEV1 percentage predicted: 80% or more (mild), 50-79% (moderate), 30-49% (severe), below 30% (very severe).\n- **Treatment:** long-acting bronchodilators (LAMA or LABA), inhaled corticosteroids if exacerbations or eosinophils are high, then escalation. Oxygen if PaO2 is 55 mmHg or less (or 60 mmHg with cor pulmonale).\n- **Oxygen target:** 88-92% in patients who retain CO2 (chronic hypercapnia), to avoid CO2 narcosis.\n- **Pearl:** a COPD patient with a chronic cough and weight loss needs lung cancer to be excluded.\n\n**Asthma**\n- **Diagnosis:** variable airflow limitation (reversibility on bronchodilator, or bronchial hyperresponsiveness on challenge); peak flow variability.\n- **Stepwise management (GINA):** SABA as needed; add low-dose ICS-formoterol (anti-inflammatory reliever) at step 2-3; add LAMA, biologics at higher steps.\n- **Acute severe asthma:** high-dose inhaled salbutamol and ipratropium, oral prednisolone, IV magnesium sulfate if still severe, consider aminophylline or IV salbutamol, and ICU review. Signs of life-threatening asthma: silent chest, cyanosis, exhaustion, drowsiness, PEFR below 33%.\n- **Pearl:** beta-blockers (including ophthalmic timolol) can precipitate asthma; avoid them in asthmatics.\n\n**Community-acquired pneumonia (CAP)**\n- **CURB-65 score (1 point each):** confusion, urea above 7 mmol/L, respiratory rate 30 or more, BP systolic below 90 or diastolic 60 or less, age 65 or over. Score 0-1: outpatient; 2: consider admission; 3 or more: hospital (ICU if 4 or more, or if pH is low).\n- **Common organisms:** Streptococcus pneumoniae (commonest); Haemophilus influenzae; atypical (Mycoplasma, Legionella, Chlamydia); Klebsiella (alcoholics, aspiration).\n- **Treatment:** amoxicillin for outpatients; amoxicillin-clavulanate or ceftriaxone plus azithromycin (or doxycycline) for admitted patients; respiratory quinolone if penicillin allergic.\n\n**Pleural effusion**\n- **Light's criteria (exudate if one or more):** pleural-to-serum protein ratio above 0.5; pleural-to-serum LDH ratio above 0.6; pleural LDH above two-thirds of the upper limit of normal serum LDH.\n- **Transudate causes:** heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome. **Exudate causes:** infection (empyema), malignancy, TB, pulmonary embolism, rheumatoid arthritis.\n- **Management:** diagnostic aspiration; drainage for symptoms; pleurodesis for recurrent malignant effusion; empyema needs drainage (chest tube) and antibiotics.\n- **Pearl:** a pleural fluid pH below 7.2 with glucose below 60 mg/dL suggests complicated parapneumonic effusion needing drainage.\n\n**Pulmonary embolism (PE)**\n- **Wells score and D-dimer:** a low-risk patient with a negative D-dimer can be discharged. High-risk patients need CT pulmonary angiography (CTPA).\n- **Treatment:** anticoagulation (LMWH, DOAC); thrombolysis for haemodynamic instability.\n- **Pearl:** an ECG showing S1Q3T3, right heart strain, or a right bundle branch block suggests PE.\n\n#### Table: Light's Criteria: Exudative Pleural Effusion\n\n| Criterion (Any 1 of 3 confirms Exudate) | Diagnostic Cutoff Value | Common Exudate Causes | Common Transudate Causes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. Pleural / Serum Protein Ratio | > 0.5 | Infections (Tuberculosis, Parapneumonic empyema) | Congestive Heart Failure (elevated hydrostatic) |\n| 2. Pleural / Serum LDH Ratio | > 0.6 | Malignancy (Lung, Breast, Mesothelioma) | Cirrhosis / Hepatic hydrothorax |\n| 3. Pleural Fluid Absolute LDH | > 2/3 of laboratory upper limit of normal serum LDH | Connective Tissue Diseases (RA, SLE), Pulmonary Embolism | Nephrotic Syndrome (decreased oncotic) |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Tension Pneumothorax:** PURELY CLINICAL DIAGNOSIS! Never wait for an X-ray. Immediate needle decompression in 2nd ICS midclavicular or 5th ICS anterior axillary line.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **COPD Spirometry:** Post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC < 0.70 is mandatory for diagnosis. Only two interventions reduce mortality: (1) Smoking cessation, (2) Long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT >= 15h/day).\n\n## 11. Integrated Patterns (High-Yield Associations)\n\n- **Triad of pleural effusion, pulmonary fibrosis and oesophageal dilatation:** typical of systemic sclerosis (scleroderma).\n\n## 20. Medicine (additions)\n\n- **Asthma:**\n  - *Stepwise escalation:* SABA, low-dose ICS, add LABA, add LAMA or montelukast, consider biologics (omalizumab, mepolizumab).\n  - *Benralizumab:* anti-IL-5 receptor alpha monoclonal antibody for eosinophilic asthma.\n  - *Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (Samter triad):* asthma, nasal polyps, aspirin or NSAID sensitivity; avoid NSAIDs.\n  - *Pearl:* Samter triad is asthma, nasal polyposis and NSAID-induced bronchospasm.\n- **Tropical and zoonotic infections:**\n  - *Leptospirosis and Weil disease:* jaundice, renal failure, haemorrhage, conjunctival suffusion, calf pain; doxycycline or penicillin; ceftriaxone for severe.\n  - *Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia:* filariasis; nocturnal cough and wheeze; marked eosinophilia; DEC.\n  - *Pearl:* conjunctival suffusion (not exudate) is characteristic of leptospirosis.\n- **Pneumonia:**\n  - *CURB-65 severity:* confusion, urea above 7 mmol/L, respiratory rate 30 or more, BP systolic below 90 or diastolic 60 or below, age 65 or above; score 2 or more: hospital admission.\n  - *Empiric management of community-acquired pneumonia:* amoxicillin or amoxicillin-clavulanate; add macrolide for atypical cover; for severe, add ceftriaxone plus azithromycin.\n  - *Lobar consolidation with bronchial breathing and increased vocal resonance:* pneumonia (pneumococcus).\n  - *Pearl:* CURB-65 score 0-1 is managed at home; 2 or more needs admission.\n- **Inflammatory arthritis:** rheumatoid (anti-CCP, symmetrical small joints, morning stiffness); psoriatic (DIP, dactylitis); ankylosing spondylitis (sacroiliitis, HLA-B27); gout (urate crystals, negatively birefringent needle-shaped).\n  - *Pearl:* gout crystals are negatively birefringent under polarised light; pseudogout crystals are positively birefringent.\n- **Pulmonary vascular disease:** pulmonary embolism (CTPA; anticoagulation); pulmonary hypertension (PAH: endothelin antagonists, PDE5 inhibitors, prostacyclin analogues); CTEPH (surgery, riociguat).\n  - *Pearl:* CTEPH is treated with pulmonary endarterectomy (surgery) in selected patients.\n- **COPD:** spirometry (FEV1/FVC below 0.7 post-bronchodilator); GOLD grades; LABA + LAMA; ICS for frequent exacerbations; roflumilast; oxygen targets 88-92%.\n  - *Pearl:* in COPD, target oxygen saturation is 88-92% to avoid CO2 retention.\n- **Pleural disease:** transudate vs exudate (Light criteria); empyema (pH below 7.2, glucose low); malignant effusion (cytology); pneumothorax management; mesothelioma (asbestos; calretinin positive).\n  - *Pearl:* Light criteria: protein ratio above 0.5, LDH ratio above 0.6, or LDH above two-thirds of the normal upper limit suggest exudate.\n","MED_UNIT_03":"## 20. Medicine (additions)\n\n- **Neuromuscular disorders:**\n  - *Lower motor neuron facial nerve palsy:* whole face affected including forehead; Bell palsy; steroids.\n  - *Duchenne muscular dystrophy:* clinical course (calf hypertrophy, Gowers sign); muscle biopsy (dystrophin absent).\n  - *Anterior horn cell lesion:* LMN signs (wasting, fasciculations, hyporeflexia); ALS, polio, SMA.\n  - *Pearl:* Gowers sign is a feature of proximal muscle weakness in Duchenne dystrophy.\n- **Movement disorders:**\n  - *Parkinson disease basal ganglia dysfunction:* substantia nigra dopamine loss; resting tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia; L-dopa.\n  - *Lewy body dementia:* cognitive decline, visual hallucinations, parkinsonism; fluctuating cognition; neuroleptic sensitivity.\n  - *Hemiballismus:* violent flinging movements from contralateral subthalamic nucleus lesion.\n  - *Pearl:* subthalamic nucleus lesions cause hemiballismus (contralateral).\n- **Headache disorders:** tension-type; migraine (aura; triptans); cluster (oxygen, sumatriptan, verapamil); SAH (thunderclap); giant cell arteritis; idiopathic intracranial hypertension (papilloedema, acetazolamide).\n- **Myelopathy and nutritional neurology:** subacute combined degeneration (B12; dorsal column and lateral corticospinal); Wernicke encephalopathy (thiamine); pellagra (niacin); copper deficiency myelopathy.\n  - *Pearl:* in B12 deficiency, check methylmalonic acid and homocysteine.\n\n#### Table: Myasthenia Gravis vs Lambert-Eaton (LEMS) Comparison\n\n| Feature | Myasthenia Gravis | Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome (LEMS) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Autoantibody Target | Postsynaptic Nicotinic AChR (85%) or MuSK | Presynaptic P/Q-type Voltage-Gated Calcium Channels (VGCC) |\n| Clinical Weakness Pattern | Ocular first (ptosis, diplopia) -> Bulbar. WORSENS with exertion | Proximal lower limbs first; IMPROVES with repeated contraction |\n| Deep Tendon Reflexes | Normal / Preserved | Depressed or ABSENT (recover post-exercise) |\n| Autonomic Dysfunction | Absent | Common (dry mouth, erectile dysfunction, constipation) |\n| Malignancy Association | Thymoma / Thymic hyperplasia (75%) | Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC in 50-60%) |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Wallenberg Syndrome (PICA / Vertebral Artery):** Nucleus ambiguus (dysphagia/hoarseness), Horner's syndrome, and Crossed sensory loss (ipsilateral face, contralateral body). Spares motor power!\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **GBS Rule:** Albuminocytological Dissociation (high CSF protein with normal cell count). Precipitated by Campylobacter jejuni enteritis. Steroids are INEFFECTIVE!\n\n## Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Acute ischaemic stroke, thrombolysis window:** IV alteplase or tenecteplase within 4.5 hours of symptom onset (after CT excludes haemorrhage and contraindications are checked); mechanical thrombectomy for large-vessel occlusion, up to 24 hours in selected patients.\n","MED_UNIT_04":"## 1. General Medicine (25-30 questions)\n\n### 1.2 Infectious diseases\n\n- **Warning signs:** abdominal pain or tenderness, persistent vomiting, clinical fluid accumulation (ascites, pleural effusion), mucosal bleeding, lethargy, liver enlargement, rising haematocrit with falling platelets.\n\n### 1.4 Gastroenterology and hepatology\n\n**Cirrhosis**\n- **Causes:** alcohol, viral hepatitis (B, C), NAFLD, autoimmune hepatitis, Wilson disease, haemochromatosis, primary biliary cholangitis, primary sclerosing cholangitis.\n- **Child-Pugh score:** bilirubin, albumin, INR, ascites, encephalopathy (each scored 1-3; total 5-6 = class A, 7-9 = class B, 10-15 = class C).\n- **MELD score:** based on bilirubin, INR, creatinine and sodium; used for transplant listing.\n- **Complications and management:**\n  - *Variceal bleeding:* vasoactive drug (terlipressin, octreotide), IV ceftriaxone, band ligation within 12 hours, plus restrictive transfusion (target Hb 7 g/dL).\n  - *Ascites:* sodium restriction, diuretics (spironolactone plus furosemide); large-volume paracentesis with albumin replacement.\n  - *Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP):* ascitic fluid neutrophils 250/mm3 or more; cefotaxime or ceftriaxone; albumin.\n  - *Hepatic encephalopathy:* lactulose; rifaximin; treat precipitants (infection, bleeding, constipation, electrolyte disturbance).\n  - *Hepatorenal syndrome:* terlipressin and albumin.\n- **Pearl:** NSAIDs and aminoglycosides are avoided in ascites (renal failure).\n\n**Hepatitis B**\n- **Serology:** HBsAg (infection); anti-HBs (immunity); HBeAg (high infectivity); anti-HBc IgM (acute infection); anti-HBc IgG (past or chronic); HBV DNA (viral load).\n- **Window period:** HBsAg has become negative and anti-HBs has not yet appeared; anti-HBc IgM is the marker that identifies acute infection during this time.\n- **Treatment:** tenofovir or entecavir for chronic infection with raised viral load and liver disease.\n\n**Hepatitis C**\n- **Diagnosis:** anti-HCV antibody; HCV RNA confirms active infection.\n- **Treatment:** direct-acting antivirals (sofosbuvir-based regimens) achieve sustained virological response in over 95% of cases.\n- **Pearl:** HCV RNA, not antibody, decides whether the patient is currently infected.\n\n**Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD)**\n- **Ulcerative colitis:** continuous mucosal inflammation from the rectum upwards; bloody diarrhoea; crypt abscesses; pseudopolyps; primary sclerosing cholangitis association. Treatment: aminosalicylates (5-ASA), steroids for flares, colectomy in refractory disease.\n- **Crohn disease:** skip lesions; transmural; fistulae, strictures; perianal disease; granulomas on biopsy; cobblestone mucosa; string sign on barium.\n- **Pearl:** smoking worsens Crohn disease and protects against ulcerative colitis.\n\n**Acute pancreatitis**\n- **Causes (mnemonic I GET SMASHED: Idiopathic, Gallstones, Ethanol, Trauma, Steroids, Mumps, Autoimmune, Scorpion sting, Hypertriglyceridaemia, ERCP, Hypercalcaemia, Drugs):** idiopathic, gallstones, ethanol, trauma, steroids, mumps, autoimmune, scorpion sting, hypertriglyceridaemia, ERCP, hypercalcaemia, drugs.\n- **Severity:** Ranson criteria (on admission and at 48 hours); BISAP score (blood urea, impaired mental status, SIRS, age over 60, pleural effusion).\n- **Management:** early aggressive IV fluid (lactated Ringer), analgesia, early enteral feeding; antibiotics only for infected necrosis; ERCP for gallstone pancreatitis with cholangitis.\n- **Pearl:** serum amylase rises in many conditions; lipase is more specific.\n\n#### Table: Serum-Ascites Albumin Gradient (SAAG) Guide\n\n| SAAG Category | Pathophysiology | Ascitic Total Protein | Etiological Diseases |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| High SAAG (>= 1.1 g/dL) | Portal Hypertension Present | < 2.5 g/dL | Cirrhosis, Late Budd-Chiari |\n| High SAAG (>= 1.1 g/dL) | Portal Hypertension Present | >= 2.5 g/dL | Congestive Heart Failure, Constrictive Pericarditis, Early Budd-Chiari |\n| Low SAAG (< 1.1 g/dL) | No Portal Hypertension (Peritoneal cause) | >= 2.5 g/dL | Peritoneal Carcinomatosis, Tuberculous Peritonitis |\n| Low SAAG (< 1.1 g/dL) | No Portal Hypertension | < 2.5 g/dL | Nephrotic Syndrome (systemic hypoalbuminemia) |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Wilson Disease (ATP7B Chromosome 13q):** Decreased serum ceruloplasmin (< 20 mg/dL), Increased 24-hr urinary copper (> 100 mcg), Kayser-Fleischer rings on slit lamp. Treatment = Trientine or D-Penicillamine.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **SBP Diagnostic Rule:** Ascitic absolute PMN count >= 250 cells/mm3. Empirical DOC = IV Cefotaxime/Ceftriaxone + IV Albumin.\n\n## 11. Integrated Patterns (High-Yield Associations)\n\n- **Wilson disease:** ATP7B; Kayser-Fleischer ring; low ceruloplasmin; hepatic and neuro symptoms; penicillamine, zinc.\n\n## 20. Medicine (additions)\n\n- **Liver disease:**\n  - *Cirrhosis complications, hepatorenal syndrome, SBP:* SBP (ascitic fluid neutrophils above 250/mm3; cefotaxime or ceftriaxone); hepatorenal syndrome (terlipressin and albumin; avoid nephrotoxins).\n  - *Hepatitis B acute serology and treatment of chronic:* HBsAg, IgM anti-HBc (acute); tenofovir or entecavir for chronic active disease with raised HBV DNA.\n  - *Obstructive jaundice biochemical pattern:* raised ALP and GGT; conjugated bilirubin.\n  - *Wilson disease diagnostic investigation:* low ceruloplasmin, raised urinary copper, Kayser-Fleischer ring, liver biopsy copper content.\n  - *Pearl:* in SBP, ascitic fluid neutrophil count above 250/mm3 is diagnostic.\n- **Malabsorption disorders:** coeliac disease (tTG-IgA, endomysial antibody, villous atrophy); tropical sprue (folate, B12); Whipple disease (PAS-positive macrophages, Tropheryma whipplei; doxycycline and ceftriaxone); pancreatic insufficiency (faecal elastase).\n  - *Pearl:* Whipple disease shows PAS-positive foamy macrophages in the small bowel.\n","MED_UNIT_05":"## 11. Integrated Patterns (High-Yield Associations)\n\n- **MEN 2A:** medullary thyroid carcinoma, phaeochromocytoma, parathyroid hyperplasia.\n- **MEN 2B:** medullary thyroid carcinoma, phaeochromocytoma, mucosal neuromas, marfanoid habitus.\n\n#### Table: Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN) Comparison\n\n| Syndrome | Gene / Chromosome | Core Tumor Manifestations | High-Yield Clinical Clues |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| MEN 1 (Wermer) | MEN1 / 11q13 (Menin) | 3 Ps: Parathyroid (95%), Pancreas (Gastrinoma, Insulinoma), Pituitary (Prolactinoma) | Recurrent nephrolithiasis and peptic ulcer disease |\n| MEN 2A (Sipple) | RET / 10q11.2 (Tyrosine kinase) | 1 M, 2 Ps: Medullary Thyroid Ca (100%), Pheochromocytoma (50%), Parathyroid hyperplasia (20%) | Calcitonin elevation; screen for pheochromocytoma BEFORE neck surgery! |\n| MEN 2B (Gorlin) | RET / 10q11.2 (M918T mutation) | 1 M, 1 P, 2 Ms: Medullary Thyroid Ca, Pheochromocytoma, Mucosal neuromas, Marfanoid habitus | NO hyperparathyroidism! Aggressive MTC in early childhood. |\n\n## 20. Medicine (additions)\n\n- **Adrenal disorders:**\n  - *Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison):* hyperpigmentation, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, hypotension; adrenal crisis needs IV hydrocortisone; ACTH high.\n  - *Pheochromocytoma and preoperative alpha-blockade:* phenoxybenzamine (alpha) before surgery; then beta-blockers; tachycardia and hypertension; metanephrines.\n  - *Pearl:* in pheochromocytoma, beta-blockers must be started only after alpha-blockade, to avoid crisis.\n- **Diabetes mellitus and complications:**\n  - *Type 1 diabetes stage 3 disease and fluid management in DKA:* stage 3 is the insulin-deficient stage; DKA: 0.9% saline, then potassium replacement; insulin when K above 3.3.\n  - *Insulin resistance and acanthosis nigricans:* velvety pigmented skin in folds; insulin resistance.\n  - *Chronic diabetic distal symmetric polyneuropathy:* glove-and-stocking sensory loss; foot ulcer risk.\n  - *Pearl:* in DKA, insulin is started only after potassium is above 3.3 mmol/L.\n- **Thyroid disorders:**\n  - *Graves disease TSH-receptor antibody confirmation and thyroid storm:* TRAb; thyroid storm (fever, tachycardia, delirium): propylthiouracil, beta-blockers, iodine after thionamide, steroids.\n  - *Hypothyroidism-associated anaemia:* typically normocytic or macrocytic; iron-deficiency anaemia from menorrhagia; B12 deficiency from pernicious anaemia.\n  - *Pearl:* in thyroid storm, give thionamide before iodine to prevent release of hormone.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **DKA Potassium Rule:** NEVER start insulin if serum K+ < 3.3 mEq/L! Give IV KCl until K+ > 3.3, otherwise insulin drives potassium intracellularly causing fatal asystole.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **MEN Pheo Rule:** ALWAYS resect Pheochromocytoma BEFORE thyroid surgery in MEN 2 to avoid fatal hypertensive adrenergic crisis on the operating table!\n","MED_UNIT_06":"## 1. General Medicine (25-30 questions)\n\n### 1.2 Infectious diseases\n\n- **Species:** P. falciparum (most dangerous; cerebral malaria, acute kidney injury, hypoglycaemia, anaemia, black-water fever); P. vivax and P. ovale (relapse from dormant liver hypnozoites); P. malariae (quartan fever, nephrotic syndrome).\n\n### 1.5 Nephrology and electrolytes\n\n**Acute kidney injury (AKI)**\n- **KDIGO definition:** creatinine rise of 0.3 mg/dL or more in 48 hours, or 1.5 times baseline within 7 days, or urine output below 0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 hours or more.\n- **Prerenal:** hypovolaemia, low perfusion; FENa below 1%; urine osmolality above 500 mOsm/kg; bland sediment.\n- **Intrinsic (acute tubular necrosis):** FENa above 2%; muddy-brown granular casts; urine osmolality below 350 mOsm/kg.\n- **Postrenal:** obstruction; bladder scan and ultrasound identify it.\n- **Fractional excretion of urea (FEUrea):** below 35% suggests prerenal, useful when diuretics have been given.\n- **Pearl:** FENa is unreliable after furosemide; use FEUrea.\n\n**Chronic kidney disease (CKD)**\n- **Staging:** G1-G5 by eGFR (G3a 45-59, G3b 30-44, G4 15-29, G5 below 15) plus A1-A3 by albuminuria.\n- **Complications:** anaemia (erythropoietin), secondary hyperparathyroidism (phosphate binders, vitamin D analogues), metabolic acidosis (bicarbonate), hyperkalaemia.\n- **Pearl:** diabetic nephropathy is characterised by increasing albuminuria, then falling eGFR; Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules on biopsy.\n\n**Glomerulonephritis**\n- **Nephritic syndrome:** haematuria (RBC casts), oliguria, hypertension, mild proteinuria (below 3.5 g/day), oedema. Examples: post-streptococcal GN (low C3, resolves in 6-8 weeks), IgA nephropathy (synpharyngitic haematuria), lupus nephritis, anti-GBM (Goodpasture: lung and kidney disease).\n- **Nephrotic syndrome:** proteinuria above 3.5 g/day, hypoalbuminaemia below 3 g/dL, oedema, hyperlipidaemia, lipiduria (oval fat bodies). Examples: minimal change disease (children, steroid-responsive, normal light microscopy, effaced podocytes on electron microscopy), focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, membranous nephropathy (adults; anti-PLA2R antibodies; subepithelial deposits, spike and dome), diabetic nephropathy, amyloidosis.\n- **Complications:** infection (encapsulated organisms), thromboembolism (renal vein), hypercoagulability.\n- **Pearl:** membranous nephropathy in an adult with no cause is screened for malignancy and for anti-PLA2R antibody.\n\n**Hyponatraemia**\n- **Approach:** serum osmolality, urine osmolality, urine sodium, volume status.\n- **SIADH:** euvolaemic; high urine osmolality (above 100 mOsm/kg); high urine sodium; low uric acid. Causes: small-cell lung cancer, CNS disease, drugs (carbamazepine, SSRIs, cyclophosphamide).\n- **Hypovolaemia:** low urine sodium (renal losses high). **Cerebral salt wasting:** neurosurgical patients, hypovolaemic with high urine sodium.\n- **Correction limit:** no more than 8 mmol/L in 24 hours (risk of osmotic demyelination, especially in chronic hyponatraemia with malnutrition or alcohol use).\n- **Pearl:** hypertonic saline (3%) is given in severe symptomatic hyponatraemia (seizures, coma).\n\n**Hyperkalaemia and hypokalaemia**\n- **Hyperkalaemia emergency:** calcium gluconate (stabilises membrane), insulin with glucose, salbutamol, then remove K+ (diuretics, dialysis, potassium binders).\n- **Hypokalaemia:** causes include diuretics, vomiting, diarrhoea, hyperaldosteronism; ECG shows U waves, flattened T waves; replace with oral or IV potassium, check magnesium.\n\n**Acid-base**\n- **Metabolic acidosis:** anion gap = Na - (Cl + HCO3); high-anion-gap causes: MUDPILES (methanol, uraemia, DKA, propylene glycol, paraldehyde/phenformin, isoniazid/iron, lactic acidosis, ethylene glycol, salicylates).\n- **Respiratory acidosis:** hypoventilation, raised PaCO2. Compensation: raised HCO3 (chronic).\n\n#### Table: Prerenal Azotemia vs Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN)\n\n| Diagnostic Index | Prerenal Azotemia (Intact Tubules) | Acute Tubular Necrosis (Damaged Tubules) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Fractional Excretion of Sodium (FENa) | < 1% (avid Na+ reabsorption) | > 2% (inability to conserve Na+) |\n| Urine Sodium Concentration | < 20 mEq/L | > 40 mEq/L |\n| Urine Osmolality | > 500 mOsm/kg (concentrated) | < 350 mOsm/kg (isosthenuric dilute) |\n| BUN / Serum Creatinine Ratio | > 20:1 (passive urea reabsorption) | 10:1 to 15:1 (normal ratio) |\n| Urinary Sediment | Normal or occasional hyaline casts | Muddy brown granular casts / tubular epithelial cells |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Hyperkalemia Emergency:** Tall peaked T-waves -> Wide QRS -> Sine wave. Step 1 is ALWAYS 10% IV Calcium Gluconate (stabilizes membrane in 1-3 mins; does NOT lower K+!).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Hyponatremia Correction Limit:** Max correction <= 8-10 mEq/L in 24 hours to prevent Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (Central Pontine Myelinolysis), confirmed by Brain MRI.\n\n## 20. Medicine (additions)\n\n- **Electrolyte disorders:**\n  - *Hyperkalaemia ECG changes:* peaked T waves, PR prolongation, widened QRS, sine wave; treat with calcium gluconate (membrane stabilisation), insulin plus glucose, salbutamol, and then removal (dialysis, resin).\n  - *Hyponatraemia (SIADH) and osmotic demyelination after rapid correction:* correct sodium by no more than 8-10 mEq/L in 24 hours; overly rapid correction causes central pontine myelinolysis.\n  - *Central diabetes insipidus and desmopressin:* desmopressin for central DI; watch for hyponatraemia.\n  - *Hypokalaemia ECG:* ST depression, flattened T waves, prominent U waves.\n  - *Pearl:* rapid correction of chronic hyponatraemia causes central pontine myelinolysis (dysarthria, quadriparesis, locked-in state).\n- **Acid-base disorders:**\n  - *Hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis after large-volume normal saline:* normal anion gap; chloride-rich fluid causes acidosis; use balanced crystalloids.\n  - *High anion gap ABG:* MUDPILES; calculate delta-delta.\n  - *Primary respiratory acidosis on ABG:* high PCO2, low pH; COPD, neuromuscular disease, sedatives.\n  - *Vomiting-related contraction alkalosis:* hypochloraemic hypokalaemic alkalosis; low chloride; high bicarbonate; treat with saline and potassium.\n  - *Pearl:* vomiting causes metabolic alkalosis with low chloride; saline corrects it.\n- **CKD and dialysis:** CKD staging (eGFR); anaemia (ESA, iron); mineral bone disease (phosphate binders, calcitriol); indications for dialysis (AEIOU: acidosis, electrolytes, intoxication, overload, uraemia).\n  - *Pearl:* AEIOU are the urgent indications for dialysis.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Liddle Syndrome:** ENaC gain of function -> HTN + hypokalemic alkalosis with LOW renin and LOW aldosterone. Treated with AMILORIDE (spironolactone is ineffective!).\n","MED_UNIT_07":"## 1. General Medicine (25-30 questions)\n\n### 1.2 Infectious diseases\n\n- **Cerebral malaria pearl:** hypoglycaemia (quinine can cause it), lactic acidosis, and hyperparasitaemia (above 5%) are the markers of severity.\n\n**Dengue**\n\n### 1.6 Haematology\n\n**Anaemias**\n- **Microcytic (MCV below 80 fL):** iron deficiency (low ferritin, high TIBC, low transferrin saturation; commonest cause in India is nutritional and blood loss); thalassaemia trait (normal or high RBC count, normal ferritin, HbA2 above 3.5%); anaemia of chronic disease (normal or high ferritin, low TIBC); sideroblastic anaemia (ring sideroblasts on Prussian blue).\n- **Macrocytic (MCV above 100 fL):** B12 deficiency (raised MMA and homocysteine; pernicious anaemia with anti-intrinsic factor antibodies; hypersegmented neutrophils, subacute combined degeneration of posterior and lateral columns); folate deficiency (raised homocysteine only; normal MMA); drugs (methotrexate, hydroxyurea, phenytoin, alcohol); liver disease; reticulocytosis.\n- **Normocytic:** anaemia of chronic disease, aplastic anaemia (pancytopenia with hypocellular marrow), haemolysis (raised reticulocytes, LDH, indirect bilirubin, low haptoglobin).\n- **Autoimmune haemolytic anaemia:** Coombs test (direct = IgG or complement on red cells); warm type (IgG, treat with steroids, rituximab).\n- **Pearl:** in B12 deficiency, homocysteine and MMA are both raised; in folate deficiency only homocysteine is raised.\n\n**Leukaemias**\n- **AML:** Auer rods; myeloperoxidase positive; acute promyelocytic leukaemia t(15;17) with DIC, treated with ATRA plus arsenic trioxide.\n- **ALL:** lymphoblasts; TdT positive; children; t(12;21) (good prognosis), Philadelphia-positive ALL (poor prognosis, imatinib added).\n- **CML:** t(9;22) (BCR-ABL); high WBC with left shift; low leukocyte alkaline phosphatase; imatinib, dasatinib, nilotinib.\n- **CLL:** smudge (smear) cells; CD5 and CD23 positive; CD19, CD20 (B cell); lymphadenopathy; treatment indicated only when symptomatic (Rai/Binet staging).\n- **Pearl:** ATRA differentiation syndrome (fever, dyspnoea, weight gain) requires dexamethasone.\n\n**Coagulation**\n- **Prolonged PT (extrinsic pathway, factor VII):** warfarin, early liver disease, vitamin K deficiency.\n- **Prolonged aPTT (intrinsic pathway):** heparin, haemophilia A (VIII) and B (IX), lupus anticoagulant (paradoxical thrombosis).\n- **Low platelets:** immune thrombocytopenia (ITP; steroids, IVIG, splenectomy), TTP (microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia, neurological signs, renal failure; plasma exchange), HUS (E. coli O157:H7, renal failure, usually children), DIC (raised PT and aPTT, low fibrinogen, raised D-dimer).\n- **Pearl:** TTP has the pentad of fever, thrombocytopenia, haemolysis, renal failure and neurological signs; ADAMTS13 is deficient.\n\n**Transfusion**\n- **Principles:** transfuse only when needed (restrictive threshold Hb 7 g/dL in most patients, 8 g/dL in cardiac disease).\n- **Reactions:** febrile non-haemolytic (cytokines, leukoreduction), allergic (urticaria, antihistamines), acute haemolytic (ABO incompatibility; fever, back pain, haemoglobinuria, DIC), transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI; hypoxia within 6 hours, bilateral infiltrates).\n- **Pearl:** an acute haemolytic transfusion reaction is stopped at once and the blood bag is returned to the blood bank with the patient's sample.\n\n#### Table: Autoantibodies in Rheumatology & Connective Tissue Diseases\n\n| Disease | Best Screening Test | Most Specific Autoantibody | Clinical Utility / Flare Tracking |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) | ANA (> 98% sensitive) | Anti-Smith (Sm) & Anti-dsDNA | Anti-dsDNA titers correlate with lupus nephritis and disease flares! |\n| Drug-Induced Lupus (DILE) | ANA | Anti-Histone antibodies (> 95%) | Triggered by Hydralazine, Procainamide, Isoniazid (slow acetylators) |\n| Sjögren's Syndrome | ANA | Anti-Ro (SS-A) and Anti-La (SS-B) | Correlates with sicca symptoms, lymphocytic infiltrate of exocrine glands |\n| Diffuse Systemic Sclerosis | ANA | Anti-Scl-70 (Anti-topoisomerase I) | Rapid skin thickening, early interstitial lung disease (ILD) |\n| Limited Systemic Sclerosis (CREST) | ANA | Anti-Centromere antibody | Calcinosis, Raynaud, Esophageal dysmotility, Sclerodactyly, Telangiectasia |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Mentzer Index** = MCV (fL) / RBC count (millions/uL). < 13 indicates Beta-Thalassemia Trait (high RBC count); > 13 indicates Iron Deficiency Anemia (low iron stores, low ferritin).\n\n## 20. Medicine (additions)\n\n- **HIV and opportunistic infections:**\n  - *TB with HIV, ART timing and rifampicin:* start ART within 2-8 weeks of TB treatment; rifampicin interacts with dolutegravir (double dose); IRIS.\n  - *Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS):* paradoxical worsening after starting ART; treat underlying infection and continue ART.\n  - *Disseminated histoplasmosis in advanced HIV:* fever, pancytopenia; amphotericin B.\n  - *Kaposi sarcoma in HIV:* HHV-8; skin and visceral; ART first.\n  - *Post-COVID rhino-orbital-cerebral mucormycosis:* diabetes and steroids; surgical debridement and amphotericin B.\n  - *Pearl:* in HIV with TB, start ART within 2 weeks if CD4 is low (within 8 weeks in general).\n- **Connective-tissue disorders:**\n  - *Systemic sclerosis:* nucleolar ANA (anti-Scl-70, anti-centromere in limited); pulmonary arterial hypertension; Raynaud; renal crisis (ACE inhibitor treatment).\n  - *Sjogren syndrome:* anti-Ro (SSA) and anti-La (SSB); salivary gland biopsy (focal lymphocytic sialadenitis); dry eyes and mouth.\n  - *Rheumatoid arthritis pannus:* synovial hyperplasia with inflammatory infiltrate; erosions.\n  - *Pearl:* anti-centromere antibody is typical of limited systemic sclerosis (CREST).\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Scrub Typhus (Orientia tsutsugamushi):** Trombiculid chigger bite -> Pathognomonic ESCHAR (black cigarette-burn crust with erythematous halo) in groin/axilla. DOC = DOXYCYCLINE.\n","MICRO_UNIT_01":"## 6. Microbiology (15-18 questions)\n\n### 6.1 General microbiology and sterilisation\n\n- **Sterilisation:** destroys all microorganisms including spores. Methods: moist heat (autoclave 121 C, 15 psi, 15-20 min), dry heat (hot air oven 160 C, 2 h), gamma irradiation, ethylene oxide gas (heat-sensitive instruments), plasma sterilisation (hydrogen peroxide).\n- **Disinfection:** reduces microorganisms (not spores). Chemicals: glutaraldehyde (high-level disinfectant for endoscopes), hypochlorite (bleach), alcohol (70%), phenolics, iodophors.\n- **Pasteurisation:** 63 C for 30 min or 72 C for 15 s; kills most pathogens but not spores.\n- **Biosafety:** Level 1-4 labs; BSL-3 for TB, SARS; BSL-4 for Ebola.\n- **Pearl:** autoclave is the gold standard for sterilising surgical instruments and culture media; glutaraldehyde is used for heat-sensitive equipment such as endoscopes.\n\n### 6.2 Bacteriology: general principles\n\n**Staining**\n- **Gram stain:** crystal violet (primary), iodine (mordant), alcohol (decolouriser), safranin (counterstain). Gram-positive: thick peptidoglycan retains violet; Gram-negative: outer membrane and thin peptidoglycan lose violet, take safranin (pink).\n- **Ziehl-Neelsen (acid-fast):** carbol fuchsin (heated) penetrates mycolic acid; acid-alcohol decolourises non-acid-fast bacteria. M. tuberculosis is red (acid-fast).\n- **Albert stain:** Corynebacterium diphtheriae metachromatic granules (volutin) appear as blue-black dots on a green background (\"Chinese letters\").\n- **Giemsa:** malaria, trypanosomes, Leishman-Donovan bodies.\n- **India ink:** Cryptococcus (capsule, no stain uptake).\n- **Fontana-Masson:** silver stain for melanin.\n- **Pearl:** Chinese letter arrangement indicates Corynebacterium diphtheriae.\n\n**Culture media**\n- **Blood agar:** general purpose; haemolysis (alpha, beta, gamma).\n- **MacConkey agar:** selective for Gram-negatives; lactose fermenters (pink; E. coli, Klebsiella) vs non-fermenters (colourless; Pseudomonas, Proteus, Salmonella, Shigella).\n- **Chocolate agar:** heated blood; Haemophilus influenzae, Neisseria.\n- **Lowenstein-Jensen:** TB; egg-based, malachite green.\n- **Thayer-Martin:** Neisseria gonorrhoeae (antibiotics inhibit contaminants).\n- **TCBS (thiosulphate-citrate-bile salts-sucrose):** Vibrio cholerae (yellow colonies; V. parahaemolyticus green).\n- **Loeffler serum slope:** C. diphtheriae (rapid growth, metachromatic granules).\n- **Sabouraud dextrose agar:** acidic culture medium used to grow fungi (dermatophytes, yeasts).\n- **Pearl:** TCBS is selective for Vibrio; yellow colonies indicate sucrose fermentation (V. cholerae).\n\n**Haemolysis**\n- **Alpha:** partial (green) haemolysis; viridans streptococci, S. pneumoniae.\n- **Beta:** complete (clear) haemolysis; Streptococcus pyogenes, S. aureus, Group B strep.\n- **Gamma haemolysis:** no haemolysis; the colony and agar look unchanged.\n\n### 6.3 Gram-positive cocci\n\n**Staphylococcus**\n- **S. aureus:** coagulase positive; catalase positive; golden colonies; toxins: protein A (binds IgG), leukocidin, enterotoxins (superantigens; food poisoning, toxic shock syndrome via TSST-1), exfoliative toxin (scalded skin syndrome); MRSA (mecA gene, PBP2a).\n- **S. epidermidis:** coagulase negative; catheter-related infections (biofilm); novobiocin sensitive.\n- **S. saprophyticus:** coagulase negative; urinary tract infection in young women; novobiocin resistant.\n- **Clinical:** skin and soft tissue infections, osteomyelitis, endocarditis (IVDU, prosthetic valves), food poisoning (preformed toxin; short incubation of 1-6 hours), TSS (tampons, nasal packing).\n- **Pearl:** Staphylococcal food poisoning has a short incubation period (1-6 hours) because the toxin is preformed in food.\n\n**Streptococcus**\n- **S. pyogenes (Group A):** beta haemolytic; bacitracin sensitive; pharyngitis, impetigo, erysipelas, cellulitis, necrotising fasciitis (M protein, streptolysin O and S, pyrogenic exotoxins); rheumatic fever (molecular mimicry with M protein); post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis.\n- **S. agalactiae (Group B):** neonatal sepsis, meningitis; beta haemolytic; CAMP test positive.\n- **S. pneumoniae:** alpha haemolytic; optochin sensitive; bile soluble; lancet-shaped diplococci; capsule (virulence); pneumonia, meningitis, otitis media, sinusitis; vaccines (PCV13, PPSV23).\n- **Viridans streptococci:** alpha haemolytic; dental plaque; subacute endocarditis (S. mutans, S. sanguinis).\n- **Enterococcus:** Group D; bile-esculin positive; vancomycin resistance (VRE); UTI, endocarditis.\n- **Pearl:** S. pneumoniae is optochin sensitive and bile soluble; S. pyogenes is bacitracin sensitive.\n\n#### Table: Gram-Positive Cocci Differentiation Matrix\n\n| Organism | Catalase | Hemolysis | Key Biochemical Test | Clinical Hallmark |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Staphylococcus aureus | POSITIVE | Beta | Coagulase POSITIVE, Protein A | Abscesses, toxic shock, osteomyelitis, MRSA |\n| Staphylococcus epidermidis | POSITIVE | Gamma (None) | Novobiocin SENSITIVE, Biofilm | Infects prosthetic valves, IV catheters, shunts |\n| Staphylococcus saprophyticus | POSITIVE | Gamma | Novobiocin RESISTANT | Second most common cause of UTI in young sexually active females |\n| Streptococcus pneumoniae | NEGATIVE | Alpha (Green) | Optochin SENSITIVE, Bile soluble | Lobar pneumonia, meningitis, otitis, lancet diplococci |\n| Streptococcus pyogenes (GAS) | NEGATIVE | Beta | Bacitracin SENSITIVE, PYR positive | Pharyngitis, scarlet fever, impetigo, rheumatic fever |\n\n### 6.4 Gram-positive bacilli\n\n**Corynebacterium diphtheriae**\n- Club-shaped, metachromatic granules (Albert stain). Toxin (diphtheria toxin, ADP-ribosylation of EF-2, blocks protein synthesis) causes pseudomembrane (grey, adherent, bleeds on removal) in pharynx.\n- **Complications:** myocarditis (heart block), neuropathy (palate paralysis, paralysis of accommodation).\n- **Diagnosis:** Loeffler medium, Tinsdale medium (brown halo), toxin tests (Elek test).\n- **Treatment:** diphtheria antitoxin (urgent), penicillin or erythromycin; vaccine (DTP).\n- **Pearl:** palatal paralysis and myocarditis are the classic complications.\n\n**Listeria monocytogenes**\n- Gram-positive rod; motile (tumbling motility at 25 C); cold enrichment; pregnancy (abortion, neonatal sepsis, granulomatosis infantiseptica); meningitis in neonates and elderly and immunosuppressed; ampicillin plus gentamicin.\n\n**Bacillus anthracis**\n- Large Gram-positive rod with boxcar appearance (box-shaped ends); capsule (D-glutamate polypeptide); anthrax toxin (protective antigen, lethal factor, oedema factor); cutaneous (black eschar), pulmonary (woolsorter's disease, haemorrhagic mediastinitis), GI forms.\n- **Treatment:** ciprofloxacin or doxycycline plus additional agents for systemic disease.\n\n**Clostridium species (anaerobic spore-formers)**\n- **C. tetani:** drumstick appearance; tetanospasmin (blocks glycine and GABA release in spinal cord); spastic paralysis, trismus, risus sardonicus, opisthotonus; treatment: human tetanus immunoglobulin, metronidazole, wound debridement, benzodiazepines, muscle relaxants, ventilation.\n- **C. botulinum:** botulinum neurotoxin (blocks acetylcholine release at presynaptic neuromuscular junction; flaccid paralysis, descending, cranial nerves first; diplopia, dysphagia); foodborne (preformed toxin), wound, infant (honey); antitoxin, ventilation.\n- **C. perfringens:** gas gangrene, food poisoning (enterotoxin); alpha toxin (lecithinase); double zone haemolysis.\n- **C. difficile:** antibiotic-associated diarrhoea and pseudomembranous colitis (toxins A and B); treatment: oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin (metronidazole second-line); faecal microbiota transplant for recurrent infection.\n- **Pearl:** C. difficile is the most common cause of hospital-acquired diarrhoea; PPIs and broad-spectrum antibiotics are risk factors.\n\n**Actinomyces and Nocardia**\n- Actinomyces: sulphur granules; cervicofacial actinomycosis; penicillin.\n- Nocardia: partially acid-fast, branching filaments; pulmonary and CNS infections in immunosuppressed; sulfonamide.\n\n### 6.5 Gram-negative cocci\n\n- **Neisseria meningitidis:** diplococci; capsule; meningitis, septicaemia (purpuric rash, Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome: adrenal haemorrhage); chemoprophylaxis (rifampicin, ciprofloxacin, ceftriaxone) for contacts; vaccine (MenACWY, MenB).\n- **Neisseria gonorrhoeae:** diplococci (kidney bean shape, intracellular in neutrophils); urethritis, PID, disseminated gonococcal infection (arthritis, dermatitis); ceftriaxone plus azithromycin (resistance concern).\n- **Moraxella catarrhalis:** otitis media, sinusitis in children; beta-lactamase producer.\n- **Pearl:** N. meningitidis causes a petechial or purpuric rash; Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome is adrenal haemorrhage with shock.\n\n### 6.7 Mycobacteria\n\n**Mycobacterium tuberculosis**\n- Acid-fast bacilli; slow-growing; cord formation; Lowenstein-Jensen medium; ZN stain; MGIT liquid culture for faster results.\n- **Latent TB:** Mantoux test (above 10 mm induration in high-risk, above 15 mm in general); IGRA (interferon-gamma release assay); chest X-ray normal; treatment with isoniazid for 6 months (or rifapentine weekly for 3 months).\n- **Active TB:** pulmonary (cough over 2 weeks, haemoptysis, night sweats, weight loss; upper zone infiltrates, cavitation); extrapulmonary (lymph nodes, pleura, spine, meninges, genitourinary, bone).\n- **Drug resistance:** MDR-TB (isoniazid and rifampicin resistance); XDR-TB (plus fluoroquinolone and injectable second-line drug resistance).\n- **Pearl:** Pott disease (TB spine) often affects the lower thoracic and upper lumbar vertebrae, causing kyphosis and paraplegia.\n\n**Mycobacterium leprae**\n- Obligate intracellular; cannot grow in culture; armadillo as reservoir; ZN stain (globi in foam cells).\n- Tuberculoid (few hypopigmented anaesthetic patches, nerve thickening; cell-mediated immunity; paucibacillary) vs lepromatous (many lesions, nodules, facies leonina, madarosis; humoral immunity; multibacillary).\n- Reactions: type 1 (reversal) and type 2 (erythema nodosum leprosum).\n- Treatment: multidrug therapy (WHO): paucibacillary (rifampicin and dapsone, 6 months); multibacillary (rifampicin, dapsone and clofazimine, 12 months).\n- **Pearl:** lepromatous leprosy is multibacillary and infectious; tuberculoid is paucibacillary.\n\n**Non-tuberculous mycobacteria**\n- Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC): AIDS (CD4 below 50), pulmonary disease in COPD and bronchiectasis; azithromycin plus ethambutol plus rifampicin or rifabutin.\n- Mycobacterium kansasii: pulmonary disease.\n- Mycobacterium marinum: swimming pool granuloma.\n- **Pearl:** MAC prophylaxis in HIV is considered when CD4 is below 50 and no other prophylaxis.\n\n### 6.20 Quick microbiology pearls\n\n- Chinese letter arrangement: C. diphtheriae.\n- Cord formation: M. tuberculosis.\n- Cold enrichment and tumbling motility: Listeria.\n- Pseudomembrane on pharynx: diphtheria.\n- Boxcar: Bacillus anthracis.\n- Drumstick: C. tetani.\n- Curved, microaerophilic: Campylobacter, Helicobacter.\n- Catalase positive: Staphylococcus, Aspergillus, Nocardia.\n- Coagulase positive: S. aureus.\n- Optochin sensitive: S. pneumoniae.\n- Bacitracin sensitive: S. pyogenes.\n- Bile soluble: S. pneumoniae.\n\n## 32. Microbiology (additions)\n\n- **Filarial infections (additions):**\n  - *Lymphatic filariasis: Wuchereria and Brugia morphology:* both are sheathed microfilariae found in night blood. Wuchereria's nuclei extend right to the tip of the tail; Brugia's nuclei stop short of the tip, leaving a terminal nucleus-free space. Antigen-guided microscopy and rapid antigen tests support diagnosis; lymphatic obstruction causes elephantiasis.\n  - *Pearl:* Wuchereria nuclei extend to the tail tip; Brugia nuclei do not, and Brugia has two separated terminal nuclei.\n- **Bacterial meningitis (additions):**\n  - *Meningococcaemia and fulminant meningococcal meningitis:* petechial or purpuric rash; DIC; Waterhouse-Friderichsen; ceftriaxone plus steroids.\n  - *Haemophilus influenzae meningitis and satellitism:* satellitism on blood agar near Staphylococcus aureus.\n  - *Pearl:* satellitism (growth around S. aureus colonies on blood agar) identifies H. influenzae.\n- **Dimorphic systemic mycoses (additions):**\n  - *Histoplasma morphology:* small intracellular yeasts in macrophages.\n  - *Blastomycosis morphology:* broad-based budding yeast.\n  - *Coccidioidomycosis desert rheumatism:* erythema nodosum; desert regions; arthralgia.\n  - *Pearl:* Coccidioides spherules contain endospores; desert rheumatism is a presentation.\n- **Arboviral infections:** dengue (Aedes; NS1 antigen; platelets), chikungunya (arthralgia), JE, West Nile, yellow fever, Zika (congenital microcephaly).\n- **Paediatric viral infections and vaccines:** measles, mumps, rubella, polio, varicella, rotavirus, RSV.\n- **Immune-mediated gastrointestinal disease:** coeliac disease (tTG-IgA, villous atrophy); Whipple disease (PAS positive); autoimmune enteropathy.\n- **Spirochetal infections:** syphilis, Lyme, leptospirosis, relapsing fever (Borrelia recurrentis; louse-borne; relapsing fever); yaws.\n- **Trematode infections:** Schistosoma, Fasciola, Paragonimus, Clonorchis (cholangiocarcinoma), Opisthorchis (cholangiocarcinoma).\n- **Rickettsial infections:** typhus, scrub typhus, RMSF (Rocky Mountain spotted fever: triad of fever, rash, headache), Q fever (see 6.9).\n","MICRO_UNIT_02":"## 6. Microbiology (15-18 questions)\n\n### 6.6 Gram-negative bacilli\n\n**Enterobacteriaceae**\n- **Escherichia coli:**\n  - *Uropathogenic (UPEC):* commonest cause of UTI; P fimbriae.\n  - *ETEC:* traveller's diarrhoea; heat-labile and heat-stable enterotoxins; watery diarrhoea.\n  - *EPEC:* infants; attaching and effacing lesions.\n  - *EHEC (O157:H7):* verotoxin (Shiga-like toxin); bloody diarrhoea; haemolytic uraemic syndrome (HUS: haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, renal failure); avoid antibiotics (increase toxin release).\n  - *EIEC (enteroinvasive E. coli):* invades colonic mucosa; dysentery-like illness.\n  - *EAEC (enteroaggregative E. coli):* adheres in stacked clusters; persistent diarrhoea in children.\n  - *Neonatal meningitis (E. coli):* the K1 capsule antigen is the virulence factor for E. coli meningitis in newborns.\n- **Klebsiella pneumoniae:** capsule (mucoid colonies); hospital-acquired pneumonia; alcoholics, diabetics; \"currant jelly\" sputum; ESBL and carbapenem resistance.\n- **Proteus mirabilis:** urease producer; swarming motility; alkaline urine; struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate) stones.\n- **Salmonella:** non-lactose fermenters; H2S producers (black colonies on XLD); enteric fever (Typhi, Paratyphi); gastroenteritis (Typhimurium, Enteritidis); carriers in gallbladder.\n- **Shigella:** non-motile; non-lactose fermenters; invasive; dysentery (bloody diarrhoea, tenesmus); Shiga toxin (S. dysenteriae 1); HUS complication.\n- **Vibrio cholerae:** comma-shaped; cholera toxin (ADP-ribosylation of Gs, raises cAMP, secretory diarrhoea: rice water stools); oral rehydration therapy is the mainstay.\n- **Pearl:** Shigella is non-motile, unlike Salmonella and E. coli (motile); Shigella is also a cause of HUS, like EHEC.\n\n**Pseudomonas aeruginosa**\n- Gram-negative rod; oxidase positive; pyocyanin (blue-green pigment); grape-like odour; resistant to many antibiotics; nosocomial infections (ventilator-associated pneumonia, burns, cystic fibrosis, UTI with catheter); treatment: antipseudomonal penicillin, ceftazidime, cefepime, carbapenem, aminoglycoside, colistin.\n- **Pearl:** Pseudomonas is oxidase positive; Enterobacteriaceae are oxidase negative.\n\n**Haemophilus influenzae**\n- Small coccobacillus; requires X (haemin) and V (NAD) factors (chocolate agar); meningitis (children, now rare with Hib vaccine), epiglottitis, sinusitis, otitis media, pneumonia (COPD).\n- **Pearl:** Hib vaccine has nearly eliminated invasive H. influenzae type b disease.\n\n**Bordetella pertussis**\n- Coccobacillus; whooping cough; catarrhal stage (cough, coryza), paroxysmal stage (paroxysms, whoop, post-tussive vomiting, lymphocytosis), convalescent stage; Bordet-Gengou medium; azithromycin.\n- **Pearl:** lymphocytosis (above 20,000/mm3) is typical in pertussis.\n\n**Legionella pneumophila**\n- Gram-negative; poorly stained; BCYE agar (requires cysteine, iron); Legionnaires disease (pneumonia, hyponatraemia, diarrhoea, confusion); Pontiac fever (mild); water systems and air conditioning; urinary antigen test; treatment: azithromycin or levofloxacin.\n\n**Brucella**\n- Gram-negative coccobacillus; undulant fever; raw milk; osteomyelitis, sacroiliitis, endocarditis; doxycycline plus rifampicin.\n\n**Yersinia pestis**\n- Plague; bipolar staining (\"safety pin\"); bubonic (buboes, flea bite), septicaemic, pneumonic; streptomycin or gentamicin; doxycycline.\n\n**Campylobacter jejuni**\n- Gram-negative curved rod; diarrhoea (bloody), Guillain-Barre syndrome (molecular mimicry with ganglioside); erythromycin or azithromycin.\n\n**Helicobacter pylori**\n- Curved Gram-negative rod; urease positive; peptic ulcer, gastric cancer (MALT lymphoma); eradication: PPI plus clarithromycin plus amoxicillin (or metronidazole) for 14 days.\n- **Pearl:** H. pylori is a class I carcinogen linked to gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma.\n\n**Pearl (Gram-negative zoonoses):** Leptospira (weil disease: jaundice, renal failure, haemorrhage; doxycycline or penicillin); Bartonella (cat scratch disease; Bartonella henselae; bacillary angiomatosis in HIV); Rickettsia (spotted fever; Weil-Felix test with Proteus OX-19 and OX-2; doxycycline).\n\n#### Table: Salmonella vs Shigella Comparison\n\n| Feature | Salmonella enterica (Typhi) | Shigella dysenteriae |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Motility (Flagella) | MOTILE (Flagellated) | NON-MOTILE |\n| H2S Production | PRODUCES H2S (Black colonies) | NO H2S produced (Colorless colonies) |\n| Infectious Dose | HIGH (acid-labile, killed by gastric acid) | EXTREMELY LOW (< 100 organisms, acid-stable) |\n| Spread Mechanism | Disseminates hematogenously via monocytes | Spreads laterally cell-to-cell via actin tails (IcsA) |\n| Primary Pathology | Typhoid fever (Peyer patch necrosis, rose spots) | Bacillary dysentery (bloody, mucoid stools with tenesmus) |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **ETEC Toxins:** Labile in the Air (cAMP), Stable on the Ground (cGMP).\n\n### 6.8 Spirochaetes\n\n- **Treponema pallidum (syphilis):** primary (chancre: painless ulcer with indurated base), secondary (rash on palms and soles, mucous patches, lymphadenopathy, condyloma lata), latent, tertiary (gummas, aortitis, tabes dorsalis, general paresis). Diagnosis: VDRL or RPR (non-treponemal; titres); TPHA or FTA-ABS (treponemal; confirm). Treatment: benzathine penicillin G (primary, secondary, early latent); doxycycline or ceftriaxone for penicillin-allergic.\n- **Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction:** fever, myalgia, and rash within 24 hours of treatment, from release of antigens.\n- **Borrelia burgdorferi (Lyme disease):** erythema migrans (bull's-eye rash after tick bite); arthritis (knee), carditis (AV block), neuroborreliosis (Bell palsy, meningitis); doxycycline.\n- **Leptospira:** see the zoonoses section.\n- **Pearl:** the Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction is a fever after starting treatment for syphilis or Lyme disease; reassure, and give steroids if severe.\n\n### 6.9 Chlamydia, Mycoplasma and Rickettsia\n\n- **Chlamydia trachomatis:** obligate intracellular; elementary bodies (infectious) and reticulate bodies (replicating); serovars A-C (trachoma), D-K (sexually transmitted, urethritis, PID, lymphogranuloma venereum (L1-L3)), neonatal conjunctivitis, pneumonia in infants. Treatment: azithromycin or doxycycline.\n- **Mycoplasma pneumoniae:** no cell wall (resistant to beta-lactams); atypical pneumonia; cold agglutinins (IgM, haemolysis); erythromycin or doxycycline.\n- **Rickettsia:** obligate intracellular; flea, tick, and louse vectors; typhus (epidemic: louse-borne, Rickettsia prowazekii; murine: flea-borne), spotted fevers (tick), scrub typhus (Orientia tsutsugamushi; mite; eschar); Weil-Felix test (cross-reactivity with Proteus OX-19 and OX-2, not specific); doxycycline.\n- **Coxiella burnetii:** Q fever; obligate intracellular; raw milk; endocarditis; doxycycline.\n- **Pearl:** Mycoplasma lacks a cell wall, so beta-lactams are ineffective; scrub typhus is treated with doxycycline or azithromycin.\n\n### 6.20 Quick microbiology pearls\n\n- Safety pin: Yersinia pestis.\n- Comma-shaped: Vibrio cholerae.\n- Pigmented blue-green: Pseudomonas (pyocyanin).\n- Grape-like colonies with odour: Pseudomonas.\n- Non-motile, lactose non-fermenter: Shigella.\n- Motile, lactose non-fermenter: Salmonella.\n- Lactose fermenter (pink on MacConkey): E. coli, Klebsiella.\n- Oxidase positive: Pseudomonas, Vibrio, Neisseria, Campylobacter.\n\n## 32. Microbiology (additions)\n\n- **Enteric and food-borne bacterial infections:**\n  - *Campylobacter jejuni after unpasteurised milk:* bloody diarrhoea; can trigger Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS).\n  - *Vibrio parahaemolyticus and Kanagawa phenomenon:* seafood diarrhoea; Kanagawa test (haemolysis on Wagatsuma agar).\n  - *Staphylococcal food poisoning from cream pastry (preformed enterotoxin):* see 6.3.\n  - *Early enteric-fever diagnosis by blood culture:* blood culture is most sensitive in the first week; the Widal test is less reliable and comes later.\n  - *Culture-media selection for suspected Shigella:* MacConkey and XLD; stool culture.\n  - *Clostridioides difficile toxin-based diagnosis:* toxin A and B EIA; PCR; culture not for diagnosis.\n  - *Pearl:* in suspected enteric fever, blood culture is positive in the first week, and stool culture later.\n- **Sexually transmitted infections (additions):**\n  - *Secondary syphilis: condyloma lata (moist wart-like plaques), generalised rash and alopecia:* see 6.8.\n  - *Primary syphilis: dark-field microscopy of chancre exudate:* Treponema pallidum motile spirochaetes.\n  - *Trichomonas vaginalis twitching motility on wet mount:* flagellate; saline wet mount.\n  - *Gonococcal urethritis: Gram-stain diagnosis and ceftriaxone treatment:* intracellular Gram-negative diplococci in neutrophils; ceftriaxone plus azithromycin.\n  - *HPV oncogenesis and associated carcinoma:* HPV 16, 18 (high risk); cervical, anal, oropharyngeal cancer; vaccine.\n  - *Pearl:* intracellular gram-negative diplococci in neutrophils indicate gonorrhoea.\n- **Opportunistic and healthcare-associated bacteria (additions):**\n  - *Nocardia identification:* branching Gram-positive filaments, partially acid-fast; TMP-SMX.\n  - *Coagulase-negative staphylococcal UTI:* S. saprophyticus in young women; nitrofurantoin.\n  - *Bacterial pneumonia in advanced HIV:* pneumococcus, H. influenzae, Pseudomonas; ART and prophylaxis.\n  - *Pseudomonas aeruginosa in burn wound:* green pus; topical silver sulfadiazine; systemic antipseudomonal.\n  - *Pearl:* Pseudomonas is a common burn wound invader; green pigment (pyocyanin) and grape-like smell.\n- **Bacterial virulence and biofilms:** biofilms (polysaccharide matrix) resist antibiotics and host defences; catheter infections (S. epidermidis, Pseudomonas); quorum sensing; capsule and fimbriae.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Branching Rods: Actinomyces** = Anaerobic, NOT acid-fast. Nocardia = Aerobic, Partially Acid-Fast.\n","MICRO_UNIT_03":"## 6. Microbiology (15-18 questions)\n\n### 6.10 Virology: general principles\n\n- **Classification:** DNA vs RNA; enveloped vs non-enveloped; single-stranded vs double-stranded; positive-sense vs negative-sense.\n- **Replication:** attachment, entry, uncoating, replication (genome copy and mRNA), assembly, release.\n- **Antiviral targets:** entry inhibitors (maraviroc), reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs, NNRTIs), integrase inhibitors (dolutegravir), protease inhibitors (lopinavir), neuraminidase inhibitors (oseltamivir), polymerase inhibitors (sofosbuvir, ribavirin).\n- **Pearl:** reverse transcriptase is the target of HIV drugs; HBV DNA polymerase is inhibited by tenofovir and entecavir.\n\n### 6.11 Hepatitis viruses\n\n| Virus | Genome | Transmission | Chronic? | Key feature |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| HAV | RNA, picornavirus | Faecal-oral | No | Acute hepatitis; vaccine; no chronic state |\n| HBV | DNA, hepadnavirus | Blood, sex, perinatal | Yes | HBsAg; window period; HCC; vaccine |\n| HCV | RNA, flavivirus | Blood | Yes (80%) | DAA cure; no vaccine |\n| HDV | RNA (defective) | Blood (needs HBV) | Yes | Co-infection or super-infection with HBV |\n| HEV | RNA, hepevirus | Faecal-oral (water) | No (usually) | High mortality in pregnancy (third trimester) |\n- **HBV serology:**\n  - HBsAg positive: infection (acute or chronic; chronic if above 6 months).\n  - Anti-HBs positive: immunity (vaccine or natural).\n  - HBeAg positive: high infectivity (replication).\n  - Anti-HBc IgM: acute infection.\n  - Anti-HBc IgG: past or chronic infection.\n  - HBV DNA: viral load.\n- **Window period:** HBsAg has disappeared and anti-HBs not yet detectable; anti-HBc IgM positive.\n- **HDV:** requires HBsAg for packaging; co-infection causes acute hepatitis; super-infection in chronic HBV carriers causes more severe disease.\n- **Pearl:** HEV in pregnancy causes fulminant hepatic failure; HBV vaccination at birth is universal.\n\n#### Table: Hepatitis B Serological Interpretations\n\n| Clinical State | HBsAg | Anti-HBs | Anti-HBc IgM | Anti-HBc IgG | HBeAg |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Acute Infection | POSITIVE | Negative | POSITIVE | Negative | Positive (active replication) |\n| Window Period | Negative | Negative | POSITIVE | Negative | Negative / Anti-HBe+ |\n| Chronic Carrier (High) | POSITIVE | Negative | Negative | POSITIVE | POSITIVE |\n| Resolved Past Infection | Negative | POSITIVE | Negative | POSITIVE | Negative |\n| Vaccine-Induced Immunity | Negative | POSITIVE | Negative | NEGATIVE | Negative |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Vaccinated** = Anti-HBs alone is positive (Anti-HBc is NEGATIVE).\n\n### 6.12 HIV\n\n- **Structure:** RNA retrovirus; gp120 (binds CD4 and CCR5/CXCR4), gp41 (fusion), reverse transcriptase, integrase, protease.\n- **Lifecycle:** entry, reverse transcription, integration (provirus), transcription, translation, assembly, budding.\n- **Stages:** acute (flu-like, viraemia, CD4 falls), latency (asymptomatic, years), AIDS (CD4 below 200, opportunistic infections).\n- **Diagnosis:** 4th-generation antigen/antibody test (p24 antigen + antibodies); confirm with HIV-1/2 differentiation; RNA PCR for window period and infants.\n- **Opportunistic infections (CD4 thresholds):** Pneumocystis (below 200), toxoplasmosis (below 100), cryptococcal meningitis (below 100), CMV retinitis (below 50), MAC (below 50), PML (JC virus, any CD4 level, below 200 risk).\n- **Treatment:** ART for all; first-line: tenofovir plus lamivudine (or emtricitabine) plus dolutegravir.\n- **Pearl:** toxoplasmosis is the commonest cause of focal brain lesions in AIDS; cryptococcal meningitis is the commonest meningitis in AIDS.\n\n### 6.13 Herpesviruses\n\n| Virus | Disease | Key feature |\n|---|---|---|\n| HSV-1 | Cold sores, encephalitis (temporal lobe) | Herpetic encephalitis: acyclovir early |\n| HSV-2 | Genital herpes, neonatal herpes | Neonatal: disseminated, CNS, skin |\n| VZV | Chickenpox, shingles | Shingles: dermatomal rash; reactivation |\n| CMV | Congenital infection, mononucleosis, retinitis | Owl's eye inclusion; hearing loss, microcephaly |\n| EBV | Infectious mononucleosis, Burkitt, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin | Atypical lymphocytes; heterophile positive |\n| HHV-6 | Roseola infantum (exanthem subitum) | Infants |\n| HHV-8 | Kaposi sarcoma | Spindle cells |\n- **EBV:** B lymphocytes via CD21 (CR2); atypical lymphocytes on blood film; heterophile antibody (Paul-Bunnell test); IgM anti-VCA; EBNA appears later.\n- **CMV:** congenital infection causes microcephaly, periventricular calcification, sensorineural deafness; inclusion bodies (owl's eye).\n- **Pearl:** congenital CMV is the commonest congenital infection causing sensorineural hearing loss.\n\n### 6.14 Respiratory and other viruses\n\n- **Influenza:** orthomyxovirus; segmented RNA; antigenic drift (point mutations, seasonal) and shift (reassortment, pandemic); HA (haemagglutinin) and NA (neuraminidase); oseltamivir (neuraminidase inhibitor; start within 48 hours).\n- **RSV:** paramyxovirus; bronchiolitis in infants; palivizumab prophylaxis in high-risk infants.\n- **Parainfluenza:** croup (barking cough, steeple sign on X-ray).\n- **Adenovirus:** conjunctivitis (pharyngoconjunctival fever), pneumonia; no specific antiviral.\n- **Rhinovirus:** common cold; picornavirus.\n- **Measles:** paramyxovirus; Koplik spots (white spots on buccal mucosa), maculopapular rash, cough, coryza, conjunctivitis (3 Cs); vitamin A; MMR vaccine.\n- **Rabies:** rhabdovirus; Negri bodies; hydrophobia; encephalitis; post-exposure prophylaxis (wound washing, rabies immunoglobulin, vaccine).\n- **Polio:** enterovirus; anterior horn cell destruction; flaccid paralysis; OPV (live) vs IPV (inactivated).\n- **Pearl:** rabies is nearly 100% fatal once symptoms appear; post-exposure prophylaxis must be started early.\n\n### 6.15 Haemorrhagic fevers and emerging viruses\n\n- **Dengue:** flavivirus; Aedes mosquito; four serotypes; severe dengue in secondary infection (antibody-dependent enhancement).\n- **Ebola:** filovirus; haemorrhagic fever; direct contact with body fluids; supportive care plus monoclonal antibodies.\n- **Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever (CCHF):** tick-borne bunyavirus causing haemorrhagic fever with high mortality.\n- **Yellow fever:** flavivirus; Aedes; jaundice, haemorrhage; live attenuated vaccine (WHO certificate for travellers).\n- **Pearl:** yellow fever is prevented by live attenuated vaccine; the certificate is required for entry in some countries.\n\n## 32. Microbiology (additions)\n\n- **Congenital and haematologic viral infections:** congenital CMV, rubella, HSV, varicella, HIV; EBV (infectious mononucleosis); parvovirus B19 (aplastic crisis in sickle cell; erythema infectiosum).\n- **Viral encephalitis:** HSV (temporal lobe; acyclovir); Japanese encephalitis (Culex; pigs); enterovirus; arbovirus.\n- **HIV diagnosis and monitoring:** 4th-generation test; viral load (RNA PCR); CD4 count; resistance testing.\n- **Serology and immunodiagnostic pitfalls:** false-positive VDRL (lupus, pregnancy, malaria); window period of HIV; cross-reactions (Weil-Felix); IgM persistence; prozone phenomenon (very high antibody titre gives false-negative).\n  - *Pearl:* prozone phenomenon gives false-negative results with very high antibody concentration; dilute to detect.\n","MICRO_UNIT_04":"## 6. Microbiology (15-18 questions)\n\n### 6.16 Parasitology\n\n**Protozoa**\n- **Plasmodium:** see Medicine (malaria). Morphology: P. falciparum (crescent gametocytes, multiple ring forms), P. vivax (enlarged infected RBCs, Schuffner dots).\n- **Entamoeba histolytica:** trophozoite with ingested RBCs; cyst with 4 nuclei; flask-shaped ulcers in colon; amoebic liver abscess (anchovy-sauce pus); metronidazole plus luminal agent (diloxanide or paromomycin).\n- **Giardia lamblia:** trophozoite (pear-shaped, \"falling leaf\" motility, ventral sucking disc); cyst (falciform); malabsorption, diarrhoea; metronidazole or tinidazole.\n- **Cryptosporidium:** acid-fast oocysts; watery diarrhoea in immunosuppressed; nitazoxanide.\n- **Cyclospora:** acid-fast; diarrhoea; co-trimoxazole.\n- **Toxoplasma gondii:** cat definitive host; oocysts; congenital toxoplasmosis (hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis, intracranial calcification); tachyzoites; pyrimethamine plus sulfadiazine plus folinic acid.\n- **Leishmania:** sand fly vector; visceral (kala-azar: fever, splenomegaly, pancytopenia, hypergammaglobulinaemia; amastigotes in macrophages; rK39 test; liposomal amphotericin B); cutaneous (oriental sore; ulcer; topical or systemic antimony).\n- **Trypanosoma:** T. brucei (sleeping sickness; trypanosomes in CSF; suramin, melarsoprol); T. cruzi (Chagas disease; amastigotes; reduviid bug; cardiomyopathy, megaoesophagus, megacolon; benznidazole).\n- **Trichomonas vaginalis:** flagellate; vaginitis (frothy discharge); metronidazole for both partners.\n- **Pearl:** Giardia trophozoites are pear-shaped with a ventral disc; cysts have a falciform shape.\n\n**Helminths (worms)**\n- **Nematodes (roundworms):**\n  - *Ascaris lumbricoides:* large round worm; eggs with mammillated outer coat (embryonated eggs); pulmonary (Loeffler syndrome) and intestinal obstruction; albendazole or mebendazole.\n  - *Ancylostoma duodenale and Necator americanus (hookworm):* eggs in stool; iron-deficiency anaemia; albendazole.\n  - *Enterobius vermicularis (pinworm):* perianal pruritus at night; scotch tape test; albendazole (single dose), repeat in 2 weeks.\n  - *Trichuris trichiura (whipworm):* barrel-shaped eggs with polar plugs; rectal prolapse in children; albendazole or mebendazole.\n  - *Strongyloides stercoralis:* larvae in stool; autoinfection; hyperinfection in immunosuppressed (steroids); ivermectin.\n  - *Wuchereria bancrofti (lymphatic filariasis):* microfilariae in night blood (nocturnal periodicity); elephantiasis; diethylcarbamazine (DEC) plus albendazole (mass drug administration).\n  - *Onchocerca volvulus:* river blindness; nodules; ivermectin.\n- **Cestodes (tapeworms):**\n  - *Taenia solium:* cysticercosis (neurocysticercosis: seizures, calcified cysts); tapeworm in intestine; praziquantel or albendazole for both.\n  - *Taenia saginata:* beef tapeworm; no cysticercosis.\n  - *Echinococcus granulosus:* hydatid cyst (dog); liver and lung cysts; surgery, albendazole.\n  - *Diphyllobothrium latum:* fish tapeworm; B12 deficiency (competes for vitamin); praziquantel.\n- **Trematodes (flukes):**\n  - *Schistosoma haematobium:* urinary schistosomiasis; terminal-spined eggs; bladder cancer (squamous cell); praziquantel.\n  - *Schistosoma mansoni and japonicum:* intestinal; hepatosplenic; portal hypertension; praziquantel.\n  - *Fasciola hepatica:* liver fluke; eosinophilia; triclabendazole.\n  - *Paragonimus westermani:* lung fluke; haemoptysis; praziquantel.\n- **Pearl:** Schistosoma haematobium causes squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder; Taenia solium causes neurocysticercosis.\n\n### 6.17 Mycology\n\n- **Superficial mycoses:** dermatophytes (Trichophyton, Microsporum, Epidermophyton; tinea corporis, cruris, pedis, capitis); tinea versicolor (Malassezia; hypopigmented scaly patches; KOH shows spaghetti and meatballs); tinea nigra (Hortaea werneckii); piedra.\n- **Subcutaneous mycoses:** sporotrichosis (Sporothrix schenckii; lymphocutaneous; rose gardener's disease); chromoblastomycosis (sclerotic bodies); mycetoma (grains, Madurella); eumycetoma; phaeohyphomycosis.\n- **Systemic mycoses:**\n  - *Histoplasma capsulatum:* intracellular yeast in macrophages (small, budding); Mississippi and Ohio valleys; bat and bird droppings; itraconazole or amphotericin B.\n  - *Coccidioides immitis:* spherules with endospores; desert regions (San Joaquin valley fever); fluconazole.\n  - *Blastomyces dermatitidis:* broad-based budding; pulmonary and skin disease; itraconazole.\n  - *Paracoccidioides brasiliensis:* mariner's wheel; South America; itraconazole.\n  - *Cryptococcus neoformans:* encapsulated yeast (India ink); meningitis in AIDS; amphotericin B plus flucytosine.\n- **Opportunistic mycoses:**\n  - *Candida albicans:* pseudohyphae and budding yeasts; oral thrush, vulvovaginitis, candidaemia, endocarditis; fluconazole, echinocandins.\n  - *Aspergillus fumigatus:* septate hyphae branching at 45 degrees; allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA: raised IgE, eosinophils, mucus plugs), aspergilloma (fungus ball in old cavity), invasive aspergillosis (neutropenia, transplant); voriconazole or liposomal amphotericin B.\n  - *Mucor, Rhizopus (Mucorales):* broad, ribbon-like, non-septate hyphae at right angles; diabetic ketoacidosis, rhinocerebral mucormycosis; amphotericin B plus surgery.\n  - *Pneumocystis jirovecii:* cysts (silver stain); pneumonia in AIDS; co-trimoxazole.\n- **Pearl:** Mucor is non-septate and broad; Aspergillus is septate with 45-degree branching; both are seen in diabetic ketoacidosis (Mucor) and neutropenia (Aspergillus).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Aspergillus** = Acute 45-degree angle branching. Mucor = Right 90-degree angle branching, non-septate.\n\n### 6.18 Immunology\n\n**Innate and adaptive immunity**\n- **Innate:** barriers, complement, phagocytes (neutrophils, macrophages), NK cells, toll-like receptors.\n- **Adaptive:** B cells (antibodies), T cells (CD4 helper, CD8 cytotoxic), antigen-presenting cells (dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells).\n- **Antibodies:** IgG (most abundant; crosses placenta; secondary response), IgM (first in primary response; pentamer; blood group antibodies), IgA (mucosal, secretory IgA), IgE (allergy, parasites), IgD (B cell receptor).\n- **MHC:** MHC class I (all nucleated cells; CD8; endogenous antigens); MHC class II (APCs; CD4; exogenous antigens).\n- **Complement:** classical (antibody), alternative (microbial surfaces), lectin (mannose-binding lectin); C5 to C9 forms the membrane attack complex; C3 deficiency causes recurrent pyogenic infections; C5-C9 deficiency causes Neisseria infections.\n- **Pearl:** C5-C9 deficiency predisposes to Neisseria infections (meningococcal and gonococcal).\n\n**Hypersensitivity**\n- **Type I (IgE):** immediate; mast cell degranulation (histamine, leukotrienes); anaphylaxis, asthma, urticaria.\n- **Type II (cytotoxic):** antibody against cell surface antigens; haemolytic anaemia (warm antibodies), thrombocytopenia (ITP), Goodpasture syndrome (anti-GBM), myasthenia gravis (anti-AChR), Graves disease (TSH receptor).\n- **Type III (immune complex):** complexes deposit in vessels; serum sickness (about 7-14 days after foreign protein), SLE, post-streptococcal GN, Arthus reaction.\n- **Type IV (delayed, T cell):** contact dermatitis (nickel), TB skin test (Mantoux), graft-versus-host disease, granulomatous reaction.\n- **Pearl:** the Mantoux test is a type IV (delayed) reaction; anaphylaxis is type I.\n\n**Immunodeficiencies**\n- **Primary humoral:** X-linked agammaglobulinaemia (BTK mutation; no B cells; infections with encapsulated organisms after 6 months when maternal IgG wanes); common variable immunodeficiency; IgA deficiency (most common; anaphylaxis with IgA-containing blood products).\n- **Primary cellular:** DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion; third and fourth pharyngeal pouch defect; hypocalcaemia, conotruncal heart defects); SCID (no T cells; X-linked IL2RG most common; severe infections; bone marrow transplant).\n- **Phagocyte disorders:** chronic granulomatous disease (NADPH oxidase; catalase-positive organisms: Staphylococcus, Aspergillus, Burkholderia; nitroblue tetrazolium test); Chediak-Higashi (LYST; giant granules; albinism; neuropathy); LAD (see pathology).\n- **Complement deficiencies:** C3 deficiency causes recurrent pyogenic infections; C5-C9 deficiency predisposes to Neisseria infections (see Section 6.18).\n- **Pearl:** catalase-positive organisms (Staphylococcus aureus, Aspergillus, Burkholderia cepacia) cause infections in chronic granulomatous disease.\n\n**Autoimmune disorders**\n- **SLE:** ANA (high sensitivity), anti-dsDNA (high specificity), anti-Smith (specific), anti-histone (drug-induced lupus); complement (low C3, C4); malar rash, arthritis, nephritis (wire loop), serositis.\n- **Rheumatoid arthritis:** anti-CCP (high specificity); RF; symmetric small-joint synovitis; pannus; erosions.\n- **Myasthenia gravis:** anti-AChR; thymoma; ptosis, diplopia, fatigability; pyridostigmine; thymectomy.\n- **Graves disease:** TSH receptor antibodies (TRAb); thyrotoxicosis; exophthalmos (retro-orbital inflammation).\n- **Hashimoto thyroiditis:** anti-thyroid peroxidase (TPO) antibodies; hypothyroidism; Hurthle cells; MALT lymphoma risk.\n- **Pearl:** anti-dsDNA is specific for SLE; anti-CCP is specific for rheumatoid arthritis.\n\n**Transplant immunology**\n- **HLA matching:** class I (A, B, C) and class II (DR, DQ, DP).\n- **Rejection:** hyperacute (preformed antibodies), acute (T cell mediated), chronic (vasculopathy).\n- **Immunosuppression:** calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, cyclosporine), mycophenolate, steroids, antithymocyte globulin, basiliximab.\n\n#### Table: Coombs & Gell Hypersensitivity Classification\n\n| Type | Name / Immune Mechanism | Key Mediators | Classic Clinical Examples |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Type I | Immediate / Anaphylactic | IgE cross-linking, Mast cell Histamine | Anaphylaxis, Urticaria, Allergic asthma |\n| Type II | Cytotoxic / Antibody-Mediated | IgG / IgM binding fixed tissue antigen | Goodpasture, Myasthenia Gravis, Graves, AIHA |\n| Type III | Immune Complex Mediated | Soluble circulating antigen-antibody complexes | SLE, Serum Sickness, Post-strep GN, Polyarteritis nodosa |\n| Type IV | Delayed / Cell-Mediated | Sensitized T cells and Macrophages (NO antibodies) | Tuberculin skin test (PPD), Poison ivy, Graft vs Host |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Hypersensitivity ACID:** Anaphylactic (I), Cytotoxic (II), Immune complex (III), Delayed T-cell (IV).\n\n### 6.19 Vaccines and immunisation\n\n- **Live attenuated:** BCG, MMR, varicella, oral polio (OPV), yellow fever, rotavirus, typhoid (oral Ty21a), intranasal influenza. Contraindicated in severe immunosuppression and pregnancy.\n- **Inactivated (killed):** IPV, influenza injection, rabies, hepatitis A, typhoid Vi polysaccharide, cholera.\n- **Subunit or recombinant:** hepatitis B (HBsAg), HPV, acellular pertussis, meningococcal polysaccharide (Men ACWY).\n- **Toxoid:** inactivated bacterial toxin given as a vaccine (diphtheria and tetanus), inducing antitoxin antibodies.\n- **Conjugate:** Hib, PCV, MenACWY (polysaccharide linked to protein; T cell response, works in infants).\n- **Pearl:** BCG protects against disseminated TB in children; it is not reliably protective against pulmonary TB in adults.\n\n### 6.20 Quick microbiology pearls\n\n- Urease positive: Proteus, Helicobacter, Cryptococcus, Ureaplasma.\n\n## 32. Microbiology (additions)\n\n- **Opportunistic mycoses (additions):**\n  - *Aspergillus morphology: acute-angle septate dichotomous branching:* septate hyphae branching at 45 degrees (see 6.17).\n  - *Angioinvasive mould infection: hyphal morphology and amphotericin B:* invasive aspergillosis; voriconazole.\n  - *Pneumocystis crescent cysts on GMS (Grocott methenamine silver) stain in HIV:* see 6.17.\n  - *Talaromyces marneffei dimorphism in HIV:* red yeast-like cells at 37 C; hyphae at 25 C; penicilliosis; amphotericin B.\n  - *Candida speciation and CHROMagar:* colour of colonies identifies C. albicans (green), C. krusei (pink), C. tropicalis (blue).\n  - *Pearl:* C. albicans is green on CHROMagar; C. krusei is rough and pink.\n- **Protozoal tissue infections (additions):**\n  - *Congenital toxoplasmosis:* see 6.16 (hydrocephalus, chorioretinitis, intracranial calcification).\n  - *Cutaneous leishmaniasis due to Leishmania tropica:* dry urban (Delhi boil); self-healing; antimony or thermotherapy.\n  - *Plasmodium falciparum HRP-2 rapid test:* detects histidine-rich protein 2; used in rapid diagnosis.\n  - *Visceral leishmaniasis vector:* sand fly Phlebotomus argentipes.\n  - *Pearl:* Leishmania tropica causes urban cutaneous leishmaniasis (Delhi boil), and Leishmania donovani causes kala-azar.\n- **Intestinal nematode infections (additions):**\n  - *Enterobius vermicularis egg and organism identification:* eggs on perianal swab (scotch tape); asymmetric eggs with flattened side.\n  - *Strongyloides hyperinfection in steroid use:* larvae disseminate; treat with ivermectin.\n  - *Pearl:* Strongyloides hyperinfection in patients on steroids can be fatal.\n- **Superficial and subcutaneous mycoses (additions):**\n  - *Chromoblastomycosis with copper-penny bodies:* sclerotic bodies in tissue; verrucous lesions.\n  - *Mycetoma with bacterial or fungal aetiology:* Actinomadura, Streptomyces (bacterial) vs Madurella (fungal); grains show different colour.\n  - *Trichophyton rubrum with red pigment and pencil-shaped macroconidia:* T. rubrum colonies are red on reverse; macroconidia.\n  - *Pearl:* T. rubrum colonies produce a red reverse pigment.\n- **Terminal complement deficiency:** C5-C9 deficiency (see 6.18, Neisseria infections).\n","OBGY_UNIT_01":"## 2. Obstetrics and Gynaecology (25-30 questions)\n\n### 2.1 Antenatal care and dating\n\n- **Gestational age:** Naegele rule (first day of last menstrual period + 7 days - 3 months, + 1 year); ultrasound in the first trimester (crown-rump length 45-84 mm for 9-14 weeks) is the most accurate.\n- **Booking tests:** blood group and Rh, haemoglobin, HIV, hepatitis B, syphilis, thyroid, urine culture, rubella immunity, and an anomaly scan at 18-20 weeks.\n- **Pearl:** the dating scan changes the EDD only if it differs by more than the local threshold (usually 7 days before 14 weeks).\n\n### 2.2 High-risk pregnancy (extremely high yield)\n\n**Hypertensive disorders of pregnancy**\n- **Gestational hypertension:** BP 140/90 mmHg or more after 20 weeks, no proteinuria or organ involvement; resolves by 6 weeks postpartum.\n- **Pre-eclampsia:** gestational hypertension plus proteinuria (300 mg/day or more) or organ dysfunction. Severe features: BP 160/110 or more on two occasions 4 hours apart; platelets below 100,000/mm3; serum creatinine above 1.1 mg/dL or doubling; transaminases at least twice the normal; pulmonary oedema; new cerebral or visual symptoms; epigastric or right upper quadrant pain.\n- **HELLP syndrome:** haemolysis (raised LDH, schistocytes), elevated liver enzymes, low platelets. Treat with delivery after stabilisation.\n- **Eclampsia:** seizures in pre-eclampsia, not explained by other causes.\n- **Management of severe pre-eclampsia:** magnesium sulfate for seizure prophylaxis (Pritchard regimen: 4 g IV loading, then 1 g/h); antihypertensives (labetalol IV, nifedipine oral, hydralazine) to keep BP below 160/110; delivery after 34 weeks, or earlier if maternal or fetal indications.\n- **Magnesium toxicity:** lost patellar reflexes first, then respiratory depression, then cardiac arrest. Antidote: calcium gluconate 1 g IV. Monitor urine output (above 30 mL/h) and reflexes.\n- **Pearl:** the first sign of magnesium toxicity is loss of knee reflex, not drowsiness or low respiratory rate.\n\n**Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM)**\n- **Screening:** 75 g oral glucose tolerance test at 24-28 weeks (or earlier in high-risk women). Thresholds vary: IADPSG (fasting 92 mg/dL, 1-hour 180 mg/dL, 2-hour 153 mg/dL), or Carpenter-Coustan (fasting 95, 1-hour 180, 2-hour 155, 3-hour 140 mg/dL). Use the local protocol.\n- **Treatment:** diet and exercise first; insulin if fasting glucose stays at or above target after 1-2 weeks, or post-meal glucose stays high. Metformin and glibenclamide are increasingly used.\n- **Risks:** macrosomia, shoulder dystocia, neonatal hypoglycaemia, neonatal jaundice, polyhydramnios, pre-eclampsia, later type 2 diabetes.\n- **Pearl:** the fetal risks of GDM are mainly macrosomia and neonatal hypoglycaemia; fetal anomalies are linked to pre-existing diabetes, not GDM.\n\n**Rh isoimmunisation**\n- **Mechanism:** an Rh-negative mother is sensitised by Rh-positive fetal red cells (during delivery, abortion, or invasive procedures). IgG anti-D crosses the placenta and causes haemolytic disease of the newborn.\n- **Prevention:** anti-D immunoglobulin at 28 weeks and within 72 hours after delivery of an Rh-positive baby, after miscarriage, abortion, or antepartum haemorrhage.\n- **Monitoring:** antibody titre (critical titre usually 1:16 or more) and middle cerebral artery peak systolic velocity (MCA-PSV) Doppler for anaemia.\n- **Kleihauer-Betke test:** quantifies fetomaternal haemorrhage to dose anti-D.\n- **Pearl:** the first pregnancy is usually not affected because sensitisation occurs at delivery; a second Rh-positive baby is at risk.\n\n**Multiple pregnancy**\n- **Chorionicity:** dichorionic (two placentas, lower risk) vs monochorionic (one placenta, shared circulation; risk of twin-twin transfusion syndrome, TTTS, and twin anaemia-polycythaemia sequence).\n- **TTTS:** donor twin is anaemic and oligohydramnotic; recipient is polycythaemic and polyhydramnotic. Treated with laser ablation of placental anastomoses.\n- **Delivery:** vaginal delivery of the first twin is possible; the second twin is delivered within 30 minutes (interval), breech extraction if needed.\n- **Pearl:** the lambda sign on ultrasound indicates dichorionic twins; the T sign indicates monochorionic.\n\n**Intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR / FGR)**\n- **Definition:** estimated fetal weight below the 10th percentile, or below the 3rd percentile for severe disease, or abdominal circumference below the 2nd-3rd percentile.\n- **Doppler surveillance:** umbilical artery (raised resistance), middle cerebral artery (brain-sparing: low resistance), ductus venosus (a-wave reversal; poor prognosis).\n- **Management:** delivery at about 34-37 weeks depending on Doppler; absent or reversed umbilical artery end-diastolic flow is an indication for delivery after steroids.\n- **Pearl:** the brain-sparing effect (low MCA resistance) indicates that the fetus is redirecting blood to the brain.\n\n**Preterm labour**\n- **Definition:** regular contractions and cervical change before 37 weeks.\n- **Management:** antenatal corticosteroids (betamethasone 12 mg IM two doses 24 hours apart, between 24 and 34 weeks); magnesium sulfate for neuroprotection below 32 weeks; tocolysis (nifedipine) to delay delivery for 48 hours.\n- **Pearl:** tocolysis is for steroid cover and transfer, not indefinite delay; it does not prevent preterm birth.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Magnesium Sulfate Monitoring:** Patellar reflex must be present (lost first), Resp rate >= 12, Urine >= 30 mL/hr. Antidote = IV Calcium Gluconate.\n\n### 2.3 Antepartum haemorrhage (APH)\n\n- **Placenta praevia:** placenta over or near the internal os; painless bright red bleeding in the third trimester. Diagnosed by transvaginal ultrasound (not digital examination). Classification: major (covers os), minor (partially covers), marginal (edge at os). Management: caesarean section for significant bleeding or at 37 weeks; expectant management for minor bleeding.\n- **Placental abruption:** painful bleeding, uterine tenderness, tetany, hypotension out of proportion to visible blood loss, concealed haemorrhage, risk of DIC (fibrinogen falls). Management: resuscitation, delivery (usually caesarean if fetus alive and in distress). Predisposing factors: pre-eclampsia, smoking, cocaine, trauma, multiple pregnancy.\n- **Vasa praevia:** fetal vessels cross the membranes over the cervix. Fresh bleeding with fetal bradycardia after membrane rupture is an emergency (fetal exsanguination). Diagnosed with colour Doppler; elective caesarean at 34-35 weeks if confirmed.\n- **Pearl:** placenta praevia is painless, abruption is painful; a painful bleed with a hard uterus suggests abruption.\n\n#### Table: Placenta Previa vs Abruptio Placentae Comparison\n\n| Clinical Feature | Placenta Previa | Abruptio Placentae |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Pain | PAINLESS, causeless, recurrent | PAINFUL, sudden severe constant abdominal/back pain |\n| Bleeding Nature | Bright red, fresh arterial blood | Dark red / venous blood (revealed or concealed) |\n| Uterine Tone | Soft, relaxed, completely non-tender | Tense, woody, board-like, exquisitely tender |\n| Fetal Heart Rate | Usually normal and reassuring | Fetal distress, bradycardia, or absent heart tones common |\n| Digital Exam (PV) | STRICTLY FORBIDDEN! (Triggers fatal hemorrhage) | Permissible to evaluate cervical dilatation in labor |\n| Complications | Maternal hypovolemic shock, malpresentation | DIC (consumptive coagulopathy), Couvelaire uterus, renal failure |\n\n### 2.8 Antepartum and intrapartum fetal monitoring summary\n\n| Monitoring | Used for |\n|---|---|\n| CTG (cardiotocograph) | Fetal heart rate and contractions; detects hypoxia |\n| Doppler ultrasound | Placental and fetal circulation (IUGR, pre-eclampsia) |\n| Biophysical profile (BPP) | Five variables (breathing, movement, tone, liquor, NST), scored out of 10 |\n| Kick count | Simple home measure of fetal movement |\n| Fetal blood sampling | pH and lactate during labour |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **VEAL CHOP: Variable** = Cord; Early = Head; Accelerations = Okay; Late = Placental insufficiency.\n\n### 2.9 Gynaecology\n\n- **Pearl:** letrozole has replaced clomiphene in many guidelines because it gives higher live birth rates with less multiple pregnancy.\n\n**Endometriosis**\n- **Risk factors:** unopposed oestrogen (obesity, PCOS, tamoxifen, nulliparity), diabetes, hypertension, Lynch syndrome.\n- **Pearl:** HSG is performed in the first half of the cycle (before ovulation), after menstruation and before infection.\n\n**Ectopic pregnancy**\n- **Diagnosis:** beta-hCG and transvaginal ultrasound. Beta-hCG above the discriminatory zone (about 1500-2000 IU/L) with no intrauterine pregnancy suggests ectopic.\n- **Pearl:** ectopic pregnancy is the commonest cause of first-trimester maternal death.\n\n**Hydatidiform mole**\n- **Management:** suction evacuation, then serial hCG until normal; contraception for 6-12 months; methotrexate prophylaxis for high-risk moles.\n\n## 22. Obstetrics and Gynaecology (additions)\n\n- **Medical disorders and drugs in pregnancy:**\n  - *Indomethacin in late pregnancy:* premature closure of the ductus arteriosus (avoid after 30 weeks).\n  - *ACE-inhibitor discontinuation before conception:* teratogenic; switch to methyldopa or labetalol.\n  - *Mechanical-valve anticoagulation (warfarin to heparin switch) near term:* LMWH or UFH before delivery.\n  - *LMWH prophylaxis after high-risk caesarean delivery:* low-molecular-weight heparin prevents venous thromboembolism after surgery in high-risk women.\n  - *Supine hypotensive syndrome:* compression of IVC by gravid uterus; turn to left lateral.\n  - *Pearl:* supine hypotensive syndrome improves when the woman lies on her left side.\n- **Hypertensive disorders of pregnancy:**\n  - *Early-onset preeclampsia and uterine artery Doppler:* high resistance index and notch predict early-onset disease.\n  - *HELLP syndrome:* haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets; delivery.\n  - *Defective extravillous trophoblast invasion:* impaired spiral artery remodelling.\n  - *Magnesium sulphate regimens and toxicity:* Pritchard or Zuspan; monitor reflexes, respiratory rate (above 12), urine output; calcium gluconate antidote.\n  - *Pearl:* loss of patellar reflexes is the first sign of magnesium toxicity.\n- **Early pregnancy assessment and dating:**\n  - *Crown-rump length:* 7-14 weeks; dating scan accuracy +/- 5 days.\n  - *Pregnancy of unknown location:* beta-hCG repeated; ectopic pregnancy cannot be excluded until resolved.\n  - *Presumptive versus probable signs of pregnancy:* presumptive (amenorrhoea, nausea); probable (Hegar, Chadwick, Braxton Hicks); positive (foetal heart, foetal movements on USG).\n  - *Pearl:* crown-rump length is the best measure of gestational age in the first trimester.\n- **Gynaecologic endoscopy:**\n  - *Hysteroscopy for intrauterine adhesions (Asherman syndrome):* adhesiolysis; hormone therapy; intrauterine device.\n  - *Hysteroscopy fluid overload causing pulmonary oedema:* glycine, sorbitol, or saline overload; monitor fluid deficit.\n  - *Unrecognised thermal injury in laparoscopic hysterectomy:* bowel injury from monopolar energy.\n  - *Pearl:* in hysteroscopy, the fluid deficit must be measured to avoid pulmonary oedema (TURP syndrome).\n","OBGY_UNIT_02":"## 2. Obstetrics and Gynaecology (25-30 questions)\n\n### 2.4 Postpartum haemorrhage (PPH)\n\n- **Definition:** blood loss of 500 mL or more after vaginal birth (1000 mL or more after caesarean section, or any loss causing haemodynamic instability).\n- **Causes (the \"4 Ts\"):**\n  - *Tone:* uterine atony (commonest cause, about 70%).\n  - *Trauma:* genital tract lacerations, uterine rupture, inversion.\n  - *Tissue:* retained placenta or products of conception.\n  - *Thrombin:* coagulopathy (DIC, von Willebrand disease, HELLP).\n- **Management steps:** call for help, establish two large-bore IV lines, bloods (haemoglobin, platelets, fibrinogen, coagulation), crystalloid and blood; uterine massage; oxytocin 10 IU IM or slow IV infusion; misoprostol (sublingual or rectal 800 mcg); ergometrine (avoid in hypertension or cardiac disease, as it causes vasoconstriction); tranexamic acid 1 g IV within 3 hours; carboprost (PGF2 alpha, avoid in asthma); bimanual compression; surgical options: B-Lynch suture, uterine artery ligation, internal iliac artery ligation, hysterectomy.\n- **Pearl:** oxytocin is first-line and is given slowly IV (rapid bolus causes hypotension and tachycardia); ergometrine is contraindicated in hypertension and pre-eclampsia.\n\n#### Table: Postpartum Hemorrhage (PPH) Uterotonic Protocol\n\n| Drug | Mechanism / Dose | Route | Strict Contraindication |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Oxytocin | 10-40 IU in 1000 mL crystalloid | IV infusion | Never give rapid undiluted IV bolus (causes severe hypotension) |\n| Methylergonovine (Methergine) | 0.2 mg IM | IM only | STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED in Hypertension and Preeclampsia! |\n| Carboprost (15-methyl PGF2a) | 250 mcg deep IM | IM only | STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED in Active Bronchial Asthma! |\n| Misoprostol (PGE1) | 800 mcg sublingual / rectal | Oral / SL / PR | Prior Cesarean scar (when used for labor induction; safe in PPH) |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **PPH 4 Ts:** Tone (Atony 80%), Trauma (lacerations 15%), Tissue (retained placenta 5%), Thrombin (DIC 1%).\n\n### 2.5 Labour, delivery and obstetric emergencies\n\n**Partograph:** plots cervical dilatation against time, with an alert line (4 hours of slow progress) and an action line (2 hours to the right of the alert line). Crossing the action line means evaluation and a plan to deliver.\n\n**Stages of labour**\n- **First stage:** from onset of regular contractions to full dilatation (10 cm). Latent phase (0-6 cm; up to 20 hours in nulliparous women) and active phase (6-10 cm; above 1 cm/h).\n- **Second stage:** full dilatation to delivery (up to 3 hours nulliparous, 2 hours multiparous, with epidural extended by 1 hour).\n- **Third stage:** delivery of the placenta (active management with oxytocin reduces PPH).\n\n**Abnormal labour:** prolonged latent phase, protracted active phase (below 1 cm/h for 4 hours), arrest of active phase (no change for 4 hours with adequate contractions), arrest of second stage.\n\n**Malpresentations and malpositions**\n- **Occipito-posterior (OP) position:** prolonged labour; manual rotation or caesarean.\n- **Face presentation:** mento-anterior; the chin is the presenting part; vaginal delivery possible if mento-anterior and the head is well flexed.\n- **Breech:** frank, complete, footling. External cephalic version (ECV) from 36 weeks; caesarean for footling or failed version.\n- **Transverse lie:** the long axis of the foetus is across the uterus; if persistent at term, caesarean section is usually needed.\n\n**Cord prolapse:** umbilical cord below the presenting part after membrane rupture. Emergency: push the presenting part up, fill the bladder if needed, and deliver by caesarean section immediately.\n\n**Shoulder dystocia:** the anterior shoulder is caught behind the pubic symphysis. Management (HELPERR): call for help, episiotomy, legs (McRoberts), suprapubic pressure, internal rotation (Rubin or Woods screw), delivery of the posterior arm, roll over onto all fours. Risk factors: macrosomia, GDM, prolonged second stage.\n\n**Instrumental delivery:** vacuum or forceps requirements: cervix fully dilated, membranes ruptured, head engaged, bladder empty, adequate analgesia, and the position of the head known. Contraindicated in face presentation and when the head is not engaged.\n\n**Caesarean section indications:** cephalopelvic disproportion, fetal distress unresponsive to resuscitation, breech after failed version, placenta praevia, previous classical incision, transverse lie, cord prolapse.\n\n**Pearl:** a fetal scalp pH below 7.20 or a lactate above 4.8 mmol/L indicates fetal acidosis; a pH of 7.20-7.25 requires repeat assessment.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Shoulder Dystocia HELPERR:** Help, Episiotomy, Legs (McRoberts), Pressure (suprapubic), Enter (Rubin/Woods), Remove arm, Roll (Gaskin).\n\n### 2.6 Cardiotocography (CTG)\n\n- **Baseline heart rate:** normal 110-160 bpm; tachycardia above 160 and bradycardia below 110.\n- **Variability:** short-term and long-term; reduced variability is a warning sign (sleep state excluded).\n- **Decelerations:** early (head compression, benign, mirror the contraction); late (uteroplacental insufficiency, abnormal, after the contraction peak); variable (cord compression, umbilical cord around the neck, shape varies).\n- **Pearl:** a sinusoidal pattern (smooth undulating waveform) is a sign of severe fetal anaemia (e.g., Rh isoimmunisation) or fetal hypoxia.\n\n### 2.7 Puerperium and lactation\n\n- **Postnatal complications:** endometritis (fever, foul lochia; treat with broad-spectrum antibiotics), puerperal sepsis (a sepsis picture after delivery, the commonest preventable cause of maternal death in some settings), mastitis (staphylococcal infection; dicloxacillin or flucloxacillin, continue breastfeeding), thromboembolism (Virchow triad; LMWH prophylaxis for high-risk women).\n- **Breastfeeding:** prolactin maintains milk production; oxytocin causes milk ejection. Drugs compatible with breastfeeding: most antibiotics (except tetracycline), paracetamol, most antidepressants (sertraline), enalapril. Avoid: lithium (caution), ergotamine, iodine.\n- **Pearl:** postpartum depression is screened with the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale (EPDS).\n\n### 2.9 Gynaecology\n\n- **Symptoms:** dysmenorrhoea (cyclical), dyspareunia, dyschezia, chronic pelvic pain, infertility. The \"classic triad\" in many texts: dysmenorrhoea, dyspareunia, infertility.\n- **Definition:** benign smooth muscle tumours; commonest pelvic tumour. Oestrogen-dependent; grow in the reproductive years, shrink after menopause.\n- **Symptoms:** menorrhagia (commonest), pressure symptoms, infertility, pregnancy complications (malpresentation, abortion, PPH).\n- **Treatment:** early stage: radical hysterectomy with pelvic lymphadenectomy; locally advanced: chemoradiation (cisplatin with radiotherapy and brachytherapy).\n- **Combined oral contraceptive pill:** oestrogen and progestin; contraindicated in smokers over 35, breastfeeding before 6 weeks postpartum, migraine with aura, thromboembolic disease, and hypertension.\n- **Male factors:** semen analysis (WHO criteria: volume 1.5 mL or more, count 15 million/mL or more, progressive motility 32% or more, normal morphology 4% or more).\n- **Complications:** tubo-ovarian abscess, infertility, ectopic pregnancy, chronic pelvic pain (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome: perihepatitis).\n\n## 22. Obstetrics and Gynaecology (additions)\n\n- **Mullerian and outflow anomalies:**\n  - *Septate uterus:* HSG and hysteroscopic metroplasty (septum resection).\n  - *Uterus didelphys:* obstetric complications (preterm labour, malpresentation).\n  - *Uterine anomaly with combined hysteroscopy and laparoscopy:* combined endoscopy visualises both the uterine cavity and outer shape to confirm the anomaly.\n  - *Genital outflow obstruction and cryptomenorrhoea:* obstructed menses; cyclic pain; surgical drainage.\n  - *Pearl:* septate uterus is the commonest uterine anomaly and carries a risk of miscarriage.\n- **Urogynaecology and pelvic organ prolapse:**\n  - *Stress urinary incontinence:* mid-urethral sling (TVT, TOT) is first-line surgery.\n  - *Vesicovaginal fistula repair and aftercare:* catheter for 2-3 weeks; ureteric involvement.\n  - *Pelvic-organ prolapse (POP-Q grid):* six points, measure in cm; stage 0-IV.\n  - *Third-degree uterine prolapse with cystocele:* vaginal hysterectomy with anterior repair.\n  - *Cardinal and Mackenrodt ligament support of the uterus:* the main support; damage causes prolapse.\n  - *Pearl:* POP-Q grades prolapse by measurement of points relative to the hymen.\n- **Obstetric haemorrhage:**\n  - *Refractory atonic PPH:* stepwise devascularisation (uterine artery ligation, B-Lynch, hypogastric artery ligation, hysterectomy).\n  - *Intrauterine balloon tamponade:* Bakri balloon; for uterine atony.\n  - *Placenta previa and placenta accreta spectrum:* previa (placenta covers os); accreta (invades myometrium); caesarean hysterectomy.\n  - *Pearl:* in placenta accreta, planned delivery in a tertiary centre with hysterectomy available.\n- **Contraception:**\n  - *Multiple missed combined oral contraceptive pills:* emergency contraception and extra precautions; restart.\n  - *Progestasert or progestin-releasing IUS for heavy menstrual bleeding during lactation:* safe; Mirena (LNG-IUS) reduces bleeding.\n  - *Copper IUD contraindication after molar evacuation:* avoid until hCG normal (risk of infection and perforation).\n  - *Immediate postpartum IUCD insertion timing:* within 10 minutes of placental delivery (lower expulsion rate).\n  - *Pearl:* copper IUD is contraindicated in gestational trophoblastic disease until hCG normalises.\n- **Labour abnormalities:**\n  - *Occiput posterior position:* longer labour; backache; manual rotation or caesarean.\n  - *Labour dystocia, deep transverse arrest and protraction:* Friedman curve; deep transverse arrest needs operative delivery.\n  - *Pearl:* deep transverse arrest of the head needs operative intervention (forceps or caesarean).\n- **Obstetric emergencies:**\n  - *Amniotic fluid embolism:* sudden cardiorespiratory collapse, DIC, pulmonary oedema; supportive care.\n  - *Umbilical cord prolapse:* immediate management is elevation of the presenting part and caesarean section.\n  - *Uterine scar rupture during labour:* emergency caesarean; prior caesarean is the risk factor.\n  - *Shoulder dystocia manoeuvre sequence:* McRoberts, suprapubic pressure, posterior arm delivery, Woods screw, Rubin.\n  - *Pearl:* McRoberts manoeuvre is the first step in shoulder dystocia.\n- **Malpresentation and breech:**\n  - *Breech with a previous classical caesarean scar:* elective caesarean section, because of the risk of uterine rupture in labour.\n  - *External cephalic version for transverse lie:* at 37 weeks; tocolysis; Rh immunoglobulin.\n  - *Lovset manoeuvre:* rotation of the shoulders for delivery of the after-coming head in breech.\n  - *Pearl:* ECV is most successful at 37 weeks when the foetus is free in the uterus.\n- **Normal labour and third stage:**\n  - *Cord avulsion during controlled cord traction:* do not pull; manual removal of placenta; antibiotic cover.\n  - *Controlled cord traction in active management of the third stage:* oxytocin, then controlled traction.\n  - *Cervical dilatation and effacement in true labour:* progressive; cervix dilates from 0 to 10 cm.\n  - *Pearl:* the active management of third stage uses oxytocin and controlled cord traction to reduce PPH.\n- **Puerperium and postpartum disorders:**\n  - *Sheehan postpartum pituitary necrosis:* severe PPH and hypotension; failure of lactation; hypopituitarism.\n  - *Postpartum blues:* transient low mood in first 2 weeks; supportive care; PPD needs longer treatment.\n  - *Normal postpartum uterine involution:* uterus returns to pre-pregnancy size by 6 weeks.\n  - *Pearl:* Sheehan syndrome presents with failure of lactation and amenorrhoea after severe haemorrhage.\n- **Repeated / asked-once OBG topics:**\n  - *Fetal anomalies and surveillance:* anomaly scan at 18-20 weeks; NT scan at 11-14 weeks.\n  - *Operative obstetrics:* forceps (outlet, low, mid); vacuum; caesarean techniques.\n  - *Cervical screening and prevention:* HPV testing, cytology; VIA; screen at age 30 onward.\n  - *Endometriosis:* ovarian endometrioma (chocolate cyst); laparoscopy; hormonal suppression.\n  - *Medical termination of pregnancy:* mifepristone plus misoprostol; up to 9 weeks at home.\n  - *Obstetric pelvis:* gynaecoid, android, anthropoid, platypelloid types.\n  - *Ectopic pregnancy:* methotrexate for stable; laparoscopic salpingectomy.\n  - *Maternal nutrition and anaemia:* IFA; anaemia classification.\n  - *Obstetric assessment and notation:* gestational age, fundal height, lie, presentation, engagement.\n  - *Pregnancy loss and cervical insufficiency:* cerclage at 12-14 weeks for history.\n  - *Multiple pregnancy:* twin-twin transfusion; chorionicity; monochorionic risk.\n  - *Amniotic fluid disorders:* oligohydramnios (PPROM; renal agenesis); polyhydramnios (diabetes, duodenal atresia).\n  - *Gestational trophoblastic disease:* complete mole (46,XX paternal); partial mole (triploidy); choriocarcinoma; hCG follow-up.\n  - *Pearl:* a complete mole has a 46,XX karyotype of paternal origin only.\n","OBGY_UNIT_03":"## 2. Obstetrics and Gynaecology (25-30 questions)\n\n### 2.9 Gynaecology\n\n**Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)**\n- **Rotterdam criteria:** two of three: oligo- or anovulation; clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism (hirsutism, acne, raised free testosterone); polycystic ovaries on ultrasound (12 or more follicles of 2-9 mm, or ovarian volume above 10 mL). Exclude other causes (thyroid disease, late-onset CAH, hyperprolactinaemia, Cushing).\n- **Associated features:** insulin resistance (acanthosis nigricans), obesity, dyslipidaemia, endometrial hyperplasia risk (unopposed oestrogen), infertility.\n- **Management:** lifestyle and weight loss; combined oral contraceptive pills for menstrual regularity and hirsutism; metformin for insulin resistance; clomiphene or letrozole for ovulation induction (letrozole is now first-line); laparoscopic ovarian drilling in clomiphene-resistant cases; spironolactone or eflornithine for hirsutism.\n- **Definition:** endometrial-like glands and stroma outside the uterus (ovaries, pouch of Douglas, uterosacral ligaments, bowel, bladder).\n- **Diagnosis:** laparoscopy with biopsy (gold standard); ultrasound may show endometrioma (\"chocolate cyst\" with ground-glass echoes).\n- **Staging:** rASRM (I-IV). Treatment: NSAIDs, hormonal suppression (combined pill, progestins, GnRH analogues with add-back therapy), surgery for endometrioma or infertility.\n- **Pearl:** endometriosis is a cause of secondary infertility; a \"powder burn\" appearance on laparoscopy is characteristic.\n\n**Uterine fibroids (leiomyomas)**\n- **Classification:** submucosal (menorrhagia, infertility), intramural (menorrhagia, pressure), subserosal (pressure, often asymptomatic). FIGO classification 0-8.\n- **Treatment:** medical (tranexamic acid for bleeding, GnRH analogues for shrinkage before surgery, ulipristal in some countries); surgical (myomectomy for fertility, hysterectomy for completed families, uterine artery embolisation as an alternative).\n- **Pearl:** a fibroid that is degenerating (red degeneration) causes pain and fever in pregnancy; it is managed conservatively.\n\n**Adenomyosis**\n- **Treatment:** total hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy; adjuvant therapy by stage.\n\n**Contraception**\n- **Levonorgestrel IUS:** hormonal; reduces menstrual blood loss; effective for 5 years.\n- **Progestin-only pill and injectables:** safe in breastfeeding; medroxyprogesterone acetate injection (3-monthly).\n- **Emergency contraception:** levonorgestrel 1.5 mg within 72 hours (efficacy declines after 72 hours but may still work up to 120 hours); ulipristal acetate (up to 120 hours); copper IUCD within 5 days (most effective).\n- **Pearl:** the combined pill is contraindicated in breastfeeding before 6 weeks because of thrombosis risk and milk supply effects.\n\n**Menstrual disorders**\n- **Amenorrhoea:** primary (no menarche by 15 years or 13 years with no secondary sex characteristics) vs secondary (absence of periods for 3 months in a woman who has had them). Causes: pregnancy, hypothalamic (stress, weight loss, exercise), pituitary (prolactinoma), ovarian (PCOS, premature ovarian failure), outflow tract (Asherman syndrome, imperforate hymen).\n- **Dysmenorrhoea:** primary (prostaglandins; NSAIDs) vs secondary (endometriosis, adenomyosis, pelvic inflammatory disease).\n\n**Infertility**\n- **Definition:** failure to conceive after one year of regular unprotected intercourse (six months if age over 35).\n- **Treatment:** ovulation induction; intrauterine insemination (IUI); in vitro fertilisation (IVF) and ICSI; surgery for tubal or uterine causes.\n- **Management:** methotrexate (single or multiple dose, if stable and hCG below 5000) or laparoscopic salpingectomy/salpingotomy.\n- **Complete mole:** 46XX from a single sperm duplicating (no fetal tissue, androgenetic); snowstorm appearance on ultrasound; hCG very high; theca lutein cysts; risk of choriocarcinoma (about 2-3%).\n- **Partial mole:** triploid (69XXY or 69XXX), fetal parts present; less risk of choriocarcinoma.\n- **Organisms:** Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, anaerobes, Mycoplasma genitalium.\n- **Treatment:** ceftriaxone plus doxycycline plus metronidazole (outpatient or inpatient depending on severity).\n\n#### Table: Primary Amenorrhea with Normal Breasts Differential\n\n| Feature | Complete Androgen Insensitivity (CAIS) | Mullerian Agenesis (MRKH Syndrome) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Karyotype | 46,XY (Genetically Male) | 46,XX (Genetically Female) |\n| Pubic & Axillary Hair | ABSENT or extremely sparse (insensitivity to androgens) | NORMAL female distribution and density |\n| Uterus & Fallopian Tubes | ABSENT (AMH secreted by testes regresses Mullerian ducts) | ABSENT (congenital Mullerian duct failure) |\n| Gonads | Testes (cryptorchid, in inguinal canal or labia) | Ovaries (normal, functional, ovulatory) |\n| Serum Testosterone | ELEVATED in normal male range | NORMAL in standard female range |\n| Breast Development | Normal female (peripheral aromatization of testosterone) | Normal female (functional ovarian estrogen) |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Rotterdam PCOS Criteria (2 of 3):** Anovulation + Hyperandrogenism + PCO Ultrasound morphology (>= 20 follicles).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Emergency Contraception:** Copper IUD is #1 most effective (up to 5 days, < 0.1% failure!).\n\n### 2.10 Menopause and hormone replacement\n\n- **Menopause:** cessation of menses for 12 months; mean age 51 years.\n- **Symptoms:** vasomotor (hot flushes), urogenital atrophy (dyspareunia, urinary symptoms), osteoporosis, cardiovascular risk.\n- **HRT:** oestrogen plus progestin in women with a uterus (to prevent endometrial hyperplasia); oestrogen alone after hysterectomy. Contraindications: breast cancer, thromboembolism, active liver disease, undiagnosed vaginal bleeding.\n- **Pearl:** HRT is used for symptom relief for the shortest possible duration; it is not a long-term prevention for osteoporosis.\n\n## 22. Obstetrics and Gynaecology (additions)\n\n- **Infections in pregnancy:**\n  - *Varicella seronegativity and post-exposure management:* VZIG within 10 days of exposure.\n  - *Primary genital herpes near term:* acyclovir; caesarean section if active lesions at delivery.\n  - *Genital herpes cytology:* Tzanck smear shows multinucleated giant cells with nuclear moulding.\n  - *Remote toxoplasmosis with isolated IgG positivity:* past infection; no need for treatment.\n  - *Pearl:* in primary genital herpes at term, caesarean section prevents neonatal herpes if lesions are present.\n- **Infertility and assisted reproduction:**\n  - *HSG, tubal factor infertility and ART:* HSG shows tubal patency; IVF is the usual route for tubal factor.\n  - *Definition and timing of infertility:* failure to conceive after 1 year of regular unprotected intercourse; investigation after 6 months if over 35.\n  - *Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome (OHSS):* abdominal distension, ascites, haemoconcentration; cabergoline, albumin, conservative management.\n  - *Pearl:* OHSS is more common with hCG trigger; GnRH agonist trigger reduces risk.\n- **Disorders of sex development:**\n  - *Turner syndrome with streak gonads:* 45,XO; primary amenorrhoea; hormone replacement.\n  - *Placental hCG stimulation of foetal Leydig-cell testosterone:* explains masculinisation in some foetal disorders.\n  - *Primary amenorrhoea with absent uterus and inguinal gonads:* complete androgen insensitivity syndrome (CAIS; 46,XY) versus hypergonadotropic hypogonadism (Turner) - a discordant recall key; CAIS has testes and normal female external genitalia.\n  - *Pearl:* CAIS presents with primary amenorrhoea, normal female phenotype, absent uterus, and testes; surgery after puberty to remove gonads.\n- **Reproductive endocrinology:**\n  - *Primary ovarian insufficiency:* before 40; high FSH; hormone replacement; fertility options.\n  - *Hyperprolactinaemic amenorrhoea:* prolactinoma; cabergoline; check TSH and renal function.\n  - *Progestin-withdrawal test in secondary amenorrhoea:* bleeding after progesterone means oestrogen present and outflow intact.\n  - *Pearl:* if progestin withdrawal produces bleeding, the endometrium is primed and outflow is patent.\n","OBGY_UNIT_04":"## 2. Obstetrics and Gynaecology (25-30 questions)\n\n### 2.9 Gynaecology\n\n- Endometrial glands and stroma within the myometrium; diffuse uterine enlargement, dysmenorrhoea, menorrhagia. Diagnosed by MRI or ultrasound. Treatment: levonorgestrel IUS, hysterectomy.\n\n**Ovarian tumours**\n- **Epithelial (about 80%):** serous (commonest; psammoma bodies; bilateral), mucinous (large, unilateral, pseudomyxoma peritonei), endometrioid, clear cell, Brenner.\n- **Germ cell (most common in young women):** dysgerminoma (LDH raised, radiosensitive), yolk sac tumour (AFP raised; Schiller-Duval bodies), teratoma (mature cystic teratoma is the commonest benign ovarian tumour; contains hair and sebaceous material), choriocarcinoma (hCG raised), immature teratoma.\n- **Sex cord-stromal:** granulosa cell tumour (inhibin B, oestrogen; Call-Exner bodies; endometrial hyperplasia), Sertoli-Leydig (virilisation), fibroma (Meigs syndrome: ascites and right pleural effusion).\n- **Tumour markers:** CA-125 (epithelial), AFP (yolk sac), hCG (choriocarcinoma), LDH (dysgerminoma), inhibin (granulosa).\n- **Pearl:** Meigs syndrome is ovarian fibroma with ascites and hydrothorax that resolves after tumour removal.\n\n**Cervical cancer**\n- **Aetiology:** HPV 16 and 18 (about 70% of cases). Persistent infection, not single infection, drives progression.\n- **Screening:** VIA (visual inspection with acetic acid), Pap smear, HPV DNA test (primary screening). Colposcopy and directed biopsy for abnormal results.\n- **Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN):** CIN 1-3 based on epithelial thickness; koilocytes are characteristic. Treatment: LEEP or cone biopsy for CIN 2-3.\n- **FIGO staging:** stage I confined to cervix; stage II extends beyond cervix but not to the pelvic wall or lower third of vagina; stage III to pelvic wall or lower vagina; stage IV spread beyond pelvis or bladder/rectum mucosa.\n- **Pearl:** koilocytes (perinuclear halo with wrinkled nucleus) are the cytological clue to HPV infection.\n\n**Endometrial cancer**\n- **Presentation:** postmenopausal bleeding (always investigate); endometrial thickness above 4 mm on transvaginal ultrasound.\n- **Diagnosis:** endometrial biopsy (pipelle); histology: endometrioid (most common).\n- **Copper IUCD:** non-hormonal; mechanism involves sperm inactivation and endometrial changes; effective for 10 years; no effect on breast milk. Contraindicated in active pelvic infection and Wilson disease.\n- **Menorrhagia:** heavy or prolonged bleeding (over 80 mL per cycle); investigate for fibroids, adenomyosis, polyps, bleeding disorders, hypothyroidism, endometrial cancer.\n- **Female factors:** ovulation (progesterone on day 21), tubal patency (HSG), ovarian reserve (AMH, antral follicle count).\n- **Presentation:** amenorrhoea, pain, bleeding; ruptured ectopic causes shock and free fluid in the pouch of Douglas (Morison pouch).\n- **Pearl:** an ovarian theca lutein cyst can be large in a complete mole; it resolves after evacuation.\n\n**Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)**\n- **Presentation:** lower abdominal pain, cervical motion tenderness, adnexal tenderness, discharge, fever.\n\n#### Table: Vaginal Discharge Differential Diagnosis\n\n| Parameter | Bacterial Vaginosis (BV) | Trichomoniasis | Vulvovaginal Candidiasis |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Causative Agent | Gardnerella vaginalis + Anaerobic dysbiosis | Trichomonas vaginalis (flagellated protozoan) | Candida albicans (dimorphic fungus) |\n| Discharge Appearance | Thin, homogenous, greyish-white adherent | Profuse, frothy, bubbly, green-yellow purulent | Thick, white, curdy, 'cottage-cheese' like |\n| Vaginal pH | ELEVATED (> 4.5, typically 5.0-6.0) | ELEVATED (> 4.5-5.5) | NORMAL / ACIDIC (< 4.5, typically 4.0-4.4) |\n| Whiff / Amine Test | POSITIVE (Putrid fishy odor with 10% KOH) | Often Positive / Variable | NEGATIVE (Odorless) |\n| Wet Mount Microscopy | CLUE CELLS (stippled squamous cells > 20%) | Motile flagellated trophozoites with jerky motion | Budding yeast blastospores and pseudohyphae |\n| Treatment of Choice | Oral Metronidazole (500 mg bid x 7 days) | Oral Metronidazole (TREAT SEXUAL PARTNER!) | Oral Fluconazole (150 mg single dose) or topical azoles |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Ovarian Tumors: High-Grade Serous** = Psammoma bodies + BRCA. Dysgerminoma = LDH. Yolk Sac = Schiller-Duval bodies + AFP. Granulosa Cell = Call-Exner bodies + Inhibin-B.\n\n### 2.11 Gynaecological oncology summary\n\n| Tumour | Key marker | Key feature |\n|---|---|---|\n| Serous ovarian | CA-125 | Psammoma bodies; bilateral |\n| Mucinous ovarian | CA-125 (variable) | Pseudomyxoma peritonei |\n| Dysgerminoma | LDH | Radiosensitive; young women |\n| Yolk sac tumour | AFP | Schiller-Duval bodies |\n| Granulosa cell | Inhibin B | Call-Exner bodies |\n| Choriocarcinoma | hCG | Haemorrhagic; lung metastases |\n| Cervical SCC | — | HPV 16/18; koilocytes |\n| Endometrial carcinoma | — | Postmenopausal bleeding; unopposed oestrogen |\n\n## 22. Obstetrics and Gynaecology (additions)\n\n- **Gynaecologic oncology:**\n  - *Locally advanced cervical cancer:* chemoradiation with cisplatin.\n  - *Cervical cancer with hydronephrosis (FIGO IIIB):* pelvic side-wall extension; urinary obstruction.\n  - *Postmenopausal bleeding:* pelvic examination and endometrial biopsy.\n  - *Thickened endometrium with abnormal bleeding:* endometrial biopsy (above 4-5 mm in postmenopausal).\n  - *Endometrial carcinoma gross specimen:* polypoid or infiltrating; myometrial invasion depth.\n  - *Pearl:* postmenopausal bleeding always needs investigation for endometrial cancer.\n- **Gynaecologic infections and discharge:**\n  - *Physiological leucorrhoea:* clear to white discharge; hormone-dependent; normal.\n  - *Female genital tuberculosis:* endometrial TB causes Asherman syndrome; infertility; HSG or endometrial biopsy shows caseating granulomas.\n  - *Bartholin gland abscess:* incise and drain; marsupialisation for recurrence.\n  - *Pearl:* female genital TB is a cause of tubal factor infertility and Asherman syndrome.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **HPV Oncoproteins: E6 degrades p53 (Six** = Fifty-three); E7 inactivates Retinoblastoma (pRb).\n","OPHTH_UNIT_01":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.1 Ophthalmology\n\n- **Vitamin A deficiency:** night blindness; Bitot spots; keratomalacia.\n- **Ocular pharmacology:** pilocarpine (miosis, reduces IOP), atropine (mydriasis, cycloplegia), tropicamide (short acting).\n- **Trachoma:** Chlamydia trachomatis serovars A-C; follicles; Herbert pits; azithromycin mass treatment (SAFE strategy).\n\n## 30. Ophthalmology (additions)\n\n- **Corneal disease:**\n  - *Keratoconus and corneal cross-linking:* riboflavin with UV-A; stops progression.\n  - *Kayser-Fleischer ring:* copper deposition in Descemet membrane; Wilson disease.\n  - *Corneal-ulcer diagnosis and fluorescein staining:* bacterial, fungal, viral (dendritic); fluorescein stains epithelial defect green.\n  - *Fuchs endothelial dystrophy:* corneal oedema from endothelial cell loss; guttae; corneal transplant (DSAEK, DMEK).\n  - *Pearl:* Kayser-Fleischer ring is seen at the limbus; fluorescein staining is green.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Microbial Corneal Ulcers Differential\n\n| Type | Etiological Agent | Precipitating Factor | Pathognomonic Slit-Lamp Sign | First-Line Therapy |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Fungal Keratitis | Fusarium, Aspergillus | Trauma with vegetative matter (sugarcane) | Dry ulcer, feathery margins, satellite lesions | Topical Natamycin 5% suspension |\n| Bacterial Keratitis | Pseudomonas aeruginosa | Extended-wear soft contact lenses | Rapid liquefactive stromal melt, greenish pus | Fortified Ciprofloxacin / Tobramycin |\n| Acanthamoeba | Acanthamoeba castellanii | Contact lens cleaned with tap water | Severe pain out of proportion, Ring infiltrate | Polyhexamethylene biguanide (PHMB) |\n| Viral (HSV-1) | Herpes Simplex Virus 1 | Reactivation from trigeminal ganglion | True branching Dendritic ulcer with end-bulbs | Topical Ganciclovir (Steroids CONTRAINDICATED!) |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Dendritic Ulcer:** Fluorescein stains ulcer bed; Rose Bengal stains devitalized margins. STEROIDS CONTRAINDICATED!\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Refractive Lenses: Myopia** = Minus (concave) lens. Hyperopia = Plus (convex) lens. Astigmatism = Cylindrical lens.\n","OPHTH_UNIT_02":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.1 Ophthalmology\n\n- **Glaucoma:** raised IOP (above 21 mmHg); optic disc cupping; field loss. Primary open-angle (POAG, commonest; trabecular meshwork); angle-closure (acute: painful red eye, mid-dilated fixed pupil, halos, vomiting; iridotomy or laser); secondary (uveitis, steroids, trauma). Treatment: prostaglandin analogues (latanoprost, first line), beta-blockers (timolol), carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (acetazolamide, dorzolamide), pilocarpine (miotic).\n- **Cataract:** opacity of lens; commonest cause of blindness worldwide; senile (most common), congenital, diabetic, steroid-induced (posterior subcapsular). Treatment: phacoemulsification with IOL.\n- **Pearl:** acute angle-closure glaucoma is an ophthalmic emergency; corneal oedema, fixed mid-dilated pupil, red eye.\n- **Pearl:** the most common cause of blindness worldwide is cataract; the commonest cause of irreversible blindness in developed countries is glaucoma or diabetic retinopathy.\n\n#### Table: Primary Open-Angle vs Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma\n\n| Parameter | Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma (POAG) | Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma (AACG) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Onset & Symptoms | Insidious, painless, asymptomatic until advanced | Hyperacute agonizing eye pain, headache, nausea, halos |\n| Anterior Chamber Angle | Wide open on gonioscopy (trabecular sclerosis) | Completely occluded / closed by peripheral iris |\n| Pupil Appearance | Normal size and reaction | Fixed, vertically oval, mid-dilated, non-reactive |\n| Cornea Appearance | Clear | Hazy, steamy edematous (epithelial microcysts) |\n| Definitive Treatment | Medical: Topical Latanoprost / Trabeculectomy | Emergency medical lowering -> Laser Peripheral Iridotomy (LPI) |\n\n### 9.9 Short-subject quick pearls\n\n- Acute angle-closure glaucoma: painful red eye, mid-dilated fixed pupil.\n\n## 30. Ophthalmology (additions)\n\n- **Glaucoma:**\n  - *Primary congenital glaucoma:* triad of epiphora, photophobia and blepharospasm; enlarged cornea (buphthalmos); surgery (goniotomy or trabeculotomy).\n  - *Pseudoexfoliation syndrome:* exfoliation material on lens and iris; open-angle glaucoma; dilated pupil irregularity; pupil dilatation problems in surgery.\n  - *Glaucomatous visual-field defects:* arcuate scotoma; nasal step; paracentral defects; altitudinal.\n  - *Pearl:* pseudoexfoliation causes poor pupil dilatation and risk of phacodonesis during cataract surgery.\n- **Cataract:**\n  - *Immature senile cataract:* glare, reduced vision; phacoemulsification with IOL.\n  - *Nuclear cataract, index myopia and second sight:* myopic shift causing improved near vision.\n  - *Posterior capsular opacification (after-cataract) and Nd:YAG capsulotomy:* the posterior capsule clouds after cataract surgery; a neodymium-YAG laser opens it.\n  - *Pearl:* second sight is an improvement in near vision due to nuclear sclerosis.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Glaucoma ISNT Rule:** Normal neuroretinal rim thickness: Inferior > Superior > Nasal > Temporal.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Buphthalmos Triad:** Epiphora (watering) + Photophobia + Blepharospasm (Haab's striae on Descemet's).\n","OPHTH_UNIT_03":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.1 Ophthalmology\n\n- **Retinal detachment:** flashes, floaters, curtain; rhegmatogenous (tears; myopia) vs tractional vs exudative.\n- **Diabetic retinopathy:** microaneurysms (earliest), hard exudates, cotton-wool spots (ischaemic), neovascularisation (PRP laser). Vitreous haemorrhage.\n- **Hypertensive retinopathy:** arteriolar narrowing, AV nipping, silver wiring, flame haemorrhages.\n- **Retinoblastoma:** leukocoria (white pupil); RB1 gene; Flexner-Wintersteiner rosettes; enucleation or chemotherapy.\n\n#### Table: Vascular Retinal Emergencies Comparison\n\n| Entity | Underlying Pathology | Visual Presentation | Fundus Examination Hallmark |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| CRAO | Thromboembolism occluding central retinal artery | Sudden, profound, painless vision loss (counting fingers) | Milky-white ischemic retina with CHERRY-RED SPOT at fovea |\n| CRVO | Thrombosis of central retinal vein at lamina cribrosa | Sudden painless moderate visual loss | 'BLOOD AND THUNDER': 4-quadrant flame hemorrhages, engorged veins |\n| Retinal Detachment | Horseshoe tear separating neurosensory retina | Flashes of light, floaters, followed by curtain falling | Elevated, corrugated greyish retina with visible retinal break |\n\n## 11. Integrated Patterns (High-Yield Associations)\n\n- **Neurofibromatosis 1:** café-au-lait spots; Lisch nodules; optic glioma; neurofibromas; phaeochromocytoma.\n\n## 30. Ophthalmology (additions)\n\n- **Neuro-ophthalmology:**\n  - *Optic-pathway lesion localisation:* optic nerve lesions (central scotoma), chiasm (bitemporal hemianopia), tract (homonymous hemianopia).\n  - *Horner syndrome and Muller's muscle ptosis:* ptosis (mild), miosis, anhidrosis; sympathetic lesion.\n  - *Pituitary gigantism or acromegaly with bitemporal hemianopia:* IGF-1 raised; oral glucose GH-suppression test (fails to suppress).\n  - *Pearl:* in acromegaly, the GH level fails to suppress after an oral glucose load.\n- **Ocular tumours and congenital lesions:**\n  - *Retinoblastoma and Flexner-Wintersteiner rosettes:* RB1 gene; leukocoria (white pupil); histology shows rosettes.\n  - *Limbal dermoid and ocular-surface choristomas:* benign congenital tumours of the cornea or conjunctiva; excised when troublesome.\n  - *Pearl:* retinoblastoma histology shows Flexner-Wintersteiner rosettes.\n- **Repeated / asked-once ophthalmology:**\n  - *Binocular vision and strabismus:* esotropia (accommodative, infantile); exotropia; amblyopia (treat before age 7-8); cover test.\n  - *Orbital disease:* thyroid eye disease (Graves); orbital cellulitis (emergency, CT).\n  - *Refractive errors:* myopia (concave lenses), hypermetropia (convex), astigmatism (cylindrical), presbyopia.\n  - *Diabetic retinopathy:* see 9.1 (microaneurysms, cotton-wool spots, PRP).\n  - *Vitamin A deficiency:* see 9.1 (night blindness, Bitot spots).\n  - *Ocular inflammation:* uveitis (anterior: pain, photophobia, hypopyon; posterior: floaters); scleritis (pain).\n  - *Retinal disease:* central retinal artery occlusion (cherry-red spot, pale retina; emergency); central retinal vein occlusion (haemorrhages).\n  - *Conjunctival disease:* conjunctivitis (viral, bacterial, allergic, chlamydial); trachoma.\n  - *Eyelid disorders:* chalazion; hordeolum; entropion; ectropion; blepharitis.\n  - *Ocular trauma:* open globe injury (no pressure on globe); hyphaema; traumatic optic neuropathy.\n  - *Pearl:* central retinal artery occlusion presents with a cherry-red spot on macula and pale retina; it is an emergency.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Visual Fields: Chiasm (Pituitary)** = Bitemporal Hemianopia. Meyer's loop (Temporal) = Upper Quadrantanopia ('Pie in the sky').\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Marcus Gunn Pupil (RAPD):** Both pupils paradoxically DILATE when light swings to the affected eye!\n","ORTHO_UNIT_01":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.6 Orthopaedics\n\n- **Fractures:** transverse, oblique, spiral, comminuted, impacted; healing: haematoma, callus, remodelling. Complications: non-union, malunion, avascular necrosis, compartment syndrome (pain on passive stretch, paraesthesia, pallor, pulselessness late).\n- **Scaphoid fracture:** snuffbox tenderness; avascular necrosis (proximal pole); cast immobilisation; MRI.\n- **Carpal tunnel syndrome:** median nerve; thenar wasting; nocturnal pain; Phalen test; steroid injection; release.\n- **Osteoporosis:** T score below -2.5; bisphosphonates.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Forearm Eponyms: 'MUGR'** = Monteggia Ulnar fracture; Galeazzi Radial fracture.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Acute Compartment Syndrome:** Earliest sign is PAIN OUT OF PROPORTION ON PASSIVE MUSCLE STRETCH. Emergency DOC = Four-Compartment Fasciotomy!\n\n## 24. Orthopaedics (additions)\n\n- **Peripheral nerve disorders:**\n  - *Radial nerve palsy:* wrist drop; splint identification; spiral groove fracture of humerus.\n  - *Carpal tunnel syndrome, Durkan compression test:* median nerve; Durkan test (direct compression of carpal tunnel for 30 s).\n  - *Anterior interosseous neuropathy, abnormal OK sign:* median nerve branch; cannot make OK sign (flexor pollicis longus, flexor digitorum profundus).\n  - *Meralgia paraesthetica:* lateral femoral cutaneous nerve entrapment; thigh numbness.\n  - *Pearl:* in anterior interosseous nerve palsy, the OK sign cannot be made and the thumb and index finger do not flex at distal joints.\n- **Fracture complications:**\n  - *Fat embolism syndrome:* long bone fracture; hypoxia, petechial rash, confusion, tachycardia; supportive care.\n  - *Acute compartment syndrome:* pain on passive stretch (earliest sign), paraesthesia, pallor, pulselessness (late); fasciotomy within hours.\n  - *Pearl:* pain on passive stretch is the earliest and most reliable sign of compartment syndrome.\n- **Fracture principles and fixation:**\n  - *Spanning external fixation for periarticular fracture:* bridging joints; temporary (damage control).\n  - *Primary bone healing after rigid plating:* direct (Haversian) remodelling without callus; requires absolute stability.\n  - *Dynamic hip screw identification:* for intertrochanteric fracture; sliding hip screw.\n  - *Pearl:* primary bone healing needs absolute stability and gap-free contact.\n- **Paediatric fractures and physeal injuries:**\n  - *Lateral condyle humerus fracture: nonunion and cubitus valgus:* common in children; carrying angle changes; ORIF.\n  - *Physeal injury and longitudinal growth disturbance:* Salter-Harris classification; type V worst.\n  - *Gartland classification of supracondylar humerus fractures:* type I (undisplaced), II (displaced with intact posterior cortex), III (completely displaced); type III needs urgent reduction and pinning; watch for neurovascular injury (anterior interosseous nerve, brachial artery).\n  - *Pearl:* Salter-Harris type V (crush) injury carries the worst prognosis for growth arrest.\n- **Repeated / asked-once orthopaedics:**\n  - *Foot and ankle injuries:* Lisfranc injuries; Maisonneuve fracture; calcaneum fractures.\n  - *Hip disorders:* Perthes (see 7.7 page), SCFE (slipped capital femoral epiphysis; obese adolescent; limp).\n  - *Knee examination and disorders:* McMurray test (meniscal tear); Lachman test (ACL); collateral ligament tests.\n  - *Upper-limb trauma:* Monteggia (ulna fracture with radial head dislocation); Galeazzi (radius fracture with DRUJ dislocation).\n  - *Crystal arthropathy:* gout (negatively birefringent) vs pseudogout (positively birefringent, CPPD).\n  - *Shoulder anatomy:* rotator cuff; subacromial; axillary nerve injury with humeral neck fracture (regimental badge sensory loss).\n  - *Amputation:* levels; prosthetic planning.\n  - *Lower-limb trauma:* tibial plateau fractures; compartment syndrome.\n  - *Spinal trauma:* unstable (flexion-distraction, burst); spinal cord injury (Frankel classification); spinal immobilisation.\n  - *Musculoskeletal infection:* septic arthritis (joint aspiration, urgent washout); osteomyelitis (sequestrum).\n  - *Pearl:* Monteggia lesion: proximal ulna fracture with radial head dislocation; Galeazzi: distal radius with DRUJ dislocation.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Forearm & Wrist Fracture-Dislocations High-Yield Comparison\n\n| Entity | Fracture Site | Dislocation Site | Nerve at Risk & Deformity |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Monteggia Fracture | Proximal third of ULNA | RADIAL HEAD at proximal radioulnar joint | Posterior Interosseous Nerve (PIN; motor finger drop) |\n| Galeazzi Fracture | Distal third of RADIUS | DISTAL RADIOULNAR JOINT (DRUJ / ulnar head) | Anterior Interosseous Nerve (AIN); Unstable |\n| Colles' Fracture | Distal radius (extra-articular) | Dorsal displacement & dorsal tilt (FOOSH on extended wrist) | Median nerve compression; 'Dinner-Fork Deformity' |\n| Smith's Fracture | Distal radius (extra-articular) | VOLAR displacement & volar tilt (fall on flexed wrist) | Median nerve; 'Garden-Spade Deformity' |\n| Barton's Fracture | Distal radius INTRA-ARTICULAR rim | Radiocarpal joint subluxation with articular fracture fragment | Requires open reduction and volar buttress plating |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Hip Dislocation Posture: Posterior** = Flexion + ADduction + Internal Rotation. Anterior = ABduction + External Rotation.\n","ORTHO_UNIT_02":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.6 Orthopaedics\n\n- **Osteomyelitis:** Staphylococcus aureus; sequestrum; involucrum; acute (fever, pain) vs chronic (sinus).\n- **Bone tumours:** osteosarcoma (metaphysis of long bones; sunburst pattern; Codman triangle; children); Ewing sarcoma (diaphysis; onion peel; young; t(11;22)); giant cell tumour (epiphysis; soap bubble; adults 20-40); chondrosarcoma (older; central pelvis).\n- **Pearl:** osteosarcoma has a sunburst pattern and Codman triangle; Ewing has an onion peel appearance.\n\n#### Table: Primary Malignant Bone Tumors Diagnostic Profile\n\n| Neoplasm | Peak Age & Location | Key Radiographic Signs | Histopathology & Genetics |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Osteosarcoma | 10 to 20 years; METAPHYSIS around knee (distal femur > proximal tibia) | 'SUNBURST' periosteal reaction + CODMAN'S TRIANGLE | Malignant osteoid production; RB1, TP53; Lungs #1 metastasis |\n| Ewing Sarcoma | 10 to 15 years; DIAPHYSIS of long bones and pelvis | Permeative 'Moth-Eaten' + 'ONION-SKIN' laminated periosteum | Small round blue cells; CD99 (MIC2)+; t(11;22) EWS-FLI1 translocation |\n| Giant Cell Tumor (GCT) | 20 to 40 years (skeletally mature); Strictly EPIPHYSEAL around knee | Eccentric expansile 'SOAP-BUBBLE' without sclerotic border | Mononuclear stromal cells (RANKL)+; Multinucleated giant cells; Denosumab |\n| Chondrosarcoma | 40 to 70 years; Axial skeleton (pelvis, proximal femur) | 'POPCORN' / 'RINGS AND ARCS' stippled calcifications | Malignant chondroid matrix; CHEMO- & RADIO-RESISTANT -> Wide excision |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Tumor Sites Mnemonic: Epiphysis** = Giant Cell Tumor (adults) & Chondroblastoma (kids). Metaphysis = Osteosarcoma & Osteochondroma. Diaphysis = Ewing Sarcoma & Multiple Myeloma.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Osteoid Osteoma:** Nocturnal pain dramatically relieved by ASPIRIN / NSAIDs. CT scan shows nidus < 1.5 cm with dense sclerosis. DOC = Radiofrequency Ablation!\n\n### 9.9 Short-subject quick pearls\n\n- Osteosarcoma: sunburst; Ewing: onion peel.\n\n## 24. Orthopaedics (additions)\n\n- **Bone tumours and dysplasias:**\n  - *Giant cell tumour of bone:* epiphyseal (end of long bone) after closure of growth plate; soap bubble; denosumab or curettage; can recur.\n  - *Multiple enchondromas of the hand (Ollier disease):* cartilage tumours; risk of chondrosarcoma; ulnar side of hand.\n  - *Fibrous dysplasia with cafe-au-lait pigmentation (McCune-Albright):* GNAS mutation; polyostotic; precocious puberty.\n  - *Osteosarcoma sunburst periosteal reaction:* aggressive; metaphysis of long bones; Codman triangle; neoadjuvant chemo and surgery.\n  - *Ewing sarcoma EWSR1-FLI1 t(11;22):* diaphysis; onion-skin periosteal reaction; young; chemo and radiotherapy.\n  - *Pearl:* Ewing sarcoma's t(11;22) fusion gene is EWSR1-FLI1.\n- **Metabolic and genetic bone disease:**\n  - *Postmenopausal osteoporosis (diagnosis):* DEXA T-score at or below -2.5; FRAX.\n  - *Paget disease:* enlarged hat size (skull); isolated raised ALP; mosaic bone; sarcomatous change.\n  - *Osteogenesis imperfecta:* type I collagen defect; blue sclerae; fragility fractures; bisphosphonates.\n  - *Vitamin D deficiency rickets (paediatric radiographic recognition):* widened, cupped and frayed metaphyses; rachitic rosary.\n  - *Hypophosphataemic rickets (low phosphate and dental abscesses):* XLH; dental abscesses (no trauma).\n  - *Pearl:* in Paget disease, ALP is raised with normal calcium and phosphate.\n","ORTHO_UNIT_03":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.6 Orthopaedics\n\n- **Hip:** fracture neck of femur (elderly; avascular necrosis; hemiarthroplasty); congenital dislocation (Ortolani sign, Barlow test); Legg-Calve-Perthes disease (avascular necrosis of femoral head; children aged 4-8).\n- **Pearl:** Ortolani sign is for congenital dislocation of the hip (reduction click).\n\n## 24. Orthopaedics (additions)\n\n- **Degenerative spine and nerve roots:**\n  - *Lumbar disc prolapse and nerve-root localisation:* L4-5 (L5 root), L5-S1 (S1 root); reflexes: knee (L4), ankle (S1).\n  - *Spondylolisthesis:* anterior slip of one vertebra; Meyerding grading; spondylolysis (pars defect).\n  - *Pearl:* L5-S1 disc prolapse compresses S1 root, causing loss of ankle jerk.\n- **Inflammatory spine disease:**\n  - *Ankylosing spondylitis enthesitis and anterior uveitis:* HLA-B27; sacroiliitis; enthesitis (Achilles, plantar fascia); recurrent anterior uveitis.\n  - *Early sacroiliitis on MRI:* bone marrow oedema before radiographic changes.\n  - *Pearl:* MRI detects early sacroiliitis before X-ray changes; in AS, HLA-B27 is positive in about 90%.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Crystal Arthropathies: Gout vs Pseudogout Comparison\n\n| Parameter | Gout (Podagra) | Pseudogout (CPPD) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Crystal Composition | Monosodium Urate (MSU) | Calcium Pyrophosphate Dihydrate (CPPD) |\n| Crystal Morphology | NEEDLE-SHAPED / Spear-shaped with sharp ends | RHOMBOID-SHAPED / Parallelepiped geometric |\n| Polarized Light Microscopy | STRONGLY NEGATIVE Birefringence (Bright YELLOW when parallel) | WEAKLY POSITIVE Birefringence (BLUE when parallel to compensator) |\n| Predominant Joint | 1st Metatarsophalangeal Joint ('Podagra' in > 60%) | Knee joint (> 50% of cases) |\n| Radiographic Hallmark | 'Punched-Out' periarticular erosions with sclerotic overhanging edges (Martel sign) | CHONDROCALCINOSIS (linear calcification of hyaline & fibrocartilage menisci) |\n| First-Line Medical Therapy | Acute: NSAIDs / Colchicine / Steroids; Chronic: ALLOPURINOL | Acute: NSAIDs / Colchicine / Intra-articular corticosteroid injections |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **CTEV CAVE Sequence:** Correct Cavus -> Adductus & Varus -> Equinus (Achilles Tenotomy). Denis Browne splint maintains abduction.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Gout Birefringence:** 'Yellow when parallel, Blue when perpendicular' (Negative). Pseudogout is Positive and Blue when parallel!\n\n- **Carpal tunnel syndrome:** median nerve compression under the flexor retinaculum; night pain and numbness in the thumb, index, middle and half of the ring finger; thenar wasting late; Phalen and Tinel signs; nerve conduction study confirms; treat with splint and steroid injection, then carpal tunnel release.\n","PATH_UNIT_01":"## 4. Pathology (15-20 questions)\n\n### 4.1 General pathology: cell injury\n\n- **Reversible injury:** cellular swelling (ATP depletion, Na+ influx, K+ efflux), fatty change (triglyceride accumulation in liver, heart, kidney; alcohol, toxins, hypoxia). Reversible if the insult is removed.\n- **Irreversible injury:** membrane damage, mitochondrial failure, lysosomal rupture, nuclear changes. Ends in necrosis or apoptosis.\n- **Necrosis patterns:**\n  - *Coagulative:* preserved tissue outline (\"ghost\" cells); hypoxia in most solid organs (heart, kidney, spleen). Not the brain.\n  - *Liquefactive:* enzymatic digestion; brain (after hypoxic infarct), bacterial abscesses.\n  - *Caseous:* cheese-like, acellular amorphous debris with granuloma; tuberculosis, systemic fungi.\n  - *Fat necrosis:* saponification of fat by lipases (pancreatic fat necrosis, traumatic breast fat necrosis); chalky white deposits.\n  - *Fibrinoid:* immune complex deposition in vessel walls with eosinophilic fibrin-like material; vasculitis, malignant hypertension, preeclampsia.\n  - *Gangrenous:* coagulative necrosis with putrefaction (bacterial superimposed); limbs and bowel.\n- **Apoptosis vs necrosis:**\n| Feature | Apoptosis | Necrosis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cell size | Shrinks | Swells |\n| Membrane | Intact; blebbing | Disrupted |\n| DNA | Laddering (180-200 bp fragments) | Random digestion |\n| Inflammation | No | Yes |\n| Energy | ATP-dependent | Energy failure |\n| Example | Physiological deletion; viral hepatitis councilman bodies | Infarct |\n- **Caspases:** cysteine proteases; initiator caspases (8, 9), executioner caspases (3, 6, 7).\n- **Intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway:** BCL-2 family (BAX, BAK form pores; BCL-2 blocks); cytochrome c release; apoptosome; caspase-9. Triggered by DNA damage, growth factor withdrawal.\n- **Extrinsic (death receptor) pathway:** Fas (CD95) with Fas ligand; TNF receptor; caspase-8.\n- **Pearl:** BCL-2 overexpression (t(14;18)) blocks apoptosis, causing follicular lymphoma.\n\n**Free radical injury**\n- **Reactive oxygen species:** superoxide, hydrogen peroxide, hydroxyl radical (most damaging), nitric oxide.\n- **Defences:** superoxide dismutase (SOD), catalase, glutathione peroxidase, vitamins E and C.\n- **Lipid peroxidation:** free radicals attack polyunsaturated fatty acids in membranes, producing damage and cell death (basis of many toxicities; vitamin E is protective).\n- **Pearl:** the hydroxyl radical is the most reactive species; catalase removes hydrogen peroxide.\n\n#### Table: Types of Tissue Necrosis\n\n| Type | Mechanism | Hallmark Morphology | Classic Clinical Scenario |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Coagulative | Ischemia / protein denaturation | Preserved tissue architecture ('ghost outlines') | Myocardial, renal, splenic infarcts (NOT brain) |\n| Liquefactive | Enzymatic digestion by lysosomal enzymes | Liquid viscous mass, cyst formation | Brain ischemic strokes and bacterial abscess cavities |\n| Caseous | Combination of coagulative and liquefactive | 'Cheesy' white acellular debris with granulomas | Tuberculosis and systemic fungal infections |\n| Fat Necrosis | Lipase action generating free fatty acids | Saponification with Calcium (chalky white spots) | Acute pancreatitis and traumatic breast fat necrosis |\n| Fibrinoid | Antigen-antibody complexes leak into vessel wall | Bright pink smudgy wall on H&E | Polyarteritis nodosa, malignant hypertension, preeclampsia |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Apoptosis** = No inflammation, DNA laddering (180-200 bp), BAX/BAK pore formation.\n\n### 4.2 Inflammation and repair\n\n**Acute inflammation**\n- **Vascular changes:** transient vasoconstriction, then vasodilatation (histamine, nitric oxide, prostaglandins), increased vascular permeability (endothelial contraction, histamine, bradykinin).\n- **Cellular events:** margination, rolling (selectins: E-selectin on endothelium, P-selectin), adhesion (integrins: LFA-1 on leukocytes binds ICAM-1), transmigration (diapedesis), chemotaxis (C5a, LTB4, IL-8).\n- **Exudate vs transudate:** exudate (high protein, cellular, infection and inflammation); transudate (low protein, oedema from hydrostatic or oncotic imbalance).\n- **Mediators:** histamine (immediate), prostaglandins (fever, pain), leukotrienes (chemotaxis, bronchospasm), complement (C3a, C5a anaphylatoxins), cytokines (IL-1, TNF, IL-6).\n- **Leukocyte adhesion deficiencies:** LAD-1 (CD18 deficiency; delayed umbilical cord separation); LAD-2 (fucosylation defect; absent sialyl-Lewis X, Bombay phenotype); LAD-3 (kindlin-3; bleeding).\n- **Pearl:** LAD-1 leads to delayed cord separation; LAD-2 to the Bombay blood group.\n\n**Chronic inflammation**\n- **Cells:** macrophages, lymphocytes, plasma cells, fibroblasts, giant cells.\n- **Granulomas:** epithelioid macrophages with a rim of lymphocytes; caseating (TB) or non-caseating (sarcoidosis, Crohn disease, berylliosis, leprosy).\n- **Pearl:** the granuloma of sarcoidosis is non-caseating; that of tuberculosis is caseating.\n\n**Repair**\n- **Regeneration:** complete restoration (liver, skin, labile cells); no regeneration of neurons or cardiac muscle.\n- **Healing by primary intention:** clean wound, edges approximated; minimal scar.\n- **Healing by secondary intention:** large wound, granulation tissue fills gap; more scar.\n- **Keloid vs hypertrophic scar:** hypertrophic scar stays within wound margins and regresses; keloid extends beyond margins and does not regress; keloid collagen is thick and hyalinised.\n\n### 4.3 Haemodynamic disorders\n\n- **Oedema:** excess fluid in interstitium; causes: increased hydrostatic pressure (heart failure, venous thrombosis), decreased oncotic pressure (hypoalbuminaemia in nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, malnutrition), lymphatic obstruction (filariasis, malignancy), sodium retention.\n- **Thrombosis:** Virchow triad: endothelial injury, stasis, hypercoagulability. Arterial thrombi (white, lines of Zahn) vs venous thrombi (red, fibrin with red cells).\n- **Embolism:** paradoxical (through a patent foramen ovale), fat (long bone fractures; Petechiae, respiratory distress), amniotic fluid, air (decompression sickness, central venous catheter).\n- **Infarction:** coagulative necrosis (most organs), haemorrhagic (lung, intestine; dual blood supply), anaemic (solid organs with single supply: heart, kidney, spleen).\n- **Shock:** hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, septic (vasodilation, cytokines, IL-6; warm shock), anaphylactic, neurogenic.\n- **Pearl:** the Virchow triad is the explanation for any venous thrombosis question; stasis (immobility, DVT) is the most common.\n\n### 4.4 Genetic disorders\n\n- **Trinucleotide repeat expansion:** Huntington disease (CAG repeats, anticipation); fragile X syndrome (CGG repeats, methylation); myotonic dystrophy (CTG repeats).\n- **Numerical chromosome disorders:** Down syndrome (trisomy 21; endocardial cushion defects, Alzheimer-type change, duodenal atresia); Edwards (trisomy 18; rocker-bottom feet, clenched hands); Patau (trisomy 13; holoprosencephaly, polydactyly, cleft lip).\n- **Sex chromosome disorders:** Klinefelter (47 XXY; small firm testes, gynaecomastia, azoospermia); Turner (45 X; webbed neck, streak gonads, coarctation of aorta); triple X.\n- **Single gene:** autosomal dominant (Huntington, familial hypercholesterolaemia), recessive (cystic fibrosis, sickle cell, PKU), X-linked (haemophilia, Duchenne, Fragile X).\n- **Pearl:** in Klinefelter syndrome, the Barr body count is 1 (XXY has one X inactivated).\n\n### 4.8 Immunopathology (pathology link)\n\n- **Hypersensitivity:** Type I (IgE, mast cell degranulation; anaphylaxis, asthma), type II (antibody against cell surface; haemolytic anaemia, Goodpasture), type III (immune complex; SLE, serum sickness), type IV (T cell delayed; contact dermatitis, TB skin test).\n- **Transplant rejection:** hyperacute (preformed antibody, minutes), acute (T cell, days to weeks), chronic (months to years; obliterative vasculopathy).\n- **Pearl:** serum sickness is a type III reaction and appears about 7-14 days after exposure to foreign protein.\n\n### 4.9 Pearls for quick recall\n\n- Lipofuscin: golden-brown, perinuclear, autofluorescent (wear-and-tear pigment; does not stain blue with Perls).\n- Haemosiderin: golden-brown, Perls positive (iron).\n- Melanin: Fontana-Masson positive.\n- Russell bodies: plasma cell inclusions (immunoglobulin).\n- Schistocytes: microangiopathic haemolysis (TTP, HUS, DIC).\n\n## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.9 Short-subject quick pearls\n\n- Cherry red livor: carbon monoxide.\n\n## 17. Pathology (additions)\n\n- **Mendelian and genomic disorders:**\n  - *Prader-Willi syndrome:* paternal deletion 15q11-13 (or maternal UPD); hypotonia, hyperphagia, obesity, hypogonadism; methylation test.\n  - *Von Hippel-Lindau:* VHL tumour suppressor (3p); haemangioblastoma, RCC, phaeochromocytoma.\n  - *X-linked recessive pedigree:* affected males; carrier mothers; no male-to-male transmission.\n  - *Mendelian disorders are predominantly autosomal dominant:* most are dominant (e.g. Huntington, neurofibromatosis), while many metabolic disorders are recessive.\n  - *Cystic fibrosis:* autosomal recessive; CFTR chloride channel; delta F508 most common.\n  - *Pearl:* in autosomal recessive disease, both parents are carriers; affected children are usually from unaffected parents.\n- **Lymphomas:**\n  - *Follicular lymphoma:* CD10, BCL2, t(14;18) (IgH-BCL2); follicular pattern; indolent.\n  - *Burkitt lymphoma:* t(8;14) (MYC); starry-sky pattern (tingible body macrophages); high proliferation; EBV association in endemic form; highly aggressive.\n  - *Pearl:* Burkitt lymphoma has a starry-sky appearance; follicular has t(14;18) with BCL2 overexpression.\n- **Cell injury, ageing and pigment:**\n  - *Caspase-dependent cell death (apoptosis):* caspase 3, 8, 9; intrinsic (mitochondria, BCL2 family) vs extrinsic (death receptors).\n  - *Wet gangrene:* infected necrosis with moist swelling; bacterial (clostridial or mixed); liquefactive; diabetic foot.\n  - *Calorie restriction and longevity:* reduces mTOR and IGF-1 signalling; extends lifespan in animal models.\n  - *Anthracosis:* carbon pigment (coal, smoke) in hilar lymph nodes; deposited in macrophages.\n  - *Pearl:* wet gangrene is an infected coagulative necrosis; dry gangrene is coagulative necrosis without infection.\n- **Renal and urinary tract pathology:**\n  - *Ureter histology:* transitional epithelium (urothelium); muscular layers.\n  - *Chromophobe RCC:* clear to pale cells; perinuclear halos (raisinoid nuclei); good prognosis; arises from intercalated cells.\n  - *Cystinuria crystals:* hexagonal cystine crystals in urine; autosomal recessive; cystine stones.\n  - *Malakoplakia with Michaelis-Gutmann bodies:* chronic granulomatous inflammation from defective phagocytosis (E. coli); PAS positive Michaelis-Gutmann bodies (calcified bacteria).\n  - *Pearl:* hexagonal cystine crystals are pathognomonic of cystinuria.\n- **CNS demyelinating, infectious and neoplastic pathology:**\n  - *Craniopharyngioma:* suprasellar; calcification; keratin pearls (squamous nests); cystic; children; bitemporal hemianopia.\n  - *Neurocysticercosis distribution:* subarachnoid and parenchymal (cystic and calcified); seizures; racemose form.\n  - *Oligodendrocyte injury in MS:* demyelination (plaques); oligodendrocytes lost; Dawson fingers on MRI.\n  - *Pearl:* craniopharyngioma arises from Rathke pouch remnants and has calcification and keratin.\n- **Diagnostic pathology and histology:**\n  - *Flow cytometry in haematological diagnosis:* CD markers (CD19, CD20, CD3, CD10) to classify leukaemias and lymphomas.\n  - *TTF-1-positive tumours:* lung adenocarcinoma, small cell lung cancer, thyroid carcinoma (papillary, follicular, medullary).\n  - *Islets of Langerhans:* endocrine cells in pancreas; alpha (glucagon), beta (insulin), delta (somatostatin), PP (pancreatic polypeptide).\n  - *Pearl:* TTF-1 positive tumours arise from thyroid, lung or some brain tumours.\n- **Thyroid tumours:**\n  - *Papillary thyroid carcinoma:* FNAC shows Orphan Annie eye nuclei and psammoma bodies; lymphatic spread to cervical nodes; BRAF V600E mutation; excellent prognosis.\n  - *Medullary thyroid carcinoma:* C cells; amyloid stroma; calcitonin raised; RET mutation (MEN2); spreads haematogenously.\n  - *Pearl:* papillary carcinoma spreads through lymphatics; medullary through blood and produces calcitonin.\n- **Transfusion complications:** acute haemolytic (ABO incompatibility; IgM; intravascular haemolysis; renal failure); febrile non-haemolytic (IL-1, HLA); allergic (urticaria); TRALI (transfusion-related acute lung injury); TACO (circulatory overload); transfusion-transmitted infections; citrate toxicity (hypocalcaemia).\n  - *Pearl:* TRALI is a leading cause of transfusion-related death; it is due to donor antibodies (anti-HLA or anti-HNA) against recipient white cells.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Congo Red + Polarized Light** = Apple-Green Birefringence (Amyloid beta-pleated sheets).\n","PATH_UNIT_02":"## 4. Pathology (15-20 questions)\n\n### 4.5 Neoplasia\n\n**Terminology**\n- **Benign vs malignant:** benign (encapsulated, slow, no metastasis); malignant (invasive, metastatic, cellular atypia).\n- **Hyperplasia:** increased cell numbers in response to stimulus (endometrial hyperplasia). **Metaplasia:** change from one mature cell type to another (Barrett oesophagus: columnar replacing squamous). **Dysplasia:** disordered growth with cytological atypia, precancerous. **Anaplasia:** loss of differentiation; malignant.\n- **Carcinoma in situ:** atypia within the basement membrane (DCIS, CIN 3).\n\n**Oncogenes and tumour suppressors**\n- **Proto-oncogenes:** normal genes that promote growth; oncogenes after mutation or amplification. Examples: RAS (point mutation), MYC (amplification in Burkitt t(8;14)), HER2 (amplification in breast), BCR-ABL (t(9;22)), RET (MEN 2).\n- **Tumour suppressor genes:** normal genes that restrain growth; loss of both alleles (Knudson two-hit hypothesis). Examples: RB (retinoblastoma), p53 (Li-Fraumeni), APC (FAP), BRCA1/2 (breast and ovary), WT1 (Wilms tumour), NF1 and NF2.\n- **Guardian of the genome:** p53 responds to DNA damage by arresting the cell cycle (p21) or triggering apoptosis (BAX). Mutated in about half of human cancers.\n- **Pearl:** Li-Fraumeni syndrome (TP53 germline) gives multiple early cancers.\n\n**Tumour markers**\n- AFP: hepatocellular carcinoma, yolk sac tumour.\n- CEA: colorectal, pancreas, lung adenocarcinoma, medullary thyroid (also calcitonin).\n- CA-125: ovarian epithelial.\n- PSA: prostate.\n- beta-hCG: choriocarcinoma, seminoma (with AFP and LDH).\n- CA-19-9: pancreatic, biliary.\n- Calcitonin: medullary thyroid carcinoma.\n- Pearl: AFP is raised in both HCC and yolk sac tumour (germ cell); the setting decides which.\n\n**Paraneoplastic syndromes**\n- SIADH: small-cell lung cancer.\n- PTHrP (hypercalcaemia): squamous cell lung, renal, breast.\n- ACTH (Cushing): small-cell lung cancer.\n- Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome: small-cell lung cancer (anti-VGCC antibodies).\n- Trousseau sign (migratory thrombophlebitis): pancreatic cancer.\n- Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy: lung cancer.\n- Dermatomyositis: ovarian and lung cancer.\n- Pearl: Trousseau sign suggests pancreatic or other visceral malignancy.\n\n**Staging and grading**\n- **TNM:** T (primary tumour size and invasion), N (regional nodes), M (distant metastasis).\n- **Grade:** degree of differentiation (G1 well differentiated to G3 poorly differentiated).\n\n**Carcinogenesis**\n- Chemical carcinogens (initiator and promoter), radiation (ionising), viruses (HPV, EBV, HBV, HCV, HTLV-1, HHV-8), chronic inflammation, hormones.\n- **Viral oncogenesis:** EBV (Burkitt, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin lymphoma); HPV 16 and 18 (cervix); HBV and HCV (hepatocellular carcinoma); HTLV-1 (adult T-cell leukaemia-lymphoma); HHV-8 (Kaposi sarcoma).\n\n#### Table: High-Yield Chromosomal Translocations in Neoplasia\n\n| Translocation | Genes Involved | Consequence | Associated Malignancy |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| t(9;22) | BCR-ABL | Constitutively active tyrosine kinase | Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML), Adult ALL |\n| t(8;14) | c-Myc & IgH | Overexpression of c-Myc transcription factor | Burkitt Lymphoma ('starry sky' histology) |\n| t(14;18) | BCL-2 & IgH | Overexpression of anti-apoptotic BCL-2 | Follicular Lymphoma |\n| t(15;17) | PML-RARA | Blocks myeloid differentiation at promyelocyte | Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia (AML M3, treats with ATRA) |\n| t(11;14) | Cyclin D1 & IgH | Promotes G1 to S phase cell cycle transition | Mantle Cell Lymphoma |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Hematogenous Carcinomas:** Four Carcinomas Route Hematogenously (Follicular thyroid, Choriocarcinoma, Renal cell, Hepatocellular).\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **HPV Oncoproteins:** E6 degrades p53 (6 is root of 53). E7 binds Rb (7 looks like R).\n","PATH_UNIT_03":"## 4. Pathology (15-20 questions)\n\n### 4.6 Haematopathology\n\n**Leukaemias**\n- **AML:** Auer rods (MPO positive); M3 (acute promyelocytic leukaemia, t(15;17), PML-RARA, DIC, ATRA).\n- **ALL:** lymphoblasts (TdT positive, CD10 common); t(12;21) favourable; Philadelphia-positive poor prognosis.\n- **CML:** t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome, BCR-ABL; low leukocyte alkaline phosphatase; treated by TKI.\n- **CLL:** smudge cells; CD5, CD19, CD23 positive; most common adult leukaemia in Western countries.\n\n**Lymphomas**\n- **Hodgkin lymphoma:** Reed-Sternberg cells (binucleate owl-eye, CD15, CD30 positive, CD45 negative); nodular sclerosis (commonest; collagen bands), mixed cellularity, lymphocyte-rich, lymphocyte-depleted; B symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss).\n- **Non-Hodgkin lymphoma:** follicular (t(14;18), BCL-2 overexpression; starry sky is for Burkitt), diffuse large B-cell (commonest aggressive), Burkitt (t(8;14), MYC; starry sky appearance; African endemic form linked to EBV), mantle cell (t(11;14), cyclin D1), MALT (Helicobacter gastric).\n- **Pearl:** Reed-Sternberg cells are the diagnostic hallmark of Hodgkin lymphoma; Burkitt has a starry-sky pattern from tingible body macrophages.\n\n**Plasma cell dyscrasias**\n- **Multiple myeloma:** CRAB features (hyperCalcaemia, Renal failure, Anaemia, Bone lesions); monoclonal protein (M-spike); Bence Jones proteins (free light chains); lytic lesions (punched out, no sclerotic rim); Russell bodies.\n- **MGUS:** monoclonal protein below 3 g/dL; less than 10% plasma cells; no end-organ damage; annual progression risk about 1%.\n- **Amyloidosis:** AL (light chains, free light chain ratio abnormal), AA (serum amyloid A; chronic inflammation; kidney), ATTR (transthyretin; cardiac and neuropathy), beta-2 microglobulin (dialysis-related amyloidosis; carpal tunnel, bone cysts). Congo red stain with apple-green birefringence under polarised light.\n- **Pearl:** dialysis-related amyloidosis is beta-2 microglobulin; it causes carpal tunnel syndrome and bone cysts.\n\n**Coagulation pathway**\n- **Intrinsic (aPTT):** factors XII, XI, IX, VIII (with PL), prekallikrein, HMWK.\n- **Extrinsic (PT):** tissue factor (III), factor VII.\n- **Common:** factor X, V, prothrombin (II), fibrinogen (I).\n- **Vitamin K-dependent factors:** II, VII, IX, X (and proteins C and S). Warfarin inhibits these.\n- **Haemophilia A:** factor VIII deficiency (X-linked); aPTT prolonged; PT normal. Haemophilia B: factor IX.\n- **Von Willebrand disease:** most common inherited bleeding disorder; prolonged bleeding time; factor VIII carrier; normal PT.\n- **Pearl:** haemophilia has a prolonged aPTT with normal PT; warfarin prolongs both.\n\n**Anaemias (pathology)**\n- **Microcytic:** iron deficiency (low ferritin, high TIBC); thalassaemia (HbA2 raised, normal ferritin); sideroblastic (ring sideroblasts, pyridoxine responsive in some); anaemia of chronic disease (normal to high ferritin).\n- **Macrocytic:** megaloblastic (B12, folate; hypersegmented neutrophils, megaloblasts); non-megaloblastic (liver disease, hypothyroidism, alcohol, myelodysplasia).\n- **Haemolytic:** hereditary spherocytosis (spherocytes; osmotic fragility; splenectomy), G6PD deficiency (Heinz bodies, bite cells; oxidative stress, primaquine, dapsone, sulfonamides), sickle cell (HbS; sickle cells; autosplenectomy), thalassaemia.\n- **Pearl:** G6PD deficiency causes bite cells and Heinz bodies; hereditary spherocytosis causes spherocytes.\n\n**Myelodysplastic syndromes (MDS)**\n- Dysplasia in one or more cell lines; ringed sideroblasts; pancytopenia; risk of AML.\n\n#### Table: Differential Diagnosis of Microcytic Anemias\n\n| Parameter | Iron Deficiency Anemia | Beta-Thalassemia Trait | Anemia of Chronic Disease | Sideroblastic Anemia |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Serum Iron | LOW | Normal to High | LOW | HIGH |\n| Serum Ferritin | LOW | Normal to High | HIGH | HIGH |\n| TIBC / Transferrin | HIGH | Normal | LOW | Normal |\n| Hemoglobin A2 | Low to Normal | ELEVATED (> 3.5%) | Normal | Normal |\n| Bone Marrow Iron | Absent | Normal to Increased | Abundant in macrophages | Ring Sideroblasts in mitochondria |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Mentzer Index** = MCV / RBC count. < 13 suggests Thalassemia; > 13 suggests Iron Deficiency.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Multiple Myeloma CRAB:** HyperCalcemia, Renal impairment, Anemia, Bone lytic lesions.\n\n## 10. High-Yield Image Areas\n\n- **Pearl:** the Reed-Sternberg cell is the diagnostic cell for Hodgkin lymphoma (binucleate \"owl's eyes\").\n\n## 11. Integrated Patterns (High-Yield Associations)\n\n- **Hereditary spherocytosis:** spherocytes; osmotic fragility; splenectomy.\n\n## 17. Pathology (additions)\n\n- **Red-cell disorders:**\n  - *Hereditary spherocytosis:* spectrin/ankyrin defect; spherocytes; increased osmotic fragility (confirmed by EMA binding or osmotic fragility test); splenectomy reduces haemolysis.\n  - *Iron-deficiency anaemia morphology:* microcytic hypochromic cells; low ferritin, high TIBC, low serum iron; pencil cells; koilonychia.\n  - *Beta-thalassaemia:* raised HbA2 (above 3.5%) and HbF; target cells; microcytosis; HbA2 raised in beta-thalassaemia trait.\n  - *Mechanical fragmentation (MAHA):* schistocytes (helmet cells) from prosthetic heart valves or microangiopathy; raised LDH, low haptoglobin.\n  - *Dietary folate-deficiency macrocytosis:* megaloblastic anaemia; hypersegmented neutrophils; raised homocysteine, normal MMA.\n  - *Pearl:* hereditary spherocytosis is confirmed by raised MCHC and positive osmotic fragility; splenectomy is curative for most of the haemolysis.\n- **Acute leukaemias:**\n  - *APL t(15;17):* PML-RARA fusion; promyelocytes with Auer rods (faggot cells); DIC; treat with ATRA and arsenic trioxide.\n  - *AML cytogenetic subtypes from smear:* Auer rods (AML), myeloperoxidase positive; t(8;21), inv(16), t(15;17), FLT3.\n  - *ALL induction therapy:* vincristine, prednisolone, asparaginase, anthracycline; CNS prophylaxis with intrathecal methotrexate.\n  - *Down syndrome and leukaemia:* 10-20-fold risk of ALL and AML (AMKL); GATA1 mutation.\n  - *Benzene and AML:* benzene exposure is a recognised risk factor for AML.\n  - *Pearl:* APL presents with DIC and bleeding; ATRA differentiates the promyelocytes.\n- **Plasma-cell neoplasms:** multiple myeloma (CRAB: hypercalcaemia, renal failure, anaemia, bone lesions); monoclonal gammopathy of uncertain significance (MGUS); Waldenstrom macroglobulinaemia (IgM, hyperviscosity). Bence Jones protein in urine (light chains).\n  - *Pearl:* Bence Jones proteinuria is a feature of myeloma; it is detected on urine electrophoresis.\n","PATH_UNIT_04":"## 4. Pathology (15-20 questions)\n\n### 4.7 Systemic pathology\n\n**Cardiovascular**\n- **Atherosclerosis:** fatty streaks, fibrous plaques, complicated lesions (ulceration, thrombosis, haemorrhage); risk factors: hypertension, diabetes, smoking, dyslipidaemia.\n- **Myocardial infarction:** coagulative necrosis after 4-12 hours (earlier: wavy fibres, neutrophils at 1-3 days, macrophages at 3-7 days, granulation tissue at 1-2 weeks, scar at 2 months); contraction bands at reperfusion (dense transverse bands from calcium overload).\n- **Rheumatic heart disease:** Aschoff bodies (Anitschkow cells: caterpillar nuclei, Aschoff giant cells), fibrinoid necrosis, mitral valve stenosis and regurgitation.\n- **Infective endocarditis:** vegetations (fibrin, platelets, organisms); embolism; Osler nodes (vasculitis) and Janeway lesions (septic emboli).\n- **Hypertensive heart disease:** concentric left ventricular hypertrophy.\n- **Pearl:** Aschoff bodies (Anitschkow cells) are pathognomonic for rheumatic fever.\n\n**Respiratory**\n- **Lobar pneumonia:** Streptococcus pneumoniae; consolidation with red hepatisation then grey hepatisation; rust-coloured sputum.\n- **Bronchopneumonia:** patchy; infants and elderly; Staphylococcus, Pseudomonas.\n- **Tuberculosis:** caseating granuloma (Langhans giant cells, epithelioid histiocytes, lymphocytes, caseous centre); acid-fast bacilli on ZN stain. Primary (Ghon focus, Ghon complex).\n- **Lung cancer:** squamous cell (central, keratin pearls, intercellular bridges, hypercalcaemia); adenocarcinoma (peripheral, glandular, mucin; commonest in never-smokers; EGFR, ALK); small cell (central, neuroendocrine, Kulchitsky cells, paraneoplastic); large cell (undifferentiated).\n- **Pearl:** Pancoast tumour is a superior sulcus lung tumour with Horner syndrome.\n\n**Renal**\n- **Nephritic syndrome:** post-streptococcal GN (subepithelial humps on electron microscopy, low C3); IgA nephropathy (mesangial IgA deposits, synpharyngitic haematuria); lupus nephritis (wire loop lesions, full house immunofluorescence); anti-GBM disease (crescentic glomerulonephritis; linear immunofluorescence).\n- **Nephrotic syndrome:** minimal change (effacement of podocyte foot processes); FSGS (segmental sclerosis, hyalinosis); membranous (spike and dome, subepithelial deposits, PLA2R antibody); diabetic nephropathy (Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules, nodular glomerulosclerosis; mesangial expansion; diffuse GBM thickening).\n- **Renal cell carcinoma:** clear cells (lipid and glycogen), von Hippel-Lindau mutation (VHL on chromosome 3p), haematuria, flank pain, palpable mass; paraneoplastic erythrocytosis (EPO).\n- **Wilms tumour:** children; WT1 mutation; triphasic (blastemal, epithelial, stromal).\n- **Pearl:** Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules are pathognomonic for diabetic nephropathy.\n\n**Central nervous system**\n- **Glioblastoma multiforme:** WHO grade IV astrocytoma; pseudopalisading necrosis; microvascular proliferation; IDH wild-type.\n- **Medulloblastoma:** children; cerebellum; Homer Wright rosettes; SHH or WNT pathways.\n- **Meningioma:** whorls, psammoma bodies; dural based; benign.\n- **Oligodendroglioma:** fried egg cells; 1p/19q codeletion; better prognosis.\n- **Ependymoma:** perivascular pseudorosettes.\n- **Rabies:** Negri bodies (eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions in Purkinje cells, hippocampus).\n- **Pearl:** Negri bodies are pathognomonic for rabies.\n\n**Gastrointestinal and hepatic**\n- **Hepatocellular carcinoma:** HBV, HCV, alcohol, haemochromatosis, aflatoxin; AFP raised; hepatic artery supply; cirrhosis background.\n- **Cirrhosis:** macronodular (alcohol, hepatitis) or micronodular (alcohol, biliary); regenerative nodules; fibrous septa.\n- **Macrovesicular steatosis:** large fat droplets pushing nucleus to periphery (alcohol, diabetes, NAFLD).\n- **Nutmeg liver:** chronic passive congestion of the liver (right heart failure).\n- **Alcoholic hepatitis:** Mallory-Denk bodies (ropy, hyaline), neutrophilic infiltrate, ballooning degeneration.\n- **Wilson disease:** copper deposition; Kayser-Fleischer ring (cornea); low ceruloplasmin.\n- **Haemochromatosis:** iron deposition; Perls Prussian blue stain positive; bronze diabetes; cirrhosis; HFE mutation.\n- **Pearl:** Mallory-Denk bodies are seen in alcoholic hepatitis, not in viral hepatitis.\n\n**Gynaecology and breast (pathology)**\n- **Serous cystadenoma and carcinoma:** common ovarian tumours; psammoma bodies (concentric calcified spheres) are typical histologically.\n- **Call-Exner bodies:** small follicle-like rosettes of eosinophilic fluid seen in granulosa cell tumour of the ovary.\n- **Endometrial carcinoma:** endometrioid (commonest), serous (aggressive), clear cell.\n- **Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia:** koilocytes; p16 immunostaining.\n- **Breast carcinoma:** invasive ductal (no special type), invasive lobular (signet ring cells, single-file pattern; E-cadherin loss), DCIS (comedonecrosis, central necrosis), inflammatory (dermal lymphatic involvement), Paget disease of the nipple (Paget cells, intraepidermal).\n- **Pearl:** E-cadherin loss is characteristic of invasive lobular carcinoma.\n\n**Skin and soft tissue**\n- **Melanoma:** Breslow thickness (depth), Clark level, ulceration, mitoses; BRAF V600E mutation.\n- **Basal cell carcinoma:** palisading nuclei; Hedgehog pathway (PTCH1); locally invasive; rarely metastasises.\n- **Squamous cell carcinoma of skin:** keratin pearls; sun exposure; HPV.\n- **Kaposi sarcoma:** HHV-8; spindle cells; slit-like vascular spaces.\n\n**Bone and joint**\n- **Osteosarcoma:** sunburst periosteal reaction, Codman triangle; metaphysis of long bones; osteoid production; p53 and RB mutations.\n- **Ewing sarcoma:** onion-skin periosteal reaction; t(11;22) EWSR1-FLI1; CD99 positive; small round blue cells.\n- **Giant cell tumour:** epiphysis of long bones; soap-bubble appearance; giant cells.\n- **Chondrosarcoma:** cartilage matrix; hyaline; IDH1/2 mutation.\n\n**Endocrine**\n- **Pituitary adenoma:** prolactinoma (most common functioning adenoma), growth hormone (acromegaly), ACTH (Cushing), TSH.\n- **Adrenal:** phaeochromocytoma (catecholamine excess, chromaffin cells, Zellballen; MEN 2 and VHL); neuroblastoma (children; HVA and VMA raised; N-myc).\n- **Thyroid:** papillary (ground glass nuclei, psammoma bodies, Orphan Annie nuclei); follicular (capsular invasion); medullary (calcitonin, amyloid stroma, MEN 2); anaplastic (elderly, poor prognosis).\n\n#### Table: Glomerular Diseases Biopsy Matrix\n\n| Disease | Light Microscopy | Immunofluorescence | Electron Microscopy |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Minimal Change | Normal glomeruli | Negative | Diffuse Foot Process Effacement |\n| PSGN | Enlarged hypercellular | Granular IgG and C3 ('starry sky') | Subepithelial Humps |\n| Membranous Nephropathy | Thickened capillary walls | Granular IgG and C3 along capillary wall | Subepithelial 'Spike and Dome' |\n| Goodpasture Syndrome | Crescentic RPGN | Smooth LINEAR IgG along GBM | No discrete deposits, GBM breaks |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **MI Rupture Window** = 3 to 7 days (macrophage degradation makes myocardium softest).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Alzheimer:** Extracellular Senile Plaques (A-beta) + Intracellular Neurofibrillary Tangles (Tau).\n\n### 4.9 Pearls for quick recall\n\n- Mallory bodies: alcoholic hepatitis (cytokeratin aggregation).\n- Councilman bodies: viral hepatitis apoptotic hepatocytes.\n- Psammoma bodies: serous ovarian cancer, meningioma, papillary thyroid.\n\n## 10. High-Yield Image Areas\n\n- **Histopathology:** Psammoma bodies (papillary thyroid, meningioma, serous ovarian); Reed-Sternberg (Hodgkin); Councilman bodies (viral hepatitis); Mallory bodies (alcoholic hepatitis); Negri bodies (rabies); Lewy bodies (Parkinson); Russell bodies (plasma cells); Aschoff bodies (rheumatic fever); Schaumann bodies (sarcoidosis); Heinz bodies (G6PD); Owl's eye (CMV).\n- **Blood film:** schistocytes (MAHA); target cells (thalassaemia, liver disease); sickle cells; teardrop cells (myelofibrosis); Auer rods (AML); Howell-Jolly bodies (asplenia); spherocytes (hereditary spherocytosis); blasts; reactive lymphocytes (EBV).\n\n## 11. Integrated Patterns (High-Yield Associations)\n\n- **Haemochromatosis:** HFE; bronze diabetes; cirrhosis; hepatocellular carcinoma; cardiomyopathy; phlebotomy.\n\n## 17. Pathology (additions)\n\n- **Glomerular diseases:**\n  - *HBV-associated membranous nephropathy:* subepithelial immune complexes; spike-and-dome pattern on GBM silver stain; nephrotic syndrome.\n  - *Poststreptococcal GN:* subepithelial hump-like deposits on EM (immune complex); low C3; nephritic syndrome.\n  - *Lupus nephritis with red cell casts:* wire-loop lesions; subendothelial deposits; haematuria and red cell casts.\n  - *Minimal change disease:* effacement of foot processes; steroid responsive; commonest cause of nephrotic syndrome in children; no changes on light microscopy.\n  - *Pearl:* minimal change disease responds to steroids; focal segmental glomerulosclerosis does not respond as well.\n- **Liver injury, cholestasis and tumours:**\n  - *HCC gross morphology:* large solitary or multiple nodules; bile-stained (green); cirrhosis background; alpha-fetoprotein.\n  - *Primary biliary cholangitis:* AMA positive (anti-mitochondrial antibody, M2); granulomatous destruction of small bile ducts; ursodeoxycholic acid.\n  - *Mallory-Denk bodies:* eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions in hepatocytes (alcoholic hepatitis and NASH).\n  - *Pearl:* Mallory-Denk bodies are seen in alcoholic hepatitis and NASH; AMA is the serological marker of PBC.\n- **Gastrointestinal congenital pathology:** Hirschsprung (absent ganglion cells), congenital pyloric stenosis, duodenal atresia, Meckel diverticulum, malrotation (volvulus).\n- **Acute inflammation, granulomatous disease, cardiomyopathy, gynaecologic and testicular pathology, vasculitis, ARDS, chronic leukaemias, haemostasis, rheumatic heart disease, lung tumours, bone marrow failure:**\n  - *Granulomatous disease:* caseating (TB), non-caseating (sarcoidosis), foreign body; Langhans giant cells; epithelioid macrophages.\n  - *Cardiomyopathy:* dilated (alcohol, viral, peripartum), hypertrophic (sarcomere mutation, MYBPC3; sudden death), restrictive (amyloid, haemochromatosis).\n  - *Gynaecologic pathology:* endometrial hyperplasia, endometrioid carcinoma, serous cystadenoma, Krukenberg tumour (stomach carcinoma metastasis to ovary), granulosa cell tumour (Call-Exner bodies).\n  - *Testicular tumours:* seminoma (placental alkaline phosphatase, lymphocytes; radiosensitive), yolk sac tumour (AFP), choriocarcinoma (hCG), teratoma.\n  - *Vasculitis:* polyarteritis nodosa (hepatitis B; medium arteries; spares lungs), granulomatosis with polyangiitis (c-ANCA, PR3; upper and lower respiratory tract, renal), giant cell arteritis.\n  - *ARDS:* diffuse alveolar damage; hyaline membranes; PaO2/FiO2 below 200; bilateral infiltrates.\n  - *Chronic leukaemias:* CML (BCR-ABL, t(9;22), Philadelphia chromosome, leukocytosis, basophilia); CLL (CD5, CD19, smudge cells, B cells).\n  - *Haemostasis and coagulation disorders:* von Willebrand (VWF), haemophilia A (factor VIII) and B (factor IX), DIC (raised PT, aPTT, D-dimer, low platelets, low fibrinogen).\n  - *Rheumatic heart disease:* Aschoff bodies; Anitschkow cells; mitral stenosis; verrucous endocarditis.\n  - *Lung tumours and occupational lung disease:* small cell (neuroendocrine, Kulchitsky cells); squamous (smoking, cavitation); adenocarcinoma (non-smokers); asbestosis (ferruginous bodies); silicosis (eggshell calcification).\n  - *Bone marrow failure:* aplastic anaemia (immune-mediated; trilineage; hypocellular marrow); Fanconi anaemia (chromosome breakage; radial hypoplasia).\n  - *Pearl:* Aschoff bodies with Anitschkow (caterpillar) cells are diagnostic of rheumatic fever.\n","PEDS_UNIT_01":"## 7. Paediatrics (12-15 questions)\n\n### 7.1 Growth and development\n\n- **Milestones:** smiles at 6 weeks; head control 3 months; sits without support 6 months; pincer grasp and walks alone 12 months; two-word sentences 2 years; runs and climbs stairs alternating feet 3 years; copies a circle 3 years, copies a cross 4 years.\n- **Birth weight:** about 3 kg (doubles by 5-6 months; triples by 1 year). Regain birth weight by 10 days.\n- **Weight-based fluid:** 100 ml/kg for first 10 kg, 50 ml/kg for next 10 kg, 20 ml/kg thereafter (maintenance per 24 hours).\n- **Fontanelles:** anterior closes by 18 months; posterior by 2 months.\n- **Dentition:** primary teeth from 6 months; permanent first molar at 6 years.\n- **Pearl:** the anterior fontanelle closes by 18 months; a bulging fontanelle indicates raised intracranial pressure.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Milestones Rule: 2 mo** = Smile, 4 mo = Steady neck, 6 mo = Hand transfer, 8 mo = Sits, 12 mo = Stands + 1st word, 2 yrs = 2-word phrase + stairs.\n\n### 7.4 Infections and immunisation\n\n- **Immunisation schedule (India, UIP):** BCG at birth; OPV and hepatitis B at birth; DTP, Hib, OPV, rotavirus, PCV at 6, 10, 14 weeks; measles at 9 months; MMR at 15-18 months; DTP booster at 16-24 months; typhoid conjugate vaccine from 6 months; HPV for adolescent girls.\n- **Diarrhoea:** oral rehydration solution (ORS); zinc supplementation (10-14 days); continue feeds; antibiotics only for dysentery or cholera.\n- **Dehydration (WHO classification):** no dehydration (under 5%), some (5-10%), severe (above 10%).\n- **Pneumonia (WHO):** cough or difficulty breathing with fast breathing; chest indrawing; danger signs; amoxicillin.\n- **Measles:** Koplik spots; vitamin A (two doses); complications: pneumonia, encephalitis, SSPE.\n- **Diphtheria:** see Microbiology (Section 6.4) and vaccination (DTP).\n- **Pertussis:** azithromycin; infants at high risk of apnoea.\n- **Pearl:** vitamin A is given to children with measles as two doses of 1 lakh IU on day 1 and day 2 (for those aged 1-5 years).\n- **Pearl:** zinc supplementation improves outcome in diarrhoea and reduces duration.\n\n### 7.5 Nutrition\n\n- **Malnutrition classification:** weight for age; height for age (stunting); weight for height (wasting).\n- **Severe acute malnutrition (SAM):** weight for height below -3 Z score, MUAC below 115 mm; oedema; therapeutic feeding (F-75, F-100; RUTF).\n- **Marasmus:** severe wasting; no oedema; ravenous appetite.\n- **Kwashiorkor:** oedema; hypoalbuminaemia; flaky paint dermatosis; fatty liver; protein deficiency.\n- **Vitamin A deficiency:** Bitot spots; xerophthalmia; night blindness; keratomalacia.\n- **Vitamin D deficiency:** rickets (craniotabes, rachitic rosary, bowing of legs); raised alkaline phosphatase; low calcium and phosphate.\n- **Pearl:** kwashiorkor shows oedema with preserved appetite; marasmus shows wasting with a ravenous appetite.\n\n#### Table: Severe Childhood Malnutrition: Kwashiorkor vs Marasmus\n\n| Parameter | Kwashiorkor | Marasmus |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Deficiency | Severe PROTEIN deficiency with relative carbohydrate calories | Severe TOTAL CALORIE / energy starvation |\n| Edema | PRESENT (Bilateral bipedal pitting edema is mandatory) | ABSENT (No edema) |\n| Body Fat & Muscle | Preserved subcutaneous fat, mild muscle wasting | Severe loss of both subcutaneous fat and muscle mass |\n| Facies | 'Moon Facies' (rounded, puffy cheeks) | 'Old Man' / 'Monkey Facies' (loss of buccal fat pads) |\n| Skin Changes | 'Flaky-Paint' / 'Crazy-Pavement' dermatosis with peeling | Dry, loose, wrinkled skin ('baggy pants' redundant folds) |\n| Liver | Hepatomegaly (Fatty liver due to decreased apolipoprotein B-100) | Normal liver, no hepatomegaly |\n| Mental State | Miserable, irritable, apathetic | Alert, voraciously hungry ('hungry vomiter') |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **SAM Criteria (Any 1 of 3):** WFH < -3 SD, MUAC < 115 mm, or Bilateral Pitting Edema. Never give Iron during stabilization!\n\n### 7.6 Genetics and syndromes\n\n- **Down syndrome:** trisomy 21; AV canal defect; duodenal atresia; hypotonia; Brushfield spots; atlantoaxial instability; leukaemia risk.\n- **Turner syndrome:** 45,XO; short stature; coarctation; webbed neck; streak gonads; primary amenorrhoea.\n- **Klinefelter syndrome:** 47,XXY; tall stature; small testes; gynaecomastia; infertility.\n- **Edwards syndrome (trisomy 18):** rocker-bottom feet; clenched hands; overlapping fingers; congenital heart disease; death within a year.\n- **Patau syndrome (trisomy 13):** holoprosencephaly; cleft lip and palate; polydactyly.\n- **Fragile X:** CGG repeat expansion; macroorchidism; intellectual disability.\n- **Cystic fibrosis:** CFTR gene (chromosome 7, delta F508); sweat chloride above 60 mEq/L; meconium ileus; pancreatic insufficiency; recurrent pneumonia (Pseudomonas, Staphylococcus).\n- **Phenylketonuria:** phenylalanine hydroxylase; mental retardation; mousy odour; screening (guthrie test); diet.\n- **Pearl:** sweat chloride above 60 mEq/L is diagnostic of cystic fibrosis.\n\n### 7.10 Paediatric quick pearls\n\n- Edwards syndrome: rocker-bottom feet, death within 1 year.\n- Patau syndrome: holoprosencephaly.\n\n## 23. Paediatrics (additions)\n\n- **Growth, development and infant feeding:**\n  - *Infant linear growth:* about 25 cm in the first year (birth about 50 cm, 1 year about 75 cm).\n  - *Growth chart interpretation:* plot on centile; crossing two major centiles is abnormal; mid-parental height for genetic potential.\n  - *Scammon growth curves:* neural (early, head growth), lymphoid (peaks around 10-12 years then declines), general (linear), genital (late puberty).\n  - *Complementary feeding for a breastfed infant:* introduce from 6 months, 6-8 times a day after 9 months.\n  - *Pearl:* Scammon curves: neural tissue grows fastest in the first years; lymphoid tissue peaks around puberty.\n- **Nutritional deficiency disorders:**\n  - *X-linked hypophosphataemic rickets:* low phosphate despite normal vitamin D; failure to respond to ordinary vitamin D; FGF23 excess; phosphate and calcitriol.\n  - *Scurvy radiography:* white line of Frankel (dense metaphyseal line); Trummerfeld zone; Wimberger ring.\n  - *Iron-deficiency anaemia profile:* increased TIBC and reduced transferrin saturation; low ferritin.\n  - *Pearl:* X-linked hypophosphataemic rickets: low phosphate with normal calcium; treat with phosphate and calcitriol.\n- **Genetic and congenital disorders:** Down, Turner, Klinefelter, Edwards, Patau (see Section 7.6); fragile X; PKU; galactosaemia.\n- **Seizure disorders:** febrile seizure (see 7.7); infantile spasms (West syndrome: hypsarrhythmia; ACTH or vigabatrin); Lennox-Gastaut; absence (3 per second spike and wave; ethosuximide).\n  - *Pearl:* infantile spasms are treated with ACTH or vigabatrin; hypsarrhythmia on EEG.\n- **Cystic fibrosis:** see 7.6; sweat chloride above 60 mEq/L; CFTR; pancreatic enzymes; Pseudomonas; mucolytics (dornase alfa).\n- **Acute diarrhoea and dehydration:** ORS (WHO formula); zinc for 10-14 days; no antimotility drugs; antibiotics for dysentery or cholera.\n- **Hydrocephalus and shunt complications:** ventriculoperitoneal shunt; infection (Staphylococcus epidermidis); blockage (headache, vomiting, lethargy); overdrainage (slit ventricles, subdural effusion); treatment: shunt revision.\n  - *Pearl:* shunt infection is most commonly due to coagulase-negative staphylococci (S. epidermidis).\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Vaccine vial monitor (VVM):** a heat-sensitive square inside a circle on the vial. Inner square lighter than the circle (stages 1-2): use the vaccine. Inner square matches or is darker than the circle (stages 3-4): discard.\n","PEDS_UNIT_02":"## 7. Paediatrics (12-15 questions)\n\n### 7.2 Neonatology\n\n- **APGAR score:** Appearance, Pulse, Grimace, Activity, Respiration (0-2 each); score at 1 and 5 minutes.\n- **Physiological jaundice:** appears after 24 hours, peaks day 3-5, resolves by day 14; unconjugated bilirubin.\n- **Pathological jaundice:** appears within 24 hours; rises above 5 mg/dl per day; prolonged beyond 2 weeks; conjugated (above 2 mg/dl) is always pathological.\n- **Kernicterus:** bilirubin-induced brain injury (basal ganglia, subthalamic nucleus); deafness, choreoathetosis.\n- **Phototherapy and exchange transfusion:** thresholds from the AAP guidelines based on gestational age and risk factors.\n- **Respiratory distress syndrome (hyaline membrane disease):** surfactant deficiency; ground-glass appearance; prevented by antenatal steroids; treatment: surfactant replacement, CPAP.\n- **Transient tachypnoea of newborn:** elective caesarean section, retained fetal lung fluid; resolves in 24-72 hours.\n- **Meconium aspiration:** post-term infants; patchy infiltrates; hyperinflation.\n- **Necrotising enterocolitis:** premature infants; pneumatosis intestinalis; pneumoperitoneum (perforation); bowel rest, antibiotics.\n- **Neonatal sepsis:** early onset (within 72 hours; GBS, E. coli); late onset (after 72 hours; coagulase negative staphylococci, Candida).\n- **Hypoglycaemia:** below 45 mg/dl in neonate; IV dextrose; symptomatic or persistent.\n- **Persistent pulmonary hypertension of newborn:** hypoxaemia; nitric oxide; ECMO.\n- **Pearl:** antenatal corticosteroids (betamethasone) given at 24-34 weeks reduce RDS and neonatal mortality.\n\n#### Table: Neonatal Respiratory Distress Differential Diagnosis\n\n| Entity | Gestational Profile | CXR Findings | Management Highlights |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Respiratory Distress Syndrome (RDS) | Preterm (< 34 wks), diabetic mother | Reticulogranular 'ground-glass' + Air bronchograms + Low lung volumes | Early CPAP + Intratracheal Surfactant; Antenatal Betamethasone |\n| Transient Tachypnea (TTNB) | Term / Late preterm, Elective C-section | Hyperaeration + Perihilar streaks + Fluid in interlobar fissures | Supportive O2 / CPAP; Self-resolves in 24-72 hours |\n| Meconium Aspiration (MAS) | Post-term (> 41 wks), SGA, fetal hypoxia | Coarse, patchy asymmetrical infiltrates + Emphysema bullae | Gentle ventilation (prevent pneumothorax) + Surfactant + iNO for PPHN |\n| Congenital Diaphragmatic Hernia | Term neonate, Scaphoid abdomen | Bowel gas loops in left chest + Mediastinal shift to right | IMMEDIATE INTUBATION (NO Bag-Mask!); Delayed surgery after PPHN stabilizes |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Kernicterus Target:** Globus Pallidus & Subthalamic nuclei -> Choreoathetoid CP + Sensorineural deafness + Upward gaze palsy.\n\n### 7.8 Paediatric emergencies and resuscitation\n\n- **Neonatal resuscitation:** dry, warm, stimulate; airway; positive pressure ventilation at 40-60 breaths/min; chest compressions if heart rate below 60; adrenaline.\n- **Paediatric BLS:** compression depth 1/3 of chest (about 4 cm); rate 100-120/min; 15:2 ratio (single rescuer 30:2 for adults).\n- **Shock:** compensated (normal BP, tachycardia) vs decompensated (hypotension); fluid bolus 20 ml/kg.\n- **Poisoning:** paracetamol (N-acetylcysteine); organophosphate (atropine, pralidoxime); iron (desferrioxamine); lead (chelation with DMSA).\n- **Pearl:** paracetamol overdose treatment is N-acetylcysteine within 8 hours.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **NRP Compressions:** 3:1 ratio (90 compressions : 30 breaths/min, 100% O2). Epinephrine 0.02 mg/kg IV only if HR < 60 bpm after effective PPV.\n\n### 7.10 Paediatric quick pearls\n\n- Physiological jaundice appears after 24 hours; pathological jaundice appears within the first 24 hours.\n\n## 23. Paediatrics (additions)\n\n- **Neonatal care and at-risk infants:**\n  - *Timing of first neonatal urination:* within 24 hours; continue breastfeeding and observe on day 1.\n  - *At-risk infant criteria for artificial feeding:* HIV-positive mother; hepatitis B positive (give vaccine and HBIG).\n  - *Normal umbilical cord anatomy:* two arteries and one vein (absence of one artery is associated with anomalies).\n  - *Pearl:* a single umbilical artery is associated with renal and cardiac anomalies.\n- **Neonatal respiratory disorders:** RDS, TTN, MAS, PPHN (see Section 7.2).\n- **Primary immunodeficiency:** SCID (no T cells; X-linked IL2RG most common); X-linked agammaglobulinaemia (BTK; no B cells); DiGeorge (22q11.2; thymic hypoplasia); chronic granulomatous disease (see Microbiology immunology).\n  - *Pearl:* SCID is a paediatric emergency; newborn screening with TREC; bone marrow transplant.\n- **Neonatal cholestasis and biliary atresia:** conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia after 2 weeks; acholic stools; Kasai portoenterostomy within 60 days.\n  - *Pearl:* biliary atresia requires Kasai portoenterostomy as early as possible (before 60 days).\n","PEDS_UNIT_03":"## 7. Paediatrics (12-15 questions)\n\n### 7.3 Congenital heart disease\n\n**Acyanotic (left-to-right shunt)**\n- **Atrial septal defect (ASD):** fixed split second heart sound; ostium secundum commonest; ejection systolic murmur at pulmonary area.\n- **Ventricular septal defect (VSD):** pansystolic murmur at left lower sternal border; commonest congenital heart disease; Eisenmenger syndrome if reversal.\n- **Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA):** continuous \"machinery\" murmur; wide pulse pressure; indomethacin or ibuprofen for closure in preterm; surgery or device.\n- **Coarctation of the aorta:** radiofemoral delay; hypertension in upper limbs; rib notching (after age 8); Turner syndrome association.\n\n**Cyanotic**\n- **Tetralogy of Fallot:** VSD, overriding aorta, RV outflow obstruction, RV hypertrophy; tet spells (squatting relieves); boot-shaped heart on X-ray.\n- **Transposition of great arteries:** aorta arises from RV; severe cyanosis in first days; prostaglandin E1 to keep ductus open; balloon atrial septostomy.\n- **Tricuspid atresia, truncus arteriosus, total anomalous pulmonary venous return:** other cyanotic congenital lesions; in each, oxygenated and deoxygenated blood mix or flow abnormally, causing cyanosis.\n\n**Heart failure in infants**: feeding difficulty, sweating, tachypnoea, hepatomegaly; treat with diuretics, ACE inhibitors.\n- **Pearl:** Tetralogy of Fallot tet spells are relieved by squatting (increases systemic vascular resistance and reduces right-to-left shunt).\n- **Pearl:** PDA is the most common congenital heart disease in preterm infants.\n- **Pearl:** Eisenmenger syndrome is pulmonary hypertension with reversal of shunt; cyanosis; surgery contraindicated.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **TOF Boot-Shaped Heart (4 defects):** VSD + Infundibular Pulmonic Stenosis + Overriding Aorta + RVH. Tet spell DOC = Knee-Chest + Morphine.\n\n### 7.7 Common paediatric conditions\n\n- **Febrile seizures:** age 6 months to 5 years; simple (generalised, brief) vs complex; no long-term neurological damage; prophylaxis with antipyretics.\n- **Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis):** parainfluenza; barking cough, stridor; steeple sign; dexamethasone.\n- **Epiglottitis:** Haemophilus influenzae type b (now rare); thumbprint sign on lateral X-ray; drooling, tripod posture; airway emergency.\n- **Intussusception:** 6 months to 2 years; colicky pain, redcurrant jelly stool, sausage-shaped mass; ultrasound; air or hydrostatic reduction.\n- **Pyloric stenosis:** first born males, 3-6 weeks; projectile vomiting; olive-shaped mass; hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis; Ramstedt pyloromyotomy.\n- **Hirschsprung disease:** absence of ganglion cells in myenteric and submucosal plexus; failure to pass meconium; rectal suction biopsy (acetylcholinesterase staining); pull-through surgery.\n- **Nephrotic syndrome:** minimal change disease commonest in children; steroids; proteinuria above 40 mg/m2/h; oedema, hypoalbuminaemia, hyperlipidaemia.\n- **Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis:** 1-3 weeks after pharyngitis or skin infection; low C3; haematuria, hypertension.\n- **Kawasaki disease:** fever over 5 days; conjunctival injection, strawberry tongue, cervical lymphadenopathy, rash, hand and foot changes; coronary artery aneurysm; IVIG and aspirin within 10 days.\n- **Henoch-Schonlein purpura (IgA vasculitis):** palpable purpura on legs and buttocks; arthralgia; abdominal pain; IgA nephropathy.\n- **Bronchiolitis:** RSV; infants; wheeze, crepitations; supportive care, oxygen if needed.\n- **Asthma:** step-wise management; inhaled corticosteroids; salbutamol for relief.\n- **Pearl:** Kawasaki disease must be treated with IVIG within 10 days to prevent coronary aneurysm.\n- **Pearl:** Hirschsprung disease is confirmed by rectal suction biopsy (no ganglion cells; increased acetylcholinesterase).\n\n#### Table: Pediatric Oncology: Wilms Tumor vs Neuroblastoma\n\n| Clinical / Pathological Feature | Wilms Tumor (Nephroblastoma) | Neuroblastoma |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Organ of Origin | Intrarenal (Metanephric blastema) | Adrenal medulla (50%) or Paravertebral sympathetic ganglia |\n| Peak Age | 2 to 4 years | < 2 years (most common solid tumor in infancy) |\n| Physical Mass Characteristics | Smooth, firm, painless; DOES NOT CROSS MIDLINE | Hard, nodular, irregular; FREQUENTLY CROSSES MIDLINE |\n| Vascular Relations | Displaces vessels without encasement ('Claw Sign') | Surrounds and encases aorta and renal vessels |\n| Radiology (CT/X-ray) | Speckled calcifications RARE (< 10%) | FINE SPECKLED CALCIFICATIONS COMMON (> 85-90%) |\n| Biomarkers | Microscopic hematuria, normal urinary catechols | ELEVATED Urinary Catecholamines: VMA and HVA |\n| Associated Syndromes & Genetics | WT1 (11p13, WAGR / Denys-Drash), WT2 (11p15, BWS) | N-myc (MYCN) amplification; Opsoclonus-Myoclonus ataxia |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Airway Distinction: Croup** = Parainfluenza, Barking cough, Steeple sign on AP neck -> Dexamethasone + Racemic Epi. Epiglottitis = Hib, Tripod, Thumbprint sign -> Intubate in OR!\n\n### 7.9 Paediatric pharmacology notes\n\n- **Dose by weight:** Clark rule (weight in lb / 150 x adult dose); Young rule (age / (age + 12) x adult dose); Fried rule (infants, age in months / 150).\n- **Gentamicin:** avoid in neonates unless needed (ototoxicity); monitor levels.\n- **Chloramphenicol:** avoid in neonates (grey baby syndrome).\n- **Tetracycline:** avoid in children below 8 years (teeth staining, bone growth).\n- **Aspirin:** avoid in children with viral illness (Reye syndrome).\n- **Pearl:** Reye syndrome is linked to aspirin in viral infections; liver failure and encephalopathy.\n- **Pearl:** grey baby syndrome is chloramphenicol toxicity (lack of glucuronidation).\n\n### 7.10 Paediatric quick pearls\n\n- Pyloric stenosis: projectile vomiting, olive mass, hypochloraemic alkalosis.\n- Intussusception: redcurrant jelly stool, sausage mass.\n- Hirschsprung: no meconium passed, rectal biopsy shows no ganglion cells.\n- Kawasaki: IVIG and aspirin within 10 days.\n- Cystic fibrosis: sweat chloride above 60 mEq/L.\n\n## 23. Paediatrics (additions)\n\n- **Paediatric airway emergencies:**\n  - *Foreign-body aspiration with unilateral air trapping:* expiratory X-ray shows air trapping on the affected side (mediastinal shift away on expiration); bronchoscopic removal.\n  - *Viral croup:* parainfluenza; barking cough; stridor; steeple sign on X-ray; dexamethasone.\n  - *Unresponsive child with choking:* begin CPR with chest compressions (not back blows alone).\n  - *Pearl:* unilateral air trapping on expiration is the key sign of a foreign body in a bronchus.\n- **Congenital and perinatal infections:**\n  - *Congenital rubella:* cataract, deafness, and patent ductus arteriosus (classic triad); blueberry muffin rash.\n  - *Congenital CMV:* thrombocytopenia (purpura), hepatosplenomegaly, periventricular calcification; microcephaly; sensorineural hearing loss.\n  - *High-risk HIV-exposed neonate:* multidrug postnatal prophylaxis after late maternal ART (zidovudine plus nevirapine or other combinations; longer duration).\n  - *Pearl:* periventricular calcification with thrombocytopenia suggests congenital CMV.\n- **Vaccine-preventable infections:**\n  - *Measles:* Koplik spots and cephalocaudal rash; SSPE as a late neurological complication (years later; measles virus in brain).\n  - *Pertussis in a young infant:* paroxysmal cough, apnoea, lymphocytosis; azithromycin; hospitalisation if young.\n  - *Pearl:* SSPE is a late complication of measles, typically 5-10 years after infection.\n- **Paediatric renal disorders:** nephrotic syndrome (see 7.7); post-streptococcal GN (see 7.7); haemolytic uraemic syndrome (EHEC; microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia, AKI).\n- **Diabetes and metabolic emergencies in children:** DKA (see Medicine); hypoglycaemia (glucagon, dextrose); inborn errors (urea cycle, organic acidaemias with hyperammonaemia; treat with ammonia scavengers).\n- **Childhood vasculitis:** Kawasaki disease (see 7.7); IgA vasculitis; polyarteritis nodosa (hepatitis B).\n- **Congenital heart disease:** see 7.3 (ASD, VSD, PDA, coarctation, TOF, TGA).\n","CLIN_PHARM_01":"## 5. Pharmacology (15-20 questions)\n\n### 5.0 General pharmacology: key terms\n\n- **Pharmacokinetics (what the body does to the drug):**\n  - *Absorption:* oral bioavailability (fraction reaching systemic circulation; IV = 1). First-pass metabolism in the liver reduces bioavailability (e.g., propranolol, lidocaine, nitrates).\n  - *Distribution:* volume of distribution (Vd); high Vd means the drug leaves the plasma (into tissues). Loading dose = target concentration x Vd / bioavailability.\n  - *Metabolism:* phase I (CYP450 oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis) and phase II (conjugation: glucuronidation, acetylation, sulfation).\n  - *Excretion:* renal (glomerular filtration, tubular secretion, reabsorption). Clearance = rate of elimination / plasma concentration.\n  - *Half-life:* time for plasma concentration to halve; about 4-5 half-lives to reach steady state; about 5 half-lives to eliminate the drug.\n- **Zero-order vs first-order kinetics:** zero-order (constant amount eliminated per time, e.g., phenytoin, alcohol, aspirin at high doses); first-order (constant fraction eliminated per time, most drugs).\n- **Pharmacodynamics (what the drug does to the body):**\n  - *Agonist:* activates a receptor. *Antagonist:* blocks it. *Partial agonist:* activates to a lower maximum (buprenorphine, pindolol).\n  - *Efficacy:* maximum effect. *Potency:* dose needed for a given effect.\n  - *Therapeutic index:* ratio of toxic to therapeutic dose; narrow (lithium, digoxin, warfarin, theophylline, phenytoin, aminoglycosides).\n  - *Competitive antagonism:* shifts the dose-response curve to the right (parallel shift, same maximum). *Non-competitive:* reduces maximum.\n- **Enzyme induction vs inhibition:** inducers (rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, St John wort) increase metabolism; inhibitors (macrolides, azoles, cimetidine, ciprofloxacin, grapefruit, ritonavir) decrease it.\n- **Pearl:** rifampicin reduces efficacy of oral contraceptives, warfarin, and many antiretrovirals; cimetidine and erythromycin raise levels of theophylline and warfarin.\n\n#### Table: First-Order vs Zero-Order Elimination Kinetics\n\n| Parameter | First-Order (Linear) | Zero-Order (Non-linear / Saturation) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Rate of Elimination | Directly proportional to drug concentration | Constant amount eliminated per unit time (independent of concentration) |\n| Fraction cleared / time | Constant fraction (e.g., 50% per t1/2) | Decreases as concentration increases |\n| Half-Life (t1/2) | CONSTANT (independent of dose) | INCREASES with dose (prolonged at high doses) |\n| Clearance | Constant | Decreases with higher concentrations |\n| Classic Drug Examples | 95% of drugs (e.g., Penicillins, Beta-blockers) | PEA: Phenytoin, Ethanol, Aspirin (toxic doses), Theophylline |\n\n#### Table: Phase I vs Phase II Drug Metabolism\n\n| Category | Key Reactions | Enzymes | Clinical Exam Pearls |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Phase I (Functionalization) | Oxidation, Reduction, Hydrolysis | Cytochrome P450 microsomal enzymes | Creates reactive polar groups (-OH, -NH2). Impaired in elderly & cirrhosis. |\n| Phase II (Conjugation) | Glucuronidation, Acetylation, Sulfation, Glutathione | UGT, NAT2, GST | Yields water-soluble inactive conjugates. Glucuronidation is preserved in liver failure ('LOT' benzodiazepines: Lorazepam, Oxazepam, Temazepam). |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **CYP450 Inducers:** 'Rifampicin Can Cause Severe Problems Going Ahead' (Rifampicin, Carbamazepine, Chronic alcohol, St. John's wort, Phenobarbital, Griseofulvin, p-Phenytoin).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Dialyzable Drugs (Low Vd):** 'SLIME' (Salicylates, Lithium, Isopropanol, Methanol, Ethylene glycol).\n\n### 5.10 Drugs in special populations\n\n**Pregnancy (teratogens)**\n- **Drugs to avoid:** ACE inhibitors and ARBs (second and third trimester); warfarin (embryopathy); valproate (neural tube defects); isotretinoin (craniofacial, cardiac); thalidomide (phocomelia); methotrexate; tetracyclines (teeth and bones); carbimazole (aplasia cutis); misoprostol; statins; lithium (cardiac, controversial).\n- **Safe-ish:** paracetamol, insulin, labetalol, methyldopa, penicillins, cephalosporins, most antiemetics (doxylamine-pyridoxine), enoxaparin.\n\n**Breastfeeding**\n- **Compatible:** most antibiotics (except tetracycline and fluoroquinolones with caution), paracetamol, ibuprofen, most antidepressants (sertraline, paroxetine), enalapril, captopril.\n- **Avoid:** lithium (caution), ergotamine, iodine (radioiodine), amiodarone (caution), cytotoxic drugs, codeine (infant respiratory depression), benzodiazepines in large doses.\n\n**Renal and hepatic impairment**\n- Drugs needing dose adjustment: aminoglycosides, vancomycin, lithium, digoxin, LMWH, metformin (avoid if eGFR below 30), DOACs, gabapentin, morphine (avoid in renal failure).\n- Drugs needing caution in liver disease: paracetamol, isoniazid, valproate, statins, ketoconazole, many antiretrovirals.\n\n**Elderly**\n- Beers criteria: avoid anticholinergics (TCAs, antihistamines), benzodiazepines, long-acting sulfonylureas, NSAIDs in renal failure, PPIs beyond 8 weeks without reason.\n\n### 5.11 Drug interactions: quick reference\n\n| Interaction | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| Warfarin + antibiotics (metronidazole, fluconazole, erythromycin) | INR rises (bleeding risk) |\n| Rifampicin + oral contraceptives | Contraceptive failure |\n| Theophylline + ciprofloxacin | Theophylline toxicity |\n| Lithium + thiazide, ACE-I, NSAID | Lithium toxicity |\n| MAOI + SSRI/pethidine/tramadol | Serotonin syndrome |\n| Digoxin + amiodarone | Digoxin toxicity |\n| Clopidogrel + omeprazole | Reduced antiplatelet effect |\n| Methotrexate + trimethoprim/NSAID | Myelosuppression |\n| Allopurinol + 6-mercaptopurine/azathioprine | Myelotoxicity (reduce dose to one-third to one-quarter) |\n| Carbamazepine + valproate | Hepatotoxicity; carbamazepine level changes |\n| Lamotrigine + valproate | Lamotrigine level doubles (halve dose) |\n| Ciprofloxacin + antacids | Reduced absorption |\n| Metronidazole + alcohol | Disulfiram-like reaction |\n| Sildenafil + nitrates | Severe hypotension |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **CYP450 Inhibitors:** 'SICKFACES.COM' (Sodium valproate, Isoniazid, Cimetidine, Ketoconazole, Fluconazole, Alcohol acute, Chloramphenicol, Erythromycin, Sulfonamides, Ciprofloxacin, Omeprazole, Metronidazole).\n\n### 5.12 Pharmacology pearls\n\n- **N-acetylcysteine (NAC):** replenishes glutathione so that the toxic metabolite NAPQI is detoxified; give early in paracetamol overdose.\n- **Magnesium sulfate:** torsades de pointes and eclampsia.\n\n## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.9 Short-subject quick pearls\n\n- Clozapine: neutrophil monitoring.\n- Lithium: narrow therapeutic index.\n\n## 18. Pharmacology (additions)\n\n- **Antiemetics and prokinetics:**\n  - *Metoclopramide:* D2 antagonist; prokinetic; acute dystonia and extrapyramidal side effects; hyperprolactinaemia.\n  - *Aprepitant:* NK1 receptor antagonist; for chemotherapy-induced nausea.\n  - *Pearl:* metoclopramide causes acute dystonia; treat with benztropine or diphenhydramine.\n- **General pharmacokinetics and prescribing:**\n  - *Loading and maintenance dose:* loading dose = Vd x target concentration / bioavailability; maintenance dose = clearance x target concentration / bioavailability; steady state after about 4-5 half-lives.\n  - *Rational alprazolam prescribing:* benzodiazepine; short-term use only; dependence risk; avoid in elderly.\n  - *Pearl:* steady state occurs after about 4-5 half-lives regardless of dose.\n- **Pituitary drugs:**\n  - *Acromegaly therapy:* octreotide (somatostatin analogue) and pegvisomant (GH receptor antagonist); surgery is first line.\n  - *Bromocriptine for prolactinoma when surgery declined:* D2 agonist; reduces prolactin and shrinks tumour.\n  - *Pearl:* pegvisomant blocks the GH receptor, so GH rises while IGF-1 falls; monitor IGF-1 to judge control.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Receptor Types & G-Protein Second Messenger Cascades\n\n| G-Protein Subunit | Effector Enzyme | Second Messenger | Receptors (Mnemonic) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Gs (Stimulatory) | Activates Adenylyl Cyclase | Increased cAMP -> PKA activation | Beta-1, Beta-2, Beta-3, D1, V2, H2 |\n| Gi (Inhibitory) | Inhibits Adenylyl Cyclase | Decreased cAMP; opens cardiac K+ channels | M2, Alpha-2, D2 ('MAD 2s') |\n| Gq (PLC Pathway) | Activates Phospholipase C | Cleaves PIP2 -> IP3 (Ca2+ release) + DAG (PKC) | H1, Alpha-1, V1, M1, M3 ('HAVe 1 M&M') |\n","CLIN_PHARM_02":"## 5. Pharmacology (15-20 questions)\n\n### 5.4 Autonomic pharmacology\n\n**Cholinergic system**\n- **Muscarinic agonists:** pilocarpine (glaucoma, dry mouth); bethanechol (urinary retention); cevimeline (dry mouth).\n- **Anticholinesterases:** neostigmine, pyridostigmine (myasthenia gravis; reversal of non-depolarising blockade with atropine); physostigmine (anticholinergic toxicity, crosses BBB); donepezil (Alzheimer disease).\n- **Muscarinic antagonists (atropine, glycopyrrolate, hyoscine, ipratropium, tiotropium):** dry mouth, mydriasis, tachycardia, urinary retention, constipation, delirium. Atropine: bradycardia, organophosphate poisoning.\n- **Pearl:** organophosphate poisoning: give atropine (muscarinic) and pralidoxime (reactivates AChE if given within 24-48 hours before ageing).\n\n**Adrenergic system**\n- **Alpha-1 agonists (phenylephrine):** vasoconstriction; pupil dilation; hypotension.\n- **Alpha-2 agonists (clonidine, dexmedetomidine):** sedation; reduce noradrenaline release; bradycardia.\n- **Beta-1 agonists (dobutamine):** increase cardiac contractility (inotropy); risk of arrhythmias.\n- **Beta-2 agonists (salbutamol, salmeterol, formoterol):** bronchodilation; tremor, tachycardia; hypokalaemia.\n- **Mixed (adrenaline, noradrenaline):** anaphylaxis (adrenaline IM 0.5 mg first-line), septic shock (noradrenaline).\n- **Dopamine:** dose-dependent: low (renal), intermediate (beta-1 inotropic), high (alpha vasoconstriction).\n- **Pearl:** adrenaline is first-line for anaphylaxis (IM into the thigh); corticosteroids and antihistamines are adjuncts, not replacements.\n\n**Neuromuscular blockers**\n- **Depolarising (succinylcholine):** activates AChR then desensitises; fasciculations; hyperkalaemia (burns, crush, denervation, prolonged immobilisation); malignant hyperthermia trigger; plasma cholinesterase deficiency prolongs effect.\n- **Non-depolarising (rocuronium, vecuronium, atracurium, cisatracurium, pancuronium):** competitive AChR antagonists; reversed by anticholinesterases (neostigmine) or sugammadex (rocuronium, vecuronium).\n- **Malignant hyperthermia:** inherited ryanodine receptor (RYR1) defect; triggered by volatile anaesthetics and succinylcholine; hyperthermia, rigidity, hypercapnia, hyperkalaemia; dantrolene is the antidote.\n- **Pearl:** succinylcholine causes hyperkalaemia; avoid in burns and denervation, and in patients with prolonged immobilisation.\n\n**Local anaesthetics**\n- **Mechanism:** block voltage-gated sodium channels from inside the cell; uncharged form crosses membranes.\n- **Classes:** esters (procaine, chloroprocaine, cocaine: metabolised by plasma cholinesterase; allergy via PABA); amides (lidocaine, bupivacaine, ropivacaine, levobupivacaine: liver metabolism).\n- **Toxicity:** CNS (perioral numbness, tinnitus, seizures) then cardiovascular (arrhythmia, cardiac arrest); bupivacaine is most cardiotoxic (avoid high doses; lipid rescue).\n- **Pearl:** lipid rescue (intravenous 20% lipid emulsion) for local anaesthetic systemic toxicity, especially with bupivacaine.\n\n#### Table: Adrenergic Receptors: Organ Distribution & Actions\n\n| Receptor | G-Protein | Key Anatomic Sites | Physiological / Pharmacological Effects |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Alpha-1 | Gq | Vascular smooth muscle, radial pupillary muscle, bladder internal sphincter | Vasoconstriction (increased BP), mydriasis, urinary retention / sphincter contraction |\n| Alpha-2 | Gi | Presynaptic adrenergic nerve terminals, pancreatic beta-cells, CNS | Inhibits NE release (autoreceptor), reduces sympathetic outflow (Clonidine, Methyldopa) |\n| Beta-1 | Gs | Heart (SA node, AV node, myocardium), Juxtaglomerular cells | Increased inotropy, chronotropy, dromotropy; stimulates renin secretion |\n| Beta-2 | Gs | Bronchial smooth muscle, vascular smooth muscle (skeletal), uterus, liver | Bronchodilation (Salbutamol), vasodilation, uterine relaxation (Tocolysis), glycogenolysis |\n| Beta-3 | Gs | Adipose tissue, detrusor muscle of urinary bladder | Lipolysis; detrusor relaxation for overactive bladder (Mirabegron) |\n\n#### Table: Cholinergic Antidotes & Toxicology\n\n| Condition | Tox Syndrome / Pathophysiology | Antidote of Choice | Endpoint of Therapy |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Organophosphate (OP) Poisoning | Irreversible AChE inhibition -> DUMBELS (diarrhea, urination, miosis, bronchospasm, emesis, lacrimation, salivation) | Atropine IV (blocks muscarinic storm) + Pralidoxime (oxime reactivates AChE before aging) | Clearing of bronchial secretions, dry axillae, HR > 80 bpm |\n| Atropine / Anticholinergic Poisoning | 'Blind as a bat, dry as a bone, red as a beet, mad as a hatter, hot as a hare' | Physostigmine (tertiary amine, crosses BBB to reverse central & peripheral toxicity) | Reversal of central delirium and severe tachycardia |\n| Anaphylactic Shock | IgE-mediated massive histamine, LTC4, LTD4 degranulation | Adrenaline 1:1000 (0.5 mg) IM into anterolateral thigh | Reversal of bronchospasm, laryngeal edema, and hypotension |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Dale's Vasomotor Reversal:** Adrenaline pressor effect is converted to a depressor effect only after Alpha-blocker pre-treatment (unmasking Beta-2 vasodilation).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Cardioselective Beta-Blockers (Beta-1 selective):** 'A BEAM of Beta-1' (Atenolol, Betaxolol, Esmolol, Acebutolol, Metoprolol).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Beta-Blockers with Vasodilating Properties:** Carvedilol & Labetalol (Alpha-1 blockade); Nebivolol (Nitric oxide release); Celiprolol (Beta-2 agonism).\n\n### 5.12 Pharmacology pearls\n\n- **Atropine:** antidote for organophosphates and bradycardia.\n- **Pralidoxime:** reactivates acetylcholinesterase in organophosphate poisoning.\n- **Desferrioxamine (deferoxamine):** iron chelator used in acute iron poisoning and chronic iron overload.\n- **EDTA (calcium disodium edetate):** lead poisoning in children (with dimercaprol).\n- **Fomepizole:** methanol and ethylene glycol poisoning.\n- **Hydroxocobalamin, or sodium nitrite plus thiosulphate:** antidotes for cyanide poisoning; hydroxocobalamin binds cyanide directly, while nitrite and thiosulphate form thiocyanate.\n- **Calcium gluconate:** hyperkalaemia and magnesium toxicity.\n- **Digoxin Fab:** antibody fragments that bind digoxin and reverse life-threatening digoxin toxicity.\n- **Physostigmine:** anticholinergic toxicity (atropine poisoning).\n\n## 18. Pharmacology (additions)\n\n- **Antiplatelet, thrombolytic and haemostatic drugs:**\n  - *Epsilon-aminocaproic acid:* antifibrinolytic; blocks plasminogen activation; used in bleeding after surgery.\n  - *Tissue plasminogen activator:* converts plasminogen to plasmin; fibrin-specific; used in acute MI and stroke.\n  - *Low-dose aspirin:* irreversible COX-1 inhibition in platelets (thromboxane A2 blocked); reduces bleeding risk at low doses.\n  - *Pearl:* aspirin inhibits platelet COX-1 irreversibly for the life of the platelet (7-10 days).\n- **Autonomic and ocular drugs:**\n  - *Atropine toxicity in organophosphate treatment:* atropine used to counter OP poisoning; hyperthermia is a sign of atropine toxicity.\n  - *Botulinum toxin:* blocks acetylcholine release at presynaptic terminal (SNAP-25 cleavage); flaccid paralysis; used for dystonia, spasticity, cosmetic use.\n  - *POAG treatment in bronchial asthma:* beta-blockers (timolol) cause bronchospasm; avoid in asthma; use prostaglandin analogues or carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.\n  - *Pearl:* topical timolol can cause bronchospasm; choose latanoprost in asthmatics.\n- **Reproductive and genitourinary drugs:**\n  - *Carboprost contraindication in asthma:* PGF2 alpha causes bronchospasm; avoid.\n  - *Pharmacotherapy for stress urinary incontinence:* duloxetine (SNRI) is used in some countries; pelvic floor physiotherapy and surgery are first line.\n  - *Pearl:* carboprost (PGF2 alpha) is used for postpartum haemorrhage but causes bronchospasm.\n- **Respiratory pharmacology and pulmonary toxicity:** amiodarone pulmonary toxicity (interstitial pneumonitis, fibrosis); bleomycin (pneumonitis); methotrexate (pneumonitis); nitrofurantoin (pulmonary fibrosis); ICS (thrush, hoarseness).\n- **Substance-use pharmacotherapy:** naltrexone (opioid antagonist); buprenorphine; methadone; varenicline (nicotinic partial agonist); acamprosate (alcohol craving); disulfiram (aversion).\n  - *Pearl:* naltrexone is contraindicated in patients on opioids.\n","CLIN_PHARM_03":"## 5. Pharmacology (15-20 questions)\n\n### 5.2 Cardiovascular drugs\n\n**Antihypertensives**\n- **ACE inhibitors (enalapril, ramipril):** inhibit ACE; adverse effects: dry cough (bradykinin), angioedema, hyperkalaemia, renal impairment (bilateral renal artery stenosis); teratogenic in second and third trimesters.\n- **ARBs (losartan, telmisartan):** block AT1 receptor; no cough; same contraindications.\n- **Calcium channel blockers:** dihydropyridines (amlodipine, nifedipine): vasodilation, reflex tachycardia, oedema; non-dihydropyridines (verapamil, diltiazem): cardiac effects (AV block, bradycardia); verapamil constipation.\n- **Thiazides (hydrochlorothiazide, chlorthalidone):** inhibit NaCl transporter in distal convoluted tubule; hypokalaemia, hyperuricaemia, hyperglycaemia, hypercalcaemia.\n- **Beta-blockers:** atenolol, metoprolol (beta-1 selective); propranolol (non-selective); carvedilol (also alpha-1); avoid in asthma (non-selective) and in heart block.\n- **Alpha-1 blockers (prazosin, doxazosin):** first-dose hypotension; BPH.\n- **Central agents:** clonidine (alpha-2 agonist; rebound hypertension on withdrawal), methyldopa (pregnancy).\n- **Direct vasodilators:** hydralazine (reflex tachycardia; lupus-like syndrome), nitroprusside (cyanide toxicity; use thiosulphate).\n- **Pearl:** clonidine withdrawal causes rebound hypertension; treat with restarting the drug, not beta-blockers alone.\n\n**Antiarrhythmics (Vaughan Williams)**\n- **Class I (sodium channel blockers):** Ia (quinidine, procainamide: prolong QT; SLE with procainamide); Ib (lidocaine, mexiletine: shorten QT; ischaemic arrhythmias); Ic (flecainide, propafenone: no QT change; avoid in structural heart disease).\n- **Class II (beta-blockers):** reduce sympathetic tone; post-MI protection.\n- **Class III (potassium channel blockers):** amiodarone (thyroid dysfunction, pulmonary fibrosis, hepatotoxicity, corneal deposits, blue-grey skin); sotalol; dofetilide; prolong action potential; QT prolongation and torsades de pointes.\n- **Class IV (calcium channel blockers):** verapamil, diltiazem; avoid in WPW with AF.\n- **Other:** adenosine (AV nodal; brief asystole; flushing), digoxin (Na+/K+ ATPase inhibition, vagal stimulation; toxicity with hypokalaemia), magnesium (torsades).\n- **Pearl:** torsades de pointes treatment: IV magnesium sulfate (not antiarrhythmics that prolong QT).\n\n**Digoxin**\n- **Mechanism:** Na+/K+ ATPase inhibition raises intracellular sodium, reduces sodium-calcium exchange, raises intracellular calcium (positive inotropy). Vagomimetic effect slows AV conduction.\n- **Indications:** AF rate control, heart failure with reduced EF (symptoms, not survival).\n- **Toxicity:** GI symptoms (nausea, anorexia), visual disturbances (yellow-green halos), arrhythmias (bigeminy, AV block, VT, VF); precipitated by hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hypercalcaemia, renal impairment, amiodarone.\n- **Treatment:** stop digoxin; correct electrolytes; digoxin-specific Fab fragments; atropine for bradycardia; pacing; lidocaine or phenytoin for ventricular arrhythmias.\n- **Pearl:** digoxin toxicity causes hyperkalaemia in severe cases; check potassium before giving Fab.\n\n**Anticoagulants**\n- **Unfractionated heparin:** binds antithrombin; monitor aPTT; protamine reverses; HIT (heparin-induced thrombocytopenia: platelet fall with thrombosis; antibodies against platelet factor 4; stop heparin, use argatroban or fondaparinux).\n- **LMWH (enoxaparin, dalteparin):** anti-Xa activity; less HIT; renal clearance; anti-Xa levels for monitoring; protamine partially reverses.\n- **Warfarin:** inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase; reduces factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S; monitor INR (target 2-3 for most indications, 2.5-3.5 for mechanical valves); reversal with vitamin K (slow), prothrombin complex concentrate (fast), FFP. Teratogenic (embryopathy in first trimester; fetal haemorrhage in third).\n- **DOACs (rivaroxaban, apixaban: factor Xa; dabigatran: thrombin):** fixed doses; no routine monitoring; reversal with andexanet alfa (Xa) or idarucizumab (dabigatran). Avoid in mechanical valves and severe renal impairment (dose adjust).\n- **Pearl:** warfarin is preferred over DOACs in mechanical heart valves and in antiphospholipid syndrome.\n\n**Antiplatelets**\n- **Aspirin:** irreversible COX-1 inhibition (thromboxane A2); low dose (75-100 mg) for prevention; GI bleeding, Reye syndrome in children (avoid in viral illness), tinnitus at high dose.\n- **Clopidogrel, prasugrel, ticagrelor:** irreversible P2Y12 inhibitors (ADP receptor); prasugrel avoid in stroke and over 75; ticagrelor dyspnoea; clopidogrel is a prodrug (CYP2C19; omeprazole reduces activation).\n- **GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors (abciximab, eptifibatide, tirofiban):** block fibrinogen binding; used in PCI.\n- **Dipyridamole:** PDE3 inhibitor; combined with aspirin for stroke prevention.\n- **Pearl:** clopidogrel is activated by CYP2C19; PPIs (omeprazole) may reduce its effect; pantoprazole is preferred.\n\n**Heart failure drugs**\n- **ACE-I/ARB/ARNI:** neurohormonal blockade; sacubitril-valsartan (ARNI) with ACE-I washout of 36 hours.\n- **Beta-blockers:** carvedilol, bisoprolol, metoprolol succinate, nebivolol; start low.\n- **MRA (spironolactone, eplerenone):** aldosterone antagonist; hyperkalaemia, gynaecomastia (spironolactone).\n- **SGLT2 inhibitors (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin):** reduce hospitalisation and CV death; genital infection, euglycaemic ketoacidosis.\n- **Loop diuretics (furosemide):** symptom relief; hypokalaemia, ototoxicity, dehydration.\n- **Hydralazine with nitrate:** for patients who cannot take ACE-I or ARB.\n- **Ivabradine:** reduces heart rate in sinus rhythm (funny channel).\n- **Pearl:** an SGLT2 inhibitor can cause euglycaemic diabetic ketoacidosis; hold before surgery.\n\n**Lipid-lowering**\n- **Statins (atorvastatin, rosuvastatin):** HMG-CoA reductase inhibition; myopathy (rhabdomyolysis; check CK), hepatotoxicity (ALT), new diabetes.\n- **Ezetimibe:** blocks cholesterol absorption (NPC1L1).\n- **PCSK9 inhibitors (evolocumab, alirocumab):** monoclonal antibodies that block PCSK9, raising LDL receptor recycling and lowering LDL cholesterol.\n- **Fibrates (fenofibrate):** PPAR-alpha; triglycerides; myopathy with statins.\n- **Pearl:** the combination of statin with fibrate increases the risk of rhabdomyolysis; gemfibrozil is particularly risky.\n\n#### Table: Heart Failure GDMT - The 4 Mortality-Reducing Pillars\n\n| Pillar Class | Representative Agents | Molecular Target | Mortality Benefit & Clinical Pearls |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. ARNI / ACEi / ARB | Sacubitril-Valsartan (ARNI), Enalapril, Ramipril | Inhibits Neprilysin (increases ANP/BNP) + Blocks AT1 receptor | Cornerstone of GDMT; 36-hour washout period required when switching from ACEi to ARNI to prevent angioedema. |\n| 2. Evidence-Based Beta-Blockers | Bisoprolol, Carvedilol, Metoprolol succinate (XL) | Blocks chronic toxic sympathetic stimulation | Only these THREE beta-blockers have proven mortality benefit in HFrEF. Start low and go slow. |\n| 3. Mineralocorticoid Antagonists | Spironolactone, Eplerenone | Blocks Aldosterone receptor in collecting tubules | Decreases cardiac fibrosis. Eplerenone does not cause painful gynecomastia. |\n| 4. SGLT2 Inhibitors | Dapagliflozin, Empagliflozin | Inhibits proximal tubular SGLT2 cotransporter | Reduces cardiovascular mortality and HF hospitalizations regardless of diabetes status. |\n\n#### Table: Diuretics: Site of Action & Electrolyte Patterns\n\n| Class | Site of Action | Transporter Blocked | Electrolyte Changes (Must-Know) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitor (Acetazolamide) | Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT) | Carbonic Anhydrase | Hyperchloremic normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, alkaline urine, hypokalemia. |\n| Loop Diuretic (Furosemide, Torsemide) | Thick Ascending Limb of Henle (TAL) | Na+/K+/2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC2) | HYPOCALCEMIA, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis, ototoxicity. |\n| Thiazide Diuretic (HCTZ, Chlorthalidone) | Distal Convoluted Tubule (DCT) | Na+/Cl- cotransporter (NCCT) | HYPERCALCEMIA, hypokalemia, hyperuricemia (gout), hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia. |\n| Potassium-Sparing (Spironolactone, Amiloride) | Cortical Collecting Tubule (CCT) | Aldosterone receptor / ENaC channel | HYPERKALEMIA, normal anion gap metabolic acidosis. |\n\n#### Table: Specific Anticoagulant Reversal Agents\n\n| Anticoagulant | Target | Specific Reversal Agent / Antidote | Mechanism of Action |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Dabigatran | Direct Thrombin (Factor IIa) | IDARUCIZUMAB (Praxbind) | Humanized Fab fragment that binds dabigatran with 350x higher affinity than thrombin. |\n| Rivaroxaban, Apixaban, Edoxaban | Direct Factor Xa | ANDEXANET ALFA | Recombinant modified human Factor Xa decoy protein. |\n| Unfractionated Heparin | Antithrombin III | PROTAMINE SULFATE | Positively charged alkaline protein that neutralizes acidic heparin. |\n| Warfarin | VKORC1 (Factors II, VII, IX, X) | 4-Factor PCC + IV Vitamin K | Restores functional clotting factors immediately. |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Loop vs Thiazide Calcium Rule:** 'Loops LOSE calcium (Hypocalcemia); Thiazides TAKE-IN calcium (Hypercalcemia)'.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Antiarrhythmics Vaughan Williams:** 'No Bad Boy Keeps Clean' (Class I: Na+ channel, Class II: Beta-blockers, Class III: K+ channel, Class IV: Ca2+ channel).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Safe Antihypertensives in Pregnancy:** 'Better Not Lie Mother' (Beta-blockers [Labetalol], Nifedipine, hydraLazine, Methyldopa). Strictly avoid ACEi/ARBs!\n\n### 5.12 Pharmacology pearls\n\n- **Sugammadex:** rocuronium and vecuronium reversal.\n- **Vitamin K:** warfarin reversal (slow); prothrombin complex concentrate (fast).\n- **Andexanet alfa:** rivaroxaban and apixaban reversal.\n- **Glucagon:** beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose (and hypoglycaemia).\n- **Protamine:** positively charged protein that binds heparin and reverses its anticoagulant effect.\n- **Phytonadione:** vitamin K1, used to reverse warfarin effect and treat vitamin K deficiency bleeding.\n\n## 18. Pharmacology (additions)\n\n- **Antihypertensives and RAAS drugs:**\n  - *Clonidine withdrawal:* rebound hypertension (abrupt stop after prolonged use); taper gradually.\n  - *Losartan:* AT1 blocker; also reduces thromboxane A2 effect (uricosuric).\n  - *Omapatrilat:* dual ACE and neprilysin inhibitor; angioedema risk.\n  - *ACE-inhibitor angioedema:* bradykinin accumulation; switch to ARB rarely; stop ACE inhibitor.\n  - *Antihypertensives in diabetic CKD:* ACE inhibitor or ARB preferred for proteinuria.\n  - *Antihypertensive with oliguria:* avoid drugs that reduce renal perfusion sharply.\n  - *Pearl:* ACE inhibitors and ARBs are contraindicated in pregnancy; they reduce proteinuria in diabetic nephropathy.\n- **Antiarrhythmics and cardiac glycosides:**\n  - *Digoxin:* Na+/K+ ATPase inhibitor; toxicity (yellow vision, nausea, arrhythmias); interactions with amiodarone, verapamil, quinidine (raise levels); hypokalaemia increases toxicity; treat with digoxin immune Fab.\n  - *Amiodarone:* class III; iodine-containing; thyroid dysfunction (hypo or hyper), pulmonary fibrosis, corneal deposits, blue-grey skin; long half-life.\n  - *Pearl:* digoxin toxicity: treat with digoxin immune Fab; hypokalaemia makes toxicity worse.\n- **Diuretics and electrolyte therapy:**\n  - *Thiazides:* reduce urinary calcium excretion; used in idiopathic hypercalciuria; cause hyponatraemia and hyperglycaemia.\n  - *Beta-adrenergic intracellular potassium shift in hyperkalaemia:* beta-2 agonists (salbutamol) move K+ into cells.\n  - *Pearl:* thiazides reduce urinary calcium; loop diuretics increase it.\n- **Anticoagulants:**\n  - *DVT anticoagulation in pregnancy:* LMWH preferred (warfarin is teratogenic in the first trimester).\n  - *Warfarin-induced purple toe syndrome:* cholesterol emboli; skin necrosis; stop warfarin.\n  - *Anticoagulation after cardioembolic stroke with AF:* DOACs or warfarin; avoid early anticoagulation in large infarcts (haemorrhagic transformation).\n  - *Pearl:* warfarin skin necrosis occurs in patients with protein C deficiency (first few days).\n- **Heart-failure and antianginal drugs:** ACE inhibitors and ARBs; beta-blockers (carvedilol, bisoprolol, metoprolol succinate); MRA (spironolactone); SGLT2 inhibitors; nitrates (nitrate-free interval); ivabradine (funny current inhibitor).\n- **Lipid-lowering drugs:** statins (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor; myopathy; CK monitoring); ezetimibe (NPC1L1 inhibitor); fibrates (PPAR-alpha; raise HDL; gallstones); niacin (flushing); PCSK9 inhibitors (evolocumab); bile acid sequestrants (cholestyramine).\n  - *Pearl:* fibrates increase gallstone risk; statins raise CK and cause myopathy.\n","CLIN_PHARM_04":"## 5. Pharmacology (15-20 questions)\n\n### 5.3 CNS drugs\n\n**Antiepileptics**\n- **Sodium valproate:** broad-spectrum; hepatotoxicity; pancreatitis; teratogen (neural tube defects, reduced IQ); weight gain; thrombocytopenia; hyperammonaemia (check); avoid in women of childbearing potential without contraception.\n- **Carbamazepine:** partial and generalised tonic-clonic; HLA-B*15:02 (SJS/TEN, Han Chinese); HLA-A*31:01 (DRESS); enzyme inducer; hyponatraemia (SIADH); aplastic anaemia.\n- **Phenytoin:** zero-order kinetics (toxicity at small dose increases); gingival hyperplasia; hirsutism; nystagmus, ataxia at high level; teratogen (foetal hydantoin syndrome); cardiac arrhythmias with rapid IV infusion.\n- **Lamotrigine:** partial and generalised; SJS (rash; titrate slowly); valproate doubles its levels (halve dose); folate antagonist (weak).\n- **Levetiracetam:** SV2A binding; behavioural effects (irritability, depression); renally cleared.\n- **Gabapentin and pregabalin:** partial seizures; alpha-2-delta subunit; neuropathic pain; oedema, weight gain.\n- **Topiramate:** broad; kidney stones; teratogen (cleft lip); metabolic acidosis; cognitive effects.\n- **Benzodiazepines (diazepam, lorazepam, midazolam):** status epilepticus first-line (GABA-A); respiratory depression; tolerance.\n- **Pearl:** phenytoin toxicity follows zero-order kinetics, so a small dose increase can cause a large rise in levels; for status epilepticus, IV phenytoin should be given slowly (under 50 mg/min) to avoid hypotension and arrhythmias.\n\n**Antidepressants**\n- **SSRIs (fluoxetine, sertraline, escitalopram):** serotonin reuptake inhibition; first-line; GI upset, sexual dysfunction, bleeding risk with NSAIDs and anticoagulants, hyponatraemia (SIADH), serotonin syndrome (with MAOIs, triptans, linezolid, tramadol).\n- **SNRIs (venlafaxine, duloxetine):** serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake; hypertension at high doses; withdrawal.\n- **TCAs (amitriptyline, imipramine):** tricyclic; anticholinergic (dry mouth, urinary retention, constipation, delirium), cardiac conduction (QRS prolongation, arrhythmias), sedation; overdose causes arrhythmia and convulsions (sodium bicarbonate treats QRS widening).\n- **MAOIs (phenelzine, tranylcypromine, selegiline):** irreversible MAO-A and MAO-B inhibition; hypertensive crisis with tyramine (cheese); serotonin syndrome; interacts with SSRIs (avoid), pethidine, sympathomimetics.\n- **Mirtazapine:** alpha-2 antagonist; sedation, weight gain.\n- **Bupropion:** NDRI; seizures risk; used for smoking cessation.\n- **Pearl:** serotonin syndrome: clonus, hyperthermia, agitation, diarrhoea, tachycardia; stop the drugs and give cyproheptadine.\n\n**Antipsychotics**\n- **Typical (haloperidol, chlorpromazine):** D2 blockade in mesolimbic pathway (positive symptoms), nigrostriatal (EPS), tuberoinfundibular (hyperprolactinaemia); extrapyramidal symptoms (acute dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia); NMS (fever, rigidity, autonomic instability, raised CK; dantrolene, bromocriptine, supportive care).\n- **Atypical (risperidone, olanzapine, quetiapine, clozapine, aripiprazole):** serotonin-dopamine antagonism; lower EPS; metabolic effects (weight, glucose, lipids); clozapine: agranulocytosis (monitor neutrophils), seizures, myocarditis, hypersalivation.\n- **Pearl:** clozapine is the drug of choice for treatment-resistant schizophrenia; check neutrophil count weekly for six months, then less often.\n\n**Lithium**\n- **Mechanism:** inhibits inositol monophosphatase and GSK-3 (mood stabilising).\n- **Indications:** bipolar disorder (maintenance and acute mania).\n- **Toxicity:** narrow therapeutic index (0.6-1.0 mmol/L maintenance; toxicity above 1.5); tremor, ataxia, confusion, nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, hypothyroidism, Ebstein anomaly (first trimester), SIADH.\n- **Interactions:** thiazides, ACE inhibitors, NSAIDs raise levels (reduced renal clearance).\n- **Treatment of toxicity:** IV fluids; haemodialysis in severe cases.\n- **Pearl:** lithium toxicity is worsened by thiazides and NSAIDs; hold before procedures and in dehydration.\n\n**Anxiolytics and hypnotics**\n- **Benzodiazepines:** GABA-A positive allosteric modulation; tolerance, dependence, withdrawal (seizures); flumazenil reverses (use with caution: seizures).\n- **Buspirone:** 5-HT1A partial agonist; slow onset; no dependence.\n- **Zolpidem:** non-benzodiazepine hypnotic; complex sleep behaviours.\n- **Pearl:** flumazenil can precipitate seizures in benzodiazepine-dependent patients.\n\n**Parkinson disease drugs**\n- **Levodopa with carbidopa (or benserazide):** levodopa crosses the BBB and is converted to dopamine; carbidopa blocks peripheral decarboxylation; on-off phenomenon; dyskinesias; nausea.\n- **Dopamine agonists (pramipexole, ropinirole, rotigotine):** impulse control disorders; somnolence; less dyskinesia.\n- **MAO-B inhibitors (selegiline, rasagiline):** reduce dopamine breakdown; avoid with pethidine and SSRIs.\n- **COMT inhibitors (entacapone):** prolong levodopa action; diarrhoea, urine discolouration.\n- **Anticholinergics (trihexyphenidyl, benztropine):** tremor-dominant; elderly avoid.\n- **Amantadine:** NMDA antagonist; dyskinesias.\n- **Pearl:** metoclopramide and haloperidol (D2 blockers) worsen Parkinson disease; domperidone is preferred as an antiemetic (does not cross BBB). Ondansetron and cyclizine are safe alternatives.\n\n**Antiepileptic in pregnancy**\n- Lamotrigine and levetiracetam are preferred. Valproate is avoided (teratogenic). Folic acid supplementation (4-5 mg) is advised for all women on antiepileptics.\n\n#### Table: Antipsychotic Extrapyramidal Symptoms (EPS) Timeline & Treatment\n\n| Clinical Syndrome | Typical Onset Window | Clinical Presentation | Treatment of Choice |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. Acute Dystonia | Hours to Days (1-5 days) | Spasm of neck (torticollis), tongue protrusion, oculogyric crisis | Central Anticholinergic (Promethazine IM or Benztropine / Diphenhydramine) |\n| 2. Akathisia | Days to Weeks (5-60 days) | Subjective inner motor restlessness, inability to sit still | Beta-blocker (PROPRANOLOL) is DOC; or Benzodiazepine |\n| 3. Parkinsonism | Weeks to Months (5-90 days) | Bradykinesia, cogwheel rigidity, masked facies, resting tremor | Central Anticholinergic (Trihexyphenidyl / Benztropine). NEVER Levodopa! |\n| 4. Tardive Dyskinesia | Months to Years (Chronic) | Choreoathetoid involuntary movements of tongue/face/mouth | VMAT2 Inhibitors (Valbenazine, Deutetrabenazine); switch to Clozapine |\n| 5. Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) | Anytime (Idiosyncratic) | 'Lead-pipe' rigidity, hyperpyrexia, autonomic instability, high CK | Stop drug stat + DANTROLENE (ryanodine blocker) + Bromocriptine |\n\n#### Table: Antiepileptic Drug (AED) Mechanisms & Indications\n\n| Drug | Primary Molecular Mechanism | First-Line Clinical Indication | High-Yield Toxicity / Pearl |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Sodium Valproate | Blocks Na+ & T-type Ca2+, increases GABA | Broad-spectrum; Myoclonic seizures | Highest teratogenicity (neural tube defects), fatal hepatotoxicity, pancreatitis. |\n| Ethosuximide | Blocks T-type Ca2+ channels in thalamic neurons | Pure Absence Seizures (DOC) | Does NOT treat tonic-clonic seizures. Safe, causes mild GI upset/headache. |\n| Carbamazepine | Prolongs fast inactivation of Na+ channels | Focal Seizures & Trigeminal Neuralgia (DOC) | Aplastic anemia, agranulocytosis, SIADH (hyponatremia), CYP inducer. |\n| Lacosamide | Enhances SLOW inactivation of Na+ channels | Refractory Focal Seizures | PR interval prolongation on ECG; no fast-inactivation blunting. |\n| Levetiracetam | Binds synaptic vesicle protein SV2A | Broad-spectrum; First-line in Pregnancy | Minimal teratogenicity; psychiatric irritability/depression. |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **TCA Overdose Triad:** '3 Cs' -> Coma, Convulsions, Cardiotoxicity (widened QRS > 100 ms). Antidote = IV Sodium Bicarbonate (narrows QRS).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Serotonin Syndrome vs NMS:** Serotonin Syndrome has HYPERREFLEXIA & CLONUS (tremors). NMS has 'LEAD-PIPE' HYPOREFLEXIC RIGIDITY.\n\n### 5.9 Analgesics, NSAIDs and opioids\n\n**NSAIDs**\n- **Mechanism:** COX-1 and COX-2 inhibition; reduce prostaglandins.\n- **Adverse effects:** GI ulcer and bleeding (COX-1 inhibition), renal impairment (afferent arteriole vasoconstriction), platelet dysfunction, bronchospasm in aspirin-sensitive asthma, Reye syndrome in children (aspirin).\n- **COX-2 inhibitors (celecoxib, etoricoxib):** less GI toxicity; increased cardiovascular risk; avoid in severe heart disease.\n- **Paracetamol (acetaminophen):** central COX inhibition; minimal anti-inflammatory; hepatotoxicity in overdose (NAPQI, glutathione depletion). NAC (N-acetylcysteine) within 8 hours is the antidote (up to 24 hours in some cases).\n- **Pearl:** paracetamol toxicity is evaluated by the Rumack-Matthew nomogram (4-hour level).\n\n**Opioids**\n- **Mechanism:** mu-opioid receptor agonism (analgesia, euphoria, respiratory depression, miosis, constipation).\n- **Morphine:** prototype; histamine release; renal metabolite M6G accumulates in renal failure.\n- **Fentanyl:** potent; transdermal; short duration.\n- **Pethidine (meperidine):** serotonergic (serotonin syndrome with MAOIs); shivering; avoid in renal failure.\n- **Tramadol:** partial mu agonist and SNRI; seizure risk; serotonin syndrome.\n- **Methadone:** long-acting; QT prolongation; used for opioid maintenance.\n- **Buprenorphine:** partial mu agonist; high affinity; precipitates withdrawal in heroin-dependent patients if started too early (wait for mild to moderate withdrawal).\n- **Naloxone:** pure antagonist; reverses opioid overdose; short duration (repeat dosing).\n- **Pearl:** partial agonists (buprenorphine) can act as antagonists in the presence of a full agonist, displacing it and precipitating withdrawal.\n\n### 5.12 Pharmacology pearls\n\n- **Flumazenil:** benzodiazepine overdose (caution: seizures).\n- **Dantrolene:** blocks ryanodine receptors and reduces calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum; the antidote for malignant hyperthermia.\n- **Penicillamine and trientine:** Wilson disease (copper chelation).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Malignant Hyperthermia Trigger & DOC:** Triggered by Succinylcholine + Halothane. Ryanodine receptor (RYR1) mutation. DOC = DANTROLENE.\n\n## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.9 Short-subject quick pearls\n\n- Malignant hyperthermia: dantrolene.\n\n## 18. Pharmacology (additions)\n\n- **Analgesics and clinical toxicology:**\n  - *Paracetamol in CKD:* safe at standard doses; NAC for overdose (glutathione replenishment).\n  - *Salicylate poisoning and urinary alkalinisation:* alkaline urine increases salicylate excretion; sodium bicarbonate.\n  - *Naloxone:* opioid antagonist; reversal of respiratory depression; short acting (re-sedation risk).\n  - *Sulfonamide-triggered acute intermittent porphyria:* sulphonamides and barbiturates induce ALA synthase; avoid.\n  - *Pearl:* acute intermittent porphyria is triggered by barbiturates, sulfonamides, and oral contraceptives; give haem arginate.\n- **Neurological and psychiatric drugs:**\n  - *Atomoxetine for ADHD:* selective noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor; non-stimulant; avoid with MAOIs.\n  - *Selegiline as MAO-B inhibitor in Parkinson disease:* prevents dopamine breakdown; interacts with tyramine at higher doses.\n  - *Sumatriptan for acute migraine:* 5-HT1B/1D agonist; contraindicated in ischaemic heart disease, uncontrolled hypertension and with ergots.\n  - *Pearl:* triptans are contraindicated in ischaemic heart disease.\n","CLIN_PHARM_05":"## 5. Pharmacology (15-20 questions)\n\n### 5.1 Antimicrobials (very high yield)\n\n**Mechanism classes**\n- **Cell wall synthesis inhibitors:** beta-lactams (penicillins, cephalosporins, carbapenems, monobactams: transpeptidase inhibition), glycopeptides (vancomycin: D-Ala-D-Ala terminus).\n- **Protein synthesis inhibitors:** aminoglycosides (30S; irreversible), tetracyclines (30S), macrolides and lincosamides (50S), chloramphenicol (50S), linezolid (50S).\n- **Nucleic acid synthesis inhibitors:** fluoroquinolones (gyrase and topoisomerase IV), rifampicin (RNA polymerase), metronidazole (reduced to toxic radicals in anaerobes).\n- **Folate pathway inhibitors:** sulfonamides (inhibit dihydropteroate synthase), trimethoprim (dihydrofolate reductase); co-trimoxazole is both.\n- **Cell membrane:** polymyxins (cationic detergent, Gram negatives), daptomycin.\n\n**Beta-lactams**\n- **Penicillins:** penicillin G (narrow spectrum; syphilis, streptococci, meningococcus); ampicillin, amoxicillin (broader Gram-negatives); cloxacillin, flucloxacillin (penicillinase-resistant, staphylococci); piperacillin-tazobactam (anti-pseudomonal, plus beta-lactamase inhibitor).\n- **Cephalosporins:** first generation (cefazolin, Gram-positives and some Gram-negatives); second (cefuroxime); third (ceftriaxone, cefotaxime: broad, crosses BBB; ceftazidime: pseudomonas); fourth (cefepime); fifth (ceftaroline, MRSA).\n- **Carbapenems:** imipenem (with cilastatin, which inhibits renal dehydropeptidase), meropenem, ertapenem; very broad; reserved for ESBL producers.\n- **Adverse effects:** hypersensitivity (cross-reactivity with cephalosporins is low, about 1-2%); C. difficile colitis; haemolytic anaemia (high-dose penicillin, cephalosporins).\n- **Pearl:** cross-reactivity between penicillins and cephalosporins is low (about 1-2%), and it is higher when the side chains are similar. A history of severe (anaphylactic or SJS/TEN) penicillin reaction means avoiding all beta-lactams with similar side chains; a mild rash does not preclude a cephalosporin with a dissimilar side chain.\n\n**Glycopeptides**\n- **Vancomycin:** MRSA; infusion reaction (red man syndrome, histamine release; slow the infusion); nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity; monitor trough levels.\n\n**Fluoroquinolones**\n- **Ciprofloxacin:** Gram-negatives and pseudomonas; levofloxacin: pneumococci.\n- **Adverse effects:** tendon rupture (especially with corticosteroids, over 60), QT prolongation, CNS effects, cartilage damage in children (avoid in children and pregnancy).\n- **Pearl:** fluoroquinolones chelate calcium, iron, zinc, magnesium; separate from antacids, dairy, and iron.\n\n**Aminoglycosides**\n- **Gentamicin, amikacin, streptomycin:** Gram-negative aerobes; synergy with beta-lactams for enterococci and endocarditis.\n- **Toxicity:** nephrotoxicity (proximal tubule), ototoxicity (vestibular: gentamicin, streptomycin; cochlear: amikacin, kanamycin), neuromuscular blockade. Monitor peak and trough levels.\n- **Pearl:** aminoglycosides are inactive in anaerobes and have poor CNS penetration.\n\n**Macrolides and lincosamides**\n- **Erythromycin, clarithromycin, azithromycin:** atypical organisms (Mycoplasma, Legionella, Chlamydia); H. pylori eradication (clarithromycin); MAC prophylaxis (azithromycin).\n- **Adverse effects:** QT prolongation (interactions with amiodarone, ketoconazole); cholestatic jaundice (erythromycin estolate); GI upset.\n- **Pearl:** erythromycin is a CYP3A4 inhibitor that increases levels of many drugs including statins, theophylline, and warfarin.\n\n**Tetracyclines**\n- **Doxycycline:** atypical pneumonias, rickettsia, chlamydia, brucella, malaria prophylaxis; acne.\n- **Adverse effects:** tooth discolouration and bone growth impairment (children under 8, pregnancy); photosensitivity; oesophageal ulceration.\n\n**Antitubercular drugs**\n- **Isoniazid (INH):** inhibits mycolic acid synthesis (InhA); bactericidal; hepatotoxicity (age over 35, alcohol, slow acetylators); peripheral neuropathy (pyridoxine prevents); drug-induced lupus; haemolysis in G6PD deficiency. Acetylator status affects toxicity.\n- **Rifampicin (R):** inhibits RNA polymerase; orange body fluids; enzyme inducer; flu-like syndrome; hepatitis; thrombocytopenia.\n- **Pyrazinamide (Z):** active in acidic pH (inside macrophages); hepatotoxicity; hyperuricaemia (gout).\n- **Ethambutol (E):** inhibits arabinosyl transferase (cell wall); optic neuritis (red-green colour blindness); reduce dose in renal failure.\n- **Streptomycin (S):** aminoglycoside; ototoxicity (vestibular), nephrotoxicity.\n- **Bedaquiline and delamanid:** MDR-TB regimens; QT prolongation (bedaquiline).\n- **Pearl:** rifampicin turns urine, sweat, tears, and contact lenses orange; ethambutol causes red-green colour blindness.\n\n**Antifungals**\n- **Azoles (fluconazole, itraconazole, voriconazole, posaconazole):** inhibit ergosterol synthesis (CYP51, lanosterol 14-alpha demethylase); CYP interactions (strong inhibitors); hepatotoxicity; QT prolongation (ketoconazole).\n- **Amphotericin B:** binds ergosterol and forms pores; nephrotoxicity, electrolyte loss, infusion reaction (fever, rigors); liposomal form has less toxicity.\n- **Echinocandins (caspofungin, micafungin):** inhibit beta-1,3-glucan synthase; Candida and Aspergillus; IV only.\n- **Terbinafine:** inhibits squalene epoxidase (dermatophytes); hepatotoxicity.\n- **Griseofulvin:** dermatophytes; inhibits mitosis; teratogenic.\n- **Pearl:** amphotericin B nephrotoxicity is reduced by saline loading and liposomal formulation.\n\n**Antivirals**\n- **Acyclovir:** guanosine analogue; activated by viral thymidine kinase (HSV, VZV); nephrotoxicity (hydration).\n- **Ganciclovir:** CMV; bone marrow suppression.\n- **Oseltamivir:** neuraminidase inhibitor; influenza (start within 48 hours).\n- **Tenofovir, lamivudine, emtricitabine:** nucleoside or nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors (HIV, HBV).\n- **Dolutegravir:** integrase inhibitor (HIV).\n- **Sofosbuvir:** HCV polymerase inhibitor.\n- **Pearl:** tenofovir can cause nephrotoxicity and bone loss; monitor creatinine and phosphate.\n\n**Antimalarials**\n- **Chloroquine:** blocks haem polymerisation (food vacuole); retinopathy with long-term use; resistant P. falciparum in most regions.\n- **Artemisinin derivatives (artesunate, artemether):** free radical generation; first-line for falciparum; combined with partner drug (ACT).\n- **Primaquine:** hypnozoiticidal (vivax, ovale); haemolysis in G6PD deficiency.\n- **Quinine:** severe falciparum with artesunate; cinchonism (tinnitus, headache, visual disturbance); hypoglycaemia; QT prolongation.\n- **Pearl:** quinine causes hypoglycaemia by stimulating insulin release; malaria can present with hypoglycaemia as a result.\n\n**Antiprotozoals and anthelmintics**\n- **Metronidazole:** anaerobes and protozoa (Giardia, Entamoeba, Trichomonas); disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol; peripheral neuropathy.\n- **Albendazole and mebendazole:** intestinal nematodes; inhibit microtubule polymerisation in worms.\n- **Ivermectin:** strongyloidiasis, onchocerciasis; glutamate-gated chloride channels.\n- **Praziquantel:** schistosomiasis; calcium influx and tegument damage.\n- **Pearl:** metronidazole and alcohol cause flushing, vomiting, and tachycardia; avoid alcohol for 48 hours after the course.\n\n#### Table: First-Line Anti-Tubercular Therapy (ATT) Master Chart\n\n| Drug | Target / Mechanism | Resistance Gene Mutation | Classic Adverse Effects (Must-Know) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Isoniazid (H) | Inhibits mycolic acid synthesis (enoyl-ACP reductase InhA) | katG (catalase-peroxidase) > inhA | Peripheral Neuropathy (prevented by Pyridoxine B6), Hepatotoxicity, Sideroblastic anemia, Drug-induced lupus. |\n| Rifampicin (R) | Inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase | rpoB (beta-subunit) | Red-orange body secretions, potent CYP450 inducer, cholestatic jaundice, flu-like syndrome. |\n| Pyrazinamide (Z) | Disrupts mycobacterial membrane potential (active at acidic pH) | pncA (pyrazinamidase) | Most hepatotoxic first-line ATT, Hyperuricemia (arthralgia/gout). |\n| Ethambutol (E) | Inhibits arabinosyl transferase (cell wall synthesis) | embB | Optic Neuritis (decreased visual acuity, red-green color blindness). Bacteriostatic! Avoid in young children. |\n| Streptomycin (S) | Binds 30S ribosomal subunit (causes misreading of mRNA) | rpsL / rrs | Ototoxicity (vestibulotoxic > cochlear), Nephrotoxicity, Neuromuscular blockade. |\n\n#### Table: Ribosomal Protein Synthesis Inhibitors: 30S vs 50S\n\n| Subunit | Drug Classes (Mnemonic) | Mechanisms | Characteristic Toxicity |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 30S Inhibitors | 'Buy AT 30' -> Aminoglycosides, Tetracyclines | Aminoglycosides: Misreading of genetic code; Tetracyclines: Blocks aminoacyl-tRNA attachment | Aminoglycosides: Ototoxicity, Nephrotoxicity; Tetracyclines: Tooth discoloration, bone deformity, Fanconi syndrome (expired pills) |\n| 50S Inhibitors | 'CELL at 50' -> Chloramphenicol, Erythromycin (Macrolides), Linezolid, cLindamycin | Linezolid: Blocks initiation complex; Macrolides/Clindamycin: Blocks peptide translocation | Chloramphenicol: Aplastic anemia, Grey baby syndrome; Linezolid: Thrombocytopenia, Serotonin syndrome with SSRIs; Clindamycin: C. difficile pseudomembranous colitis |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Ribosomal Inhibitors:** 'Buy AT 30, CELL at 50' (Aminoglycosides, Tetracyclines bind 30S; Chloramphenicol, Erythromycin, Linezolid, cLindamycin bind 50S).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Vancomycin Infusion Reaction:** 'Red Man Syndrome' -> Direct non-IgE mast cell histamine release caused by RAPID infusion (prevented by infusing over >= 60-120 mins).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Antimalarial Radical Cure:** PRIMAQUINE is the only drug active against hypnozoites (dormant liver stage) of P. vivax and P. ovale. ALWAYS test G6PD levels first to prevent acute hemolytic anemia!\n\n### 5.12 Pharmacology pearls\n\n- **Adrenaline:** first-line for anaphylaxis, IM.\n\n## 18. Pharmacology (additions)\n\n- **Antitubercular drugs:**\n  - *MDR TB:* resistance to isoniazid and rifampicin; treated with bedaquiline, delamanid, linezolid, and fluoroquinolones.\n  - *Isoniazid-induced pyridoxine deficiency neuropathy:* isoniazid blocks pyridoxine (vitamin B6) activation, causing peripheral neuropathy; prevent with pyridoxine.\n  - *Rifampicin and oral contraceptives:* enzyme induction reduces contraceptive efficacy; advise barrier methods.\n  - *Pearl:* rifampicin is a potent CYP inducer; it lowers warfarin, OCP and many other drug levels.\n- **Haematopoietic and anticancer drugs:**\n  - *Darbepoetin:* long-acting erythropoiesis-stimulating agent for CKD anaemia; raises haemoglobin; check for hypertension and thrombosis.\n  - *Myeloma therapy adverse effect:* bortezomib (peripheral neuropathy); thalidomide (VTE, teratogenic).\n  - *Oprelvekin (IL-11):* stimulates platelet production; used after chemotherapy for thrombocytopenia; fluid retention and atrial arrhythmia.\n  - *Pearl:* thalidomide and lenalidomide cause venous thromboembolism; they are teratogenic.\n- **Antimalarial drugs:** chloroquine (haemolysis in G6PD); mefloquine (neuropsychiatric effects); artemisinin combination therapy (first line for P. falciparum); primaquine (radical cure for P. vivax; haemolysis in G6PD); atovaquone-proguanil.\n  - *Pearl:* primaquine must be avoided in G6PD deficiency; test before giving.\n","CLIN_PHARM_06":"## 5. Pharmacology (15-20 questions)\n\n### 5.7 Endocrine drugs\n\n**Diabetes mellitus**\n- **Metformin:** reduces hepatic gluconeogenesis; lactic acidosis (avoid in renal impairment, contrast, heart failure); B12 deficiency; first-line for type 2.\n- **Sulfonylureas (glibenclamide, glimepiride, gliclazide):** stimulate insulin release (K-ATP channel); hypoglycaemia (especially glibenclamide in elderly and renal failure).\n- **Glinides (repaglinide, nateglinide):** short-acting insulin secretagogues.\n- **DPP-4 inhibitors (sitagliptin, vildagliptin):** increase incretins (GLP-1, GIP); well tolerated; pancreatitis risk.\n- **GLP-1 agonists (liraglutide, semaglutide, exenatide):** increase insulin, reduce glucagon, slow gastric emptying; weight loss; GI upset; thyroid C-cell tumours (medullary thyroid carcinoma; contraindicated in MEN 2 and family history).\n- **SGLT2 inhibitors:** glycosuria; genital infection; euglycaemic DKA.\n- **Thiazolidinediones (pioglitazone):** PPAR-gamma; fluid retention; heart failure; bone fractures.\n- **Insulin:** rapid-acting (lispro, aspart), short (regular), intermediate (NPH), long (glargine, detemir); hypoglycaemia; lipodystrophy.\n- **Pearl:** GLP-1 agonists are contraindicated in patients with MEN 2 or a family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma.\n\n**Thyroid drugs**\n- **Thyroxine (levothyroxine):** hypothyroidism; take on an empty stomach; increase in pregnancy; overdose causes thyrotoxicosis.\n- **Carbimazole (methimazole) and propylthiouracil:** inhibit thyroid peroxidase; agranulocytosis; PTU hepatotoxicity (preferred in first trimester only).\n- **Radioactive iodine (I-131):** destroys thyroid tissue; contraindicated in pregnancy.\n- **Beta-blockers:** control adrenergic symptoms in thyrotoxicosis.\n- **Pearl:** propylthiouracil is preferred over carbimazole in the first trimester of pregnancy (carbimazole is teratogenic) but carries hepatotoxicity risk.\n\n**Corticosteroids**\n- **Mechanism:** glucocorticoid receptor; genomic and non-genomic effects; anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive.\n- **Adverse effects:** Cushing features, osteoporosis, hyperglycaemia, hypertension, infections, cataract, glaucoma, peptic ulcer, adrenal suppression.\n- **Adrenal suppression:** taper slowly; stress dose during illness.\n- **Pearl:** prednisolone cover is needed in patients who have been on steroids for more than 3 weeks, even at low doses.\n\n**Osteoporosis drugs**\n- **Bisphosphonates (alendronate, zoledronate):** inhibit osteoclasts; oesophagitis (take upright); osteonecrosis of the jaw; atypical femur fracture.\n- **Denosumab:** RANKL antibody; rebound vertebral fractures if stopped without bisphosphonate.\n- **Teriparatide:** PTH analogue given daily; anabolic (stimulates new bone formation) for severe osteoporosis.\n- **Raloxifene:** selective oestrogen receptor modulator (SERM); protects bone but raises venous thromboembolism risk.\n- **Calcium and vitamin D:** supplementation for bone health, particularly in osteoporosis and vitamin D deficiency.\n- **Pearl:** denosumab discontinuation causes rebound bone loss; follow with a bisphosphonate.\n\n#### Table: Modern Oral Antidiabetic Drugs: Mechanisms & Exam Pitfalls\n\n| Drug Class | Representative Drug | Primary Mechanism | Key Adverse Effects / Exam Pearls |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Biguanides | Metformin | Activates AMPK -> inhibits hepatic gluconeogenesis | First-line; euglycemic (no hypoglycemia); Lactic Acidosis if eGFR < 30 mL/min; Vitamin B12 deficiency. |\n| SGLT2 Inhibitors | Empagliflozin, Dapagliflozin | Blocks SGLT2 in proximal renal tubule -> glucosuria | Cardiorenal protection; EUGLYCEMIC DKA (euDKA), genital mycotic infections (Candida), Fournier's gangrene. |\n| GLP-1 Receptor Agonists | Semaglutide, Liraglutide | Activates GLP-1 receptor -> glucose-dependent insulin release | Profound weight loss; reduces MACE in CVD; GI nausea; Black box: Medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC) / MEN 2. |\n| Dual GIP / GLP-1 Agonist | Tirzepatide | Co-stimulates GIP and GLP-1 receptors ('twincretin') | Greatest HbA1c lowering (> 2.5%) and weight reduction (> 20%). |\n| DPP-4 Inhibitors | Sitagliptin, Linagliptin | Inhibits dipeptidyl peptidase-4 -> prolongs endogenous incretins | Weight neutral, low hypoglycemia risk. Linagliptin requires NO dose adjustment in renal failure (bile excretion). |\n\n#### Table: Antithyroid Drugs in Pregnancy & Storm\n\n| Trimester / Setting | Drug of Choice | Pharmacological Rationale | Key Complications |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1st Trimester of Pregnancy | Propylthiouracil (PTU) | Higher protein binding -> crosses placenta less readily; avoids methimazole teratogenicity | Maternal fulminant hepatic necrosis (rare black box warning). |\n| 2nd & 3rd Trimesters | Methimazole (MMI) | Avoids severe maternal PTU hepatotoxicity | Avoid in 1st trimester due to Aplasia Cutis Congenita (scalp defects) & choanal atresia. |\n| Thyroid Storm | PTU + Beta-blocker + Lugol's + Steroids | PTU also inhibits peripheral conversion of T4 to active T3 by blocking 5'-deiodinase | Give Potassium Iodide (Lugol's) >= 1 hour AFTER antithyroid drug to prevent Wolff-Chaikoff escape. |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Linagliptin Renal Safety:** 'Linagliptin leaves via the LIVER' (no renal dose adjustment needed, unlike all other gliptins!).\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Bisphosphonate Dosing:** Take first thing in morning on empty stomach with a full glass of plain water, and remain completely UPRIGHT for >= 30-60 mins to prevent PILL ESOPHAGITIS.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Teriparatide:** Recombinant human PTH (1-34) fragment given intermittently -> ANABOLIC for bone (stimulates osteoblasts > osteoclasts). Contraindicated in Paget's disease / prior radiation (osteosarcoma risk).\n\n### 5.12 Pharmacology pearls\n\n- **Dimercaprol and succimer (DMSA):** chelators that bind lead and other heavy metals so they can be excreted; succimer is oral.\n\n## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.9 Short-subject quick pearls\n\n- Hanging mark: oblique, above thyroid cartilage.\n\n## 18. Pharmacology (additions)\n\n- **Other antimicrobials:**\n  - *Topical clotrimazole for inhaled steroid candidiasis:* prevent oral thrush with inhaled corticosteroids; rinse mouth.\n  - *Fluoroquinolone DNA gyrase inhibition and foetal cartilage toxicity:* quinolones damage cartilage in children; avoid in pregnancy.\n  - *Metronidazole and alcohol:* disulfiram-like reaction (inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase).\n  - *Pearl:* quinolones inhibit DNA gyrase (bacteria) and topoisomerase IV; they are avoided in children and pregnancy.\n- **Anti-inflammatory, antirheumatic and gout drugs:**\n  - *Allopurinol:* xanthine oxidase inhibitor; reduces uric acid; check for HLA-B*5801 (Asian) before starting (rash, SJS).\n  - *Methotrexate:* DHFR inhibition; reduces DNA synthesis; folic acid rescue; hepatotoxicity, bone marrow suppression.\n  - *Tocilizumab:* anti-IL-6 receptor monoclonal antibody; for RA and cytokine release syndrome.\n  - *Pearl:* allopurinol causes severe skin reactions in HLA-B*5801 carriers; test before starting.\n- **Thyroid, metabolic and bone drugs:** methimazole (thyroid peroxidase inhibition); propylthiouracil (also inhibits T4 to T3 conversion; hepatotoxicity; first trimester); bisphosphonates (osteoclast inhibition; osteonecrosis of jaw); denosumab (RANKL inhibition).\n- **Diabetes drugs and DKA treatment:** insulin (regular, rapid, long-acting); metformin (lactic acidosis; stop before contrast); sulphonylureas (hypoglycaemia); DPP-4 inhibitors; GLP-1 agonists (pancreatitis, thyroid C-cell tumours); SGLT2 inhibitors (euglycaemic DKA). DKA: IV fluids, IV insulin, potassium replacement.\n  - *Pearl:* SGLT2 inhibitors can cause euglycaemic DKA; hold before surgery.\n","CLIN_PHARM_07":"## 5. Pharmacology (15-20 questions)\n\n### 5.5 Chemotherapy and cancer drugs\n\n**Drug classes**\n- **Alkylating agents (cyclophosphamide, ifosfamide, chlorambucil, melphalan, busulfan, cisplatin):** cross-link DNA; cyclophosphamide: haemorrhagic cystitis (mesna protects), SIADH; cisplatin: nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity, emesis (give with hydration and NK1 antagonist); busulfan: pulmonary fibrosis.\n- **Antimetabolites:** methotrexate (dihydrofolate reductase inhibitor; leucovorin rescue; mucositis, myelosuppression, hepatotoxicity, pulmonary toxicity); 5-fluorouracil (thymidylate synthase; DPD deficiency causes severe toxicity); cytarabine (DNA polymerase; cerebellar toxicity); 6-mercaptopurine (purine synthesis; TPMT and NUDT15 variants; allopurinol interaction, reduce dose to a third or quarter).\n- **Antitumour antibiotics:** doxorubicin, daunorubicin (intercalate DNA, topoisomerase II; cardiotoxicity, cumulative, prevented by dexrazoxane and by limiting cumulative dose); bleomycin (pulmonary fibrosis, skin changes; worsened by high FiO2 in surgery); mitomycin.\n- **Vinca alkaloids (vincristine, vinblastine):** microtubule disruption in M phase; neurotoxicity (vincristine: peripheral neuropathy, constipation, ileus); vinblastine myelosuppression.\n- **Taxanes (paclitaxel, docetaxel):** stabilise microtubules; peripheral neuropathy, alopecia, neutropenia, hypersensitivity (premedicate with steroids and antihistamines).\n- **Topoisomerase inhibitors:** irinotecan (topoisomerase I; diarrhoea, UGT1A1 variant), etoposide (topoisomerase II; myelosuppression, alopecia).\n- **Hormonal therapies:** tamoxifen (oestrogen antagonist in breast; endometrial cancer, VTE), aromatase inhibitors (anastrozole, letrozole: postmenopausal breast cancer; bone loss), androgen deprivation (GnRH analogues, antiandrogens).\n- **Targeted therapies:** imatinib (BCR-ABL; oedema, myelosuppression); trastuzumab (HER2; cardiotoxicity); rituximab (CD20; infusion reaction, hepatitis B reactivation); bevacizumab (VEGF; hypertension, bleeding, GI perforation, wound healing); erlotinib (EGFR; rash, diarrhoea).\n- **Immunotherapy:** PD-1/PD-L1 inhibitors (pembrolizumab, nivolumab; immune-related adverse effects: pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis); CTLA-4 inhibitors (ipilimumab).\n\n**Toxicity management**\n- **Nausea and vomiting:** NK1 antagonist (aprepitant), 5-HT3 antagonist (ondansetron, granisetron), dexamethasone; olanzapine adds benefit. For high emetogenic risk (cisplatin), all four.\n- **Myelosuppression:** G-CSF (filgrastim) for neutropenia; transfusions for anaemia; platelet transfusion below 10,000/mm3.\n- **Mucositis:** cryotherapy (ice chips) for 5-FU; methotrexate rescue with leucovorin (folinic acid).\n- **Cardiotoxicity:** dexrazoxane (doxorubicin); monitor ejection fraction.\n- **Pearl:** methotrexate toxicity from trimethoprim (dihydrofolate reductase inhibition) and NSAIDs (reduced renal clearance) is treated with leucovorin.\n\n#### Table: Chemotherapy Specific Dose-Limiting Toxicities & Protective Antidotes\n\n| Antineoplastic Drug | Dose-Limiting Toxicity / Pathophysiology | Specific Antidote / Rescue Agent | Mechanism of Protection |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Cyclophosphamide / Ifosfamide | Sterile Hemorrhagic Cystitis (caused by toxic metabolite ACROLEIN) | MESNA (Sodium 2-mercaptoethanesulfonate) + Hydration | Mesna concentrates in urine; its sulfhydryl groups bind acrolein, forming a nontoxic adduct. |\n| Doxorubicin / Daunorubicin | Dilated Cardiomyopathy & Congestive Heart Failure (free radicals) | DEXRAZOXANE | Intracellular iron chelator that prevents iron-mediated free radical generation. |\n| Methotrexate (High Dose) | Lethal Bone Marrow Aplasia & Mucositis (DHFR inhibition) | LEUCOVORIN (Folinic acid / 5-formyl-THF) | Bypasses blocked DHFR enzyme; rescues healthy cells from folate starvation. |\n| Cisplatin | Nephrotoxicity (acute tubular damage) & Ototoxicity | AMIFOSTINE + Aggressive Hydration | Free radical scavenger that protects renal tubules. |\n| 5-Fluorouracil (5-FU) Overdose | Severe mucositis, neutropenia, encephalopathy | URIDINE TRIACETATE | Competitively inhibits 5-FU incorporation into RNA. |\n\n#### Table: Monoclonal Antibody Suffixes & Molecular Targets\n\n| Target Antigen / Pathway | Monoclonal Antibody | Clinical Indication | Unique Adverse Effect / Pearl |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| HER2 / neu Receptor Tyrosine Kinase | Trastuzumab (Herceptin) | HER2-positive Breast Cancer & Gastric Cancer | Type II Cardiotoxicity (reversible decrease in LVEF without necrosis). Check 2D-Echo! |\n| CD20 Surface Antigen on B-cells | Rituximab | Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma, CLL, Rheumatoid Arthritis | Risk of Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy (PML - JC virus) & Hepatitis B reactivation. |\n| VEGF-A (Angiogenesis Inhibitor) | Bevacizumab | Colorectal, Ovarian, Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer | Severe hypertension, impaired wound healing, bowel perforation. |\n| PD-1 / PD-L1 (Immune Checkpoints) | Pembrolizumab, Nivolumab | Melanoma, NSCLC, MSI-H Solid Tumors | Immune-Related Adverse Events (irAEs: hypophysitis, thyroiditis, colitis). |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Chemo Toxicities Rule:** 'Bleo blows lungs (Pulmonary fibrosis), Doxo damages heart (Cardiomyopathy), Cis cranks kidneys & ears (Nephro/Ototoxicity), Vinca veers nerves (Peripheral neuropathy)'.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Triptan Contraindication:** Sumatriptan is a 5-HT1B/1D agonist causing cerebral vasoconstriction. Contraindicated in Coronary Artery Disease (CAD), Prinzmetal angina, and uncontrolled hypertension!\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Triple Therapy for CINV:** NK1 Antagonist (Aprepitant) + 5-HT3 Antagonist (Ondansetron) + Corticosteroid (Dexamethasone).\n\n### 5.6 Gastrointestinal drugs\n\n- **Proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole, pantoprazole, esomeprazole):** irreversibly inhibit H+/K+ ATPase; H. pylori eradication; long-term risks: hypomagnesaemia, C. difficile, fractures, B12 deficiency.\n- **H2 blockers (ranitidine, famotidine):** block histamine H2 receptors; cimetidine interacts with many drugs (CYP inhibitor).\n- **Antacids:** aluminium (constipation), magnesium (diarrhoea), calcium carbonate.\n- **Sucralfate:** forms a protective layer; binds other drugs (separate by 2 hours).\n- **Misoprostol:** prostaglandin E1 analogue; protects gastric mucosa; causes abortion and uterine contraction (contraindicated in pregnancy); used with mifepristone for medical abortion.\n- **Antiemetics:** metoclopramide (D2 blocker; EPS; avoid in Parkinson disease); domperidone (peripheral D2 blocker; QT prolongation); ondansetron (5-HT3; QT prolongation; constipation); prochlorperazine (phenothiazine; EPS); cyclizine (H1; anticholinergic).\n- **Laxatives:** osmotic (lactulose, PEG), stimulant (senna, bisacodyl), bulk-forming (psyllium); avoid in intestinal obstruction.\n- **Anti-diarrhoeals:** loperamide (opioid receptor; avoid in C. difficile and bloody diarrhoea), diphenoxylate.\n- **Pearl:** misoprostol is contraindicated in pregnancy (abortifacient); metoclopramide is contraindicated in Parkinson disease.\n\n### 5.8 Respiratory drugs\n\n- **Salbutamol:** SABA; tremor, tachycardia, hypokalaemia.\n- **Ipratropium and tiotropium:** muscarinic antagonists (bronchodilators in COPD and asthma).\n- **Inhaled corticosteroids (budesonide, fluticasone, beclometasone):** oral thrush, dysphonia, adrenal suppression at high dose.\n- **Montelukast:** leukotriene receptor antagonist; neuropsychiatric effects.\n- **Theophylline:** PDE inhibitor and adenosine antagonist; narrow therapeutic index; toxicity: seizures, arrhythmias; interacts with ciprofloxacin, erythromycin, cimetidine (raise levels); smoking lowers levels.\n- **Omalizumab, mepolizumab, dupilumab:** biologics for severe asthma (anti-IgE, anti-IL5, anti-IL4/13).\n- **Pearl:** theophylline toxicity signs: nausea, vomiting, arrhythmia, seizures; check levels when interacting drugs are added.\n\n### 5.12 Pharmacology pearls\n\n- **Naloxone:** opioid antagonist used to reverse opioid overdose (respiratory depression); short-acting, so re-sedation can occur.\n- **Idarucizumab:** monoclonal antibody fragment that reverses the anticoagulant effect of dabigatran in bleeding or urgent surgery.\n","PHYS_UNIT_01":"## 15. Physiology\n\n- **Nerve and muscle electrophysiology:**\n  - *Resting membrane potential:* about -70 to -90 mV; determined mainly by K+ (Nernst and GHK equation); extracellular K+ changes shift the potential. Hyperkalaemia depolarises the membrane and can cause a fall in excitability.\n  - *Skeletal muscle length-tension and cross-bridges:* maximum tension at optimal length (overlap of thick and thin filaments); cross-bridge cycling needs ATP and Ca2+ (troponin C).\n  - *Action potential and refractory periods:* absolute refractory (no second AP possible) and relative refractory (AP possible with stronger stimulus). Refractory period prevents tetany in cardiac muscle.\n  - *Nerve fibre types:* A-alpha (proprioception, motor, largest, fastest), A-beta (touch), A-delta (pain, temperature), C (pain, slowest, unmyelinated). B fibres are preganglionic autonomic.\n  - *Conduction and compression:* nerve compression blocks the largest fibres first (A-alpha, then A-beta, then C); pain fibres are the most resistant.\n  - *Pearl:* local anaesthetics block small myelinated fibres (A-delta, pain) first; large fibres (motor) last.\n- **Cell transport and intestinal absorption:**\n  - *Duodenal iron absorption:* ferric iron (Fe3+) is reduced to ferrous (Fe2+) by duodenal cytochrome b (DcytB, ferrireductase) before uptake through DMT1.\n  - *Facilitated diffusion:* passive transport through carrier proteins (GLUT for glucose); no ATP needed; saturable.\n  - *Intestinal glucose absorption:* SGLT1 (secondary active, apical membrane, Na+ co-transport) then GLUT2 on basolateral side.\n  - *Pearl:* SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin) act in the proximal tubule; SGLT1 is the intestinal transporter.\n- **Thermoregulation, fever and heat illness:** hypothalamus (preoptic area) is the thermostat; pyrogens (IL-1, IL-6, TNF) raise the set point via PGE2. Heat exhaustion (salt and water loss, reversible); heat stroke (CNS dysfunction, body temperature above 40 C, emergency; cooling).\n  - *Pearl:* fever is a raised set point; hyperthermia (heat stroke) has a normal set point, with heat production exceeding loss.\n- **Cell signalling and cytoskeleton:** second messengers (cAMP via Gs/Gi and adenylate cyclase; IP3 and DAG via Gq and PLC; Ca2+ via calmodulin); receptor tyrosine kinases (insulin, growth factors); JAK-STAT for cytokines; microtubules (kinesin, dynein transport); microfilaments (actin); intermediate filaments (keratin, vimentin, neurofilament).\n  - *Pearl:* colchicine binds tubulin and inhibits microtubule polymerisation.\n- **Insulin physiology:** insulin released from beta cells; increases glucose uptake (GLUT4 in muscle and adipose); glycogenesis; lipogenesis; suppresses gluconeogenesis; promotes K+ uptake. C-peptide reflects endogenous insulin production.\n  - *Pearl:* C-peptide is raised in insulinoma and type 2 diabetes; low in type 1 diabetes.\n- **Cardiac electrophysiology:** SA node (pacemaker, about 60-100/min), AV node (delay, 0.1 sec), His-Purkinje (rapid conduction). Phase 4 spontaneous depolarisation in pacemaker cells due to funny current (If) and Na+ influx; calcium channels mediate the upstroke in nodal cells.\n  - *Pearl:* beta-blockers slow the SA node and AV node by reducing the slope of phase 4.\n\n#### Table: Myasthenia Gravis vs Lambert-Eaton Syndrome\n\n| Feature | Myasthenia Gravis | Lambert-Eaton Syndrome (LEMS) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Target Antigen | Post-synaptic Nicotinic AChR | Pre-synaptic P/Q-type Ca2+ Channels |\n| Clinical Pattern | Weakness worsens with exertion (ptosis/diplopia) | Weakness improves with exercise (proximal lower limbs) |\n| Repetitive Stimulation | Decremental response (> 10% drop) | Incremental response (> 100% facilitation) |\n| Malignancy Association | Thymoma (15%) or Thymic Hyperplasia (65%) | Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC, > 50%) |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** Fast Na+ influx drives Phase 0 in ventricles; Slow L-type Ca2+ influx drives Phase 0 in SA/AV nodes.\n\n## Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Myasthenia** = Post-synaptic, worsens with use. Lambert-Eaton = Pre-synaptic, improves with use.\n","PHYS_UNIT_02":"## 15. Physiology\n\n- **Cardiac haemodynamics and myocardial work:**\n  - *Capillary Starling forces:* net filtration = (Pc - Pi) - (pi c - pi i). Increased capillary pressure (heart failure) causes oedema; low plasma protein (nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis) also causes oedema.\n  - *Pressure-volume work:* the area of the PV loop equals stroke work. Myocardial O2 demand is determined mainly by pressure (afterload) and volume (preload).\n  - *Jugular venous pulse and right-heart haemodynamics:* waves: a (atrial contraction), c (tricuspid bulge), x (atrial relaxation), v (venous filling), y (tricuspid opening). Cannon a waves: AV dissociation (complete heart block, VT).\n  - *Pearl:* giant a waves (cannon) occur when the atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve, as in complete heart block.\n- **Sensory receptors and pain physiology:**\n  - *Cutaneous mechanoreceptors:* Meissner (light touch, rapidly adapting), Pacinian (vibration, rapidly adapting), Merkel (pressure, slowly adapting), Ruffini (stretch, slowly adapting).\n  - *Pain sensitisation and allodynia:* central sensitisation (wind-up via NMDA receptors) causes allodynia (pain from normally non-painful stimuli) and hyperalgesia.\n  - *Taste receptor transduction:* sweet, salt, sour, bitter, umami; G-protein coupled receptors (T1R and T2R) except salt and sour (ion channels).\n  - *Pearl:* Pacinian corpuscles detect high-frequency vibration.\n- **Respiratory mechanics and lung volumes:** TLC = VC + RV; FRC = ERV + RV; FEV1/FVC ratio is reduced in obstructive disease (below 70%), normal or increased in restrictive disease. Compliance = change in volume / change in pressure; reduced compliance in fibrosis; increased in emphysema.\n  - *Pearl:* in obstructive disease, RV and FRC rise; in restrictive disease, TLC, VC and FRC fall.\n- **Menstrual cycle physiology:** follicular phase (FSH, oestradiol rise; LH surge at mid-cycle triggers ovulation); luteal phase (progesterone from corpus luteum; if no pregnancy, falls, leading to menstruation). Ovulation occurs 14 days before menses.\n  - *Pearl:* the LH surge precedes ovulation by about 24-36 hours.\n- **Blood pressure regulation:** baroreceptors (carotid sinus, aortic arch); RAAS; sympathetic tone; ADH; ANP. Short term: baroreceptor reflex. Long term: kidney (pressure natriuresis).\n  - *Pearl:* the carotid sinus baroreceptor reflex is mediated by CN IX (afferent) and CN X.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **CADET face Right:** CO2, Acidosis/Altitude, 2,3-DPG, Exercise, Temperature.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Upright Lung: Apex** = Highest V/Q (~3.0), Base = Lowest V/Q (~0.6).\n\n- **Bohr effect:** a rise in CO2 or H+ (fall in pH), 2,3-BPG or temperature shifts the oxygen-haemoglobin curve to the right (lower affinity, more O2 release to tissues); the opposite shifts it left.\n\n- **Heart sounds:** S1 = closure of mitral and tricuspid valves (start of systole); S2 = closure of aortic and pulmonary valves (A2 before P2; P2 delayed in inspiration, physiological split); S3 = rapid ventricular filling (normal in children and pregnancy, pathological in heart failure); S4 = atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle (hypertrophy, ischaemia).\n\n## Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Oxygen-Hemoglobin Dissociation Curve Shifts\n\n| Direction | Affinity for O2 | Physiological Triggers | Key Manifestations |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Shift to RIGHT (CADET) | Decreased (unloads O2 to tissues) | Increased CO2, Acidosis (low pH), 2,3-DPG, Exercise, Temp | Metabolically active muscle, high altitude |\n| Shift to LEFT | Increased (holds O2 tightly) | Alkalosis, Low temp, Low 2,3-DPG, Fetal Hb (HbF), CO poisoning | Tissue hypoxia despite high arterial saturation |\n","PHYS_UNIT_03":"## 11. Integrated Patterns (High-Yield Associations)\n\n- **Von Hippel-Lindau:** haemangioblastoma (cerebellum, retina), renal cell carcinoma, phaeochromocytoma.\n\n## 15. Physiology\n\n- **Acid-base disorders:**\n  - *Metabolic acidosis:* low HCO3, low pH; compensation by hyperventilation. Winter formula: expected PCO2 = 1.5 x HCO3 + 8 (plus or minus 2).\n  - *CKD compensation:* metabolic acidosis in CKD is a normal anion gap acidosis due to reduced ammonia excretion.\n  - *Respiratory alkalosis (hyperventilation-induced):* low PCO2, high pH; acute (no compensation) and chronic (renal HCO3 loss).\n  - *Primary metabolic alkalosis:* high HCO3, high pH; compensation by hypoventilation.\n  - *Respiratory acidosis:* high PCO2, low pH; renal compensation increases HCO3 over days.\n  - *ABG interpretation:* step 1: pH; step 2: primary disorder (PCO2 or HCO3); step 3: compensation; step 4: anion gap (Na - (Cl + HCO3), normal 8-12).\n  - *Pearl:* a high anion gap metabolic acidosis (MUDPILES: methanol, uraemia, DKA, paraldehyde, isoniazid, lactic acidosis, ethylene glycol, salicylates).\n- **Renal and micturition physiology:** GFR about 125 ml/min; filtered load = GFR x plasma concentration; clearance of inulin equals GFR. Micturition reflex: parasympathetic (S2-S4) contracts detrusor; pudendal nerve (somatic) controls the external sphincter. Pontine micturition centre coordinates.\n  - *Pearl:* inulin is the gold-standard marker for GFR (filtered, neither secreted nor reabsorbed); creatinine is secreted slightly, overestimating GFR.\n- **Gas exchange and high-altitude physiology:** hypoxia stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors; increased 2,3-BPG shift; acclimatisation: increased erythropoietin, increased 2,3-DPG, increased ventilation (hypoxic ventilatory response), increased capillary density. Acute mountain sickness (headache, nausea); high-altitude pulmonary oedema (HAPE); high-altitude cerebral oedema (HACE; ataxia).\n  - *Pearl:* acetazolamide accelerates acclimatisation by inducing metabolic acidosis, increasing ventilation.\n- **Fluid and electrolyte balance:** ECF about 14 L (20% body weight); ICF about 28 L; plasma about 3 L. Aldosterone (distal Na+ reabsorption, K+ secretion); ADH (water reabsorption); ANP (natriuresis). Osmolality = 2 Na + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8.\n  - *Pearl:* aldosterone increases both Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion in the collecting duct.\n\n#### Table: Bartter vs Gitelman vs Liddle Syndromes\n\n| Syndrome | Nephron Site & Defect | Urine Calcium | Renin / Aldosterone | Blood Pressure |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Bartter | TAL: NKCC2 / ROMK loss (loop mimic) | Hypercalciuria | Elevated / Elevated | Normal / Low |\n| Gitelman | DCT: NCCT loss (thiazide mimic) | Hypocalciuria | Elevated / Elevated | Normal / Low |\n| Liddle | Collecting Duct: ENaC gain of function | Normal | Low / Low (Suppressed) | HYPERTENSIVE |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **High Anion Gap Acidosis** = MUDPILES. Normal Anion Gap (Hyperchloremic) = HARDASS.\n\n- **PAH clearance:** para-aminohippurate is filtered and secreted, so almost all is cleared in one renal pass (about 90 percent); PAH clearance measures effective renal plasma flow. Inulin clearance measures GFR.\n\n## Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Tubuloglomerular feedback:** the macula densa senses increased NaCl delivery to the distal tubule and releases adenosine, which constricts the afferent arteriole and lowers GFR (a negative feedback that keeps distal delivery stable).\n","PHYS_UNIT_04":"## 10. High-Yield Image Areas\n\n- **CT brain:** hyperdense (haemorrhage); crescent (extradural: lens-shaped; subdural: crescentic); hydrocephalus; ring enhancement (abscess, glioma).\n\n## 11. Integrated Patterns (High-Yield Associations)\n\n- **MEN 1:** pituitary, parathyroid, pancreatic (gastrinoma, insulinoma).\n\n## 15. Physiology\n\n- **Central sensory pathways, sleep and cortical plasticity:**\n  - *Cortical plasticity and phantom limb:* after amputation, the deafferented cortex is taken over by adjacent areas; phantom limb pain relates to this reorganisation (mirror therapy helps).\n  - *Sleep-wake regulation:* adenosine (accumulates during wakefulness; caffeine blocks adenosine receptors); orexin/hypocretin; VLPO promotes sleep; ascending reticular activating system promotes waking. REM sleep is associated with muscle atonia (pons).\n  - *Dorsal column pathway:* fine touch, vibration, proprioception; crosses in the medulla (internal arcuate fibres); lesion causes ipsilateral loss of proprioception and vibration (sensory ataxia; Romberg positive).\n  - *Pearl:* Romberg test is positive in dorsal column lesions (tabes dorsalis, B12 deficiency).\n- **Pituitary-adrenal physiology:** CRH from hypothalamus, ACTH from anterior pituitary, cortisol from zona fasciculata. Cortisol feeds back to hypothalamus and pituitary. Diurnal rhythm: peak in the morning.\n  - *Pearl:* a morning cortisol below 3 micrograms/dL suggests adrenal insufficiency; above 18 rules it out in most cases.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Hypothalamus: Ventromedial** = Satiety. Lateral = Hunger. Anterior = Cooling. Posterior = Heating.\n\n## Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: EEG Sleep Stages Architecture\n\n| Stage | EEG Waveform | Distinctive Characteristic | Clinical Correlate |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Awake (Eyes Open) | Beta waves (14-30 Hz) | High frequency, low voltage | Mental concentration |\n| Awake (Eyes Closed) | Alpha waves (8-13 Hz) | Occipital rhythm | Relaxed wakefulness |\n| N1 | Theta waves (4-7 Hz) | Light sleep, easily aroused | Hypnic jerks |\n| N2 | Sleep spindles & K-complexes | Greatest percentage of total sleep (~50%) | Bruxism (teeth grinding) |\n| N3 (Slow Wave) | Delta waves (< 3.5 Hz) | Deep restorative sleep | Sleepwalking, Night terrors, Enuresis |\n| REM | Beta / Sawtooth waves | Muscle atonia, Rapid eye movements, Vivid dreams | Penile tumescence, Nightmares |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **BATS Drink Blood:** Beta, Alpha, Theta, Spindles, Delta, Beta (REM).\n","PSM_UNIT_01":"## 8. Preventive and Social Medicine (PSM) (18-22 questions)\n\n### 8.1 Epidemiology: definitions\n\n- **Epidemiology:** study of distribution and determinants of health events in populations.\n- **Incidence:** new cases in a time period divided by population at risk; a rate.\n- **Prevalence:** all existing cases at a point (point prevalence) or period (period prevalence) divided by population.\n- **Prevalence = incidence x duration** (in a steady state): a long disease with a moderate incidence gives high prevalence.\n- **Attack rate:** cumulative incidence in outbreak settings.\n- **Mortality rate:** deaths divided by population; case fatality rate (CFR) = deaths among cases divided by cases.\n- **Infant mortality rate (IMR):** deaths under 1 year per 1000 live births.\n- **Neonatal mortality rate:** deaths in first 28 days per 1000 live births.\n- **Maternal mortality ratio (MMR):** maternal deaths per 100,000 live births.\n- **Under-5 mortality rate (U5MR):** deaths under 5 per 1000 live births.\n- **Pearl:** CFR is a proportion (deaths/cases); mortality rate uses population at risk as denominator.\n\n### 8.2 Study designs\n\n| Design | Type | Key feature | Strength |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cross-sectional | Descriptive | Snapshot; prevalence | Quick; cheap |\n| Case-control | Analytical, observational | Starts with outcome; odds ratio | Rare diseases; multiple exposures |\n| Cohort | Analytical, observational | Starts with exposure; relative risk | Incidence; temporal sequence |\n| Randomised controlled trial | Analytical, experimental | Randomisation | Strongest causal inference |\n| Ecological | Descriptive | Group-level data | Hypothesis generation; ecological fallacy |\n- **Measures:** odds ratio (case-control), relative risk (cohort), attributable risk, population attributable risk, number needed to treat (NNT = 1 / absolute risk reduction).\n- **Sensitivity:** true positives / (true positives + false negatives); rule in (high sensitivity, SnNout).\n- **Specificity:** true negatives / (true negatives + false positives); rule out (high specificity, SpPin).\n- **PPV:** depends on prevalence; increases with prevalence.\n- **Pearl:** SnNout (highly Sensitive test, Negative result rules out disease); SpPin (highly Specific test, Positive result rules in disease).\n\n#### Table: Epidemiological Study Designs Comparison\n\n| Feature | Case-Control Study | Cohort Study | Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Direction of Study | Retrospective (Outcome -> Exposure) | Prospective (Exposure -> Outcome) | Prospective (Intervention -> Outcome) |\n| Primary Measure | Odds Ratio (OR) | Incidence & Relative Risk (RR) | Efficacy / Number Needed to Treat (NNT) |\n| Ideal Scenario | Rare diseases / long latency periods | Rare exposures / multiple outcomes | Evaluating therapeutic interventions |\n| Key Limitation | Recall bias & selection bias | Loss to follow-up (attrition), expensive | Ethical constraints, strict eligibility |\n\n### 8.3 Bias, confounding and error\n\n- **Selection bias:** non-random sampling; Berkson bias (hospital-based studies).\n- **Information bias:** recall bias (case-control), observer bias, detection bias.\n- **Confounding:** a third factor associated with both exposure and outcome; controlled by randomisation, matching, stratification, multivariate analysis.\n- **Lead-time bias:** screening detects disease earlier, appearing to increase survival without changing course.\n- **Length-time bias:** slow-growing cases are more likely to be detected by screening.\n- **Pearl:** lead-time bias makes survival look longer after early detection even if death date is unchanged.\n\n### 8.4 Screening\n\n- **Criteria (Wilson and Jungner):** important disease; natural history known; treatment available and effective; cost-effective; acceptable test.\n- **Types:** mass, targeted (high risk), multiphasic.\n- **Validity:** sensitivity, specificity, PPV, NPV.\n- **Reliability (precision):** consistency of repeated measurements (same result on repeat testing).\n- **Pearl:** screening for a disease that is not treatable is not justified.\n\n### 8.5 Biostatistics\n\n- **Mean:** average; affected by outliers.\n- **Median:** middle value; robust to outliers.\n- **Mode:** the most frequent value in a data set.\n- **Standard deviation:** spread; about 68% within 1 SD, 95% within 2 SD (normal distribution).\n- **Standard error:** SD divided by the square root of n; measures precision of the sample mean.\n- **Normal distribution:** symmetric; mean equals median equals mode.\n- **Tests:** t-test (two means), chi-square (categorical), ANOVA (more than two means), Mann-Whitney U (non-parametric two groups), Wilcoxon signed rank (paired non-parametric), Pearson correlation, Kaplan-Meier survival.\n- **Type I error (alpha):** false positive; typically 0.05.\n- **Type II error (beta):** false negative; power = 1 - beta (typically 0.8).\n- **P value:** probability of observing data (or more extreme) if null hypothesis is true.\n- **Confidence interval:** 95% CI; if it includes 1 (for ratio), not significant.\n- **Pearl:** a p value of 0.05 means a 5% chance of a result at least this extreme if there is no true difference.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Qualitative data** = Chi-Square. Quantitative 2 groups = Student t-test. > 2 groups = ANOVA.\n\n### 8.15 Epidemiological quick pearls\n\n- Incidence is a rate; prevalence is a proportion.\n- Sensitivity rules out (SnNout); specificity rules in (SpPin).\n- PPV increases with prevalence.\n- Herd immunity threshold = 1 - (1/R0).\n- Lead-time bias inflates survival in screened groups.\n- Odds ratio for case-control; relative risk for cohort.\n- NNT = 1 / ARR.\n- DALY = YLL + YLD.\n- Replacement TFR: 2.1.\n\n## 31. Community Medicine (PSM) (additions)\n\n- **Study designs and research methods (additions):**\n  - *Pre-post intervention studies:* before-after comparison, paired design.\n  - *RCT standard and cluster randomisation:* individual vs group allocation; cluster for community-level interventions.\n  - *Case-control selection of controls:* controls from same source population as cases.\n  - *Retrospective cohort using historical exposure records:* the outcome has already occurred when the study starts; exposure is traced from records.\n  - *Stratified random sampling:* sample proportionally within strata (e.g., urban, rural).\n  - *Pearl:* cluster randomisation is used when individuals within a group are likely to influence each other (e.g. villages).\n- **Surveillance and screening (additions):**\n  - *Surveillance methods: passive reporting and WHO STEPwise domains:* STEPS approach for NCD risk factors (behaviour, physical, biochemical).\n  - *Lead-time bias in screening:* see Section 8.3.\n  - *Pearl:* in STEPwise surveillance, step 1 is questionnaire; step 2 is physical measurements; step 3 is biochemical.\n- **Repeated / asked-once PSM topics:**\n  - *Biostatistics:* see Section 8.5.\n  - *Health-care delivery and urban health:* urban health centres; urban slum programmes.\n  - *Disaster management:* phases (mitigation, preparedness, response, recovery); triage.\n  - *Measures of association:* relative risk, odds ratio, attributable risk.\n  - *International health initiatives:* WHO, UNICEF, Global Fund; SDGs (sustainable development goals).\n  - *School health services:* screening, immunisation, nutrition, health education.\n  - *Pearl:* attributable risk is the difference in incidence between exposed and unexposed.\n","PSM_UNIT_02":"## 8. Preventive and Social Medicine (PSM) (18-22 questions)\n\n### 8.6 Communicable diseases and surveillance\n\n- **Herd immunity:** threshold = 1 - (1 / R0).\n- **R0 (basic reproduction number):** average number of secondary cases from one case in a fully susceptible population.\n- **Incubation period:** time from exposure to onset.\n- **Serial interval:** time between successive cases.\n- **Surveillance types:** passive, active, sentinel.\n- **Outbreak investigation:** confirm diagnosis; define case; epidemic curve; attack rate; line listing; hypothesis; control measures.\n- **Epidemic curve:** point source (single peak) vs propagated (serial waves).\n- **Pearl:** the epidemic curve pattern helps distinguish point source from person-to-person spread.\n\n**Specific diseases (PSM relevant)**\n- **Tuberculosis:** RNTCP (now National TB Elimination Programme, NTEP); DOTS; BCG; Mantoux; drug-sensitive regimen 2HRZE/4HR.\n- **Malaria:** National Vector Borne Disease Control Programme; Plasmodium falciparum; artemisinin combination therapy.\n- **Leprosy:** National Leprosy Eradication Programme; MDT; WHO classification.\n- **HIV programme:** NACO (National AIDS Control Organisation); ART (antiretroviral therapy); PrEP (pre-exposure prophylaxis, prevents HIV in high-risk people); PMTCT (prevention of mother-to-child transmission).\n- **Vector-borne:** Dengue (Aedes; NVBDCP); Chikungunya; Kala-azar; Japanese encephalitis (Culex; JE vaccine).\n- **Pearl:** National Health Programmes frequently appear as factual questions; learn the programme name and the principal target.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Rabies Category III** = Bite with bleeding -> Wound wash + ARV + Rabies Immunoglobulin!\n\n### 8.13 Immunisation and vaccines (PSM)\n\n- Cold chain: 2-8 C; vaccine vial monitor.\n- Vaccine vial monitor (VVM): stage changes indicate heat exposure.\n- Live vaccines should not be given to immunocompromised individuals.\n- Pearl: a vaccine vial monitor with a changed colour should be discarded.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Cold chain: Most heat sensitive** = OPV > Measles. Most freeze sensitive = HepB > Td > DPT.\n\n## 31. Community Medicine (PSM) (additions)\n\n- **Immunisation and cold chain (additions):**\n  - *Td schedule and tetanus prophylaxis for a clean wound after prior vaccination:* if fully vaccinated and last dose less than 5 years ago, no booster; if more than 5 years, give Td.\n  - *Open-vial policy:* reusable liquid vaccines in multidose vials can be used for up to 28 days after opening (reconstituted lyophilised within 6 hours); discard afterwards.\n  - *Live-vaccine contraindication in severe HIV-related immunodeficiency:* live vaccines (MMR, varicella) contraindicated when CD4 is low.\n  - *Japanese encephalitis vaccine route:* live attenuated SA 14-14-2 given subcutaneously; route depends on product.\n  - *Vaccine viability assessment:* vaccine vial monitor; freeze-indicator.\n  - *Pearl:* live vaccines are contraindicated in severe immunosuppression, including AIDS with low CD4.\n- **Outbreak, zoonotic and vector control (additions):**\n  - *Varicella outbreak control and duration of case isolation:* isolate until all lesions crusted (about 5 days after rash onset).\n  - *Urban rabies control through stray-dog control and canine vaccination:* ABC programme (animal birth control); vaccinate dogs; mass dog vaccination.\n  - *Flood-associated leptospirosis: zinc phosphide for rodent control:* rodenticide (not an insecticide); doxycycline prophylaxis.\n  - *Anopheles larva: absent siphon, palmate hairs and parallel resting position:* Anopheles identification (vs Culex with siphon).\n  - *Pearl:* Anopheles larvae lie parallel to the water surface and have no siphon; Culex has a siphon.\n- **Communicable disease programmes (additions):**\n  - *NTEP definition of cure and CBNAAT after smear-negative presumptive pulmonary TB:* CBNAAT (GeneXpert) for smear-negative presumptive TB; NTEP definition of cure (smear negative at end of treatment).\n  - *Syndromic STI management: grey kit for cervicitis:* syndromic management in STI clinics.\n  - *National Leprosy Eradication Programme logo identification:* programme identification.\n  - *Pearl:* CBNAAT is preferred for diagnosis of smear-negative presumptive TB; it detects rifampicin resistance.\n- **Natural history and transmission of disease (additions):**\n  - *Generation time in infectious-disease transmission:* time between infection of primary case and infection of secondary case (vs serial interval).\n  - *Iceberg phenomenon and submerged disease burden:* most cases are subclinical or undetected.\n  - *Tracking phenomenon of blood pressure from childhood to adulthood:* children with high BP tend to remain hypertensive.\n  - *Pearl:* the iceberg phenomenon describes the large unreported proportion of disease below the clinical surface.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Universal Immunization Program (UIP) at a Glance\n\n| Age | Vaccines Administered | Dose & Route | Site |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| At Birth | BCG, OPV-0, HepB birth dose | 0.05 mL ID, 2 drops oral, 0.5 mL IM | Left deltoid, Oral, Anterolateral thigh |\n| 6, 10, 14 Weeks | Pentavalent, bOPV, Rota, fIPV, PCV | 0.5 mL IM, 2 drops, 5 drops, 0.1 mL ID | Thigh, Oral, Oral, Right deltoid, Thigh |\n| 9 Months | Measles-Rubella (MR-1), PCV booster, Vit A | 0.5 mL SC, 0.5 mL IM, 1 mL (1 lakh IU) | Right arm, Thigh, Oral |\n| 16 - 24 Months | MR-2, DPT booster-1, OPV booster | 0.5 mL SC, 0.5 mL IM, 2 drops oral | Right arm, Left thigh, Oral |\n","PSM_UNIT_03":"## 8. Preventive and Social Medicine (PSM) (18-22 questions)\n\n### 8.7 Nutrition and food\n\n- **Recommended daily allowance:** protein 1 g/kg/day (adult).\n- **Energy requirement:** approximately 2400 kcal/day for adult male (sedentary).\n- **Iodine deficiency disorders:** goitre, cretinism; iodised salt; universal salt iodisation.\n- **Vitamin A:** prophylaxis programme in children under 5 (two doses per year).\n- **Iron:** IFA supplementation for pregnant women and adolescents (anaemia control).\n- **Pearl:** the most common cause of anaemia in India is iron deficiency; IFA is the mainstay of prophylaxis.\n\n### 8.8 Water, sanitation and environment\n\n- **Chlorination:** residual chlorine 0.2-0.5 mg/L (after 30 minutes contact).\n- **Water quality:** coliform (MPN, membrane filter) indicates faecal contamination; E. coli is the index organism.\n- **Fluorosis:** excess fluoride above 1.5 mg/L; dental and skeletal fluorosis.\n- **Arsenicosis:** groundwater contamination; skin pigmentation, hyperkeratosis.\n- **Waste disposal:** sanitary landfill; incineration; biomedical waste (colour-coded bins).\n- **Air pollution:** particulate matter (PM2.5); respiratory disease.\n- **Pearl:** chlorine is the most commonly used disinfectant for drinking water.\n\n### 8.9 Occupational health\n\n- **Pneumoconiosis:** silicosis (quartz; eggshell calcification of hilar nodes), asbestosis (ferruginous bodies; pleural plaques; mesothelioma), coal worker's pneumoconiosis.\n- **Byssinosis:** chest tightness and breathlessness from cotton or flax dust exposure ('Monday fever'); a byssinosis-type occupational lung disease.\n- **Lead poisoning:** basophilic stippling; anaemia; colic; lead line; chelation with DMSA or EDTA.\n- **Noise-induced hearing loss:** NIHL; high frequency (4000 Hz notch).\n- **Radiation:** ALARA principle; lead apron; dosimeter.\n- **Pearl:** silicosis is associated with TB and eggshell calcification of hilar lymph nodes.\n\n### 8.10 Health care delivery\n\n- **Primary health centre (PHC):** 30,000 population (plains); 20,000 (hilly/tribal); 1 medical officer; 4-6 sub-centres.\n- **Sub-centre:** 5000 population (plains); 3000 (hilly); 1 ANM (auxiliary nurse midwife).\n- **Community health centre (CHC):** 1,20,000 population (plains); four specialists (surgeon, physician, gynaecologist, paediatrician).\n- **Accredited social health activist (ASHA):** one per 1000 population; community link.\n- **District hospital:** the main referral unit for a district, providing specialist services and referral support to PHCs and CHCs.\n- **Pearl:** the PHC is the first point of contact; the CHC is the first referral level.\n\n### 8.11 Health indicators\n\n- **Crude birth rate:** births per 1000 population per year.\n- **Crude death rate:** deaths per 1000 population per year.\n- **Natural increase:** birth rate minus death rate.\n- **Total fertility rate (TFR):** average number of children a woman would have; replacement level 2.1.\n- **Life expectancy:** average years of life remaining.\n- **Pearl:** replacement level TFR is about 2.1 children per woman.\n\n### 8.12 Population and demographic transition\n\n- **Demographic transition model:** stage 1 (high birth, high death), stage 2 (falling death, high birth), stage 3 (falling birth), stage 4 (low birth, low death).\n- **Pyramid types:** expansive (developing), constrictive (developed), stationary (in between).\n- **Pearl:** India is in the late stage 2 to early stage 3 of the demographic transition.\n\n### 8.14 Health economics and management\n\n- **Planning cycle:** situation analysis; priority setting; implementation; evaluation.\n- **Management functions:** planning, organising, staffing, directing, controlling (POSDCORB).\n- **Cost-effectiveness:** cost of an intervention per unit of health gain (here, cost per DALY averted).\n- **Disability-adjusted life year (DALY):** years of life lost plus years lived with disability.\n- **Quality-adjusted life year (QALY):** life years weighted by quality.\n- **Pearl:** DALY = YLL + YLD.\n\n### 8.15 Epidemiological quick pearls\n\n- CFR is cases-based; mortality rate is population-based.\n- PHC: 30,000 population (plains); CHC: 1,20,000 population.\n\n## 31. Community Medicine (PSM) (additions)\n\n- **Demography and health indicators:**\n  - *Mortality and burden-of-disease indicators:* DALY = YLL + YLD; years of potential life lost (YPLL) measures premature death.\n  - *De facto census enumeration:* counts people where they are on census night (vs de jure: usual residence).\n  - *Human Development Index:* life expectancy, education, income (GNI per capita).\n  - *Demographic-cycle stage identification:* stage 2 (high birth, falling death) to stage 3 (falling birth).\n  - *Pearl:* de facto counts people where they are; de jure counts where they usually live.\n- **Environmental health and water sanitation:**\n  - *Kata thermometer:* measures cooling power of air (air movement and thermal environment).\n  - *Fluorosis prevention, Nalgonda technique:* defluoridation with alum and lime; bone fluorosis; dental fluorosis.\n  - *Water-sample collection for bacteriological examination during an outbreak:* sterile bottles; sodium thiosulphate for chlorinated samples; examine within 6 hours.\n  - *Air Quality Index categories:* good, satisfactory, moderately polluted, poor, very poor, severe.\n  - *Illuminance and its SI unit:* lux (lumen per square metre).\n  - *Pearl:* the Nalgonda technique uses alum and lime for defluoridation of water.\n- **Nutritional assessment and dietary standards (additions):**\n  - *Child malnutrition: severe acute malnutrition referral; stunting and infection as a conditioning factor:* infection worsens stunting; refer SAM.\n  - *Global Hunger Index components:* undernourishment, child wasting, child stunting, child mortality.\n  - *Recommended Dietary Allowance:* coverage of nearly all healthy individuals (mean plus two SD).\n  - *Recommended dietary cholesterol intake:* below 300 mg/day (less than 200 for high-risk).\n  - *Pearl:* the Global Hunger Index uses four indicators.\n- **Nutrition programmes and nutrition-related diseases (additions):**\n  - *Lathyrism (beta-ODAP from Lathyrus sativus):* spastic paraparesis; avoid excess Khesari dal; prevention by diversified diet.\n  - *Applied Nutrition Programme and UNICEF support:* nutrition education programme in India.\n  - *ICDS age-group coverage for childhood nutrition:* children under 6 and pregnant and lactating mothers; supplementary nutrition.\n  - *Weekly iron-folic acid supplementation for adolescents:* weekly IFA for anaemia prevention (WIFS).\n  - *Pearl:* lathyrism is caused by beta-ODAP in Lathyrus sativus; spastic paraparesis.\n- **Maternal and newborn health programmes (additions):**\n  - *Essential newborn care: delayed bathing and prevention of hypothermia:* bathe after 24 hours; dry, warm; skin-to-skin contact.\n  - *Janani Suraksha Yojana cash incentives for urban institutional delivery:* cash incentive for institutional delivery (urban and rural).\n  - *ASHA antenatal nutrition counselling:* ASHA facilitates antenatal visits and counselling.\n  - *Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (JSSK) expansion:* free drugs, diagnostics, transport for mothers and sick newborns.\n  - *Pearl:* JSSK provides free services including transport to pregnant women and sick infants.\n- **Health planning and communication (additions):**\n  - *Health-programme evaluation: process indicators and achievement of objectives:* process (inputs, activities) and outcome (results).\n  - *Semantic barriers in health communication:* jargon, language mismatch, technical terms.\n  - *Pearl:* evaluation uses process indicators (inputs and activities) and outcome indicators (achievements).\n- **Biomedical waste management and disinfection (additions):**\n  - *Biomedical-waste segregation of throat swabs and broken vaccine vials:* yellow bag (infectious), white or blue (sharps and glass).\n  - *Disinfection of a blood spill:* 1% sodium hypochlorite (or 0.5% for surfaces); absorb, then disinfect.\n  - *Pearl:* broken glass vials go to the puncture-proof container (white or blue).\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules 2016 Summary\n\n| Color Category | Type of Waste | Treatment & Disposal Method | Key Examples |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Yellow Bag | Human anatomical, animal waste, soiled dressings, expired drugs | Incineration / Plasma Pyrolysis | Placenta, amputated limbs, cotton swabs, chemotherapy |\n| Red Bag | Contaminated recyclable plastics | Autoclaving followed by Shredding / Recycling | IV tubes, bottles, catheters, urine bags, gloves |\n| White Container | Waste sharps (needles, scalpels, blades) | Puncture-proof container -> Autoclave + Encapsulation | Needles, syringes with fixed needles, surgical blades |\n| Blue Box | Glassware and metallic body implants | Disinfection (sodium hypochlorite) / Autoclaving | Broken medicine ampoules, glass bottles, orthopedic implants |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **BMW Colors: Yellow** = Burn (incinerate); Red = Recycle (plastics); White = Sharps; Blue = Glass.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **MUAC: Red (< 11.5 cm)** = Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM). Yellow (11.5-12.5 cm) = Moderate.\n","PSYCH_U1":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.4 Psychiatry\n\n- **Schizophrenia:** positive (hallucinations, delusions), negative (apathy, blunting), cognitive symptoms; DSM-5 duration 6 months; antipsychotics (risperidone, olanzapine, clozapine for resistant); clozapine requires neutrophil monitoring (agranulocytosis).\n- **Depression:** SIGECAPS (sleep, interest, guilt, energy, concentration, appetite, psychomotor, suicide); 5 of 9 for 2 weeks; SSRIs first line; MAOIs (tranylcypromine), TCAs (amitriptyline), ECT for severe.\n- **Bipolar disorder:** mania (elevated mood, decreased sleep, grandiosity, flight of ideas); lithium (therapeutic index narrow; monitor levels, thyroid, kidney); valproate; lamotrigine for depression.\n- **Neuroleptic malignant syndrome:** fever, rigidity, autonomic instability, raised CK; dantrolene, bromocriptine, supportive care.\n- **Pearl:** clozapine requires regular neutrophil monitoring due to agranulocytosis; lithium has a narrow therapeutic index.\n- **Pearl:** neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a medical emergency; stop the antipsychotic.\n\n#### Table: Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) vs Serotonin Syndrome\n\n| Feature | Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) | Serotonin Syndrome |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Precipitating Agents | D2 dopamine antagonists (Haloperidol, Fluphenazine) | Serotonergic drugs (SSRIs, MAOIs, TCAs, MDMA) |\n| Neuromuscular Signs | Lead-pipe rigidity, hyporeflexia | Hyperreflexia, clonus (ocular/ankle), tremor |\n| Onset & Pupils | Gradual (1-3 days); Normal pupils | Rapid (< 12 hours); Dilated pupils (mydriasis) |\n| Laboratory | Marked CK elevation (> 10,000 U/L), leukocytosis | Mild CK elevation |\n| Antidote / Treatment | Discontinue drug + Dantrolene / Bromocriptine | Discontinue drug + Cyproheptadine |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **SIGECAPS (MDD Criteria):** Sleep changes, Interest lost (anhedonia), Guilt/worthlessness, Energy fatigue, Concentration drop, Appetite/weight changes, Psychomotor changes, Suicidal ideation (>= 5 of 9 for >= 2 weeks).\n\n## 26. Psychiatry (additions)\n\n- **Sexual and paraphilic disorders:**\n  - *Exhibitionistic disorder:* exposing genitals to unsuspecting persons for sexual gratification; treatment: CBT, SSRIs, anti-androgens in severe cases.\n  - *Transvestic disorder:* sexual arousal from cross-dressing with distress or impairment; psychotherapy.\n  - *Premature ejaculation:* squeeze technique as behavioural management; SSRIs (dapoxetine).\n  - *Pearl:* transvestic disorder requires distress or impairment, unlike cross-dressing alone.\n- **Psychotic disorders and psychopathology:**\n  - *Formal thought disorder, derailment:* shift between unrelated topics; schizophrenia.\n  - *Delusional perception:* assigning delusional meaning to a normal perception (Kurt Schneider first rank symptom).\n  - *Command hallucinations after violence:* formal psychiatric and forensic assessment; risk of harm to others.\n  - *Pearl:* delusional perception is a first-rank symptom of schizophrenia (Schneider).\n- **Antipsychotic adverse effects:** EPS (dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia); NMS (fever, rigidity, autonomic instability, CK raised); hyperprolactinaemia; metabolic (weight, diabetes); QT prolongation (sertindole, haloperidol).\n- **Mood disorders:** depression (see 9.4); bipolar (lithium, valproate); cyclothymia; dysthymia (2 years of low mood).\n- **Psychological concepts and learning:** classical conditioning (Pavlov); operant conditioning (Skinner, reinforcement, punishment); modelling (Bandura); systematic desensitisation (Wolpe); cognitive theories (Beck).\n  - *Pearl:* systematic desensitisation is a behavioural treatment for phobias using graded exposure with relaxation.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Extrapyramidal Symptoms (EPS) Timeline & Treatment\n\n| EPS Type | Onset | Clinical Features | Treatment of Choice |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Acute Dystonia | Hours to days | Torticollis, oculogyric crisis, laryngospasm | Anticholinergics (Benztropine / Diphenhydramine IM/IV) |\n| Akathisia | Days to weeks | Subjective and objective motor restlessness, pacing | Centrally acting beta-blocker (Propranolol) |\n| Pseudoparkinsonism | Weeks to months | Cogwheel rigidity, resting tremor, bradykinesia | Trihexyphenidyl / Benztropine |\n| Tardive Dyskinesia | Months to years | Orofacial dyskinesia, choreiform movements | VMAT-2 inhibitors (Valbenazine / Deutetrabenazine) |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Wernicke Encephalopathy Triad (CAN):** Confusion (encephalopathy), Ataxia, Nystagmus / ophthalmoplegia. Administer Thiamine BEFORE Dextrose!\n","PSYCH_U2":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.4 Psychiatry\n\n- **Anxiety disorders:** generalised anxiety; panic disorder; phobias; OCD (obsessions, compulsions; SSRIs at high dose, CBT).\n- **Personality disorders:** Cluster B (antisocial, borderline, histrionic, narcissistic); Cluster A (paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal); Cluster C (avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive).\n- **Substance use:** alcohol withdrawal (tremor, tachycardia, delirium tremens at 48-72 hours; benzodiazepines, thiamine); opioid (methadone, buprenorphine); nicotine (varenicline).\n\n#### Table: Alcohol Withdrawal Syndromes & Timeline\n\n| Stage | Onset After Cessation | Clinical Presentation | Treatment |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Minor Withdrawal | 6 - 12 hours | Tremulousness, insomnia, mild anxiety, tachycardia | Benzodiazepines (Chlordiazepoxide) |\n| Withdrawal Seizures | 12 - 48 hours | Generalized tonic-clonic convulsions | IV Lorazepam / Diazepam |\n| Alcoholic Hallucinosis | 12 - 48 hours | Visual or auditory hallucinations with intact sensorium | Benzodiazepines + supportive |\n| Delirium Tremens (DT) | 48 - 96 hours | Delirium, gross tremors, fever, severe autonomic instability | High-dose IV Benzodiazepines (ICU) |\n\n#### Table: Cluster Classification of Personality Disorders\n\n| Cluster | Descriptor | Disorders Included | Core Defense / Therapy |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Cluster A | Odd, Eccentric | Paranoid, Schizoid, Schizotypal | Projection / Social detachment |\n| Cluster B | Dramatic, Erratic | Antisocial, Borderline, Histrionic, Narcissistic | Splitting / Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) |\n| Cluster C | Anxious, Fearful | Avoidant, Dependent, Obsessive-Compulsive (OCPD) | Avoidance, Reaction formation / CBT |\n\n## 26. Psychiatry (additions)\n\n- **Anxiety disorders:** generalised anxiety (6 months of excessive worry); panic disorder (recurrent attacks; SSRIs, CBT); social phobia; specific phobia (exposure therapy); OCD (SSRIs, clomipramine, CBT with exposure and response prevention).\n- **Alcohol-related disorders:** alcohol withdrawal (delirium tremens; benzodiazepines; thiamine before glucose); Wernicke-Korsakoff (thiamine); alcoholic hallucinosis; disulfiram.\n- **Trauma and dissociative disorders:** PTSD (re-experiencing, avoidance, hyperarousal; trauma-focused CBT, SSRIs such as sertraline, paroxetine); acute stress disorder; dissociative amnesia; dissociative identity disorder.\n- **Personality disorders:** Cluster B (antisocial, borderline, histrionic, narcissistic); Cluster A (paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal); Cluster C (avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive). Borderline: DBT is the most effective psychotherapy.\n- **Eating disorders:** anorexia nervosa (BMI below 17.5; amenorrhoea; lanugo; CBT-E, refeeding); bulimia nervosa (binge-purge; CBT); binge eating disorder (lisdexamfetamine).\n  - *Pearl:* anorexia nervosa carries the highest mortality among psychiatric disorders.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **PTSD Timeline:** Symptoms < 1 month = Acute Stress Disorder (ASD); Symptoms > 1 month = Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD). Prazosin for PTSD nightmares.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Anorexia Refeeding Hallmark:** Hypophosphatemia, Hypokalemia, Hypomagnesemia due to carbohydrate-stimulated insulin secretion driving ions into cells.\n","RAD_U1":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.8 Radiology\n\n- **X-ray basics:** radiolucent (air, fat); radio-opaque (bone, metal, calcium); Hounsfield units (CT): air -1000, fat -100, water 0, bone +1000.\n- **Contrast:** iodinated (renal risk, allergy); barium (GI); gadolinium (MRI; nephrogenic systemic fibrosis).\n- **MRI:** good for soft tissue, brain, spine, joints; contraindicated with pacemakers and metallic implants (depends).\n- **Ultrasound:** no radiation; good for gallbladder, obstetric, thyroid, renal; operator dependent.\n- **CT:** head trauma, pulmonary embolism (CT pulmonary angiography), haemorrhage.\n- **Radiation protection:** justification, optimisation (ALARA), dose limits; lead aprons; time, distance, shielding.\n- **Pearl:** Hounsfield unit for water is 0; bone is +1000; air is -1000.\n\n#### Table: CT Attenuation Scale (Hounsfield Units)\n\n| Tissue Type | Hounsfield Unit (HU) Value | Appearance on Standard Window |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Air | -1000 HU | Pitch Black |\n| Fat (Adipose) | -120 to -30 HU | Dark gray |\n| Water / Urine / CSF | 0 HU (-10 to +10) | Neutral gray |\n| Muscle / Soft Tissue | +35 to +50 HU | Intermediate gray |\n| Acute Clotted Blood | +60 to +80 HU | Hyperdense (Bright white) |\n| Cortical Bone / Contrast | +700 to +1000+ HU | Extremely dense bright white |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **ALARA Tenets:** Time (minimize duration), Distance (maximize distance via inverse-square law: 1/d^2), Shielding (0.5 mm lead apron attenuates >90% scatter).\n\n## 10. High-Yield Image Areas\n\n- **Chest X-ray:** Kerley B lines (interstitial oedema); silhouette sign; cannon ball (metastases); egg-on-side (TGA); boot-shaped heart (Tetralogy of Fallot); figure-of-3 sign (coarctation); \"sail\" sign; pneumothorax (visceral pleural line, no lung markings).\n- **Ultrasound:** double bubble (duodenal atresia); cystic hygroma; whirlpool sign (volvulus); bull's-eye (intussusception).\n\n## 27. Radiodiagnosis (additions)\n\n- **Pulmonary infection imaging:**\n  - *Pulmonary consolidation on CT with air bronchograms:* pneumonia; air bronchograms indicate alveolar filling with air-bronchus visible.\n  - *Right middle lobe consolidation on chest radiograph:* silhouette sign of right heart border.\n  - *Right-lung opacity differential:* consolidation vs collapse vs effusion.\n  - *Pearl:* the silhouette sign indicates which lobe is affected (loss of an adjacent border).\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Bone Tumor Radiographic Signatures\n\n| Neoplasm | Typical Location | Classic Periosteal / Radiographic Sign | Key Pathology |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Osteosarcoma | Metaphysis (Knee) | Sunburst spiculation, Codman's triangle | Immature malignant osteoid bone matrix |\n| Ewing Sarcoma | Diaphysis of long bones | Multilayered 'Onion-skin' lamellated reaction | Small round blue cells, t(11;22) translocation |\n| Giant Cell Tumor | Epiphysis (abuts joint) | Expansile 'Soap-bubble' radiolucency | Multinucleated giant osteoclasts, RANKL-positive |\n| Unicameral Bone Cyst | Proximal humerus/femur | 'Fallen fragment' sign after cortical fracture | Fluid-filled simple unicameral cavity |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Silhouette Sign Rule:** Right Heart Border obliteration = Right Middle Lobe; Left Heart Border obliteration = Lingula; Hemidiaphragm obliteration = Lower Lobe; Aortic Knob = Left Upper Lobe.\n","RAD_U2":"## 9. Short Subjects\n\n### 9.8 Radiology\n\n- **Pearl:** gadolinium contrast is associated with nephrogenic systemic fibrosis in renal impairment.\n\n## 10. High-Yield Image Areas\n\n- **MRI:** T2 (bright CSF); T1 (dark CSF); demyelination (periventricular plaques, Dawson fingers).\n\n## 11. Integrated Patterns (High-Yield Associations)\n\n- **Pearl:** Von Hippel-Lindau is linked to renal cell carcinoma and phaeochromocytoma; screen with abdominal MRI.\n\n## 27. Radiodiagnosis (additions)\n\n- **Genitourinary imaging:**\n  - *Genitourinary tuberculosis (putty kidney, sterile pyuria, renal calcification):* IVP shows calyceal destruction; calcification.\n  - *Cystoscopy in the diagnostic and therapeutic role:* TURP, biopsy, stone removal.\n  - *Heterogeneously enhancing renal-region mass on CT:* renal cell carcinoma vs angiomyolipoma (fat-containing).\n  - *Pearl:* putty kidney is caseous TB involving the kidney with calcification and a non-functioning kidney.\n- **Oesophageal imaging:**\n  - *Oesophageal foreign-body localisation on radiography:* lateral view; cervical oesophagus; coins are seen en face.\n  - *Button battery in the oesophagus:* double-ring or halo sign on X-ray; urgent removal (alkali injury).\n  - *Achalasia on high-resolution manometry:* progressive dysphagia; bird's beak on barium swallow; manometry confirms.\n  - *Pearl:* a button battery in the oesophagus needs urgent removal; alkaline burns.\n- **Vascular imaging:**\n  - *Unstable suspected aortic dissection:* transoesophageal echocardiography (TEE) at bedside.\n  - *Acute limb ischaemia:* spectral Doppler ultrasound (triphasic waveform normal; monophasic indicates ischaemia).\n  - *Pulsatile upper-cervical lesion on carotid angiography:* carotid body tumour (splaying of ICA and ECA) vs aneurysm.\n  - *Pearl:* carotid body tumour splays the internal and external carotid arteries on angiography.\n- **Repeated / asked-once radiology:**\n  - *Intracranial mass imaging:* glioma, meningioma (dural tail), metastases (ring enhancement), abscess.\n  - *Paediatric neuroimaging:* hydrocephalus, Chiari, cortical malformations.\n  - *Radiation physics and biology:* ALARA; ionising radiation dose; deterministic vs stochastic effects.\n  - *Congenital thoracic imaging:* congenital cystic adenomatoid malformation; pulmonary sequestration.\n  - *Mediastinal and thoracic masses:* thymoma (anterior mediastinum); lymphoma; teratoma.\n  - *Pancreatic and biliary imaging:* MRCP for biliary; CT for pancreatic masses.\n  - *Acute abdominal radiography:* free air (perforation); air-fluid levels (obstruction).\n  - *Gynaecologic pelvic MRI:* adenomyosis, fibroids, endometriosis, ovarian tumours.\n  - *Intracranial haemorrhage:* CT shows hyperdense blood; subarachnoid haemorrhage (blood in cisterns).\n  - *Musculoskeletal infection imaging:* MRI for osteomyelitis; bone marrow oedema, abscess.\n  - *Paediatric gastrointestinal imaging:* pyloric stenosis (ultrasound), intussusception (target sign), malrotation (upper GI contrast).\n  - *Nuclear imaging:* bone scan (metastases), thyroid scan, MIBG (phaeochromocytoma), PET-CT.\n  - *Congenital cardiac imaging:* echocardiography first; cardiac MRI for complex anatomy.\n  - *Trauma imaging:* FAST (focused assessment with sonography for trauma) looks for free fluid; CT and chest X-ray for detail; eFAST adds lung sliding for pneumothorax.\n  - *Pearl:* FAST is the bedside ultrasound for free fluid in abdominal trauma.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Intracranial Hemorrhages - Diagnostic Comparison\n\n| Type | Vessel Injured | CT Morphology | Suture Crossing & Clinical Course |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Epidural (EDH) | Middle Meningeal Artery | Biconvex / Lentiform hyperdensity | Does NOT cross sutures; Classic Lucid interval |\n| Subdural (SDH) | Bridging Cortical Veins | Crescentic / Concavoconvex shape | CROSSES sutures; Common in elderly & alcoholics |\n| Subarachnoid (SAH) | Ruptured Berry Aneurysm | Hyperdensity in basal cisterns ('Star of David') | Thunderclap headache; Blood in CSF / xanthochromia |\n| Diffuse Axonal (DAI) | Shearing of axons | Punctate microhemorrhages (SWI/GRE) | Severe coma with deceptively normal non-contrast CT |\n\n#### Table: Classic Barium Swallow & Fluoroscopic Signs\n\n| Pathology | Eponymic Radiographic Sign | Pathophysiological Mechanism |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Achalasia Cardia | Bird's Beak / Rat-tail sign | Anerobic loss of ganglion cells -> Non-relaxing LES |\n| Diffuse Esophageal Spasm | Corkscrew / Rosary-bead esophagus | Uncoordinated simultaneous non-propulsive contractions |\n| Carcinoma Esophagus | Apple-Core / Shouldering defect | Annular constricting infiltrating malignant tumor |\n| Intussusception | Target / Doughnut / Pseudokidney sign | Telescoping of proximal bowel segment into distal lumen |\n| Pyloric Stenosis | String sign / Caterpillar sign | Hypertrophied pyloric muscle canal elongation >= 14 mm |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Triphasic Liver CT Enhancement:** Hemangioma = Peripheral nodular puddling with centripetal fill-in. HCC = Rapid arterial 'wash-in' + rapid venous 'wash-out'. FNH = Central radiating stellate scar.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Bosniak Cyst Categories:** I = Simple benign (0% risk). II = Minimally complicated (0%). IIF = Follow-up (5%). III = Thick septa/wall (50% - surgery). IV = Malignant with solid nodules (>90% - surgery).\n","SURG_UNIT_01":"## 3. Surgery (20-25 questions)\n\n### 3.1 General surgical principles\n\n- **Asepsis and antisepsis:** sterilisation (heat, autoclave, ethylene oxide), disinfection (chemicals), antisepsis (skin preparation); surgical site infection prevention bundle includes antibiotic timing (within 60 minutes before incision, or 120 minutes for vancomycin and fluoroquinolones).\n- **Wound healing:** primary (edges approximated, minimal inflammation), secondary (granulation tissue, contraction), tertiary (delayed primary closure). Healing phases: inflammatory (0-3 days), proliferative (3-21 days, collagen synthesis), remodelling (weeks to years).\n- **Factors impairing healing:** diabetes, smoking, malnutrition (vitamin C, zinc, protein), corticosteroids, infection, radiation.\n- **Shock:** inadequate tissue perfusion. Classes of haemorrhagic shock (ATLS): class I (under 15% blood loss, normal vitals), class II (15-30%, tachycardia, normal BP), class III (30-40%, hypotension, confusion), class IV (over 40%, severe hypotension, obtundation).\n- **Pearl:** early shock is compensated (normal BP, tachycardia, cool peripheries); hypotension is a late sign.\n\n### 3.2 Trauma (ATLS)\n\n**Primary survey (ABCDE)**\n- **A - Airway with C-spine protection:** look for obstruction, facial fractures, stridor; jaw thrust, suction, airway adjuncts; definitive airway if GCS 8 or less or airway at risk.\n- **B - Breathing:** tension pneumothorax, open pneumothorax, flail chest, haemothorax; oxygen, chest drain.\n- **C - Circulation:** haemorrhage control (direct pressure, tourniquet for limbs); two large-bore IV lines; crystalloid 1 L bolus; blood if transfusion needed (massive transfusion protocol).\n- **D - Disability:** GCS, pupils, lateralising signs, blood glucose.\n- **E - Exposure and environment:** undress fully, prevent hypothermia.\n\n**Secondary survey:** head-to-toe examination, AMPLE history (allergies, medications, past history, last meal, events).\n\n**Head injury**\n- **GCS:** eye (4), verbal (5), motor (6); total 15. Mild: 13-15; moderate: 9-12; severe: 8 or less (intubate).\n- **Epidural haematoma:** middle meningeal artery; lucid interval; biconvex (lens-shaped) collection on CT that does not cross sutures; emergency craniotomy if above 30 mL or with midline shift.\n- **Subdural haematoma:** bridging veins; crescent-shaped collection crossing sutures; elderly and alcoholics; acute (surgery), chronic (burr-hole drainage).\n- **Subarachnoid haemorrhage:** thunderclap headache; CT within 6 hours is highly sensitive; lumbar puncture if CT negative; nimodipine for vasospasm.\n- **Pearl:** the lucid interval of an extradural haematoma is typical but not always present; a skull fracture is often the clue.\n\n**Abdominal trauma**\n- **FAST (focused assessment with sonography in trauma):** looks for free fluid in the hepatorenal pouch (Morison), splenorenal, pelvis, and pericardium.\n- **Management:** unstable patient with positive FAST goes straight to laparotomy; stable patient may have CT. Non-operative management of solid organ injury (spleen, liver) is used in stable patients with no peritonitis.\n- **Pearl:** a stable patient with splenic injury can be observed; splenectomy increases the risk of overwhelming post-splenectomy infection (pneumococcus, meningococcus, Haemophilus), so vaccinate.\n\n**Chest trauma**\n- **Tension pneumothorax:** trachea deviated away, absent breath sounds, hypotension, raised JVP; immediate needle decompression (second intercostal space mid-clavicular line, or fifth intercostal space anterior axillary line), then chest drain.\n- **Haemothorax:** dullness, absent breath sounds; drain (over 1500 mL initially, or continued drainage above 200 mL/h = thoracotomy).\n- **Flail chest:** segmental rib fractures (three or more ribs in two places); paradoxical movement; analgesia and ventilation.\n- **Cardiac tamponade:** Beck triad (hypotension, raised JVP, muffled heart sounds); pericardiocentesis or surgery.\n\n**Pelvic and long bone injury**\n- **Pelvic fracture:** haemorrhage from venous plexus; binder; angiography or pelvic packing.\n- **Compartment syndrome:** pain out of proportion, pain on passive stretch, paraesthesia, pallor, pulselessness (late). Fasciotomy within hours.\n- **Pearl:** pulselessness is a late sign of compartment syndrome; pain on passive stretch is the earliest.\n\n**Burns**\n- **Rule of nines:** head 9% (adult), each arm 9%, each leg 18%, anterior trunk 18%, posterior trunk 18%, perineum 1%.\n- **Depth:** superficial (epidermis, red, painful, no blisters), superficial partial-thickness (blisters, red, painful), deep partial-thickness (pale, less painful, slow capillary refill), full-thickness (white or charred, painless).\n- **Parkland formula:** fluid = 4 mL x weight (kg) x %TBSA over 24 hours, half in the first 8 hours (from time of injury), half over the next 16 hours.\n- **Complications:** hypovolaemic shock, inhalation injury (carbonaceous sputum, hoarseness, singed hair; bronchoscopy), electrolyte disturbance, sepsis, Curling ulcer (stress ulcer), contractures.\n- **Pearl:** circumferential full-thickness burns of the limbs or chest need escharotomy because the eschar does not stretch.\n\n**Hemorrhagic shock management in trauma**\n- Permissive hypotension (systolic 80-90 mmHg) in penetrating trauma without head injury.\n- Tranexamic acid within 3 hours of injury (CRASH-2).\n- Massive transfusion protocol: 1:1:1 ratio of packed red cells, plasma, and platelets.\n\n#### Table: ATLS Hemorrhagic Shock Classification\n\n| Parameter | Class I (< 15%) | Class II (15-30%) | Class III (30-40%) | Class IV (> 40%) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Blood Loss (mL) | < 750 mL | 750 - 1500 mL | 1500 - 2000 mL | > 2000 mL |\n| Heart Rate (bpm) | < 100 | > 100 | > 120 (Tachycardia) | > 140 (Extreme) |\n| Blood Pressure | Normal | Normal (Narrowed PP) | DECREASED (Hypotension) | Severely Decreased |\n| Urine Output (mL/h) | > 30 mL/hr | 20 - 30 mL/hr | 5 - 15 mL/hr (Oliguria) | Negligible / Anuria |\n| Resuscitation | Crystalloid alone | Crystalloid | Blood products (1:1:1) | Massive Transfusion Protocol |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Lethal Triad in Trauma:** Hypothermia + Acidosis + Coagulopathy -> Triggers Damage Control Surgery!\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Parkland Formula:** 4 mL x kg x % TBSA. Give 50% in first 8 hours from time of burn, 50% over next 16 hours.\n\n### 3.8 Neurosurgery and orthopaedic emergencies\n\n- **Spinal cord compression:** back pain, neurological signs, sphincter dysfunction; MRI and emergency decompression.\n- **Cauda equina syndrome:** urinary retention, saddle anaesthesia, bilateral leg weakness; emergency MRI and decompression within 48 hours.\n\n### 3.10 Surgical nutrition and fluids\n\n- **Parenteral nutrition:** given when enteral route is not possible; calories from dextrose (3.4 kcal/g), amino acids (4 kcal/g), lipid emulsion (10 kcal/g). Central line needed for hypertonic solutions.\n- **Refeeding syndrome:** hypophosphataemia, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia after starvation; prevent by slow feeding and electrolyte monitoring.\n- **Maintenance fluid:** 4-2-1 rule (4 mL/kg/h for first 10 kg, 2 mL/kg/h for next 10 kg, 1 mL/kg/h thereafter).\n- **Pearl:** the commonest cause of post-operative fever is atelectasis in the first 24-48 hours; the \"5 Fs\" approach (fever, fluid, feeding, fall, fluids) helps find causes.\n\n### 3.11 Pre-operative assessment\n\n- **ASA physical status:** I (healthy), II (mild systemic disease), III (severe), IV (life-threatening), V (moribund), VI (brain-dead donor).\n- **Cardiac risk:** RCRI (Revised Cardiac Risk Index).\n- **Anticoagulation:** warfarin reversal (vitamin K, prothrombin complex concentrate); DOAC interruption times depend on renal function.\n- **Pearl:** elective surgery is delayed for 2-4 weeks after myocardial infarction to reduce perioperative cardiac risk.\n\n## 21. Surgery (additions)\n\n- **Burns and reconstruction:**\n  - *Hyperbaric oxygen not routinely indicated for burns.*\n  - *Burn wound coverage:* dressing versus graft or flap; early excision and grafting for deep burns.\n  - *Skin-graft inosculation:* the process of blood vessel connection between graft and bed (around day 3-5).\n  - *Escharotomy:* for full-thickness circumferential burns to restore expansion and perfusion (limbs, chest).\n  - *Pearl:* escharotomy is needed for circumferential full-thickness burns that restrict chest expansion or limb perfusion.\n- **Abdominal trauma:**\n  - *CECT in haemodynamically stable blunt trauma:* FAST then CT; non-operative management for stable patients.\n  - *Penetrating abdominal trauma:* laparotomy for peritonitis, instability, evisceration; selective management otherwise.\n  - *Pearl:* penetrating abdominal trauma with peritonitis or instability goes straight to laparotomy.\n- **Chest trauma:**\n  - *Tension pneumothorax:* immediate needle decompression (second intercostal space, mid-clavicular) then chest drain.\n  - *Traumatic haemothorax:* intercostal chest drain; thoracotomy if output above 1500 ml initially or 200 ml/h for 2-4 hours.\n  - *Flail chest:* segmental rib fractures; respiratory support, analgesia, ventilation.\n  - *Pearl:* tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis; do not wait for X-ray.\n- **Necrotising soft-tissue infections:** Fournier gangrene (perineal necrotising fasciitis; polymicrobial; urgent debridement and broad-spectrum antibiotics; colostomy if needed).\n  - *Pearl:* Fournier gangrene is a surgical emergency needing urgent debridement; the infection is polymicrobial.\n- **Surgical techniques and equipment:** Romovac closed-suction drain (Jackson-Pratt type; closed suction); surgeon's knot (extra throw first); monofilament PDS (polydioxanone, slow absorbable, monofilament).\n- **Upper GI and oesophageal disease:**\n  - *Severe caustic oesophageal injury and feeding jejunostomy:* strictures; feeding jejunostomy; avoid oral feeding; steroids in selected cases.\n  - *Peptic-ulcer bleeding risk factors:* NSAIDs, H. pylori, steroids, anticoagulants, age.\n  - *Nissen fundoplication:* anti-reflux surgery (360-degree wrap of fundus).\n  - *Pearl:* the caustic injury needs early endoscopy; avoid induced vomiting.\n- **Neurosurgical emergencies:**\n  - *Subarachnoid haemorrhage diagnosis and angiography:* CT head within 6 hours; lumbar puncture; CT angiography or cerebral angiography; nimodipine.\n  - *Chronic subdural haematoma after trauma:* elderly; burr hole drainage.\n  - *Pearl:* in SAH, nimodipine prevents vasospasm.\n- **Surgical infection and sepsis:**\n  - *Hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch):* common site of intraperitoneal abscess and fluid; drain.\n  - *Perforated hollow viscus:* resuscitation and laparotomy.\n  - *qSOFA components:* respiratory rate 22 or more, altered mentation, systolic BP 100 or below.\n  - *Pearl:* in septic patients qSOFA of 2 or more predicts poor outcome.\n- **Scrotal and penile disorders:** testicular torsion (surgical emergency; within 6 hours); epididymo-orchitis; hydrocele; Fournier; Peyronie disease (penile curvature, plaque).\n- **Urologic trauma:** renal trauma (CT with contrast; conservative for most); bladder rupture (extraperitoneal: catheter; intraperitoneal: laparotomy); urethral injury (retrograde urethrogram before catheter).\n  - *Pearl:* in suspected urethral injury, do retrograde urethrogram before inserting a catheter.\n- **Urolithiasis and endourology:** renal colic (NSAIDs; alpha-blockers for stone expulsion); stones above 10 mm: PCNL; 5-10 mm: ureteroscopy or ESWL; calcium oxalate most common stone type.\n  - *Pearl:* struvite stones are due to urease-producing organisms (Proteus).\n- **Cleft and craniofacial surgery:** cleft lip (repair at about 3 months); cleft palate (repair at about 9-12 months); Pierre Robin sequence (micrognathia, glossoptosis).\n- **Surgical nutrition:** enteral feeding preferred if the gut works; parenteral nutrition for failed gut; refeeding syndrome (phosphate, potassium, magnesium).\n  - *Pearl:* refeeding syndrome is hypophosphataemia after starting feeding in malnourished patients.\n- **Urologic infections:** cystitis; pyelonephritis; prostatitis (fluoroquinolones); catheter-associated UTI; urosepsis.\n- **Skin tumours:** basal cell carcinoma (pearly, rolled edge; hedgehog pathway; local, rarely metastasises); squamous cell carcinoma (keratinising; metastasis risk); melanoma (Breslow depth, Clark level).\n  - *Pearl:* Breslow thickness is the most important prognostic factor in melanoma.\n- **Trauma resuscitation and triage:** green triage (minor, walking wounded); Glasgow Coma Scale calculation: E2 V3 M4 = 9 (GCS 9, moderate brain injury); Ringer lactate (balanced crystalloid); intraosseous access (emergency); adult choking first aid (back blows, abdominal thrusts).\n  - *Pearl:* in adult choking, alternate back blows and abdominal thrusts.\n- **Wounds and ulcers:** stage 4 pressure injury (full-thickness, exposed bone or tendon); trophic or chronic ulcer (venous, arterial, diabetic); VAC (negative pressure) at about -125 mmHg; hypertrophic scars and keloids (keloids extend beyond wound).\n  - *Pearl:* keloids extend beyond the original wound margin; hypertrophic scars do not.\n","SURG_UNIT_02":"## 3. Surgery (20-25 questions)\n\n### 3.3 Gastrointestinal surgery\n\n**Upper GI**\n- **Oesophageal atresia:** commonest type with distal tracheo-oesophageal fistula (type C); polyhydramnios, inability to pass a nasogastric tube, coughing and choking with feeds. Repair in the neonatal period.\n- **Achalasia:** loss of ganglion cells in Auerbach plexus; dysphagia to both solids and liquids; barium swallow shows bird's beak; manometry shows failure of relaxation of the lower oesophageal sphincter. Treatment: pneumatic dilatation, Heller myotomy, POEM.\n- **Peptic ulcer disease:** H. pylori testing (urea breath test, stool antigen); eradication therapy (clarithromycin, amoxicillin, PPI for 14 days). Surgery for perforation, bleeding not controlled by endoscopy, obstruction, or intractable disease.\n- **Gastric outlet obstruction:** vomiting of undigested food; surgery if malignant or refractory.\n- **Stomach cancer:** Virchow node (left supraclavicular); Krukenberg tumour (ovarian metastasis); linitis plastica; Helicobacter pylori is the main risk factor.\n- **Pearl:** perforated gastric ulcer is treated with a Graham patch (omental plication); perforated duodenal ulcer is commonly closed with omental patch.\n\n**Intestinal obstruction**\n- **Small bowel:** central distension, vomiting early, tinkling bowel sounds early, step-ladder appearance on abdominal X-ray. Common causes: adhesions (commonest), hernias, Crohn disease, malignancy.\n- **Large bowel:** peripheral distension, late vomiting, absent bowel sounds in advanced stage, haustral markings on X-ray. Common causes: colon cancer, volvulus, diverticulitis.\n- **Sigmoid volvulus:** coffee-bean sign on X-ray; decompression with flexible sigmoidoscopy; elective sigmoid colectomy.\n- **Caecal volvulus:** bird's beak sign; right colectomy if necrosis.\n- **Strangulated hernia:** surgical emergency; ischaemia of bowel within the sac; irreducible hernia with systemic signs.\n- **Pearl:** the presence of a fever and tachycardia with obstruction suggests strangulation or perforation, and needs urgent surgery.\n\n**Acute appendicitis**\n- **Clinical features:** periumbilical pain that migrates to the right iliac fossa (McBurney point), anorexia, nausea, low-grade fever, rebound tenderness, Rovsing sign, psoas sign, obturator sign.\n- **Alvarado score (MANTRELS):** migration of pain, anorexia, nausea or vomiting, tenderness in right iliac fossa, rebound tenderness, elevated temperature, leukocytosis, left shift. Score 7 or more: strong likelihood.\n- **Investigation:** ultrasound (first in children and women), CT (adults, if unclear).\n- **Treatment:** appendicectomy (laparoscopic or open); antibiotics alone for uncomplicated cases in selected patients.\n- **Pearl:** in pregnancy, appendicitis is the commonest non-obstetric surgical emergency; laparoscopy is safe in the second trimester.\n\n**Colorectal cancer**\n- **Lynch syndrome:** mismatch repair gene mutation (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2); early onset; right-sided tumours; high microsatellite instability.\n- **Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP):** APC gene mutation; hundreds of adenomas; prophylactic colectomy.\n- **Diagnosis:** colonoscopy with biopsy; CT for staging; CEA as a marker.\n- **Staging:** TNM; adjuvant chemotherapy for stage III and selected stage II.\n- **Pearl:** left-sided colon cancer presents with obstruction and change in bowel habit; right-sided presents with anaemia and weight loss.\n\n**Hernias**\n- **Inguinal hernia:** direct (medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, Hesselbach triangle, through the posterior wall, older men); indirect (lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, through the deep ring, congenital or acquired, children and young men).\n- **Femoral hernia:** below the inguinal ligament, medial to the femoral vein; more prone to strangulation.\n- **Repair:** Lichtenstein (tension-free mesh repair); laparoscopic TEP or TAPP.\n- **Pearl:** the femoral hernia is more likely to strangulate than an inguinal hernia, and surgery is recommended even when small.\n\n**Ulcerative colitis surgery:** indications include severe refractory disease, toxic megacolon, dysplasia, and bleeding. Restorative proctocolectomy with ileal pouch-anal anastomosis (IPAA) is the usual procedure.\n\n#### Table: Indirect vs Direct Inguinal Hernia\n\n| Feature | Indirect Inguinal Hernia | Direct Inguinal Hernia |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Deep Inguinal Ring | Traverses deep ring LATERAL to inferior epigastric artery | Protrudes MEDIAL to inferior epigastric artery through Hesselbach triangle |\n| Etiology | Congenital patent processus vaginalis | Acquired transversalis fascia weakness |\n| Scrotal Descent | Frequently descends into scrotum | Rarely descends into scrotum |\n| Ring Occlusion Test | Hernia controlled / does NOT protrude | Hernia NOT controlled / protrudes despite occlusion |\n| Strangulation Risk | Higher risk of strangulation | Lower risk of strangulation |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Inferior Epigastric Artery Rule: Indirect** = LATERAL to artery; Direct = MEDIAL to artery.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Goodsall's Rule: Anterior fistula** = Straight tract; Posterior fistula = Curved tract to 6 o'clock.\n\n### 3.7 Urology\n\n**Renal stones**\n- **Types:** calcium oxalate (commonest; radiopaque), uric acid (radiolucent; alkalinise urine), struvite (infection; staghorn; radiopaque), cystine (radiopaque; hereditary).\n- **Management:** ESWL for stones up to 2 cm; PCNL (percutaneous nephrolithotomy) for stones above 2 cm or staghorn; ureteroscopy for ureteric stones.\n- **Renal colic:** NSAIDs or opioids for pain; alpha-blockers (tamsulosin) to help passage of stones in the ureter.\n- **Pearl:** ESWL is less effective for lower pole stones and stones over 2 cm.\n\n**Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)**\n- **Symptoms:** LUTS (hesitancy, weak stream, incomplete emptying, frequency, nocturia).\n- **Management:** alpha-blockers (tamsulosin, alfuzosin); 5-alpha reductase inhibitors (finasteride, dutasteride) for large glands; TURP for retention or failure; laser or open prostatectomy for very large glands.\n- **Pearl:** 5-alpha reductase inhibitors reduce prostate size and PSA by about half, so PSA must be doubled when interpreting results.\n\n**Prostate cancer**\n- **Screening:** PSA; digital rectal examination; MRI-guided biopsy.\n- **Gleason score:** sum of two most common grades (out of 5); 6 (low), 7 (intermediate), 8-10 (high).\n- **Treatment:** active surveillance (low risk), radical prostatectomy, radiotherapy, androgen deprivation therapy.\n\n**Bladder cancer**\n- **Presentation:** painless haematuria (always investigate).\n- **Types:** urothelial carcinoma (commonest); squamous (schistosomiasis, chronic infection); adenocarcinoma (urachal).\n- **Management:** TURBT (transurethral resection of bladder tumour) for superficial disease; BCG intravesical for high-grade non-muscle-invasive disease; cystectomy for muscle-invasive.\n- **Pearl:** smoking and aromatic amines are the main risk factors; schistosomiasis causes squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder.\n\n**Urological emergencies**\n- **Testicular torsion:** sudden severe testicular pain, high-riding testis, absent cremasteric reflex; surgery (detorsion, orchidopexy) within 6 hours for salvage.\n- **Priapism:** prolonged erection more than 4 hours; ischaemic (low-flow; emergency aspiration and phenylephrine) vs non-ischaemic (high-flow).\n- **Acute retention:** catheterise; investigate cause.\n- **Fournier gangrene:** necrotising fasciitis of the perineum; urgent debridement and broad-spectrum antibiotics.\n\n### 3.9 Surgical infection\n\n- **Surgical site infection:** superficial (skin), deep (fascia), organ or space.\n- **Necrotising fasciitis:** severe pain out of proportion, skin changes, crepitus (gas in tissue), rapid spread; emergency debridement; broad-spectrum antibiotics (including clindamycin for toxin suppression).\n- **Anaerobic infection:** Bacteroides and Clostridium; foul smell; gas on X-ray.\n- **Gas gangrene:** Clostridium perfringens; crepitus; haemolytic anaemia; emergency debridement, hyperbaric oxygen, penicillin.\n- **Tetanus:** Clostridium tetani; spastic paralysis, risus sardonicus, opisthotonus; human tetanus immunoglobulin, metronidazole, benzodiazepines, muscle relaxants, ventilatory support.\n\n## 10. High-Yield Image Areas\n\n- **Abdomen X-ray:** coffee-bean sign (sigmoid volvulus); string of pearls (small bowel obstruction); thumbprinting (ischaemic colitis); step-ladder (small bowel); renal calculi (radio-opaque).\n\n## 21. Surgery (additions)\n\n- **Anorectal disease:**\n  - *Fistula-in-ano:* perianal abscess with recurrent discharge; Parks classification (intersphincteric, transsphincteric, suprasphincteric, extrasphincteric); seton, fistulotomy.\n  - *Anal canal lymphatic drainage below the pectinate line:* inguinal nodes (skin and lower anal canal); above the line: internal iliac nodes.\n  - *Pearl:* the dentate (pectinate) line divides the lymphatic drainage of the anal canal.\n- **Biliary disease:**\n  - *Recurrent CBD stone after cholecystectomy:* retained stones; ERCP with sphincterotomy.\n  - *Gallstone ileus (Rigler triad):* small bowel obstruction, pneumobilia, ectopic gallstone on X-ray.\n  - *Gallstone pancreatitis with raised cholestatic enzymes:* ERCP within 72 hours if cholangitis; cholecystectomy.\n  - *Strasberg type C bile duct injury:* leak from a cystic duct stump or aberrant duct; ERCP with stenting.\n  - *Pearl:* Rigler triad (small bowel obstruction, pneumobilia and gallstone in the bowel) indicates gallstone ileus.\n- **Hernias (additions):** direct and indirect inguinal hernia (see Anatomy); lateral femoral cutaneous nerve injury; strangulated umbilical hernia.\n- **Congenital urology:**\n  - *PUJ obstruction and vesicoureteric reflux:* pyeloplasty for PUJ; antibiotic prophylaxis and surgery for VUR.\n  - *Bladder exstrophy, urachal anomalies and posterior urethral valves:* exstrophy (bladder open on abdomen); PUV (boys; hydronephrosis; valve ablation).\n  - *Pearl:* posterior urethral valves cause hydronephrosis and bladder dysfunction in boys.\n","SURG_UNIT_03":"## 3. Surgery (20-25 questions)\n\n### 3.4 Hepatobiliary and pancreatic surgery\n\n**Gallstones**\n- **Biliary colic:** episodic right upper quadrant pain after fatty food, lasting 1-5 hours.\n- **Acute cholecystitis:** persistent pain, Murphy sign, fever, leukocytosis, raised CRP; ultrasound findings (gallbladder wall thickening above 3 mm, pericholecystic fluid, stones). HIDA scan confirms cystic duct obstruction.\n- **Tokyo Guidelines (TG18):** grade I (mild), II (moderate: WBC above 18,000, palpable mass, duration over 72 h), III (severe: organ dysfunction). Early laparoscopic cholecystectomy within 7 days for grade I and II.\n- **Choledocholithiasis:** CBD stones; raised bilirubin and ALP; dilated CBD on ultrasound. ERCP with sphincterotomy and stone extraction before or after cholecystectomy.\n- **Cholangitis:** Charcot triad (fever, jaundice, right upper quadrant pain); Reynolds pentad adds hypotension and altered mental status. Urgent ERCP and antibiotics.\n- **Gallstone ileus:** a large gallstone passes into the duodenum and lodges at the ileocaecal valve; Rigler triad (air in biliary tree, small bowel obstruction, ectopic gallstone on X-ray).\n- **Mirizzi syndrome:** stone in the Hartmann pouch compressing the common hepatic duct.\n- **Pearl:** Calot triangle (cystic duct, common hepatic duct, inferior surface of the liver) is dissected to identify the cystic artery and duct before clipping; the \"critical view of safety\" is required before cutting.\n\n**Liver abscess**\n- **Amoebic liver abscess:** Entamoeba histolytica; anchovy-sauce pus; metronidazole first-line; aspiration only if rupture is threatened or no response.\n- **Pyogenic liver abscess:** Klebsiella pneumoniae (diabetes, Asia); multiple; biliary origin; drainage plus antibiotics.\n\n**Portal hypertension**\n- **Causes:** pre-hepatic (portal vein thrombosis), intra-hepatic (cirrhosis), post-hepatic (Budd-Chiari, veno-occlusive disease).\n- **Management of variceal bleeding:** vasoactive drugs (terlipressin, octreotide), IV antibiotics, endoscopic band ligation, TIPS (transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt) if refractory, surgical shunts.\n- **Pearl:** the hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG) above 10 mmHg is associated with variceal bleeding; above 12 mmHg with a higher risk.\n\n**Pancreatic surgery**\n- **Pancreatic cancer:** adenocarcinoma of the head; painless obstructive jaundice with a palpable gallbladder (Courvoisier law: a palpable non-tender gallbladder with obstructive jaundice is unlikely to be from gallstones); CA-19-9; resection by Whipple procedure (pancreaticoduodenectomy) if resectable.\n- **Pancreatic pseudocyst:** collection of fluid with a fibrous wall after pancreatitis; drain endoscopically or surgically if large or symptomatic.\n- **Pearl:** a \"Courvoisier sign\" (palpable gallbladder, painless jaundice) points to pancreatic head cancer rather than gallstones.\n\n**Splenic surgery**\n- **Splenic injury:** observe if stable; splenectomy for unstable patient; spleen-preserving techniques where possible.\n- **Hereditary spherocytosis, ITP, and haemolytic anaemias** may need splenectomy; vaccinate first (pneumococcal, meningococcal, Haemophilus).\n\n#### Table: Calot's Triangle Anatomy & Contents\n\n| Boundary / Component | Anatomical Structure | Clinical Significance |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Superior Boundary | Inferior visceral surface of Liver (Segment V) | Dissection must stay below liver plate |\n| Medial Boundary | Common Hepatic Duct (CHD) | Must be protected; never clip without direct view! |\n| Lateral / Inferior | Cystic Duct | Must be divided; clip safely away from CBD |\n| Key Content | Cystic Artery (from Right Hepatic Artery) | Must be clipped after achieving Critical View of Safety |\n| Lymph Node Landmark | Cystic Node of Lund (Mascagni's node) | Overlies the cystic artery; reliable surgical landmark |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Courvoisier's Law:** Jaundice + Palpable non-tender Gallbladder = Malignant obstruction (Pancreatic head cancer), NOT stones!\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **OPSI Encapsulated Bacteria:** Streptococcus pneumoniae (#1, > 60%), Neisseria meningitidis, Haemophilus influenzae.\n\n## 21. Surgery (additions)\n\n- **Pancreatic disease:**\n  - *Palliation of gastric outlet obstruction in metastatic pancreatic cancer:* gastrojejunostomy or endoscopic stent.\n  - *Courvoisier sign:* palpable non-tender gallbladder with obstructive jaundice (usually pancreatic head carcinoma).\n  - *Pancreatic pseudocyst:* post-pancreatitis collection; drainage (endoscopic or surgical) if above 6 cm or symptomatic.\n  - *Puestow (lateral pancreaticojejunostomy):* for dilated pancreatic duct in chronic pancreatitis.\n  - *Pearl:* Courvoisier sign suggests malignant obstruction (pancreatic head or distal CBD).\n- **Paediatric GI surgery:**\n  - *Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis acid-base disturbance:* hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis; correct with IV fluids and potassium before pyloromyotomy.\n  - *Intussusception and the claw sign:* ultrasound shows target or claw sign; air or hydrostatic reduction.\n  - *Patent vitellointestinal duct:* Meckel-like remnant with umbilical discharge; excision.\n  - *Pearl:* correct hypochloraemia and hypokalaemia before pyloromyotomy.\n- **Endocrine syndromes:** MEN 1 (pituitary, parathyroid, pancreas); MEN 2A and 2B (see 11); insulinoma (Whipple triad).\n  - *Pearl:* Whipple triad for insulinoma: hypoglycaemia during fasting, symptoms, relief with glucose.\n- **Thyroid disease (additions):** post-thyroidectomy hypocalcaemia (carpopedal spasm; Chvostek and Trousseau signs; IV calcium; check PTH); urinary iodine in a child with raised TSH and normal thyroid hormones (congenital hypothyroidism work-up); initial evaluation of thyroid swelling (serum TSH, then ultrasound and FNAC); thyroglossal duct cyst (midline, moves on tongue protrusion).\n  - *Pearl:* Chvostek sign (facial twitch on tapping) indicates hypocalcaemia.\n- **Hepatic and portal disease:** hepatic hydatid disease (Echinococcus granulosus; cyst wall; surgery or PAIR); Gharbi type II hydatid cyst (detached membrane, water-lily sign on imaging); hepatocellular carcinoma and AFP; uncomplicated liver abscess (amoebic; metronidazole; percutaneous drainage if large); portal hypertension and oesophageal varices (band ligation; beta-blockers; TIPS).\n  - *Pearl:* water-lily sign and detached membrane indicate a Gharbi type II hydatid cyst.\n","SURG_UNIT_04":"## 3. Surgery (20-25 questions)\n\n### 3.5 Breast surgery\n\n**Triple assessment:** clinical examination, imaging (mammography for over 40s in most guidelines; ultrasound for under 40s and dense breasts), and core needle biopsy (histology and receptor status).\n\n**BI-RADS categories:** 1 (negative), 2 (benign), 3 (probably benign, follow-up), 4 (suspicious, biopsy; 4A, 4B, 4C by likelihood), 5 (highly suggestive of malignancy), 6 (known malignancy).\n\n**Benign breast conditions**\n- **Fibroadenoma:** mobile, rubbery, well-circumscribed; young women; core biopsy or excision.\n- **Fibrocystic change:** diffuse lumpiness, cyclical pain; reassurance.\n- **Duct ectasia:** dilated subareolar ducts, nipple discharge, nipple retraction; periductal inflammation; excision if symptomatic.\n- **Fat necrosis:** after trauma or surgery; firm mass; mimic of carcinoma; calcification on mammography.\n- **Pearl:** a fibroadenoma that grows in a postmenopausal woman needs excision.\n\n**Breast carcinoma**\n- **Types:** invasive ductal (most common), invasive lobular (E-cadherin loss; signet ring cells; single-file pattern; often bilateral or multifocal), ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS), Paget disease of the nipple (eczematous change; underlying carcinoma).\n- **Receptor status:** ER, PR, HER2 (guides therapy: tamoxifen or aromatase inhibitor; trastuzumab; endocrine therapy).\n- **Surgical options:** breast-conserving surgery (lumpectomy) plus radiotherapy; mastectomy (simple, modified radical, radical). Margins: clear margin of 2 mm or more (in invasive) or 1 mm (in DCIS, some guidelines).\n- **Axillary staging:** sentinel lymph node biopsy (SLNB) for clinically node-negative disease; axillary clearance if positive nodes.\n- **Pearl:** breast-conserving surgery needs radiotherapy afterwards; mastectomy generally does not, unless high risk features.\n\n### 3.6 Thyroid, parathyroid and vascular surgery\n\n**Thyroid nodules**\n- **Fine needle aspiration cytology (FNAC):** Bethesda classification (I-VI). Bethesda VI (malignant), III or IV (indeterminate), V (suspicious).\n- **Papillary carcinoma:** commonest; ground glass nuclei, psammoma bodies, Orphan Annie nuclei; spreads to lymph nodes; excellent prognosis.\n- **Follicular carcinoma:** capsular or vascular invasion needed for diagnosis (cannot diagnose on FNAC); haematogenous spread (bones, lungs).\n- **Medullary carcinoma:** parafollicular C cells; calcitonin raised; MEN 2A and 2B; RET mutation; screen relatives.\n- **Anaplastic carcinoma:** elderly; very aggressive; poor prognosis.\n- **Treatment:** total thyroidectomy for malignancy; postoperative radioactive iodine for papillary and follicular; levothyroxine suppression.\n\n**Hyperparathyroidism**\n- **Primary:** parathyroid adenoma (most common); hypercalcaemia, low phosphate, high PTH; renal stones, bones, stones, groans, psychic moans.\n- **Parathyroidectomy:** indicated for symptomatic disease, complications, or raised calcium above 1 mg/dL over the upper limit.\n\n**Vascular surgery**\n- **Aortic dissection:** Stanford type A (ascending aorta, surgery) vs type B (descending, medical management unless complicated).\n- **Abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA):** repair if above 5.5 cm (or growing rapidly); endovascular or open repair.\n- **Peripheral arterial disease:** intermittent claudication; ABPI (ankle-brachial pressure index) below 0.9; statin, antiplatelet, exercise; revascularisation for critical limb ischaemia.\n- **Pearl:** the 5.5 cm threshold for AAA repair is for men; women are repaired at smaller sizes.\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **AAA Surgical Threshold:** Diameter >= 5.5 cm in men, >= 5.0 cm in women, or rapid growth > 1 cm/year.\n\n## 21. Surgery (additions)\n\n- **Urologic oncology:**\n  - *Low-risk prostate cancer in an elderly patient:* active surveillance or watchful waiting; PSA monitoring; radiotherapy or surgery if progression.\n  - *Prostate cancer spread through Batson venous plexus:* vertebral metastases via the valveless vertebral venous plexus.\n  - *Penile cancer:* partial or total penectomy depending on local extent; inguinal nodes managed by risk-directed dissection or sentinel node.\n  - *Pearl:* Batson plexus explains vertebral spread of prostate cancer (valveless veins).\n- **Colorectal disease:**\n  - *Altemeier procedure for full-thickness rectal prolapse:* perineal approach; rectal resection and anastomosis.\n  - *Pelvic autonomic injury after abdominoperineal resection:* sexual and urinary dysfunction; nerve-sparing technique.\n  - *Juvenile hamartomatous rectal polyp:* benign; juvenile polyposis; polypectomy; risk of cancer in polyposis syndromes.\n  - *Pearl:* Altemeier is the perineal repair for full-thickness prolapse in elderly patients.\n- **Surgical oncology principles:** margins, sentinel node, neoadjuvant and adjuvant therapy, staging (TNM).\n- **Breast disease (additions):** inflammatory breast cancer (dermal lymphatic invasion; T4d; peau d'orange); oestrogen-receptor positivity (good prognosis, endocrine therapy); liver metastases (palliative or systemic); post-mastectomy lymphoedema; phyllodes tumour (leaf-like histology; wide excision).\n  - *Pearl:* inflammatory breast cancer is T4d and needs chemotherapy first.\n- **Head and neck tumours:** parotid tumours (pleomorphic adenoma most common; Warthin tumour; facial nerve preservation); suspicious lip lesion: deep incisional biopsy from the edge; toluidine blue staining of suspicious oral lesions; buccal carcinoma with multiple nodes or extranodal extension: adjuvant radiotherapy.\n  - *Pearl:* pleomorphic adenoma is the most common benign parotid tumour and has a risk of malignant transformation.\n- **Vascular disease:**\n  - *Leriche syndrome (aortoiliac occlusion):* buttock claudication, impotence, absent femoral pulses; aortobifemoral bypass.\n  - *Varicose veins CEAP classification:* C2 (varicose veins), C4a (pigmentation or eczema).\n  - *Saphenous nerve injury after varicose-vein surgery:* the saphenous nerve (sensory) supplies the medial leg; injury causes numbness there.\n  - *Gaiter-area eczema and pigmentation in chronic venous disease:* stasis dermatitis; compression.\n  - *Pulsatile neck swelling:* Doppler evaluation before biopsy (vascular lesion).\n  - *Acute aortic dissection:* tearing pain, pulse difference, widened mediastinum; Stanford type A (surgery); type B (medical).\n  - *Hand-arm vibration syndrome:* occupational; Raynaud and bony changes.\n  - *Pearl:* in aortic dissection, pulse and BP differences between arms are a clue.\n\n### Additional key tables and mnemonics\n\n#### Table: Thyroidectomy Nerve Complications\n\n| Nerve Injured | Artery in Proximity | Muscle Paralyzed | Clinical Deficit |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| External Branch of Superior Laryngeal (EBSLN) | Superior Thyroid Artery | Cricothyroid (sole vocal cord tensor) | Loss of high pitch, inability to sing high notes, vocal fatigue |\n| Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) - Unilateral | Inferior Thyroid Artery | All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid | Hoarseness of voice, breathy voice, bovine cough |\n| Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN) - Bilateral | Inferior Thyroid Artery | Bilateral intrinsic laryngeal muscles | Bilateral cord adduction to midline -> Severe acute inspiratory stridor & asphyxia (Tracheostomy!) |\n\n- **Mnemonic:** **Acute Limb Ischemia 6 Ps:** Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness, Paresthesia (earliest!), Poikilothermia (cold), Paralysis.\n"};
